window.PQ_DATA={"built":"2026-10-10","questions":[{"id":"SPC-01","topic":"space","sub":"set operations on a product space","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A retailer sells gift cards in 4 colours (red, green, blue, gold) and 6 denominations (\\$10, \\$20, \\$30, \\$40, \\$50, \\$60), so there are 24 distinct cards. Let $A$ = \"denomination is at least \\$40\", $B$ = \"the card is gold\" and $C$ = \"the denomination is a multiple of \\$20\". How many of the 24 cards belong to $A\\cup B\\cup C$?","data":"","answer":{"value":18,"display":"18"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"18","correct":true,"why":"","v":18},{"text":"30","correct":false,"why":"This adds $|A|+|B|+|C| = 12+6+12$ and ignores that many cards belong to two or three of the events.","v":30},{"text":"6","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of cards in the <i>complement</i> of the union, not in the union itself.","v":6},{"text":"12","correct":false,"why":"This is the size of $A$ (or $C$) alone; $B$ and $C$ contribute further cards.","v":12}],"solution":["The union is awkward to count directly, so count its complement: $(A\\cup B\\cup C)^c = A^c\\cap B^c\\cap C^c$.","A card in the complement is not gold (3 colours), has a denomination below \\$40 (10, 20, 30) and is not a multiple of \\$20 (so 10 or 30 only): $3\\times 2 = 6$ cards.","$|A\\cup B\\cup C| = 24 - 6 = 18$."],"hints":["Counting cards that fail all three conditions at once is easier than listing the union.","The complement of a union is the intersection of the complements (De Morgan).","Non-gold: 3 colours. Below \\$40 and not a multiple of \\$20: which denominations remain?"],"concept":"Counting a union through the complement of an intersection of complements.","misconception":"Adding the sizes of overlapping events.","why":"Every card is in the union or in its complement exactly once, so the two counts sum to 24.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"SPC-03","topic":"space","sub":"event algebra in words","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three regional warehouses may each run out of stock in a given week. Let $A$, $B$ and $C$ be the events that warehouse 1, 2 and 3 runs out. Which expression describes \"<b>exactly one</b> warehouse runs out of stock\"?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$(A\\cap B^c\\cap C^c)\\cup(A^c\\cap B\\cap C^c)\\cup(A^c\\cap B^c\\cap C)$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$(A\\cap B^c\\cap C^c)\\cup(A^c\\cap B\\cap C^c)\\cup(A^c\\cap B^c\\cap C)$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$A\\cup B\\cup C$","correct":false,"why":"This is \"at least one runs out\"; it also includes weeks when two or three run out."},{"text":"$(A\\cap B)\\cup(A\\cap C)\\cup(B\\cap C)$","correct":false,"why":"This describes \"at least two run out\", the opposite end of the scale."},{"text":"$A^c\\cup B^c\\cup C^c$","correct":false,"why":"This is \"at least one has stock\", i.e. at most two run out; it includes the case where none runs out."}],"solution":["\"Exactly one\" means one warehouse is out of stock while the other two are not. Warehouse 1 alone: $A\\cap B^c\\cap C^c$; warehouse 2 alone: $A^c\\cap B\\cap C^c$; warehouse 3 alone: $A^c\\cap B^c\\cap C$.","These three cases are disjoint and together cover the event, so the event is their union."],"hints":["Translate \"exactly one\" into: which warehouse is the only one out?","For each choice of that warehouse, the other two must be in the complement of their events.","Combine the three disjoint cases with a union."],"concept":"Translating verbal conditions into set expressions.","misconception":"Confusing \"exactly one\" with \"at least one\".","why":"Each outcome of the three-warehouse experiment falls in exactly one of the three single-failure cases.","fam":"space:event-algebra"},{"id":"SPC-04","topic":"space","sub":"counting event outcomes","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A spinner with 5 equal sectors numbered 1 to 5 and a second spinner with 4 equal sectors numbered 1 to 4 are spun once each, giving 20 equally likely outcomes. Let $E$ = \"the sum is even\" and $F$ = \"at least one spinner shows 4\". How many outcomes lie in $E\\cup F$?","data":"","answer":{"value":15,"display":"15"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"15","correct":true,"why":"","v":15},{"text":"18","correct":false,"why":"This is $|E|+|F| = 10+8$; outcomes in both events (such as (4,2) and (2,4)) were counted twice.","v":18},{"text":"10","correct":false,"why":"This is $|E|$ only; the outcomes that contain a 4 but have an odd sum are also in the union.","v":10},{"text":"12","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts too much; the overlap $E\\cap F$ has 3 outcomes, not 6.","v":12}],"solution":["$|E|$: first spinner odd (1,3,5) with second odd (1,3): 6; first even (2,4) with second even (2,4): 4. So $|E| = 10$.","$|F|$: first = 4 gives 4 outcomes, second = 4 gives 5, minus the shared (4,4): $|F| = 8$.","$E\\cap F$: (4,2), (4,4), (2,4): 3 outcomes.","$|E\\cup F| = 10+8-3 = 15$."],"hints":["Use $|E\\cup F| = |E|+|F|-|E\\cap F|$ and count each piece separately.","How many spinner pairs have an even sum when the spinners have 3 odd and 2 even values, and 2 odd and 2 even values?","The overlap needs a 4 on at least one spinner and an even sum: list them."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion for counting outcomes in a union of two events.","misconception":"Not subtracting the overlap of two events.","why":"An outcome in both events appears once in each count, so the overlap must be removed once.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"SPC-05","topic":"space","sub":"system reliability events","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A payment gateway has five independent modules $a, b, c, d, e$, each either up (1) or down (0), giving 32 possible states. The gateway is operational if modules $a$ and $b$ are both up, <b>or</b> modules $c$, $d$ and $e$ are all up, <b>or</b> modules $a$ and $e$ are both up. In how many of the 32 states is the gateway operational?","data":"","answer":{"value":14,"display":"14"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"14","correct":true,"why":"","v":14},{"text":"20","correct":false,"why":"This adds the three single-path counts $8+4+8$ without correcting for states that satisfy more than one path.","v":20},{"text":"13","correct":false,"why":"The triple overlap has been left out of the inclusion-exclusion sum: the last term $+|ABC|$ is missing.","v":13},{"text":"18","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of states in which the gateway is <i>not</i> operational ($32-14$).","v":18}],"solution":["Let $P_1$: $a,b$ up (8 states), $P_2$: $c,d,e$ up (4 states), $P_3$: $a,e$ up (8 states).","Pairs: $P_1P_2$ forces all five up (1); $P_1P_3$ needs $a,b,e$ up and leaves $c,d$ free (4); $P_2P_3$ needs $a,c,d,e$ up and leaves $b$ free (2). Triple: 1.","$|P_1\\cup P_2\\cup P_3| = 20 - (1+4+2) + 1 = 14$."],"hints":["Define one event per route and use inclusion-exclusion on three events.","To intersect two routes, take all modules required by either route; the remaining modules are free.","Singles: 8, 4, 8. Pairs: 1, 4, 2. Triple: 1."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion over three overlapping events.","misconception":"Summing the counts of overlapping events.","why":"Each state satisfying several routes is counted once by the alternating sum.","fam":"space:system-states"},{"id":"SPC-06","topic":"space","sub":"event algebra with probabilities","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three suppliers $X$, $Y$, $Z$ each may deliver late in a month. $P(X)=0.40$, $P(Y)=0.30$, $P(Z)=0.25$; $P(X\\cap Y)=0.12$, $P(X\\cap Z)=0.08$, $P(Y\\cap Z)=0.06$ and $P(X\\cap Y\\cap Z)=0.03$. What is the probability that <b>at least one of $X$ and $Y$ is late but $Z$ is not</b>, i.e. $P\\big((X\\cup Y)\\cap Z^c\\big)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.47,"display":"0.47"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.47","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.47},{"text":"0.33","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts $P(Z)$ instead of $P\\big((X\\cup Y)\\cap Z\\big)$: late deliveries by $Z$ outside $X\\cup Y$ were never in the event.","v":0.32999999999999996},{"text":"0.58","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X\\cup Y)$; it still includes months when $Z$ is also late.","v":0.58},{"text":"0.49","correct":false,"why":"This assumes $Z$ is independent of both and also ignores the overlap of $X$ and $Y$.","v":0.49}],"solution":["$P(X\\cup Y) = 0.40+0.30-0.12 = 0.58$.","The part of $X\\cup Y$ in which $Z$ is late is $(X\\cap Z)\\cup(Y\\cap Z)$, with probability $0.08+0.06-0.03 = 0.11$.","$P\\big((X\\cup Y)\\cap Z^c\\big) = 0.58-0.11 = 0.47$."],"hints":["Split $X\\cup Y$ into the part where $Z$ is late and the part where $Z$ is not.","Use $P(A\\cap Z^c)=P(A)-P(A\\cap Z)$ with $A = X\\cup Y$.","$A\\cap Z = (X\\cap Z)\\cup(Y\\cap Z)$; apply the addition rule to that union."],"concept":"Evaluating a composite event by partitioning on a third event.","misconception":"Subtracting $P(Z)$ rather than the intersection with the union.","why":"Partitioning $A$ into $A\\cap Z$ and $A\\cap Z^c$ is valid for any events.","fam":"space:event-algebra"},{"id":"SPC-07","topic":"space","sub":"non-uniform outcomes","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A juice bar offers five flavours. Mango sells three times as many cups as each of the other four flavours, which all sell equally. A cup sold is picked at random from the day's sales. What is the probability that it is mango or lemon?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5714285714285714,"display":"4/7"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"4/7","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5714285714285714},{"text":"2/5","correct":false,"why":"This treats the five flavours as equally likely; mango sells three times as often.","v":0.4},{"text":"3/7","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of mango alone; lemon must be added.","v":0.42857142857142855},{"text":"1/7","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of one of the ordinary flavours (lemon) alone.","v":0.14285714285714285}],"solution":["Let each ordinary flavour account for 1 unit of sales; mango has 3 units. Total: $3+4 = 7$ units.","$P(\\text{mango or lemon}) = \\dfrac{3+1}{7} = \\dfrac47$."],"hints":["The outcomes are not equally likely; give each flavour a weight.","Take one ordinary flavour as 1 unit. How many units is the total?","Add the weights of the two flavours named and divide by the total."],"concept":"Assigning probabilities in proportion to weights.","misconception":"Assuming all outcomes are equally likely.","why":"Probabilities are the weights divided by their total so that they sum to 1.","fam":"space:weighted-outcomes"},{"id":"SPC-09","topic":"space","sub":"diagnose: equally likely outcomes","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Two fair coins are tossed. A student lists the outcomes \"0 heads, 1 head, 2 heads\" and says each has probability $1/3$. What is the correct probability of <b>exactly one head</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5,"display":"1/2"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/2","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"These three outcomes are not equally likely. The equally likely outcomes are HH, HT, TH, TT.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of one specific outcome such as HT.","v":0.25},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This has no basis in the sample space.","v":0.6666666666666666}],"solution":["Equally likely outcomes: HH, HT, TH, TT. \"1 head\" is {HT, TH}: $2/4 = 1/2$.","Counting the <i>number</i> of heads merges two equally likely outcomes into one category."],"hints":["Are \"0 heads\", \"1 head\" and \"2 heads\" all equally likely?","List the outcomes with the two coins distinguished.","Count outcomes with exactly one head."],"concept":"Equally likely outcomes in a fine-grained sample space.","misconception":"Applying the classical formula to categories that are not equally likely.","why":"Events defined by a count combine different numbers of elementary outcomes.","fam":"space:diagnose"},{"id":"SPC-10","topic":"space","sub":"event algebra in words: partition check","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A customer's complaint to a bank is logged as relating to a card ($C$), a loan ($L$) or an account ($A$); some complaints involve more than one product, and every complaint involves at least one. Which pair of events is a <b>partition</b> of the sample space (mutually exclusive and exhaustive)?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$C$ and $C^c$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$C$ and $C^c$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$C$ and $L$","correct":false,"why":"A complaint can involve both a card and a loan, so these events are not mutually exclusive, and a complaint about only an account is in neither."},{"text":"$C\\cap L$ and $C\\cup L$","correct":false,"why":"These overlap: $C\\cap L\\subset C\\cup L$. They are neither disjoint nor exhaustive."},{"text":"$A$ and $L\\cup C$","correct":false,"why":"Complaints about an account and a loan are in both, so the events are not disjoint."}],"solution":["A partition needs events that cannot both occur and that together cover every outcome.","$C$ and $C^c$ satisfy both requirements by definition.","The other pairs overlap (complaints can involve several products) or miss outcomes."],"hints":["Test each pair for overlap: can both events occur together?","Then test whether their union is the whole sample space.","Only a pair built from an event and its complement is guaranteed to be both."],"concept":"Partitions: mutually exclusive and exhaustive events.","misconception":"Treating \"different categories\" as mutually exclusive when outcomes can belong to several.","why":"An event and its complement always split the sample space cleanly.","fam":"space:event-algebra"},{"id":"SPC-11","topic":"space","sub":"event relations: subset in words","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Every small business that qualifies for a government grant ($G$) is also registered for VAT ($V$). Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$G\\subset V$, so $P(G)\\le P(V)$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$G\\subset V$, so $P(G)\\le P(V)$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$V\\subset G$, so $P(V)\\le P(G)$","correct":false,"why":"The implication runs the other way: a grant implies VAT registration, not the reverse."},{"text":"$G$ and $V$ are mutually exclusive","correct":false,"why":"If every grant firm is VAT registered, the events overlap completely on $G$."},{"text":"$P(G)=P(V)$","correct":false,"why":"There can be VAT-registered businesses without a grant, so $V$ may be larger."}],"solution":["\"Every $G$ is a $V$\" means each outcome in $G$ is also in $V$: $G\\subset V$.","Monotonicity of probability then gives $P(G)\\le P(V)$."],"hints":["Read \"every $G$ is also $V$\" as a statement about which set sits inside which.","If $G$ lies inside $V$, which has the larger probability?","Check the statements that claim exclusivity."],"concept":"Subset relations and monotonicity.","misconception":"Reversing the direction of an implication.","why":"A subset cannot be more probable than its superset.","fam":"space:event-algebra"},{"id":"SPC-12","topic":"space","sub":"choosing the correct sample space","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair dice are rolled. A student takes as the sample space the 21 unordered pairs $\\{1,1\\},\\{1,2\\},\\dots,\\{6,6\\}$ and says each is equally likely, so $P(\\text{a 1 and a 2}) = 1/21$. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"The 21 pairs are not equally likely; $P(\\text{a 1 and a 2}) = 2/36 = 1/18$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"The 21 pairs are not equally likely; $P(\\text{a 1 and a 2}) = 2/36 = 1/18$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The student is right: unordered pairs are the natural outcomes, so $1/21$","correct":false,"why":"The pair $\\{1,2\\}$ can arise two ways (1 then 2, or 2 then 1) but $\\{1,1\\}$ only one way, so the 21 pairs have unequal probabilities."},{"text":"The correct value is $1/36$, since each ordered pair has probability $1/36$ and $(1,2)$ is one pair","correct":false,"why":"Both $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$ give \"a 1 and a 2\", so two ordered outcomes count."},{"text":"The correct value is $1/6$","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the first die shows a 1, ignoring the second die."}],"solution":["Use the 36 ordered pairs, which are equally likely for two fair dice.","\"A 1 and a 2\" consists of $(1,2)$ and $(2,1)$: 2 outcomes.","$P = 2/36 = 1/18$. The pair $\\{1,1\\}$ has probability $1/36$, half that of $\\{1,2\\}$."],"hints":["Which representation gives outcomes of equal probability?","In how many ordered ways can a 1 and a 2 appear?","Divide the count of favourable ordered pairs by 36."],"concept":"Equally likely outcomes require the right sample space.","misconception":"Using unordered pairs as equally likely outcomes.","why":"The probability model comes from the physical process, which distinguishes the dice.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"SPC-13","topic":"space","sub":"counting outcomes of a sequential experiment","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A support ticket is first classified as <i>urgent</i> or <i>routine</i>. Urgent tickets are then sent to one of 3 senior agents; routine tickets are sent to one of 5 junior agents and then flagged either <i>resolved</i> or <i>open</i>. How many distinct outcomes does this process have?","data":"","answer":{"value":13,"display":"13"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Urgent branch: 3 outcomes (one per senior agent).","Routine branch: $5\\times2 = 10$ outcomes (agent, then status).","The branches are exclusive: $3+10 = 13$."],"hints":["Draw the tree: how does it split after the first classification?","The branches have different numbers of stages; count each branch separately.","Add the branch counts, multiply within a branch."],"concept":"Counting outcomes of a tree with branches of different shape.","misconception":"Multiplying all the numbers together as if every branch had the same stages.","why":"Alternatives add; successive stages multiply.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"SPC-14","topic":"space","sub":"valid probability assignments","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A sample space has four outcomes $a,b,c,d$. Which assignment of probabilities is <b>valid</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(a)=0.1,\\ P(b)=0.4,\\ P(c)=0.3,\\ P(d)=0.2$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(a)=0.1,\\ P(b)=0.4,\\ P(c)=0.3,\\ P(d)=0.2$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(a)=0.5,\\ P(b)=0.3,\\ P(c)=0.3,\\ P(d)=0.1$","correct":false,"why":"The values sum to 1.2, which exceeds 1."},{"text":"$P(a)=0.6,\\ P(b)=0.5,\\ P(c)=-0.2,\\ P(d)=0.1$","correct":false,"why":"A probability cannot be negative, even though the values sum to 1."},{"text":"$P(a)=0.2,\\ P(b)=0.2,\\ P(c)=0.2,\\ P(d)=0.2$","correct":false,"why":"The values sum to 0.8, not 1."}],"solution":["A valid assignment needs every probability in $[0,1]$ and a total of exactly 1.","$0.1+0.4+0.3+0.2 = 1$ with all values non-negative.","The others break one of these rules."],"hints":["State the two requirements for probabilities on a finite sample space.","Add the four values for each candidate.","Check for negative entries."],"concept":"Axioms of probability on a finite sample space.","misconception":"Checking the sum but not the sign, or the reverse.","why":"Non-negativity and total probability one are the axioms that all other rules follow from.","fam":"space:regions"},{"id":"SPC-15","topic":"space","sub":"odds and probability: representation","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A forecaster states that the odds <b>in favour</b> of the central bank raising rates next month are 3 to 2. What probability does this correspond to?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6,"display":"0.6"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Odds in favour of $a$ to $b$ correspond to probability $a/(a+b)$.","$P = 3/(3+2) = 3/5 = 0.6$."],"hints":["Odds compare \"for\" with \"against\", not \"for\" with \"total\".","How many equally weighted parts are there in total?","Probability = favourable parts / all parts."],"concept":"Converting odds to probability.","misconception":"Reading 3 to 2 as a probability of 3/2 or 2/3.","why":"Odds of $a:b$ split probability mass into $a$ parts for and $b$ parts against.","fam":"space:regions"},{"id":"SPC-16","topic":"space","sub":"event algebra in words: De Morgan","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A delivery is \"problem-free\" if it is neither late nor damaged. With $L$ = \"late\" and $D$ = \"damaged\", which expression describes \"problem-free\"?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$L^c\\cap D^c$, which equals $(L\\cup D)^c$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$L^c\\cap D^c$, which equals $(L\\cup D)^c$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$L^c\\cup D^c$","correct":false,"why":"This is \"not late or not damaged\", which holds even for a delivery that is late but undamaged."},{"text":"$(L\\cap D)^c$","correct":false,"why":"This is \"not both late and damaged\", which allows a delivery that is late or damaged alone."},{"text":"$L\\cap D$","correct":false,"why":"This is \"late and damaged\", the opposite extreme."}],"solution":["Problem-free means no late and no damage: $L^c\\cap D^c$.","By De Morgan's law $(L\\cup D)^c = L^c\\cap D^c$.","The other expressions allow one of the problems to occur."],"hints":["Translate \"neither ... nor\" into a statement about each event separately.","Both complements must hold at once: which set operation?","De Morgan: the complement of a union is an intersection of complements."],"concept":"De Morgan’s laws in words.","misconception":"Converting \"neither\" into a union of complements.","why":"To avoid a union you must avoid every member of it.","fam":"space:event-algebra"},{"id":"SPC-17","topic":"space","sub":"sample space size: paths","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A stock's closing price moves up (U) or down (D) on each of 8 consecutive days. How many of the $2^8$ possible up-down sequences have <b>exactly 5 up days</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":56,"display":"56"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Choosing which 5 of the 8 days are up days determines the sequence.","Number of sequences $=\\binom85 = 56$."],"hints":["A sequence is determined by the set of days on which the price rises.","How many ways are there to choose 5 days out of 8?","Use the binomial coefficient."],"concept":"Number of sequences with a given count of successes.","misconception":"Using $5!$ or $2^5$.","why":"Each qualifying sequence corresponds to exactly one 5-element subset of days.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"SPC-18","topic":"space","sub":"equally likely fallacy","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A student says: \"A new product either succeeds or fails, so there are two outcomes and the probability of success is 1/2.\" What is wrong with this reasoning?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Two possible outcomes does not make them equally likely; the success probability has to come from evidence or a model"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Two possible outcomes does not make them equally likely; the success probability has to come from evidence or a model","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Nothing is wrong: with two outcomes each has probability 1/2","correct":false,"why":"The classical formula needs <i>equally likely</i> outcomes; success and failure rarely are."},{"text":"The student should have used 1/3 to allow for a partial success","correct":false,"why":"Adding a third category does not make probabilities equal either."},{"text":"The probability of success must be below 1/2 for any new product","correct":false,"why":"There is no such rule; the probability depends on the product and the market."}],"solution":["Classical probability = favourable / total only when all outcomes are equally likely.","Success and failure are two outcomes but not necessarily equally likely. The probability must come from data, a model or judgement."],"hints":["What condition must hold before counting outcomes gives probabilities?","Are \"succeeds\" and \"fails\" equally likely here?","Where else can a probability come from?"],"concept":"Limits of the classical definition of probability.","misconception":"Assuming equal likelihood from the number of outcomes.","why":"Probabilities describe uncertainty, not just a count of possibilities.","fam":"space:diagnose"},{"id":"SPC-19","topic":"space","sub":"regions of a Venn diagram: missing probability","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A survey classifies respondents by whether they hold savings ($S$) and whether they hold a loan ($L$). The four regions have probabilities $P(S\\cap L) = 0.18$, $P(S\\cap L^c) = 0.42$ and $P(S^c\\cap L) = 0.13$. What is $P(S^c\\cap L^c)$, the probability of holding neither?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2700000000000001,"display":"0.27"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["The four regions partition the sample space, so their probabilities sum to 1.","$P(S^c\\cap L^c) = 1-0.18-0.42-0.13 = 0.27$."],"hints":["How many disjoint regions does the Venn diagram of two events have?","The regions together make up the entire sample space.","Subtract the known regions from 1."],"concept":"Probabilities of Venn regions sum to one.","misconception":"Using the marginals instead of the regions.","why":"The regions are mutually exclusive and exhaustive.","fam":"space:regions"},{"id":"SPC-20","topic":"space","sub":"events defined by a random variable","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair coins are tossed and $X$ is the number of heads. Which of the following events is the same as \"$X \\ge 1$\"?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"\"at least one head\", whose probability is $3/4$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"\"at least one head\", whose probability is $3/4$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"\"exactly one head\", whose probability is $1/2$","correct":false,"why":"The event $X\\ge1$ also includes the outcome with two heads."},{"text":"\"the first coin shows a head\", whose probability is $1/2$","correct":false,"why":"The outcome (tails, heads) has $X=1$ but the first coin is a tail."},{"text":"\"two heads\", whose probability is $1/4$","correct":false,"why":"That is $X=2$; the event $X\\ge1$ contains $X=1$ as well."}],"solution":["$X\\ge1$ means $X=1$ or $X=2$: outcomes HT, TH, HH.","$P = 3/4$, equivalently $1-P(TT) = 1-\\tfrac14$."],"hints":["List the outcomes of the two tosses and the value of $X$ on each.","Which outcomes have $X\\ge1$?","Count them or use the complement of $X=0$."],"concept":"Describing an event through a random variable.","misconception":"Confusing \"at least\" with \"exactly\".","why":"The event is a set of outcomes; naming it by $X$ does not change which outcomes it contains.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"SPC-21","topic":"space","sub":"event algebra: distributive law","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Which expression is equal to $(A\\cup B)\\cap C$?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$(A\\cap C)\\cup(B\\cap C)$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$(A\\cap C)\\cup(B\\cap C)$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$(A\\cap B)\\cup C$","correct":false,"why":"This contains all of $C$, even outcomes of $C$ outside $A\\cup B$."},{"text":"$A\\cup(B\\cap C)$","correct":false,"why":"This contains all of $A$, including outcomes of $A$ outside $C$."},{"text":"$(A\\cup C)\\cap(B\\cup C)$","correct":false,"why":"This equals $(A\\cap B)\\cup C$ by the other distributive law, not $(A\\cup B)\\cap C$."}],"solution":["Intersection distributes over union: $(A\\cup B)\\cap C = (A\\cap C)\\cup(B\\cap C)$.","An outcome is in the left side iff it is in $C$ and in $A$ or $B$, i.e. in $A\\cap C$ or in $B\\cap C$."],"hints":["Check membership of a single outcome: what must be true for it to belong to the left side?","It must be in $C$ and in at least one of $A$, $B$.","Which candidate expresses exactly that?"],"concept":"Distributive laws of sets.","misconception":"Mixing up which operation distributes over which.","why":"Both operations distribute over each other; the correct form comes from checking membership.","fam":"space:event-algebra"},{"id":"SPC-22","topic":"space","sub":"multi-select survey coding","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A survey asks which of 5 payment methods a respondent has used in the past month; each respondent may tick any number of methods, including none. How many different responses tick <b>at least one</b> method?","data":"","answer":{"value":31,"display":"31"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Each method is ticked or not: $2^5 = 32$ possible responses.","Excluding the empty response leaves $32-1 = 31$."],"hints":["Each method has two possible states.","The choices for different methods are independent: multiply.","Remove the single response in which nothing is ticked."],"concept":"Counting subsets: $2^n$.","misconception":"Counting only responses with exactly one method ticked.","why":"Subsets correspond to binary vectors of length $n$.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"SPC-23","topic":"space","sub":"long-run frequency interpretation","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A weather service says \"there is a 30% chance of rain tomorrow\". Which statement best matches this probability in the frequency interpretation?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"On about 30% of days with conditions like tomorrow’s, it rains"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"On about 30% of days with conditions like tomorrow’s, it rains","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"It will rain in 30% of the region tomorrow","correct":false,"why":"That would be an area proportion; the probability is about occurrence of rain, not coverage."},{"text":"It will rain for 30% of the day","correct":false,"why":"That is a statement about duration, not about the chance that rain occurs at all."},{"text":"The forecaster is 30% sure that she is correct","correct":false,"why":"The probability refers to the event \"rain\", not to the correctness of the forecast."}],"solution":["In the frequency interpretation, a probability of 0.3 means that in a long series of similar situations the event occurs in about 30% of them.","It does not describe the area covered or the duration of rain."],"hints":["What would you observe over many days with the same forecast?","Is the probability about where, how long, or whether?","Pick the statement about the long-run proportion of such days."],"concept":"Frequency interpretation of probability.","misconception":"Reading a probability as a proportion of area or time.","why":"A probability is a statement about uncertainty of occurrence.","fam":"space:diagnose"},{"id":"SPC-24","topic":"space","sub":"regions: count with three sets and none","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Of 120 customers, 55 bought product $X$, 48 bought $Y$ and 40 bought $Z$. 20 bought both $X$ and $Y$, 15 both $X$ and $Z$, 12 both $Y$ and $Z$, and 6 bought all three. How many customers bought <b>none</b> of the three products?","data":"","answer":{"value":18,"display":"18"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Customers buying at least one product: $55+48+40-20-15-12+6 = 102$.","None: $120-102 = 18$."],"hints":["First find how many bought at least one product.","Use inclusion-exclusion: add singles, subtract pairs, add the triple.","Subtract from 120."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion for three sets.","misconception":"Forgetting to add back the triple intersection.","why":"The alternating sum counts each customer exactly once.","fam":"space:regions"},{"id":"SPC-25","topic":"space","sub":"event algebra with probabilities: symmetric difference","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"For events $A$ and $B$, $P(A)=0.45$, $P(B)=0.50$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.70$. What is the probability that <b>exactly one</b> of $A$ and $B$ occurs?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.44999999999999996,"display":"0.45"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.45","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.44999999999999996},{"text":"0.7","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least one occurs, which includes the case of both.","v":0.7},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(A\\cap B)$, the probability that both occur.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of A without B only; B without A must be added.","v":0.2}],"solution":["$P(A\\cap B) = 0.45+0.50-0.70 = 0.25$.","Exactly one: $(0.45-0.25)+(0.50-0.25) = 0.45$.","Equivalently $P(A\\cup B)-P(A\\cap B) = 0.70-0.25 = 0.45$."],"hints":["Find $P(A\\cap B)$ from the addition rule.","Exactly one = at least one minus both.","Compute $0.70 - P(A\\cap B)$."],"concept":"Exactly-one events from union and intersection.","misconception":"Reporting the union.","why":"The union splits into \"exactly one\" and \"both\".","fam":"space:event-algebra"},{"id":"SPC-26","topic":"space","sub":"sample space: no two consecutive","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A trader records each of 6 days as a gain (G) or a loss (L). How many of the $2^6$ records contain <b>no two consecutive losses</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":21,"display":"21"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Let $a_n$ be the number of valid records of length $n$. A valid record ends in G (preceded by any valid record of length $n-1$, so $a_{n-1}$ ways) or in L, in which case the previous day must be G (preceded by a valid record of length $n-2$, so $a_{n-2}$ ways).","$a_n = a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}$ with $a_1 = 2$, $a_2 = 3$: $5, 8, 13, 21$.","$a_6 = 21$."],"hints":["Think about how a valid record of length $n$ can end.","If it ends in a loss, what must the day before be?","This gives a Fibonacci-type recursion; start with $a_1 = 2$, $a_2 = 3$."],"concept":"Counting restricted sequences by recursion.","misconception":"Subtracting arrangement counts without a systematic scheme.","why":"The recursion splits valid records by their last symbol.","fam":"space:count-events"},{"id":"CNT-01","topic":"counting","sub":"multiplication principle with restrictions","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A company issues employee IDs made of one department letter from $\\{A,B,C,D,E\\}$ followed by a four-digit number. The first digit of the number may not be 0 and the last digit must be odd; the middle two digits are unrestricted. How many different IDs can be issued?","data":"","answer":{"value":22500,"display":"22,500"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"22,500","correct":true,"why":"","v":22500},{"text":"50,000","correct":false,"why":"This ignores both restrictions on the number: the first digit cannot be 0 and the last must be odd.","v":50000},{"text":"45,000","correct":false,"why":"The restriction on the last digit (odd) has not been applied.","v":45000},{"text":"18,000","correct":false,"why":"There are 5 odd digits (1, 3, 5, 7, 9), not 4.","v":18000}],"solution":["Fill the positions one at a time and multiply the number of choices.","Letter: 5. First digit: 9 (1 to 9). Two middle digits: 10 each. Last digit: 5 (odd).","$5\\times9\\times10\\times10\\times5 = 22\\,500$."],"hints":["Choose each character in turn and ask how many options remain for that position.","The first digit has 9 options, the last has 5; they can be chosen independently of the others.","Multiply: 5 letters, 9, 10, 10, 5."],"concept":"Multiplication principle with position-specific restrictions.","misconception":"Applying a restriction to the wrong position or forgetting one.","why":"The number of codes is the product of the choices at each independent position.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-02","topic":"counting","sub":"arrangements with a positional restriction","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four panellists and a moderator are to sit in a row of five chairs for a broadcast. The moderator must take one of the two end chairs. In how many different ways can the five people be seated?","data":"","answer":{"value":48,"display":"48"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"48","correct":true,"why":"","v":48},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the restriction on the moderator and counts all arrangements of five people.","v":120},{"text":"24","correct":false,"why":"The moderator can take either end chair; this counts only one of them.","v":24},{"text":"12","correct":false,"why":"After placing the moderator, all four panellists can be arranged in the remaining chairs: $4!$, not $3!$.","v":12}],"solution":["The moderator has 2 possible chairs. The four panellists then fill the remaining four chairs in $4! = 24$ ways.","Total: $2\\times24 = 48$."],"hints":["Place the person with the restriction first.","How many choices does the moderator have, and then how many people remain to arrange?","Multiply the two stages."],"concept":"Counting permutations after fixing a restricted object.","misconception":"Using $5!$ and forgetting the restriction.","why":"The moderator’s choice and the panellists’ order are independent stages.","fam":"count:arrange"},{"id":"CNT-03","topic":"counting","sub":"arrangements with blocks","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Six distinct books are placed on a shelf: 3 statistics books, 2 econometrics books and 1 dictionary. The three statistics books must stand together and the two econometrics books must stand together. How many arrangements are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":72,"display":"72"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"72","correct":true,"why":"","v":72},{"text":"12","correct":false,"why":"This arranges the statistics books among themselves and the econometrics books among themselves but never orders the three blocks.","v":12},{"text":"720","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the requirement that each subject’s books stand together.","v":720},{"text":"144","correct":false,"why":"The order within the two-book block is already included once as $2!$; doubling counts it again.","v":144}],"solution":["Treat the statistics books as one block and the econometrics books as another. With the dictionary there are 3 units, arranged in $3! = 6$ ways.","Within the statistics block the books can be ordered in $3! = 6$ ways, within the econometrics block in $2! = 2$ ways.","$6\\times6\\times2 = 72$."],"hints":["Think of each group that must stay together as a single object first.","How many objects are there on the shelf once the blocks are formed, and in how many orders?","Then multiply by the number of orderings inside each block."],"concept":"Counting arrangements with several blocks.","misconception":"Forgetting to order the blocks themselves.","why":"Block order and within-block order are independent choices, so their counts multiply.","fam":"count:arrange"},{"id":"CNT-04","topic":"counting","sub":"circular arrangements","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three couples are to be seated at a round table with six identical chairs. Two seatings are the same if one is a rotation of the other. In how many distinct ways can they be seated if each couple must sit side by side?","data":"","answer":{"value":16,"display":"16"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"16","correct":true,"why":"","v":16},{"text":"48","correct":false,"why":"Three blocks around a circle can be arranged in $(3-1)! = 2$ ways, not $3!$; rotations are the same seating.","v":48},{"text":"2","correct":false,"why":"This orders the couples around the table but ignores the two possible orders within each couple.","v":2},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of circular arrangements of six people with no couple restriction.","v":120}],"solution":["Treat each couple as a block: 3 blocks around a circle can be arranged in $(3-1)! = 2$ ways, since rotations are identical.","Within each block the two partners can swap places: $2^3 = 8$ ways.","$2\\times8 = 16$."],"hints":["Combine each couple into one object; how many objects sit around the table?","For $n$ objects around a circle, rotations are equivalent: there are $(n-1)!$ arrangements.","Each couple can be seated in 2 internal orders."],"concept":"Circular permutations combined with blocks.","misconception":"Using $n!$ for a circle without removing rotations.","why":"Fixing one block removes the rotational symmetry, leaving $(n-1)!$ patterns.","fam":"count:arrange"},{"id":"CNT-05","topic":"counting","sub":"arrangements with repeated letters","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A student lists every distinct way of re-ordering all nine letters of the word ECONOMICS, never writing the same string twice. How many entries will the list contain?","data":"","answer":{"value":90720,"display":"90,720"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"90,720","correct":true,"why":"","v":90720},{"text":"362,880","correct":false,"why":"This treats the two C’s and the two O’s as distinguishable letters.","v":362880},{"text":"181,440","correct":false,"why":"Only one repeated letter has been divided out; both C and O occur twice.","v":181440},{"text":"45,360","correct":false,"why":"A third repeated letter has been assumed; only C and O repeat (E, N, M, I, S occur once).","v":45360}],"solution":["ECONOMICS has 9 letters: C occurs twice, O occurs twice, and E, N, M, I, S once each.","If all letters were different there would be $9!$ arrangements; swapping the two C’s or the two O’s gives the same word, so divide by $2!\\,2!$.","$\\dfrac{9!}{2!\\,2!} = 90\\,720$."],"hints":["Count how many times each letter appears in the word.","Start from $9!$ and correct for indistinguishable repeats.","Divide by $2!$ for each letter that occurs twice."],"concept":"Permutations with indistinguishable objects.","misconception":"Using $n!$ when some objects are identical.","why":"Each distinct word is counted $2!\\,2!$ times among the $9!$ labelled arrangements.","fam":"count:multinomial"},{"id":"CNT-06","topic":"counting","sub":"at least one: complement","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fund manager must choose 3 stocks for a portfolio from a watch-list of 12 stocks, of which 4 are technology stocks. How many portfolios contain <b>at least one technology stock</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":164,"display":"164"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"164","correct":true,"why":"","v":164},{"text":"112","correct":false,"why":"This counts only portfolios with exactly one technology stock; portfolios with two or three also qualify.","v":112},{"text":"56","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of portfolios with <i>no</i> technology stock.","v":56},{"text":"220","correct":false,"why":"This counts all portfolios, including those with no technology stock.","v":220}],"solution":["Total portfolios: $\\binom{12}{3} = 220$.","Portfolios with no technology stock use only the 8 others: $\\binom{8}{3} = 56$.","At least one: $220-56 = 164$."],"hints":["\"At least one\" is usually easier through its opposite.","How many portfolios contain no technology stock at all?","Subtract that count from the total number of portfolios."],"concept":"Counting \"at least one\" with the complement.","misconception":"Choosing one special item and then anything, which double counts.","why":"Every portfolio either has no technology stock or at least one, never both.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-07","topic":"counting","sub":"selection then designation","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A department of 10 faculty members forms a 3-person hiring panel and then names one of the three as chair. In how many ways can the panel and its chair be chosen?","data":"","answer":{"value":360,"display":"360"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"360","correct":true,"why":"","v":360},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"This counts only the panels; each panel can be given a chair in three ways.","v":120},{"text":"720","correct":false,"why":"This treats the three roles as all different (ordered selection), but only the chair is special.","v":720},{"text":"1,200","correct":false,"why":"The chair must be one of the three panel members, not any of the ten faculty members.","v":1200}],"solution":["Choose the panel: $\\binom{10}{3} = 120$ ways. Choose the chair from the three: 3 ways.","$120\\times3 = 360$.","Check: choose the chair first (10 ways), then the other two members $\\binom{9}{2} = 36$: $10\\times36 = 360$."],"hints":["Is the order of the panel members relevant? What distinguishes one member?","Choose the panel first, then the chair.","You can verify by choosing the chair first."],"concept":"Combinations followed by a designation.","misconception":"Using permutations for a set where only one member is special.","why":"Two valid orders of choosing must produce the same total.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-08","topic":"counting","sub":"restrictions: inclusion-exclusion on teams","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A project team of 5 is to be chosen from 11 candidates, including individuals $A$, $B$, $P$ and $Q$. $A$ and $B$ will not serve on the same team, and the team must contain <b>at least one</b> of $P$ and $Q$. How many teams are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":287,"display":"287"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"287","correct":true,"why":"","v":287},{"text":"252","correct":false,"why":"Teams containing both $A$ and $B$ <i>and</i> neither of $P$, $Q$ have been subtracted twice; add them back once.","v":252},{"text":"378","correct":false,"why":"Only the restriction on $A$ and $B$ was applied; the team must also contain $P$ or $Q$.","v":378},{"text":"210","correct":false,"why":"This adds the two sets of unwanted teams instead of removing them from the total.","v":210}],"solution":["Total teams: $\\binom{11}{5} = 462$.","Bad type 1: both $A$ and $B$ on the team: $\\binom{9}{3} = 84$.","Bad type 2: neither $P$ nor $Q$: choose all 5 from the other 9: $\\binom{9}{5} = 126$.","Both bad types: $A$, $B$ on, $P$, $Q$ off: choose 3 from the remaining 7: $\\binom{7}{3} = 35$.","Valid: $462 - (84+126-35) = 287$."],"hints":["Count the teams that break at least one rule and subtract from the total.","The two ways of breaking the rules can happen together, so use inclusion-exclusion.","In the overlap, $A$ and $B$ are on the team, $P$ and $Q$ are off, and 3 places are filled from 7 others."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion for selections with two simultaneous constraints.","misconception":"Subtracting two overlapping bad sets without restoring their intersection.","why":"A team that breaks both rules is removed twice by simple subtraction, so it must be added back once.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-09","topic":"counting","sub":"lattice paths through a waypoint","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A delivery robot moves on a city grid from the depot at $(0,0)$ to a customer at $(5,3)$, taking only unit steps east or north. It must pass through the charging point at $(2,1)$. How many different routes are there?","data":"","answer":{"value":30,"display":"30"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"30","correct":true,"why":"","v":30},{"text":"56","correct":false,"why":"This counts all routes from $(0,0)$ to $(5,3)$, ignoring the required charging point.","v":56},{"text":"13","correct":false,"why":"The numbers of routes for the two legs must be multiplied, not added.","v":13},{"text":"60","correct":false,"why":"The second leg has 3 east and 2 north steps: $\\binom{5}{2}$ routes; an extra factor of 2 has no basis.","v":60}],"solution":["Leg 1: $(0,0)\\to(2,1)$ needs 2 east and 1 north steps: $\\binom{3}{1} = 3$ routes.","Leg 2: $(2,1)\\to(5,3)$ needs 3 east and 2 north steps: $\\binom{5}{2} = 10$ routes.","The legs are chosen independently: $3\\times10 = 30$."],"hints":["A route through a given point splits into two independent legs.","Each leg is an arrangement of east and north steps.","Count each leg with a binomial coefficient and multiply."],"concept":"Counting lattice paths with a mandatory waypoint.","misconception":"Counting all paths and forgetting the waypoint, or adding leg counts.","why":"Every valid route corresponds to one choice for each leg.","fam":"count:paths-bars"},{"id":"CNT-10","topic":"counting","sub":"partitions into unlabelled groups","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Ten students are split into a group of 4 for a macroeconomics project and two groups of 3 that will both work on the same microeconomics project (so these two groups are not distinguished from each other). In how many ways can the split be made?","data":"","answer":{"value":2100,"display":"2,100"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"2,100","correct":true,"why":"","v":2100},{"text":"4,200","correct":false,"why":"This treats the two groups of 3 as labelled; swapping them gives the same split, so divide by 2.","v":4200},{"text":"700","correct":false,"why":"Dividing by $3!$ would be right for three identical groups; only the two groups of 3 are identical.","v":700},{"text":"8,400","correct":false,"why":"Multiplying by 2 goes the wrong way: identical groups reduce the number of distinct splits.","v":8400}],"solution":["Choose the group of 4: $\\binom{10}{4} = 210$. Choose one group of 3 from the remaining 6: $\\binom{6}{3} = 20$; the rest form the other group.","That gives $210\\times20 = 4200$ ordered choices, but the two groups of 3 are interchangeable, so each split has been counted twice.","$4200/2 = 2100$."],"hints":["First count as if every group were labelled.","Which groups are interchangeable? How many times is each split counted?","Divide by the number of ways of ordering the identical groups."],"concept":"Partitions into groups with some groups indistinguishable.","misconception":"Treating identical groups as labelled.","why":"Each unlabelled split corresponds to exactly 2 labelled splits.","fam":"count:multinomial"},{"id":"CNT-11","topic":"counting","sub":"multinomial coefficient","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A six-question multiple-choice quiz has options A, B, C and D for every question. How many different answer keys contain exactly two A's, two B's, one C and one D?","data":"","answer":{"value":180,"display":"180"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"180","correct":true,"why":"","v":180},{"text":"720","correct":false,"why":"This treats all six answers as distinguishable; the two A’s are interchangeable, as are the two B’s.","v":720},{"text":"4,096","correct":false,"why":"This counts all possible answer keys, not only those with the specified composition.","v":4096},{"text":"360","correct":false,"why":"The division by $2!\\,2!$ already accounts for the repeated letters; the extra factor has no basis.","v":360}],"solution":["Choose which two questions get A: $\\binom{6}{2}$; which two of the remaining four get B: $\\binom{4}{2}$; which of the last two gets C: $\\binom{2}{1}$; the final question is D.","$15\\times6\\times2 = 180$.","Equivalently $\\dfrac{6!}{2!\\,2!\\,1!\\,1!} = 180$."],"hints":["An answer key is a way of labelling six positions with letters.","Choose the positions for each letter in turn.","The result is a multinomial coefficient."],"concept":"Multinomial coefficients for labelling positions.","misconception":"Not dividing out identical labels.","why":"The multinomial counts distinct arrangements of a multiset.","fam":"count:multinomial"},{"id":"CNT-12","topic":"counting","sub":"distribution of identical objects","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A ministry distributes 14 identical grants among 5 regions. Regions 1 and 2 must each receive at least one grant; the other three regions may receive none. How many distributions are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":1820,"display":"1,820"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"1,820","correct":true,"why":"","v":1820},{"text":"3,060","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the minimum of one grant for regions 1 and 2.","v":3060},{"text":"715","correct":false,"why":"This is the count when every region needs at least one grant; regions 3 to 5 may receive none.","v":715},{"text":"4,368","correct":false,"why":"The number of bars is one less than the number of regions (4), so the second argument should be 4.","v":4368}],"solution":["Give one grant to each of regions 1 and 2 first. That leaves 12 grants to distribute among 5 regions with no restrictions.","Non-negative solutions of $x_1+\\dots+x_5 = 12$: $\\binom{12+4}{4} = \\binom{16}{4} = 1820$."],"hints":["Remove the compulsory grants first; what is left to distribute?","After that there are no restrictions, so use stars and bars.","Non-negative solutions of a sum to $n$ in $k$ variables: $\\binom{n+k-1}{k-1}$."],"concept":"Stars and bars with lower bounds.","misconception":"Using the formula for positive integers when only some variables are bounded below.","why":"Shifting variables by their minimums turns bounded counts into unrestricted ones.","fam":"count:paths-bars"},{"id":"CNT-13","topic":"counting","sub":"sampling with replacement","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three friends each independently pick one of 5 restaurants at random for lunch (every restaurant is equally likely for each friend). What is the probability that all three choose <b>different</b> restaurants?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.48,"display":"12/25"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"12/25","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.48},{"text":"3/5","correct":false,"why":"This is $1 - 2/5$, a guess that ignores that the third friend must avoid two restaurants.","v":0.6},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The sample space for independent choices has $5^3 = 125$ outcomes, not a count of ordered selections without repeats.","v":0.5},{"text":"2/25","correct":false,"why":"The choices are ordered (friend 1, 2, 3), so each set of three restaurants corresponds to $3! = 6$ outcomes.","v":0.08}],"solution":["There are $5^3 = 125$ equally likely outcomes.","Favourable: friend 1 has 5 options, friend 2 has 4 (different from friend 1), friend 3 has 3: $5\\times4\\times3 = 60$.","$P = 60/125 = 12/25 = 0.48$."],"hints":["How many outcomes are there when each friend chooses independently?","Count outcomes in which no restaurant is repeated by choosing in order.","Divide the favourable count by $5^3$."],"concept":"Probability of no repeats in sampling with replacement.","misconception":"Using the without-replacement count for the denominator.","why":"The probability is the ratio of the count of repeat-free sequences to all sequences.","fam":"count:probability"},{"id":"CNT-14","topic":"counting","sub":"onto assignments","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Five parcels are sent, each independently and with equal probability, to one of three depots. What is the probability that <b>every depot receives at least one parcel</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6172839506172839,"display":"50/81"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"50/81","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6172839506172839},{"text":"32/81","correct":false,"why":"This counts the outcomes that leave at least one depot empty (with overlaps counted twice), the opposite of the event asked for.","v":0.3950617283950617},{"text":"49/81","correct":false,"why":"The outcomes in which two depots are empty were subtracted twice; add the 3 such outcomes back.","v":0.6049382716049383},{"text":"10/81","correct":false,"why":"Choosing which depot receives which parcel in a fixed pattern does not count all onto assignments.","v":0.12345679012345678}],"solution":["There are $3^5 = 243$ equally likely assignments.","Let $E_i$ be \"depot $i$ empty\". $|E_i| = 2^5 = 32$, $|E_i\\cap E_j| = 1$, and all three empty is impossible.","At least one empty: $3(32) - 3(1) + 0 = 93$.","Every depot used: $243-93 = 150$, so $P = 150/243 = 50/81 \\approx 0.617$."],"hints":["Count the assignments in which some depot is empty, then take the complement.","Use inclusion-exclusion over the events \"depot $i$ is empty\".","If depots $i$ and $j$ are both empty, every parcel goes to the third depot."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion for onto functions.","misconception":"Subtracting overlapping cases without correcting for their intersections.","why":"Alternating inclusion and exclusion counts each assignment with an empty depot exactly once.","fam":"count:probability"},{"id":"CNT-15","topic":"counting","sub":"inclusion of specific items in a sample","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"An auditor draws a simple random sample of 15 invoices from a batch of 120. Two particular invoices, $X$ and $Y$, are of special interest. What is the probability that <b>exactly one</b> of $X$ and $Y$ is in the sample?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.22058823529411764,"display":"15/68"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"15/68","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.22058823529411764},{"text":"1/68","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that <i>both</i> invoices are selected.","v":0.014705882352941176},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This adds the two inclusion probabilities and counts the case where both are selected twice.","v":0.25},{"text":"7/32","correct":false,"why":"The second factor should use the 119 invoices remaining after $X$ is considered, not the original 120.","v":0.21875}],"solution":["$P(X\\text{ in}) = 15/120$. Given that, $Y$ is out with probability $\\dfrac{105}{119}$ (105 of the 119 other invoices are not selected).","The same holds with $X$ and $Y$ exchanged, and the two cases are disjoint:","$P = 2\\cdot\\dfrac{15}{120}\\cdot\\dfrac{105}{119} = \\dfrac{15}{68} \\approx 0.221$."],"hints":["Split the event into two disjoint cases according to which invoice is selected.","Given $X$ is in the sample, what is the chance that $Y$ is not?","A random sample is symmetric between invoices, so the two cases have equal probability."],"concept":"Inclusion probabilities in simple random sampling.","misconception":"Using the unconditional probability for the second invoice.","why":"Sampling without replacement makes the second inclusion probability depend on the first.","fam":"count:probability"},{"id":"CNT-16","topic":"counting","sub":"dealing into hands","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four players are each dealt 13 cards from a freshly shuffled pack. What is the probability that every player ends up holding <b>exactly one of the four kings</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.10549819927971188,"display":"0.1055"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0003,"options":[{"text":"0.1055","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.10549819927971188},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This treats the four kings as if each were assigned to a player with independent equal chances, which is the right count only for the first king.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.0044","correct":false,"why":"The kings can go to the four players in $4! = 24$ ways; this counts only one assignment.","v":0.004395758303321329},{"text":"0.0938","correct":false,"why":"This uses independent draws with replacement; the kings occupy distinct cards, so the denominator is $52\\cdot51\\cdot50\\cdot49$.","v":0.09375}],"solution":["Think of the positions of the four kings among the 52 dealt positions. Each position belongs to a player (13 positions each).","Favourable ordered placements of the 4 distinct kings: choose a player for each king so all differ ($4!$), and a position in that player’s 13: $4!\\cdot13^4$.","Total ordered placements: $52\\cdot51\\cdot50\\cdot49$.","$P = \\dfrac{24\\cdot28561}{6\\,497\\,400} \\approx 0.1055$."],"hints":["Place the four kings one at a time into the 52 positions.","For a favourable outcome the kings go to four different players: how many assignments of kings to players are there?","Each king then has 13 positions in its player’s hand; the total number of ordered placements is $52\\cdot51\\cdot50\\cdot49$."],"concept":"Counting equally likely placements of distinguished cards.","misconception":"Using independent probabilities for sampling without replacement.","why":"All ordered placements of the kings are equally likely, so probability is a ratio of counts.","fam":"count:probability"},{"id":"CNT-17","topic":"counting","sub":"arrangements of repeated letters: adjacency","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The eleven letters of MISSISSIPPI are arranged in a random order. What is the probability that the four S's appear <b>consecutively</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.024242424242424242,"display":"4/165"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"4/165","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.024242424242424242},{"text":"1/330","correct":false,"why":"After gluing the S’s there are 8 objects (the block, M, four I’s, two P’s) to arrange, not 7.","v":0.0030303030303030303},{"text":"3/110","correct":false,"why":"A run of four adjacent slots among 11 can start at slots 1 to 8, so there are 8 favourable slot sets, not 9.","v":0.02727272727272727},{"text":"4/11","correct":false,"why":"There is no reason for the probability to equal a single-letter proportion.","v":0.36363636363636365}],"solution":["Total distinct arrangements: $\\dfrac{11!}{4!\\,4!\\,2!} = 34\\,650$.","Glue the four S’s into one block. The objects are the block, M, four I’s and two P’s: $\\dfrac{8!}{4!\\,2!} = 840$ arrangements.","$P = 840/34650 \\approx 0.0242$."],"hints":["Count the distinct arrangements of all letters first.","For the favourable count, treat the four S’s as a single block.","The block, M, four I’s and two P’s are the objects to arrange."],"concept":"Probability that identical objects are adjacent.","misconception":"Miscounting the objects after forming a block.","why":"Both counts use the same notion of \"distinct arrangement\", so their ratio is the probability.","fam":"count:multinomial"},{"id":"CNT-19","topic":"counting","sub":"digit problems with cases","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"How many six-digit numbers (no leading zero) have <b>no repeated digit</b> and are <b>divisible by 5</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":28560,"display":"28,560"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"28,560","correct":true,"why":"","v":28560},{"text":"30,240","correct":false,"why":"When the last digit is 5 the first digit has only 8 options (not 0 and not 5), so the two cases have different counts.","v":30240},{"text":"15,120","correct":false,"why":"This counts only numbers ending in 0; numbers ending in 5 also qualify.","v":15120},{"text":"54,432","correct":false,"why":"The last digit is fixed first (0 or 5); the other digits then lose options one at a time.","v":54432}],"solution":["A number divisible by 5 ends in 0 or 5.","Ends in 0: the other five digits are an ordered selection from 1 to 9: $9\\cdot8\\cdot7\\cdot6\\cdot5 = 15\\,120$.","Ends in 5: first digit from $\\{1,\\dots,9\\}\\setminus\\{5\\}$: 8; then four more digits from the 8 remaining (0 is now allowed): $8\\cdot7\\cdot6\\cdot5 = 1680$. Total $8\\times1680 = 13\\,440$.","Sum: $28\\,560$."],"hints":["Split by the last digit, since it determines divisibility.","When the last digit is 0, there is no further restriction on the leading digit; when it is 5, 0 is no longer excluded from the middle but the first digit still cannot be 0.","Fill the leading digit first in the second case."],"concept":"Cases with restrictions on first and last digits.","misconception":"Assuming the two cases have the same count.","why":"The leading-digit restriction interacts with the choice of last digit.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-20","topic":"counting","sub":"ordered selection","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A consultancy has 7 analysts available and 4 different tasks to allocate, one analyst per task, no analyst taking more than one task. In how many ways can the tasks be allocated?","data":"","answer":{"value":840,"display":"840"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"840","correct":true,"why":"","v":840},{"text":"35","correct":false,"why":"This ignores which task each analyst does; the tasks are different, so order matters.","v":35},{"text":"2,401","correct":false,"why":"This allows the same analyst to take several tasks.","v":2401},{"text":"5,040","correct":false,"why":"This arranges all seven analysts; only four tasks are assigned.","v":5040}],"solution":["The first task can go to any of 7 analysts, the second to any of the remaining 6, then 5, then 4.","$7\\cdot6\\cdot5\\cdot4 = 840 = P(7,4)$."],"hints":["Are the tasks interchangeable or different from each other?","Assign the tasks in sequence; how many analysts remain each time?","This is a permutation of 4 chosen from 7."],"concept":"Permutations as ordered selections.","misconception":"Using combinations when positions are distinguishable.","why":"Different tasks make each assignment an ordered selection.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-21","topic":"counting","sub":"inclusion-exclusion with sampling","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A committee of 5 is chosen at random from 7 first-year, 6 second-year and 5 third-year students. What is the probability that <b>exactly two</b> of the three year groups are represented on the committee?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2900326797385621,"display":"0.29"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.29","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.2900326797385621},{"text":"0.2966","correct":false,"why":"These counts include committees drawn entirely from one year, which represent only one group.","v":0.2965686274509804},{"text":"0.9967","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least two groups are represented, including committees with all three.","v":0.9967320261437909},{"text":"0.1471","correct":false,"why":"Only one pair of year groups was considered; any of the three pairs can be the represented pair.","v":0.14705882352941177}],"solution":["Total committees: $\\binom{18}{5} = 8568$.","For a given pair of year groups, committees using only those two groups but not just one of them: $\\binom{a+b}{5} - \\binom{a}{5} - \\binom{b}{5}$.","Years (1,2): $\\binom{13}{5}-\\binom75-\\binom65 = 1287-21-6 = 1260$. Years (1,3): $792-21-1 = 770$. Years (2,3): $462-6-1 = 455$.","Total $2485$, so $P = 2485/8568 \\approx 0.290$."],"hints":["Fix which two year groups are represented and count the committees using exactly those two.","Within a pair, subtract the committees that come entirely from one of the two years.","Add the three pair counts and divide by $\\binom{18}{5}$."],"concept":"Counting committees with exactly $k$ of several groups represented.","misconception":"Including single-group committees in each pair count.","why":"Each committee with exactly two represented groups belongs to exactly one pair.","fam":"count:probability"},{"id":"CNT-22","topic":"counting","sub":"positions in a random order","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Twelve research proposals, 4 of them on infrastructure, are presented to a panel in a uniformly random order. What is the probability that the four infrastructure proposals are presented <b>consecutively</b> (in some order, in four adjacent slots)?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.01818181818181818,"display":"1/55"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/55","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.01818181818181818},{"text":"1/33","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all four fall in the first six slots, a different event.","v":0.030303030303030304},{"text":"4/165","correct":false,"why":"A block of four slots can start at positions 1 to 9 only, so there are 9 starting points, not 12.","v":0.024242424242424242},{"text":"1/12","correct":false,"why":"The probability depends on the number of ways the four slots can be placed among all $\\binom{12}{4}$ sets of slots.","v":0.08333333333333333}],"solution":["The set of slots occupied by the infrastructure proposals is equally likely to be any of $\\binom{12}{4} = 495$ four-element subsets.","The subset is four consecutive slots if it starts at slot 1, 2, …, 9: 9 subsets.","$P = 9/495 = 1/55 \\approx 0.0182$."],"hints":["Only the set of occupied slots matters; all sets of four slots are equally likely.","How many sets of four slots are consecutive?","A run of four starting at slot $s$ needs $s+3\\le12$."],"concept":"Random placement of special items among positions.","misconception":"Counting starting points incorrectly or ignoring the equal-likelihood of slot sets.","why":"By symmetry the occupied slots are a uniformly random 4-subset.","fam":"count:probability"},{"id":"CNT-23","topic":"counting","sub":"card hands","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Five cards are dealt from a standard 52-card deck. What is the probability of being dealt <b>exactly three of a kind</b>: three cards of one rank and two other cards of two different ranks (so a full house or four of a kind does not count)?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.02112845138055222,"display":"0.0211"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0002,"options":[{"text":"0.0211","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.02112845138055222},{"text":"0.0226","correct":false,"why":"Choosing any two other cards can produce a full house or four of a kind, which must be excluded.","v":0.02256902761104442},{"text":"0.00132","correct":false,"why":"The suits of the two other cards must also be chosen: $4\\times4$ ways.","v":0.0013205282112845138},{"text":"0.00704","correct":false,"why":"Three cards of different ranks are not three of a kind; the three equal-rank cards come from one rank.","v":0.007042817126850741}],"solution":["Rank of the triple: 13. Choose 3 of its 4 suits: $\\binom43 = 4$.","Two other ranks (different from each other and from the triple): $\\binom{12}{2} = 66$; a suit for each: $4\\cdot4 = 16$.","Favourable: $13\\cdot4\\cdot66\\cdot16 = 54\\,912$. Total hands: $\\binom{52}{5} = 2\\,598\\,960$.","$P \\approx 0.0211$."],"hints":["Build the hand in stages: the rank of the triple, then its suits.","The other two cards must have different ranks, both different from the triple.","Don’t forget to choose the suit for each of the two other cards."],"concept":"Classical probability with a structured count.","misconception":"Allowing the two extra cards to match each other or the triple.","why":"Each hand of this type is generated exactly once by the staged construction.","fam":"count:probability"},{"id":"CNT-24","topic":"counting","sub":"complement counting with repetition","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A three-character access code uses the letters A, B, C, D, E, with repetition allowed. How many codes contain <b>at least one repeated letter</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":65,"display":"65"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"65","correct":true,"why":"","v":65},{"text":"60","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of codes with <i>no</i> repeated letter.","v":60},{"text":"125","correct":false,"why":"This counts all codes, not only those with a repeat.","v":125},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts only the 5 codes in which all three letters are equal, as if \"no repeat\" meant \"all letters the same\". Codes with different letters number $5\\cdot4\\cdot3 = 60$.","v":120}],"solution":["All codes: $5^3 = 125$.","Codes with no repeated letter: $5\\cdot4\\cdot3 = 60$.","At least one repeat: $125-60 = 65$."],"hints":["Count the opposite event: all letters different.","How many codes are there in total when repetition is allowed?","Subtract the no-repeat count from the total number of codes."],"concept":"Complement counting.","misconception":"Trying to count each repeat pattern separately and missing some.","why":"The events \"some repeat\" and \"no repeat\" partition the codes.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-25","topic":"counting","sub":"solving for an unknown population size","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A bag contains red and blue balls, with 4 more blue balls than red balls and fewer than 50 balls in total. Two balls are drawn without replacement. The probability that they have the <b>same colour</b> is $47/95$. How many balls are in the bag?","data":"","answer":{"value":20,"display":"20"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"20","correct":true,"why":"","v":20},{"text":"76","correct":false,"why":"This is the larger root of the quadratic ($r=36$ red, 40 blue), which is excluded by \"fewer than 50 balls\".","v":76},{"text":"8","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of red balls $r$, not the total.","v":8},{"text":"24","correct":false,"why":"This does not satisfy the equation; check $P(\\text{same})$ for $r=10$.","v":24}],"solution":["Let there be $r$ red and $r+4$ blue balls: $n = 2r+4$.","$P(\\text{same}) = \\dfrac{r(r-1)+(r+4)(r+3)}{(2r+4)(2r+3)} = \\dfrac{2r^2+6r+12}{4r^2+14r+12} = \\dfrac{47}{95}$.","Cross-multiplying: $r^2 - 44r + 288 = 0$, so $r = 8$ or $r = 36$.","$r = 36$ gives 76 balls, which violates \"fewer than 50\", so $r=8$, 12 blue, $n = 20$."],"hints":["Express the probability in terms of the number of red balls $r$.","Set it equal to $47/95$ and rearrange to a quadratic in $r$.","Two roots appear; which one satisfies the size condition?"],"concept":"Using a probability equation to recover a population size.","misconception":"Accepting both roots of the quadratic or reporting a component instead of the total.","why":"The quadratic arises because the same-colour probability is a ratio of quadratics in $r$.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-26","topic":"counting","sub":"arrangement with a forbidden adjacency","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Five analysts $A, B, C, D, E$ line up for a photograph. In how many orders are $A$ and $B$ <b>not</b> standing next to each other?","data":"","answer":{"value":72,"display":"72"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"72","correct":true,"why":"","v":72},{"text":"48","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of orders in which $A$ and $B$ <i>are</i> adjacent.","v":48},{"text":"96","correct":false,"why":"Adjacent pairs can occur as $AB$ or $BA$; the block can be ordered in 2 ways.","v":96},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the restriction.","v":120}],"solution":["Total orders: $5! = 120$.","Orders with $A$ and $B$ adjacent: treat them as a block ($4!$ orders of 4 units) and order the block internally in 2 ways: $2\\cdot24 = 48$.","Not adjacent: $120-48 = 72$."],"hints":["Count the complement: orders in which they are adjacent.","Glue $A$ and $B$ into one unit; in how many ways can the pair itself be ordered?","Subtract from $5!$."],"concept":"Complement counting with a block.","misconception":"Forgetting the internal order of the block.","why":"Restrictions \"not together\" are usually easiest through the complement.","fam":"count:arrange"},{"id":"CNT-27","topic":"counting","sub":"arrangement of letters: vowels together","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"How many distinct arrangements of the letters of the word BUDGET can be made in which the two vowels, U and E, are <b>next to each other</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":240,"display":"240"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"240","correct":true,"why":"","v":240},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"The vowel block can be written as UE or EU, doubling the count.","v":120},{"text":"720","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the requirement that the vowels be adjacent.","v":720},{"text":"48","correct":false,"why":"After forming the block there are 5 units (the block and 4 consonants), so $5!$ orders, not $4!$.","v":48}],"solution":["Form a block from U and E: 2 internal orders.","The block and the four consonants B, D, G, T give 5 units: $5! = 120$ orders.","Total $2\\times120 = 240$."],"hints":["Treat the two vowels as a single unit.","How many units are there, and in how many orders?","Multiply by the internal orderings of the block."],"concept":"Blocks in permutations.","misconception":"Counting the block as a single arrangement.","why":"Block order and internal order are independent choices.","fam":"count:arrange"},{"id":"CNT-28","topic":"counting","sub":"handshakes: combinations","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"At a networking event each of 12 attendees shakes hands exactly once with every other attendee. How many handshakes take place?","data":"","answer":{"value":66,"display":"66"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Each handshake is an unordered pair of attendees: $\\binom{12}{2} = \\dfrac{12\\cdot11}{2} = 66$."],"hints":["Is a handshake between $a$ and $b$ different from one between $b$ and $a$?","Count pairs, not ordered pairs.","Compute $\\binom{12}{2}$."],"concept":"Counting unordered pairs.","misconception":"Using $12\\times11$ without dividing by 2.","why":"Each pair is counted twice among ordered pairs.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-29","topic":"counting","sub":"selection with at least from each group","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A panel of 4 is to be chosen from 5 economists and 4 sociologists, and must contain <b>at least one</b> person from each discipline. How many panels are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":120,"display":"120"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"120","correct":true,"why":"","v":120},{"text":"126","correct":false,"why":"This includes panels made up of only economists or only sociologists.","v":126},{"text":"121","correct":false,"why":"The all-sociologist panel is also excluded: it contains no economist.","v":121},{"text":"420","correct":false,"why":"Choosing one of each first and then any two others counts panels with several members of a discipline more than once.","v":420}],"solution":["All panels: $\\binom94 = 126$.","All economists: $\\binom54 = 5$. All sociologists: $\\binom44 = 1$.","Valid: $126-5-1 = 120$.","(Direct count: $1E3S+2E2S+3E1S = 5\\cdot4+10\\cdot6+10\\cdot4 = 120$.)"],"hints":["Which panels violate the requirement?","There are two kinds of bad panel; neither overlaps the other.","Subtract both from the total."],"concept":"Counting with a requirement of representation.","misconception":"Over-counting by choosing the required members first.","why":"The complement consists of two disjoint, small cases.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-30","topic":"counting","sub":"bit strings: no consecutive ones","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A binary access code has 8 bits. How many codes contain <b>no two consecutive 1s</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":55,"display":"55"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Let $a_n$ count valid strings of length $n$. A valid string ends in 0 (any valid string of length $n-1$ before it) or in 01 (any valid string of length $n-2$ before that).","$a_n = a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}$, $a_1 = 2$, $a_2 = 3$: $5, 8, 13, 21, 34, 55$.","$a_8 = 55$."],"hints":["Look at the last bit of a valid string.","If the last bit is 1, what must the bit before it be?","Build the sequence $a_n = a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}$ up to $n=8$."],"concept":"Recursive counting of restricted sequences.","misconception":"Trying to count by inclusion-exclusion without a pattern.","why":"Each valid string is built uniquely from a shorter valid string.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-31","topic":"counting","sub":"password with character classes","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A 4-character PIN uses digits 0 to 9 with repetition allowed. How many PINs contain <b>at least one 7</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":3439,"display":"3,439"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"3,439","correct":true,"why":"","v":3439},{"text":"4,000","correct":false,"why":"Choosing the position of a 7 and then anything counts PINs with several 7s more than once.","v":4000},{"text":"6,561","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of PINs with <i>no</i> 7.","v":6561},{"text":"9,271","correct":false,"why":"There are four positions, so the number without a 7 is $9^4$, not $9^3$.","v":9271}],"solution":["All PINs: $10^4 = 10\\,000$.","PINs with no 7: $9^4 = 6561$.","At least one 7: $10\\,000-6561 = 3439$."],"hints":["Count the PINs that contain no 7 at all.","Each position then has 9 options.","Subtract from the total number of PINs."],"concept":"Complement counting for \"at least one\".","misconception":"Choosing the special digit first and over-counting.","why":"The complement is a single product.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-32","topic":"counting","sub":"combination: subsets of a set","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A fund manager is considering 7 candidate stocks and may include any subset in a portfolio, including holding none. How many different portfolios (subsets) are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":128,"display":"128"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Each stock is either in or out: $2^7 = 128$ portfolios (including the empty portfolio).","Check: $\\sum_k\\binom7k = 2^7$."],"hints":["For each stock there are two possibilities.","The decisions are independent.","Multiply seven 2’s."],"concept":"Number of subsets.","misconception":"Counting only portfolios of a fixed size.","why":"A subset corresponds to a sequence of in/out decisions.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-33","topic":"counting","sub":"multisets: selection with repetition","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A cafe sells 5 kinds of pastry. In how many ways can a customer choose a box of 8 pastries if the order does not matter and each kind may be chosen any number of times?","data":"","answer":{"value":495,"display":"495"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"495","correct":true,"why":"","v":495},{"text":"56","correct":false,"why":"This chooses 5 of 8 distinct items; here 8 pastries are taken from 5 kinds, with repetition allowed.","v":56},{"text":"390,625","correct":false,"why":"This counts ordered selections, but the order of pastries in the box does not matter.","v":390625},{"text":"1,287","correct":false,"why":"The number of bars is one less than the number of kinds: $\\binom{8+4}{4}$, not $\\binom{8+5}{5}$.","v":1287}],"solution":["The number of multisets of size 8 from 5 kinds equals the number of non-negative integer solutions of $x_1+\\dots+x_5 = 8$.","Stars and bars: $\\binom{8+5-1}{5-1} = \\binom{12}{4} = 495$."],"hints":["Only how many of each kind matters, not the order.","Translate to counting non-negative integer solutions of a sum.","Use $\\binom{n+k-1}{k-1}$."],"concept":"Combinations with repetition.","misconception":"Using ordered counts or the wrong bar count.","why":"A selection is a list of five counts adding to 8.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-34","topic":"counting","sub":"tournament: number of matches","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"In a round-robin tournament each of 9 teams plays every other team exactly <b>twice</b> (home and away). How many matches are played in total?","data":"","answer":{"value":72,"display":"72"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Pairs of teams: $\\binom92 = 36$. Each pair meets twice: $72$ matches.","Equivalently each team plays $8\\times2$ matches, and each match involves two teams: $9\\cdot16/2 = 72$."],"hints":["How many different pairs of teams are there?","Each pair plays more than once.","Multiply by the number of meetings."],"concept":"Counting pairs and repeated meetings.","misconception":"Counting each match twice.","why":"Home and away matches are different events for the same pair.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-35","topic":"counting","sub":"lottery: probability from counting","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A lottery draws 6 distinct numbers from 1 to 40. A ticket has 6 distinct numbers. What is the probability that a ticket matches <b>all six</b> drawn numbers? Give the answer as a decimal to 8 places, e.g. 0.00000026.","data":"","answer":{"value":2.6052657631605e-7,"display":"2.61e-7"},"fmt":"d","tol":5e-9,"options":[],"solution":["The number of possible draws is $\\binom{40}{6} = 3\\,838\\,380$, all equally likely.","One of them matches the ticket: $P = 1/3\\,838\\,380 \\approx 2.6\\times10^{-7}$."],"hints":["The order in which numbers are drawn does not matter.","How many different sets of 6 numbers can be drawn from 40?","Only one set matches the ticket."],"concept":"Classical probability with combinations.","misconception":"Using ordered selections ($40\\cdot39\\cdots$) in the denominator.","why":"Each 6-subset is equally likely to be drawn.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-36","topic":"counting","sub":"hockey-stick identity: counting by summing","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A committee of 4 is chosen from 10 people numbered 1 to 10. Classifying each committee by its <b>lowest-numbered member</b> $k$ shows that the number of committees equals $\\sum_{k=1}^{7}\\binom{10-k}{3}$. What is the value of this sum?","data":"","answer":{"value":210,"display":"210"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"210","correct":true,"why":"","v":210},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"The sum counts committees of 4, so it equals $\\binom{10}{4}$, not $\\binom{10}{3}$.","v":120},{"text":"330","correct":false,"why":"The hockey-stick sum stops at $\\binom{9}{3}$ for $k=1$, giving $\\binom{10}{4}$, not $\\binom{11}{4}$.","v":330},{"text":"588","correct":false,"why":"The terms decrease with $k$; they are not all equal to $\\binom93$.","v":588}],"solution":["Every committee of 4 has a unique lowest-numbered member $k$; the other 3 members are chosen from the $10-k$ people above $k$: $\\binom{10-k}{3}$ committees.","Summing over $k$ counts every committee once, so the sum is $\\binom{10}{4} = 210$."],"hints":["Classify each committee by its smallest member.","If the smallest member is $k$, how many choices remain for the other three?","Every committee has exactly one smallest member."],"concept":"Counting the same set in two ways.","misconception":"Evaluating the sum numerically with an arithmetic slip.","why":"The sum and $\\binom{10}{4}$ count the same collection of committees.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-37","topic":"counting","sub":"distinguishable objects into boxes","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Six different invoices are each assigned to one of 3 approvers (an approver may receive any number, including none). How many assignments are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":729,"display":"729"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"729","correct":true,"why":"","v":729},{"text":"216","correct":false,"why":"This would assign 3 invoices to 6 approvers; here each of the 6 invoices chooses one of 3 approvers.","v":216},{"text":"28","correct":false,"why":"This counts distributions of <i>identical</i> items; the invoices are different.","v":28},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"Approvers can receive several invoices, so this restricted count is not appropriate.","v":120}],"solution":["Each invoice independently chooses one of 3 approvers: $3^6 = 729$ assignments."],"hints":["Does an approver have to receive at most one invoice?","Each invoice has its own independent choice.","Multiply the choices."],"concept":"Assignments of distinguishable objects to labelled boxes.","misconception":"Mixing up which set is the domain.","why":"Each assignment is a function from invoices to approvers.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-38","topic":"counting","sub":"derangement count","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four employees each write their name on a gift tag and the four gifts are shuffled and handed out one per employee. In how many of the $4!$ distributions does <b>nobody</b> receive their own gift?","data":"","answer":{"value":9,"display":"9"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"9","correct":true,"why":"","v":9},{"text":"15","correct":false,"why":"This is $24-9$: the number of distributions in which <i>at least one</i> employee receives their own gift.","v":15},{"text":"6","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of distributions with exactly two correct gifts.","v":6},{"text":"3","correct":false,"why":"This counts only the distributions made of two swapped pairs; four-cycles also leave nobody with their own gift.","v":3}],"solution":["Inclusion-exclusion over \"employee $i$ gets their own gift\":","$4! - \\binom41 3! + \\binom42 2! - \\binom43 1! + \\binom44 0! = 24-24+12-4+1 = 9$."],"hints":["Count the complement: distributions with at least one fixed point.","If $k$ specific employees get their own gifts, the rest can be arranged in $(4-k)!$ ways.","Alternate signs: $24-24+12-4+1$."],"concept":"Derangements by inclusion-exclusion.","misconception":"Subtracting only the single-match cases.","why":"Each distribution with several matches is counted by the alternating sum exactly once.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-39","topic":"counting","sub":"ordering with ties","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Seven applicants are ranked into three outcome bands: 2 receive \"strong\", 3 receive \"adequate\" and 2 receive \"weak\". In how many different ways can the bands be allocated to the 7 distinct applicants?","data":"","answer":{"value":210,"display":"210"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"210","correct":true,"why":"","v":210},{"text":"5,040","correct":false,"why":"This treats the labels as all different; applicants in the same band are interchangeable in label.","v":5040},{"text":"420","correct":false,"why":"After choosing the strong and adequate groups the weak group is determined, so there is no further factor of 2.","v":420},{"text":"2,187","correct":false,"why":"This counts all assignments of three labels, not only those with the specified numbers.","v":2187}],"solution":["Choose which 2 applicants are \"strong\": $\\binom72 = 21$. From the remaining 5 choose 3 for \"adequate\": $\\binom53 = 10$. The last 2 are \"weak\".","$21\\times10 = 210 = \\dfrac{7!}{2!\\,3!\\,2!}$."],"hints":["Choose the members of each band in turn.","After two bands are filled, the third is forced.","This is a multinomial coefficient."],"concept":"Multinomial coefficient for labelling people.","misconception":"Using $7!$ or $3^7$.","why":"The multinomial counts labellings with prescribed numbers per label.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-40","topic":"counting","sub":"pigeonhole principle","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A company has 400 employees. Which statement can be guaranteed to be true?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"At least two employees share a birthday (day of the year)"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"At least two employees share a birthday (day of the year)","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"At least 10 employees share a birthday","correct":false,"why":"Spread evenly, about 1.1 per day: at least 2 per day is guaranteed, but not 10."},{"text":"At least one birthday is shared by exactly two employees","correct":false,"why":"Some days could have 3 or more employees and none exactly two; the guarantee is only that some day has at least two."},{"text":"Every day of the year is the birthday of at least one employee","correct":false,"why":"With 400 employees it is possible that some days are missed."}],"solution":["There are at most 366 possible birthdays and 400 employees.","By the pigeonhole principle at least $\\lceil400/366\\rceil = 2$ employees share a day.","No stronger statement is guaranteed."],"hints":["How many \"boxes\" (birthdays) are there?","If everyone had a different birthday, how many employees could there be at most?","Which statement follows with certainty, not just likelihood?"],"concept":"Pigeonhole principle.","misconception":"Confusing \"likely\" with \"guaranteed\".","why":"More objects than boxes forces a box with at least two.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-41","topic":"counting","sub":"selection with a fixed member","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A working group of 5 is chosen from 12 staff. The head of department must be in the group and two particular rivals, $R_1$ and $R_2$, must <b>not both</b> be chosen. How many groups are possible? (The head is neither $R_1$ nor $R_2$.)","data":"","answer":{"value":294,"display":"294"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"294","correct":true,"why":"","v":294},{"text":"330","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the restriction on the two rivals.","v":330},{"text":"210","correct":false,"why":"Fixing both rivals leaves 9 people (excluding the head and the rivals) for 2 more places: $\\binom92$.","v":210},{"text":"672","correct":false,"why":"The head of department must be in the group, so the base count is $\\binom{11}{4}$.","v":672}],"solution":["With the head fixed, choose 4 more from the other 11: $\\binom{11}{4} = 330$.","Groups containing both rivals: choose 2 more from the remaining 9: $\\binom92 = 36$.","Valid: $330-36 = 294$."],"hints":["Fix the head first; how many places and people remain?","Count the groups that contain both rivals.","Subtract the groups containing both rivals from the total."],"concept":"Counting with a required member and a forbidden pair.","misconception":"Forgetting to fix the head.","why":"Fixing members reduces the problem to a smaller selection.","fam":"count:select"},{"id":"CNT-42","topic":"counting","sub":"ways to reach a total with dice","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Three fair dice are rolled. In how many of the 216 ordered outcomes is the <b>sum equal to 10</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":27,"display":"27"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Count ordered triples by their multiset: $\\{1,3,6\\}$ and $\\{1,4,5\\}$, $\\{2,2,6\\}$, $\\{2,3,5\\}$, $\\{2,4,4\\}$, $\\{3,3,4\\}$.","Distinct values give $3! = 6$ orders; two equal values give 3 orders.","$6+6+3+6+3+3 = 27$."],"hints":["List the unordered triples of values from 1 to 6 that sum to 10.","For each, count the number of distinct orderings.","Triples with a repeated value have 3 orderings, with three distinct values 6."],"concept":"Counting ordered outcomes with a fixed sum.","misconception":"Counting only unordered triples.","why":"Each unordered triple corresponds to a number of ordered outcomes by its multiplicity pattern.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-43","topic":"counting","sub":"seating: circular with a restriction","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Seven directors sit at a round table (rotations count as the same seating). Two of them, $P$ and $Q$, refuse to sit next to each other. How many seatings are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":480,"display":"480"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"480","correct":true,"why":"","v":480},{"text":"240","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of seatings in which $P$ and $Q$ <i>are</i> adjacent.","v":240},{"text":"3,600","correct":false,"why":"These are linear-order counts; around a circular table fix one person to remove rotations: $6!$ in total.","v":3600},{"text":"600","correct":false,"why":"The adjacent pair can sit as $PQ$ or $QP$, so $2\\cdot5!$ must be removed.","v":600}],"solution":["Circular seatings of 7 people: $(7-1)! = 720$.","$P,Q$ adjacent: treat them as a block; 6 units around a circle: $5! = 120$, times 2 internal orders $=240$.","Not adjacent: $720-240 = 480$."],"hints":["How many circular arrangements of 7 people are there?","Count the arrangements in which $P$ and $Q$ sit together.","Subtract the adjacent seatings from all circular seatings."],"concept":"Circular permutations with an adjacency restriction.","misconception":"Using linear counts or forgetting the internal order of the block.","why":"Rotational symmetry is removed by fixing one person.","fam":"count:arrange"},{"id":"CNT-44","topic":"counting","sub":"paths avoiding a point","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A courier moves from $(0,0)$ to $(4,4)$ on a grid taking only unit steps east or north. A road closure blocks the junction $(2,2)$, so routes cannot pass through it. How many routes are possible?","data":"","answer":{"value":34,"display":"34"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"34","correct":true,"why":"","v":34},{"text":"70","correct":false,"why":"This counts all routes, including those through the closed junction.","v":70},{"text":"36","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of routes that <i>do</i> pass through $(2,2)$.","v":36},{"text":"58","correct":false,"why":"Routes through $(2,2)$ number $\\binom42\\times\\binom42$ (product of the two legs), not a sum.","v":58}],"solution":["All routes: $\\binom84 = 70$.","Through $(2,2)$: $\\binom42\\cdot\\binom42 = 6\\cdot6 = 36$.","Routes avoiding it: $70-36 = 34$."],"hints":["Count the routes that do pass through the closed junction.","A route through $(2,2)$ is a route to it followed by a route from it.","Subtract from all routes."],"concept":"Complement counting for lattice paths.","misconception":"Adding the leg counts instead of multiplying.","why":"Legs of a route through a given point are chosen independently.","fam":"count:paths-bars"},{"id":"CNT-45","topic":"counting","sub":"at most one of two groups","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A hiring panel of 3 is to be chosen from 6 internal and 4 external members. How many panels contain <b>more internal than external</b> members?","data":"","answer":{"value":80,"display":"80"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"80","correct":true,"why":"","v":80},{"text":"60","correct":false,"why":"Panels with 3 internal members also have more internal than external.","v":60},{"text":"100","correct":false,"why":"This counts panels with at least one external member, including those with majority external.","v":100},{"text":"116","correct":false,"why":"The case of 1 internal and 2 external has fewer internal than external and must be excluded.","v":116}],"solution":["More internal than external in a panel of 3 means 3 internal, or 2 internal and 1 external.","$\\binom63 + \\binom62\\binom41 = 20+60 = 80$."],"hints":["Which compositions of a 3-person panel have an internal majority?","Count each composition separately.","Add the counts."],"concept":"Counting by composition.","misconception":"Including the minority-internal case.","why":"Compositions are mutually exclusive cases.","fam":"count:multinomial"},{"id":"CNT-46","topic":"counting","sub":"probability from counting: birthdays in a month","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Four colleagues each independently have a birthday in one of 12 equally likely months. What is the probability that <b>all four birthdays are in different months</b>? (Give a decimal to 4 places.)","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5729166666666666,"display":"0.5729"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0006,"options":[],"solution":["$12^4 = 20\\,736$ equally likely month sequences.","All different: $12\\cdot11\\cdot10\\cdot9 = 11\\,880$.","$P = 11\\,880/20\\,736 \\approx 0.5729$."],"hints":["How many equally likely sequences of four months are there?","Count sequences with no repeated month by choosing in order.","Divide the favourable count by the total number of sequences."],"concept":"Probability of no repeats.","misconception":"Dividing by $\\binom{12}{4}$ instead of $12^4$.","why":"Ordered sequences are equally likely under independence.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"CNT-47","topic":"counting","sub":"rankings with a fixed top","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Six firms are ranked from 1 (best) to 6 (worst). In how many rankings does firm $F$ occupy a <b>top-two</b> position?","data":"","answer":{"value":240,"display":"240"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"240","correct":true,"why":"","v":240},{"text":"120","correct":false,"why":"Firm $F$ can take either of the two top positions.","v":120},{"text":"360","correct":false,"why":"This treats the firm as equally likely to be in the top half (three positions); the condition is the top two positions.","v":360},{"text":"1,440","correct":false,"why":"After placing $F$ in one of two positions there are $5!$ orders for the others, not $6!$.","v":1440}],"solution":["$F$ takes position 1 or 2: 2 choices. The other 5 firms fill the remaining positions in $5! = 120$ ways.","$2\\times120 = 240$."],"hints":["Place the special firm first: how many allowed positions?","Then arrange the other firms.","Multiply the number of allowed positions by the arrangements of the rest."],"concept":"Permutations with a positional condition.","misconception":"Not multiplying by the number of allowed positions.","why":"Allowed positions and arrangements of the rest are independent stages.","fam":"count:multiplication"},{"id":"AXM-01","topic":"axioms","sub":"addition rule","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Among small manufacturers, 41% use cloud accounting software, 37% use a mobile payments app and 12% use both. What proportion use at least one of the two?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.66,"display":"0.66"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.66","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.66},{"text":"0.78","correct":false,"why":"The 12% who use both are counted in each percentage and so have been counted twice.","v":0.78},{"text":"0.54","correct":false,"why":"This is the proportion using exactly one of the two tools (the overlap subtracted twice); users of both also belong to \"at least one\".","v":0.54},{"text":"0.12","correct":false,"why":"This is the proportion using both; the question asks for at least one.","v":0.12}],"solution":["Let $C$ = cloud software and $M$ = mobile app.","$P(C\\cup M) = P(C)+P(M)-P(C\\cap M) = 0.41+0.37-0.12 = 0.66$."],"hints":["\"At least one\" corresponds to a union of the two events.","Adding the two percentages counts one group twice; which group?","$P(C\\cup M)=P(C)+P(M)-P(C\\cap M)$."],"concept":"Addition rule for two events.","misconception":"Adding probabilities of overlapping events.","why":"Subtracting the intersection once makes each element count exactly once.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-02","topic":"axioms","sub":"addition rule: solving for the overlap","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"In a sample of firms, 52% have adopted cloud tools and 31% have adopted AI tools. A quarter (25%) have adopted neither. What proportion have adopted <b>both</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.08000000000000007,"display":"0.08"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.08","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.08000000000000007},{"text":"0.1612","correct":false,"why":"This assumes adoption of the two tools is independent, which the data do not state.","v":0.1612},{"text":"0.58","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts the \"neither\" proportion directly from the sum; the union is $1-0.25 = 0.75$, which should be subtracted instead.","v":0.58},{"text":"0.75","correct":false,"why":"This is the proportion adopting at least one tool, not both.","v":0.75}],"solution":["$P(\\text{at least one}) = 1 - 0.25 = 0.75$.","From the addition rule, $P(\\text{both}) = P(C)+P(A)-P(C\\cup A) = 0.52+0.31-0.75 = 0.08$."],"hints":["First find the probability of adopting at least one tool.","Rearrange $P(C\\cup A) = P(C)+P(A)-P(C\\cap A)$ for the intersection.","The union is the complement of \"neither\"."],"concept":"Recovering an intersection from a union and the marginals.","misconception":"Assuming independence to get the intersection.","why":"Given the union and the marginals, the addition rule determines the intersection.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-03","topic":"axioms","sub":"difference of events","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"For a randomly chosen household, $P(\\text{owns a car}) = 0.58$ and $P(\\text{owns a car or a motorbike}) = 0.89$. What is the probability that the household owns a motorbike but <b>not</b> a car?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.31000000000000005,"display":"0.31"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.31","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.31000000000000005},{"text":"0.89","correct":false,"why":"This adds the car probability to the answer; the union already contains it.","v":0.8899999999999999},{"text":"0.42","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of owning no car, which includes households with neither vehicle.","v":0.42000000000000004},{"text":"0.2436","correct":false,"why":"This assumes independence and applies a product that the information does not justify.","v":0.24359999999999998}],"solution":["The union splits into \"car\" and \"motorbike but no car\", which are disjoint:","$P(C\\cup M) = P(C) + P(M\\cap C^c)$.","$P(M\\cap C^c) = 0.89 - 0.58 = 0.31$."],"hints":["Write $C\\cup M$ as the union of two disjoint events involving $C$.","Which event remains after removing all of $C$ from the union?","Subtract $P(C)$ from $P(C\\cup M)$."],"concept":"Decomposing a union into disjoint parts.","misconception":"Looking for the overlap that is not needed.","why":"The portion of the union outside $C$ is exactly the event asked for.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-04","topic":"axioms","sub":"three-event regions","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Of 200 economics students, 80 take microeconomics, 70 take macroeconomics and 60 take econometrics. 30 take micro and macro, 25 take micro and econometrics, 20 take macro and econometrics, and 10 take all three. A student is chosen at random. What is the probability that the student takes <b>exactly two</b> of the three courses?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.225,"display":"0.225"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.225","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.225},{"text":"0.375","correct":false,"why":"The pairwise counts include the 10 students who take all three; each pair’s \"only\" part is smaller by 10.","v":0.375},{"text":"0.325","correct":false,"why":"Only one copy of the triple overlap was removed; it sits in all three pairwise counts.","v":0.325},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of taking all three courses.","v":0.05}],"solution":["Each pairwise count includes the students taking all three. The number taking exactly that pair is:","micro and macro only: $30-10 = 20$; micro and econometrics only: $25-10 = 15$; macro and econometrics only: $20-10 = 10$.","Exactly two courses: $20+15+10 = 45$, so $P = 45/200 = 0.225$."],"hints":["Each pairwise count includes students who take the third course as well.","Subtract the all-three group from each pairwise count to get \"exactly this pair\".","Add the three exact-pair counts and divide by 200."],"concept":"Regions of a three-set Venn diagram.","misconception":"Using pairwise intersections as if they excluded the third event.","why":"The three exact-pair regions and the triple region are disjoint and partition \"at least two\".","fam":"axioms:inclusion-exclusion"},{"id":"AXM-05","topic":"axioms","sub":"inclusion-exclusion then sampling","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A firm has 60 staff. 25 use Excel, 20 use Python and 15 use Stata. 8 use Excel and Python, 5 use Excel and Stata, 6 use Python and Stata, and 3 use all three tools. Two staff are chosen at random without replacement. What is the probability that <b>neither</b> uses any of the three tools?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.06779661016949153,"display":"0.0678"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0678","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.06779661016949153},{"text":"0.0711","correct":false,"why":"The second choice is made without replacement from 59 staff, 15 of whom use no tool.","v":0.07111111111111111},{"text":"0.5345","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that both use at least one tool.","v":0.5344632768361582},{"text":"0.0966","correct":false,"why":"The all-three group was left out of the union (41 instead of 44 users), overstating the number who use no tool.","v":0.09661016949152543}],"solution":["Users of at least one tool: $25+20+15-8-5-6+3 = 44$, so 16 staff use none.","$P(\\text{both none}) = \\dfrac{16}{60}\\cdot\\dfrac{15}{59} = \\dfrac{\\binom{16}{2}}{\\binom{60}{2}} = \\dfrac{120}{1770} \\approx 0.0678$."],"hints":["First find how many staff use none of the tools, using inclusion-exclusion.","Then the two chosen staff must both come from that group.","Sampling is without replacement: $\\binom{16}{2}/\\binom{60}{2}$."],"concept":"Combining inclusion-exclusion with hypergeometric sampling.","misconception":"Subtracting overlapping counts incorrectly, or sampling with replacement.","why":"The union size feeds directly into the sampling probability.","fam":"axioms:inclusion-exclusion"},{"id":"AXM-07","topic":"axioms","sub":"consistency of data","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A report on 500 firms states: 220 export, 180 invest in R&D and 150 are family-owned; 90 both export and invest in R&D, 15 both export and are family-owned, 70 both invest in R&D and are family-owned; and 20 do all three. Can all of these figures be correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"No: the 20 firms doing all three are a subset of the 15 that export and are family-owned, which is impossible"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"No: the 20 firms doing all three are a subset of the 15 that export and are family-owned, which is impossible","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Yes: inclusion-exclusion gives 395 firms in the union, which does not exceed 500","correct":false,"why":"The union size is within bounds, but a triple overlap can never exceed any of its pairwise overlaps; the figures fail that earlier test."},{"text":"No: the three pairwise figures sum to more than any single total","correct":false,"why":"There is no such requirement; the pairwise figures are subsets of different sets."},{"text":"Yes: a count of 20 for all three is smaller than each single-set total","correct":false,"why":"The relevant bound is the pairwise count (15), not the single-set totals."}],"solution":["The firms that export, invest in R&D and are family-owned are, in particular, firms that export and are family-owned. Hence the triple count cannot exceed that pairwise count: $20 \\le 15$ is false.","The union size ($220+180+150-90-15-70+20 = 395\\le500$) is fine, so a check of the union alone would miss the inconsistency."],"hints":["Does any subset-relationship between the reported groups fail?","The triple intersection is a subset of each pairwise intersection.","Compare 20 with the smallest pairwise figure."],"concept":"Consistency checks using monotonicity of counts and probabilities.","misconception":"Testing only the total union and overlooking subset relations.","why":"If $A\\cap B\\cap C\\subset A\\cap C$ then $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)\\le P(A\\cap C)$.","fam":"axioms:inclusion-exclusion"},{"id":"AXM-08","topic":"axioms","sub":"repeated-digit patterns","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A four-digit PIN is generated by choosing each digit independently and uniformly from 0 to 9. What is the probability that the PIN contains <b>exactly one pair</b> of equal digits, with the other two digits different from the pair and from each other (pattern $a,a,b,c$ with $a,b,c$ distinct)?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.432,"display":"0.432"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.432","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.432},{"text":"0.072","correct":false,"why":"The positions of the pair among the four places have not been counted: $\\binom42 = 6$ choices.","v":0.072},{"text":"0.486","correct":false,"why":"The two remaining digits must also differ from each other: $9\\cdot8$, not $9\\cdot9$.","v":0.486},{"text":"0.06","correct":false,"why":"This counts PINs in which two particular positions match with no condition on the other digits.","v":0.06}],"solution":["Digit for the pair: 10 choices. Positions of the pair among 4: $\\binom{4}{2} = 6$.","The other two positions: first from the 9 remaining digits, second from the 8 that differ from both: $9\\cdot8 = 72$.","Favourable: $10\\cdot6\\cdot72 = 4320$; total $10^4$. $P = 0.432$."],"hints":["Count the PINs of the pattern $a,a,b,c$ by choosing the pair digit, its positions, then the other digits.","The other two digits must differ from $a$ and from each other.","Divide by $10^4$ equally likely PINs."],"concept":"Pattern counting under classical probability.","misconception":"Forgetting position choices or distinctness constraints.","why":"Every PIN of this pattern is generated once by the staged construction.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-10","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability with unequal outcomes","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two spinners are spun once each. Spinner 1 lands on red, blue or green with probabilities $\\tfrac12$, $\\tfrac13$, $\\tfrac16$. Spinner 2 lands on red, blue or green with probabilities $\\tfrac14$, $\\tfrac14$, $\\tfrac12$. What is the probability that both show the <b>same colour</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.29166666666666663,"display":"7/24"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"7/24","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.29166666666666663},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This would be the answer if each spinner were uniform over the three colours.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This adds the probabilities of one spinner and is always 1.","v":0.9999999999999999},{"text":"5/24","correct":false,"why":"The green-green case was left out; any colour shared by both spinners contributes.","v":0.20833333333333331}],"solution":["The spinners are independent. For each colour multiply the two probabilities and add:","$\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac14 + \\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac14 + \\tfrac16\\cdot\\tfrac12 = \\tfrac18+\\tfrac1{12}+\\tfrac1{12} = \\tfrac{7}{24}$."],"hints":["\"Same colour\" is a union of three disjoint events, one per colour.","For a given colour, the spins are independent: multiply.","Add the three products."],"concept":"Probability of a match between independent random outcomes.","misconception":"Assuming equal colour probabilities.","why":"The three matching events are disjoint, so their probabilities add.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-11","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability on dice","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair eight-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the larger number exceeds the smaller number by <b>exactly 3</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.15625,"display":"5/32"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"5/32","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.15625},{"text":"5/64","correct":false,"why":"Each pair of values can occur in two orders; this counts only one.","v":0.078125},{"text":"5/28","correct":false,"why":"This divides by the 56 ordered pairs of different values; the dice may also show equal numbers, so the sample space has 64 outcomes.","v":0.17857142857142858},{"text":"1/8","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of any particular value for the second die; it does not use the difference condition.","v":0.125}],"solution":["Pairs $(a,b)$ with $b = a+3$: $a = 1,\\dots,5$, so 5 pairs. The same number with $a = b+3$: 5 more.","$P = 10/64 = 5/32$."],"hints":["How many ordered pairs have the second die exactly 3 above the first?","The pairs can also occur with the dice reversed.","Divide the count of favourable ordered pairs by 64."],"concept":"Classical probability by counting ordered outcomes.","misconception":"Ignoring order.","why":"Equally likely ordered pairs make the probability a ratio of counts.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-12","topic":"axioms","sub":"at most k categories","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A drawer holds 4 black, 5 white and 6 grey socks. Three socks are drawn at random. What is the probability that <b>at most two colours</b> appear among them?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7362637362637363,"display":"0.7363"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.7363","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.7362637362637363},{"text":"0.0747","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three socks have the same colour only.","v":0.07472527472527472},{"text":"0.2637","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three colours appear, the complement of the event asked for.","v":0.26373626373626374},{"text":"0.2088","correct":false,"why":"The number of ways to take one sock of each colour is $4\\cdot5\\cdot6$ once; it should not be multiplied by 3.","v":0.20879120879120883}],"solution":["Total selections: $\\binom{15}{3} = 455$.","All three colours: $4\\cdot5\\cdot6 = 120$.","$P(\\text{at most two colours}) = 1-\\dfrac{120}{455} = \\dfrac{335}{455} \\approx 0.736$."],"hints":["\"At most two colours\" fails only when a very specific pattern occurs. Which?","Count the selections with one sock of each colour.","Subtract that probability from 1."],"concept":"Complement in sampling without replacement.","misconception":"Counting the allowed patterns piece by piece and missing some.","why":"Exactly one pattern violates the event, so the complement is a single product count.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-13","topic":"axioms","sub":"with replacement: all different","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A spinner lands on red, green, blue or yellow with probabilities $0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1$. It is spun three times independently. What is the probability that the three spins show <b>three different colours</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.30000000000000004,"display":"0.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.30000000000000004},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of one specific order for each set of colours; the three spins can show the colours in $3! = 6$ orders.","v":0.05},{"text":"0.375","correct":false,"why":"This assumes the four colours are equally likely.","v":0.375},{"text":"0.144","correct":false,"why":"Only the set {red, green, blue} was counted; other sets of three colours are also possible.","v":0.14400000000000002}],"solution":["Choose a set of three colours and multiply their probabilities. The sets and products: $0.4\\cdot0.3\\cdot0.2 = 0.024$, $0.4\\cdot0.3\\cdot0.1 = 0.012$, $0.4\\cdot0.2\\cdot0.1 = 0.008$, $0.3\\cdot0.2\\cdot0.1 = 0.006$; sum $0.050$.","Each set can appear in $3! = 6$ orders: $6\\times0.050 = 0.30$."],"hints":["List the possible sets of three different colours.","For each set, how many orders can the spins take?","Multiply the set probability by $3!$ and add over sets."],"concept":"Probability of distinct outcomes under unequal probabilities.","misconception":"Using equal probabilities or ignoring order.","why":"Orderings of a set are disjoint events with equal probability.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-14","topic":"axioms","sub":"hypergeometric","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"An examiner will choose 4 of 12 case studies at random for an exam. A student has prepared 8 of the 12. What is the probability that she has prepared <b>at least 3</b> of the 4 chosen?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.593939393939394,"display":"0.5939"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.5939","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.593939393939394},{"text":"0.4525","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of <i>exactly</i> 3 prepared case studies; \"at least 3\" also includes 4.","v":0.45252525252525255},{"text":"0.2963","correct":false,"why":"This treats the selections as independent with probability $8/12$ each.","v":0.2962962962962962},{"text":"0.2545","correct":false,"why":"When exactly 3 are prepared the fourth is chosen from the 4 unprepared ones; that factor is missing.","v":0.2545454545454545}],"solution":["Total: $\\binom{12}{4} = 495$.","Exactly 3 prepared: $\\binom83\\binom41 = 56\\cdot4 = 224$. All 4 prepared: $\\binom84 = 70$.","$P = \\dfrac{224+70}{495} = \\dfrac{294}{495} \\approx 0.594$."],"hints":["Split \"at least 3\" into exactly 3 and exactly 4.","For exactly $k$ prepared, choose $k$ from the prepared and $4-k$ from the others.","Divide by $\\binom{12}{4}$."],"concept":"Hypergeometric tail probability.","misconception":"Using binomial probabilities for sampling without replacement.","why":"Cases are exclusive and the count of selections is the product of two combinations.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-15","topic":"axioms","sub":"complement of at least one","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A trader makes 5 independent trades; each is profitable with probability 0.4. What is the probability that <b>at least one</b> trade is profitable?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.9222400000000001,"display":"0.9222"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.9222","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.9222400000000001},{"text":"2","correct":false,"why":"Adding the five probabilities exceeds 1 and double counts outcomes with several profitable trades.","v":2},{"text":"0.0778","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of no profitable trade, the complement.","v":0.07775999999999998},{"text":"0.0102","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all five trades are profitable.","v":0.010240000000000003}],"solution":["$P(\\text{none profitable}) = 0.6^5 = 0.07776$.","$P(\\text{at least one}) = 1-0.07776 = 0.92224$."],"hints":["What is the opposite of \"at least one profitable\"?","Independent trades: multiply their loss probabilities.","Subtract from 1."],"concept":"Complement rule for \"at least one\".","misconception":"Adding probabilities of non-disjoint events.","why":"The complement is a single product, which is easy to compute.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-16","topic":"axioms","sub":"positions in a random order","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Six speakers are scheduled in a uniformly random order. What is the probability that there are exactly <b>three</b> speakers between speaker $X$ and speaker $Y$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.13333333333333333,"display":"2/15"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"2/15","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.13333333333333333},{"text":"1/15","correct":false,"why":"Each pair of positions has two orderings of $X$ and $Y$; only one has been counted.","v":0.06666666666666667},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This does not correspond to the count of position pairs with a gap of three speakers.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/9","correct":false,"why":"The two speakers cannot occupy the same slot: there are $6\\cdot5 = 30$ ordered position pairs, not 36.","v":0.1111111111111111}],"solution":["The positions of $X$ and $Y$ are equally likely to be any of the $6\\cdot5 = 30$ ordered pairs of different slots.","Three speakers between them means the slots differ by 4: pairs $(1,5),(2,6)$ and their reverses: 4 ordered pairs.","$P = 4/30 = 2/15$."],"hints":["Only the slots of $X$ and $Y$ matter; how many ordered pairs of slots are there?","What must the difference of the slot numbers be for exactly three between them?","Count favourable ordered pairs."],"concept":"Counting position patterns in a random order.","misconception":"Not accounting for the two orders of the two speakers.","why":"All ordered slot pairs are equally likely under a uniform random schedule.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-17","topic":"axioms","sub":"birthday-type problem","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Each person independently has a favourite weekday, equally likely to be any of the 7 days. What is the smallest group size for which the probability that <b>at least two people share a favourite day</b> is at least $\\tfrac12$?","data":"","answer":{"value":4,"display":"4"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"4","correct":true,"why":"","v":4},{"text":"3","correct":false,"why":"For $n=3$ the probability of a shared day is $1-\\frac{7\\cdot6\\cdot5}{343} \\approx 0.388$, below one half.","v":3},{"text":"5","correct":false,"why":"The probability already exceeds one half at $n=4$ (about 0.650), so 5 is not the smallest.","v":5},{"text":"7","correct":false,"why":"Seven people guarantee a match only at 8; the probability passes 1/2 much earlier.","v":7}],"solution":["$P(\\text{no shared day}) = \\dfrac{7}{7}\\cdot\\dfrac{6}{7}\\cdots$.","$n=3$: $\\frac{7\\cdot6\\cdot5}{7^3} = 0.612$, so $P(\\text{share}) = 0.388$.","$n=4$: $\\frac{7\\cdot6\\cdot5\\cdot4}{7^4} = 0.350$, so $P(\\text{share}) = 0.650\\ge0.5$.","Smallest $n = 4$."],"hints":["Compute the probability that all favourite days are different.","Subtract it from 1 and test small group sizes.","Find the first $n$ for which the shared-day probability is at least $0.5$."],"concept":"Birthday problem via the complement.","misconception":"Expecting a group about half the number of categories.","why":"The number of pairs grows quickly, so repeats appear earlier than intuition suggests.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-18","topic":"axioms","sub":"random split of a group","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Ten colleagues, four of them women, are allocated at random to two teams of five. Find the chance that each team contains the <b>same number of women</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.47619047619047616,"display":"0.4762"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4762","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.47619047619047616},{"text":"0.2381","correct":false,"why":"The first team is the reference team; there is no further division by 2.","v":0.23809523809523808},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"There is no reason for the answer to be one half: equal numbers (2 and 2) is only one of several splits.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.0238","correct":false,"why":"The numbers of men in the team must be chosen too: $\\binom{6}{3}$ ways.","v":0.023809523809523808}],"solution":["Equal numbers of women means 2 women (and 3 men) in the first team; then the second team also has 2 women.","$P = \\dfrac{\\binom42\\binom63}{\\binom{10}{5}} = \\dfrac{6\\cdot20}{252} = \\dfrac{10}{21} \\approx 0.476$."],"hints":["Focus on one team; what composition makes both teams equal in women?","With 4 women in total, the first team has exactly how many women?","Use hypergeometric counts for that team."],"concept":"Hypergeometric probability for a random split.","misconception":"Dividing by 2 unnecessarily or ignoring one gender.","why":"Choosing the first team determines the second.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-19","topic":"axioms","sub":"exactly one of three","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A visitor to a store buys a jacket with probability 0.50, shoes with probability 0.40 and a bag with probability 0.30. Jacket and shoes: 0.20; jacket and bag: 0.15; shoes and bag: 0.10; all three: 0.05. What is the probability that the visitor buys <b>exactly one</b> of the three items?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.45000000000000007,"display":"0.45"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.45","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.45000000000000007},{"text":"1.2","correct":false,"why":"The sum of marginals counts customers buying several items more than once.","v":1.2},{"text":"0.8","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least one item, not exactly one.","v":0.8},{"text":"0.75","correct":false,"why":"Customers who buy two items are counted once here but should not be counted at all; subtract each pair twice and add back three times the triple.","v":0.75}],"solution":["Jacket only: $0.50-0.20-0.15+0.05 = 0.20$.","Shoes only: $0.40-0.20-0.10+0.05 = 0.15$.","Bag only: $0.30-0.15-0.10+0.05 = 0.10$.","Exactly one: $0.20+0.15+0.10 = 0.45$."],"hints":["Compute the probability of buying only each item.","\"Jacket only\" removes the pairs with shoes and with bag but must restore the triple once.","Add the three \"only\" probabilities."],"concept":"Exactly-one regions in a three-event Venn diagram.","misconception":"Mistaking at least one for exactly one.","why":"The three \"only\" regions are disjoint and form the event \"exactly one\".","fam":"axioms:inclusion-exclusion"},{"id":"AXM-20","topic":"axioms","sub":"symmetry","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Cards from a thoroughly mixed pack are turned over one by one. Find the probability that <b>card number 30</b> is either a heart or a king.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3076923076923077,"display":"4/13"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"4/13","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3076923076923077},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of a heart only; the three kings that are not hearts were left out.","v":0.25},{"text":"581/2652","correct":false,"why":"The answer does not depend on the position: by symmetry every card is equally likely to be in the 30th place.","v":0.21907993966817496},{"text":"17/52","correct":false,"why":"The king of hearts is a heart and a king; there are $13+4-1 = 16$ qualifying cards, not 17.","v":0.3269230769230769}],"solution":["By symmetry the 30th card is equally likely to be any of the 52 cards.","Qualifying cards: 13 hearts plus 3 kings that are not hearts: 16.","$P = 16/52 = 4/13$."],"hints":["Does the position in the deck change which cards are possible?","Count cards that are hearts or kings, taking care of the overlap.","Divide the number of qualifying cards by 52."],"concept":"Symmetry in random orderings.","misconception":"Conditioning on irrelevant earlier cards.","why":"Every card is equally likely to be at any given position.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-21","topic":"axioms","sub":"first success in a sequence","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A pack is thoroughly mixed and its cards are revealed one at a time. What is the chance that the <b>very first heart</b> is revealed at the fifth card?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.08228391356542616,"display":"0.0823"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0823","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.08228391356542616},{"text":"0.0791","correct":false,"why":"With replacement the factors would not change; here each non-heart removed lowers both the numerator and denominator.","v":0.0791015625},{"text":"0.3038","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the first four cards are not hearts; the fifth must also be a heart.","v":0.30381752701080433},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that a particular card is a heart; the first heart arriving on the 5th card requires four non-hearts first.","v":0.25}],"solution":["The first four cards are non-hearts: $\\dfrac{39}{52}\\cdot\\dfrac{38}{51}\\cdot\\dfrac{37}{50}\\cdot\\dfrac{36}{49}$. The fifth is a heart given 48 cards remain, 13 of them hearts: $\\dfrac{13}{48}$.","$P = \\dfrac{39\\cdot38\\cdot37\\cdot36\\cdot13}{52\\cdot51\\cdot50\\cdot49\\cdot48} = \\dfrac{25\\,662\\,312}{311\\,875\\,200} \\approx 0.0823$."],"hints":["What must happen on each of the first four cards, and then on the fifth?","Use the multiplication rule with the shrinking deck.","The fifth card is a heart out of the 48 remaining cards."],"concept":"Sequential probabilities without replacement.","misconception":"Using fixed probabilities across draws.","why":"Each factor conditions on all earlier cards.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-22","topic":"axioms","sub":"sequential selection","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The top three cards of a mixed pack are turned over. Find the probability that <b>no two of them share a suit</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3976470588235294,"display":"0.3976"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3976","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3976470588235294},{"text":"0.375","correct":false,"why":"Without replacement the denominators fall to 51 and 50 and the numerators count remaining cards of unused suits.","v":0.375},{"text":"0.3899","correct":false,"why":"After two cards have been dealt only 50 cards remain, so the last denominator should be 50.","v":0.3898500576701268},{"text":"0.0166","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of three cards of specified suits in a fixed order, not any three different suits.","v":0.01656862745098039}],"solution":["First card: any (probability 1). Second: must be in one of the other 3 suits: $39/51$. Third: one of the 2 unused suits: $26/50$.","$P = \\dfrac{39}{51}\\cdot\\dfrac{26}{50} = \\dfrac{169}{425} \\approx 0.398$."],"hints":["Does the first card impose any condition?","Given the suits already used, how many cards remain in unused suits?","Multiply the conditional probabilities."],"concept":"Multiplication rule for sequential distinctness.","misconception":"Using $52$ in every denominator.","why":"After each card the deck shrinks and the unused-suit cards are counted afresh.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-24","topic":"axioms","sub":"committees: at least one of each","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A panel of 5 is chosen at random from 4 economists, 5 statisticians and 3 sociologists. What is the probability that the panel contains <b>at least one economist and at least one statistician</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.9027777777777778,"display":"0.9028"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.9028","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.9027777777777778},{"text":"0.9293","correct":false,"why":"This excludes only panels with no economist; panels with no statistician also fail the requirement.","v":0.9292929292929293},{"text":"0.0972","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of the opposite: the panel lacks economists or lacks statisticians.","v":0.09722222222222222},{"text":"0.1389","correct":false,"why":"This treats two members as independently chosen from the whole group and ignores the panel size.","v":0.1388888888888889}],"solution":["Total: $\\binom{12}{5} = 792$. No economist: $\\binom{8}{5} = 56$. No statistician: $\\binom{7}{5} = 21$. Neither (all sociologists, only 3): $\\binom35 = 0$.","Lacking at least one: $56+21-0 = 77$.","$P = 1-\\dfrac{77}{792} \\approx 0.903$."],"hints":["Use the complement: the panel lacks economists or lacks statisticians.","Apply inclusion-exclusion to those two \"lacks\" events.","A panel with neither is impossible because only 3 sociologists exist."],"concept":"Complement with inclusion-exclusion in sampling.","misconception":"Adding unwanted cases without removing the overlap.","why":"The complement of \"both present\" is the union of two \"absent\" events.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-25","topic":"axioms","sub":"matching letters","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"One tile is picked blindly from a set bearing the ten letters of STATISTICS, and another from a different set bearing the twelve letters of ECONOMETRICS. Find the probability that both tiles show the same letter.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.08333333333333333,"display":"1/12"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/12","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.08333333333333333},{"text":"1/26","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that two letters from a uniformly random alphabet match; the words have unequal letter frequencies.","v":0.038461538461538464},{"text":"5/12","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of ECONOMETRICS letters that appear in STATISTICS; it is not a probability of matching one specific draw.","v":0.4166666666666667},{"text":"7/40","correct":false,"why":"Letters that appear in both words are S, T, I, C; each contributes the product of its two frequencies.","v":0.175}],"solution":["Letter frequencies: STATISTICS has S:3, T:3, I:2, C:1, A:1; ECONOMETRICS has E:2, C:2, O:2, N:1, M:1, T:1, R:1, I:1, S:1.","Common letters S, T, I, C: $\\frac{3}{10}\\frac1{12}+\\frac{3}{10}\\frac1{12}+\\frac{2}{10}\\frac1{12}+\\frac{1}{10}\\frac{2}{12} = \\frac{10}{120} = \\frac1{12}$."],"hints":["Which letters appear in both words, and how often in each?","For each common letter multiply its probabilities in the two words.","Add the products."],"concept":"Probability of a match between independent draws from different populations.","misconception":"Using unconditional alphabet probabilities.","why":"The matching events for different letters are disjoint.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-26","topic":"axioms","sub":"symmetry: last item","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A box holds 15 white and 5 black marbles. They are removed one at a time in a uniformly random order until the box is empty. What is the probability that <b>all black marbles are removed before the last white marble</b>, i.e. that the last marble removed is white?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.75,"display":"3/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"3/4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.75},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the last marble is black.","v":0.25},{"text":"21/38","correct":false,"why":"Only the last two marbles have been considered; the question concerns only the last one.","v":0.5526315789473684},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The proportions of white and black marbles are not equal, so the last marble is not equally likely to be either colour.","v":0.5}],"solution":["All 20 orderings are equally likely, so each marble is equally likely to be last.","The last marble is white with probability $15/20 = 3/4$, which is exactly the event that all black marbles have been removed before the last white one."],"hints":["Which single marble position decides the event?","By symmetry, which marbles are equally likely to be last?","Count white marbles among 20."],"concept":"Symmetry argument for the last element in a random order.","misconception":"Trying to condition on the entire removal sequence.","why":"The last position is occupied by a uniformly random marble.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-27","topic":"axioms","sub":"complement of a union","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The probability that stock $X$ rises on a given day is 0.50, that stock $Y$ rises is 0.30, and that both rise is 0.10. What is the probability that <b>neither</b> rises?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.29999999999999993,"display":"0.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.29999999999999993},{"text":"0.35","correct":false,"why":"This multiplies the two \"does not rise\" probabilities, which assumes independence; the data give $P(X\\cap Y)=0.10 \\ne 0.5\\times0.3$.","v":0.35},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"Days on which both stocks rise were subtracted twice; the overlap must be added back.","v":0.2},{"text":"0.7","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least one stock rises.","v":0.7}],"solution":["$P(X\\cup Y) = 0.50+0.30-0.10 = 0.70$.","$P(\\text{neither}) = 1-0.70 = 0.30$."],"hints":["\"Neither rises\" is the complement of \"at least one rises\".","Find the probability of at least one using the addition rule.","Subtract from 1."],"concept":"Complement of a union.","misconception":"Subtracting marginals without adding back the intersection.","why":"Every day belongs either to the union or to its complement.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-28","topic":"axioms","sub":"exactly one of two","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"In a given quarter, a company wins contract $A$ with probability 0.45 and contract $B$ with probability 0.35. It wins both with probability 0.20. What is the probability that it wins <b>exactly one</b> of the two contracts?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4,"display":"0.4"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.4},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of winning at least one contract, including the case of winning both.","v":0.6000000000000001},{"text":"0.8","correct":false,"why":"This counts the \"both\" outcome twice and includes it.","v":0.8},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of winning both contracts.","v":0.2}],"solution":["Only $A$: $0.45-0.20 = 0.25$. Only $B$: $0.35-0.20 = 0.15$.","Exactly one: $0.25+0.15 = 0.40$."],"hints":["Exactly one means $A$ only or $B$ only.","Remove the overlap from each marginal probability.","Add the two \"only\" probabilities."],"concept":"Exactly-one events from marginals and the intersection.","misconception":"Using the union probability for \"exactly one\".","why":"The \"only\" regions are disjoint.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-29","topic":"axioms","sub":"bounds on probabilities","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Suppose $P(A)=0.7$ and $P(B)=0.8$, and nothing else is known about how the two events relate. Over all admissible joint distributions, what is the greatest probability that $A$ occurs while $B$ does not?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.19999999999999996,"display":"0.2"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.19999999999999996},{"text":"0.7","correct":false,"why":"This would require $A$ and $B$ to be disjoint, but $P(A)+P(B) = 1.5 > 1$ rules that out.","v":0.7},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is $1-P(A)$, unrelated to the maximum of $P(A\\cap B^c)$.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.14","correct":false,"why":"This assumes independence; the maximum over all possible dependence structures is larger.","v":0.13999999999999999}],"solution":["$A\\cap B^c$ is contained in $B^c$, so $P(A\\cap B^c)\\le P(B^c) = 0.2$. It is also contained in $A$, so $\\le 0.7$. The binding bound is $0.2$.","It is attained when $B^c\\subset A$: then $P(A\\cap B^c)=0.2$ and $P(A\\cap B) = 0.5$, which is consistent."],"hints":["An event inside two events has probability at most either one.","What is $P(B^c)$?","Check that the bound can be attained with a valid $P(A\\cap B)$."],"concept":"Upper bounds using monotonicity.","misconception":"Using only $P(A)$ as the bound.","why":"A subset has probability no larger than its superset.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-30","topic":"axioms","sub":"partition of a sample space","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Firms in a registry are classed as Small, Medium, Large or Multinational. A random firm is Small with probability 0.55, Medium with 0.30 and Large with 0.10. Foreign-trade documents are required for Large and Multinational firms. What is the probability that a randomly chosen firm needs them?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.14999999999999997,"display":"0.15"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.15","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.14999999999999997},{"text":"0.1","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of Large firms only; Multinational firms also need documents.","v":0.1},{"text":"0.85","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of Small or Medium firms, who do not need them.","v":0.8500000000000001},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of Multinational firms only; Large firms need documents too.","v":0.05}],"solution":["The four classes partition the registry, so $P(\\text{Multinational}) = 1-0.55-0.30-0.10 = 0.05$.","Large and Multinational are disjoint: $0.10+0.05 = 0.15$.","(Equivalently $1-P(\\text{Small}\\cup\\text{Medium}) = 0.15$.)"],"hints":["The probabilities of all four classes must sum to 1.","Find the missing class probability.","Add the two classes that need documents."],"concept":"Using a partition summing to 1.","misconception":"Forgetting the unstated class.","why":"Disjoint classes covering the space sum to probability 1.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-31","topic":"axioms","sub":"geometric probability","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A point is chosen uniformly at random in the unit square $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le1$. What is the probability that it lies <b>below the line $y = 1-x$ and above the curve $y = x^2/2$</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.39871747423554393,"display":"0.3987"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3987","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.39871747423554393},{"text":"0.3333","correct":false,"why":"This would be the area between the curves if they met at $x=1$; they actually intersect at $x \\approx 0.732$.","v":0.33333333333333337},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the area below the line $y=1-x$ alone, ignoring the curve.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.732","correct":false,"why":"This is the intersection abscissa, not the area.","v":0.732}],"solution":["The curves meet where $x^2/2 = 1-x$, i.e. $x^2+2x-2=0$, so $x = \\sqrt3-1 \\approx 0.732$.","Area $= \\int_0^{0.732}\\left(1-x-\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\right)dx = x - \\dfrac{x^2}{2}-\\dfrac{x^3}{6}\\Big|_{0.732} \\approx 0.3987$.","With a uniform distribution on the unit square, probability equals area."],"hints":["Find where the line and the curve intersect.","The region lies between the curve (below) and the line (above) for $x$ from 0 to that point.","Integrate $1-x-x^2/2$."],"concept":"Geometric probability via integration.","misconception":"Using the wrong integration limits.","why":"For a uniform distribution, probability is area divided by total area (here 1).","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-32","topic":"axioms","sub":"three-event union","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"In a survey, 50% of respondents use bank $A$, 40% use bank $B$ and 30% use bank $C$; 20% use both $A$ and $B$, 10% use $A$ and $C$, 15% use $B$ and $C$, and 5% use all three. What proportion use <b>none</b> of the three banks?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.19999999999999996,"display":"0.2"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.19999999999999996},{"text":"0.8","correct":false,"why":"This is the proportion using at least one bank, not none.","v":0.8},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"The triple intersection must be added back when computing the union.","v":0.25},{"text":"-0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is negative; summing the marginals double counts overlapping customers.","v":-0.19999999999999996}],"solution":["$P(A\\cup B\\cup C) = 0.5+0.4+0.3-0.2-0.1-0.15+0.05 = 0.80$.","$P(\\text{none}) = 1-0.80 = 0.20$."],"hints":["\"None\" is the complement of the union.","Use the three-event inclusion-exclusion formula.","Remember the triple term has a plus sign."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion for three events.","misconception":"Omitting the triple intersection.","why":"The alternating signs correct for multiple counting.","fam":"axioms:inclusion-exclusion"},{"id":"AXM-33","topic":"axioms","sub":"matching problem","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Five employees each receive, at random, one of five labelled parcels (each parcel to exactly one employee, all assignments equally likely). What is the probability that <b>no employee</b> receives the parcel labelled with their own name?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.36666666666666664,"display":"0.3667"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3667","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.36666666666666664},{"text":"0.9917","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that not everybody gets their own parcel; \"no one\" is much stronger.","v":0.9916666666666667},{"text":"0.8","correct":false,"why":"This treats each employee independently, ignoring that the assignments are a permutation.","v":0.8},{"text":"0.3679","correct":false,"why":"This is the limiting value as the number of employees grows; for 5 the exact value is $44/120$.","v":0.36787944117144233}],"solution":["By inclusion-exclusion, the number of permutations with no fixed point is","$5!\\left(1-1+\\tfrac1{2!}-\\tfrac1{3!}+\\tfrac1{4!}-\\tfrac1{5!}\\right) = 120-120+60-20+5-1 = 44$.","$P = 44/120 = 11/30 \\approx 0.367$."],"hints":["\"No one receives their own parcel\": use inclusion-exclusion over the events \"employee $i$ gets their own parcel\".","The number of permutations fixing $k$ specified employees is $(5-k)!$.","Alternate signs: $120 - 5\\cdot24 + 10\\cdot6 - 10\\cdot2 + 5\\cdot1 - 1$."],"concept":"Derangements via inclusion-exclusion.","misconception":"Treating the matches as independent events.","why":"The alternating sum counts permutations by the number of forced matches.","fam":"axioms:matching"},{"id":"AXM-34","topic":"axioms","sub":"matching problem: comparing sizes","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Let $q_n$ be the probability that none of $n$ people receives their own item when $n$ items are handed back at random. For which of $n = 5, 8, 13, 24$ is $q_n$ <b>largest</b>? (Recall $q_n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n}(-1)^k/k!$.)","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"n = 8"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"n = 8","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"n = 5","correct":false,"why":"$q_5 = 44/120 \\approx 0.3667$ lies below $e^{-1} = 0.3679$ because $n$ is odd."},{"text":"n = 13","correct":false,"why":"$q_{13}$ lies below $e^{-1}$ (odd $n$) by about $1/14!$."},{"text":"n = 24","correct":false,"why":"$q_{24}$ is above $e^{-1}$ but only by about $1/25!$, far less than for $n=8$."}],"solution":["$q_n$ is a partial sum of an alternating series for $e^{-1}$, so it lies above $e^{-1}$ when the first omitted term, $k=n+1$, is negative, i.e. when $n$ is even, and below it when $n$ is odd.","Odd $n$ (5, 13) give values below $e^{-1}$. Among the even values 8 and 24 the excess is about $1/(n+1)!$, which is larger for $n=8$ ($1/9!$).","Therefore $q_8$ is largest."],"hints":["Consider the sign of $q_n - e^{-1}$ in terms of the first omitted term of the series.","Which of the four $n$ have $q_n$ above $e^{-1}$?","Among those, the excess is of size about $1/(n+1)!$."],"concept":"Alternating series and the behaviour of $q_n$.","misconception":"Assuming $q_n$ is monotone in $n$.","why":"The partial sums oscillate around the limit with shrinking amplitude.","fam":"axioms:matching"},{"id":"AXM-35","topic":"axioms","sub":"matching problem: exactly k matches","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four candidates are randomly assigned to four interview rooms, one candidate per room, each assignment equally likely. Each candidate has been told one \"home\" room. What is the probability that <b>exactly two</b> candidates are placed in their own home room?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"0.25"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.25","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.25},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"After fixing two candidates in place, the other two must both be misplaced: only one arrangement (swap) works, not two.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.0417","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all four are in their own room.","v":0.041666666666666664},{"text":"0.3333","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly one candidate in the home room: 4 choices for that candidate and 2 ways to misplace the other three.","v":0.3333333333333333}],"solution":["Choose which 2 candidates are in their home room: $\\binom42 = 6$.","The other two must both be in the wrong room: with only two rooms left that means swapping, which is 1 way.","$P = 6/24 = 1/4$."],"hints":["Choose the two candidates in the correct rooms.","How many ways can the other two be placed so that both are wrong?","Divide by $4! = 24$."],"concept":"Counting permutations with exactly $k$ fixed points.","misconception":"Not requiring the remaining candidates to be misplaced.","why":"Fixed-point patterns partition the permutations.","fam":"axioms:matching"},{"id":"AXM-36","topic":"axioms","sub":"sampling without replacement","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A card is dealt from a freshly shuffled pack and kept aside; then a second and a third card are dealt. Find the probability that the first is a heart and the second and third are both black.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.06372549019607843,"display":"0.0637"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0637","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.06372549019607843},{"text":"0.0625","correct":false,"why":"Without replacement the denominators fall to 51 and 50 and the black count drops after the first black card.","v":0.0625},{"text":"0.0663","correct":false,"why":"After the second card (black) is removed, only 25 black cards remain.","v":0.06627450980392156},{"text":"0.3824","correct":false,"why":"The cards are drawn in a specified order, so the denominator is $52\\cdot51\\cdot50$, not $\\binom{52}{3}$.","v":0.38235294117647056}],"solution":["$P(\\text{heart}) = 13/52$. Given that, 26 black cards among 51: $26/51$. Then 25 black among 50: $25/50$.","$P = \\dfrac{13}{52}\\cdot\\dfrac{26}{51}\\cdot\\dfrac{25}{50} \\approx 0.0637$."],"hints":["The order is specified, so apply the chain rule.","After each draw, how many cards and how many black cards remain?","Multiply the three conditional probabilities."],"concept":"Chain rule for sequential draws.","misconception":"Using the original deck size each time.","why":"Each conditional probability reflects the cards removed so far.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-37","topic":"axioms","sub":"complement of an intersection","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A stock fund beats its benchmark in a year with probability 0.60 and a bond fund beats its own with probability 0.30. Both beat their benchmarks in the same year with probability 0.20. What is the probability that <b>at least one</b> of the two funds fails to beat its benchmark?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8,"display":"0.8"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.8},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that neither fund beats its benchmark.","v":0.30000000000000004},{"text":"1.1","correct":false,"why":"Adding the failure probabilities counts years when both fail twice and exceeds 1.","v":1.1},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that both succeed, the complement of the event asked for.","v":0.2}],"solution":["\"At least one fails\" is the complement of \"both succeed\". $P = 1 - 0.20 = 0.80$.","Check via the union of failures: $0.40+0.70-P(\\text{both fail}) = 1.10-0.30 = 0.80$."],"hints":["What is the opposite of \"at least one fails\"?","Use $(A^c\\cup B^c)=(A\\cap B)^c$.","Subtract the probability of both succeeding from 1."],"concept":"De Morgan’s law for probabilities.","misconception":"Confusing \"at least one fails\" with \"neither succeeds\".","why":"The union of the complements is the complement of the intersection.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-38","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability on two dice","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fair four-sided die (faces 1 to 4) and a fair six-sided die are rolled. What is the probability that the sum is <b>at most 5</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4166666666666667,"display":"5/12"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"5/12","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.4166666666666667},{"text":"5/18","correct":false,"why":"The sample space has $4\\times6 = 24$ outcomes, not 36.","v":0.2777777777777778},{"text":"7/12","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the sum is at least 6.","v":0.5833333333333334},{"text":"5/24","correct":false,"why":"This counts only combinations that sum to 5; sums of 2, 3 and 4 are also at most 5.","v":0.20833333333333334}],"solution":["The 24 outcomes are equally likely. For the four-sided die value $a$, the six-sided die must be at most $5-a$: $a=1$: 4 values; $a=2$: 3; $a=3$: 2; $a=4$: 1.","$P = 10/24 = 5/12$."],"hints":["How many equally likely outcomes are there for these two different dice?","For each value of the first die, how many values of the second keep the sum at most 5?","Add and divide."],"concept":"Classical probability with unequal dice.","misconception":"Using a 6-by-6 grid.","why":"Counting favourable pairs over the actual sample space gives the probability.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-42","topic":"axioms","sub":"joint table","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Firms are classified by revenue growth (rows) and cost growth (columns) into Low (L), Medium (M) and High (H). The joint probabilities are:<table class=\"jt\"><tr><th></th><th>cost L</th><th>cost M</th><th>cost H</th></tr><tr><th>revenue L</th><td>.08</td><td>.06</td><td>.02</td></tr><tr><th>revenue M</th><td>.10</td><td>.20</td><td>.09</td></tr><tr><th>revenue H</th><td>.05</td><td>.15</td><td>.25</td></tr></table>What is the probability that a randomly selected firm's revenue growth category is <b>strictly higher</b> than its cost growth category?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.30000000000000004,"display":"0.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.30000000000000004},{"text":"0.53","correct":false,"why":"These are the cells where revenue and cost categories are equal (the diagonal).","v":0.53},{"text":"0.17","correct":false,"why":"These are the cells where revenue is strictly lower than cost.","v":0.16999999999999998},{"text":"0.83","correct":false,"why":"The diagonal (equal categories) has been added although \"strictly higher\" excludes it.","v":0.8300000000000001}],"solution":["Revenue strictly higher than cost: (M,L)=0.10, (H,L)=0.05, (H,M)=0.15.","Sum: $0.30$."],"hints":["Mark the cells in which the row category exceeds the column category.","These cells are mutually exclusive outcomes.","Add their probabilities."],"concept":"Reading probabilities from a joint table.","misconception":"Including the equal-category cells.","why":"Cells are disjoint, so the probability of a union of cells is the sum.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-43","topic":"axioms","sub":"dependent failures","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A data centre runs two identical servers in parallel. On a given day <b>exactly one</b> of them fails with probability 0.10, and <b>both</b> fail with probability 0.02. What is the probability that server 1 fails that day?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.07,"display":"0.07"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.07","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.07},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"Half of the \"exactly one\" probability is the chance that server 1 fails <i>alone</i>; the case where both fail must be added.","v":0.05},{"text":"0.12","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(\\text{at least one fails})$, not the probability for one particular server.","v":0.12},{"text":"0.1","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that exactly one server fails, not the marginal probability for server 1.","v":0.1}],"solution":["Let $F_1, F_2$ be the failures. By symmetry the servers are identical: $P(F_1\\cap F_2^c) = P(F_1^c\\cap F_2) = 0.10/2 = 0.05$.","$P(F_1) = P(F_1\\cap F_2^c) + P(F_1\\cap F_2) = 0.05+0.02 = 0.07$."],"hints":["Split \"exactly one fails\" into two events using the identical servers.","Server 1 fails either alone or together with server 2.","Add the probability of failing alone to the probability of both failing."],"concept":"Using symmetry to split an event.","misconception":"Reporting the \"alone\" probability as the marginal.","why":"Server 1’s failures are partitioned by whether server 2 also fails.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-44","topic":"axioms","sub":"solving for an unknown composition","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Jar 1 holds 3 red and 7 blue marbles. Jar 2 holds $r$ red and 4 blue marbles. One marble is drawn at random from each jar. The probability that the two marbles have the <b>same colour</b> is 0.40. How many red marbles are in jar 2?","data":"","answer":{"value":12,"display":"12"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"12","correct":true,"why":"","v":12},{"text":"10","correct":false,"why":"With $r=10$ the probability is $(0.3\\cdot10+2.8)/14 \\approx 0.414$, not 0.40.","v":10},{"text":"16","correct":false,"why":"With $r=16$ the probability is $(4.8+2.8)/20 = 0.38$, not 0.40.","v":16},{"text":"4","correct":false,"why":"With $r=4$ the probability is $(1.2+2.8)/8 = 0.50$, not 0.40.","v":4}],"solution":["$P(\\text{same}) = \\dfrac{3}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{r}{r+4} + \\dfrac{7}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{4}{r+4} = \\dfrac{0.3r+2.8}{r+4}$.","Set equal to 0.40: $0.3r+2.8 = 0.4r+1.6$, so $0.1r = 1.2$ and $r = 12$."],"hints":["Write $P(\\text{same colour})$ as a sum over the two colours.","Express the probabilities for jar 2 in terms of $r$.","Set the expression equal to 0.40 and solve the linear equation."],"concept":"Using a probability equation to find an unknown count.","misconception":"Leaving $r$ out of the denominator.","why":"The same-colour probability is a rational function of $r$; equating it to a value fixes $r$.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-45","topic":"axioms","sub":"problem of points","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two firms $A$ and $B$ compete in a series in which the first to win 5 contests takes the prize; each contest is won by either firm with probability $\\tfrac12$, independently. The series is cancelled when $A$ has won 3 and $B$ has won 2. What fraction of the prize should go to $A$ so that the division matches each firm's chance of winning the series?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6875,"display":"11/16"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"11/16","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6875},{"text":"3/5","correct":false,"why":"This divides in proportion to contests already won; it ignores how many more each firm needs.","v":0.6},{"text":"7/8","correct":false,"why":"This would be the answer if $B$ needed three more wins and $A$ needed only one.","v":0.875},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The firms are symmetric in strength, but $A$ is closer to the target and has a better chance.","v":0.5}],"solution":["$A$ needs 2 more wins, $B$ needs 3. Play out 4 more contests (enough to decide). $B$ wins the series only if it wins at least 3 of the 4.","$P(B) = \\dfrac{\\binom43+\\binom44}{16} = \\dfrac{5}{16}$, so $P(A) = \\dfrac{11}{16}$."],"hints":["How many more contests would settle the series at most?","B wins the series only if it wins 3 or more of those contests.","Use binomial counts with $p = 1/2$."],"concept":"Problem of points via binomial probabilities.","misconception":"Dividing in proportion to wins so far.","why":"A fair division awards each side its probability of winning had play continued.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-46","topic":"axioms","sub":"diagnose: adding overlapping events","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"\"A fair die is rolled three times. The probability of a six on each roll is $1/6$, so the probability of at least one six is $3\\times\\frac16 = \\frac12$.\" Which statement identifies the error and gives the correct value?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"The events \"six on roll $i$\" overlap, so their probabilities cannot be added; the correct value is $1-(5/6)^3\\approx0.421$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"The events \"six on roll $i$\" overlap, so their probabilities cannot be added; the correct value is $1-(5/6)^3\\approx0.421$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The error is that the rolls are dependent; the correct value is $1/6$","correct":false,"why":"The rolls are independent; the error is in adding overlapping events."},{"text":"There is no error: the three events are disjoint","correct":false,"why":"They are not disjoint: a six on roll 1 and a six on roll 2 can both occur."},{"text":"The correct value is $(1/6)^3 = 1/216$","correct":false,"why":"That is the probability of three sixes, not at least one."}],"solution":["Addition of probabilities is valid only for mutually exclusive events. Sixes on different rolls can occur together.","Use the complement: $P(\\text{no six}) = (5/6)^3 = 0.5787$, so $P(\\text{at least one}) = 0.4213$."],"hints":["Are the events \"six on roll 1\", \"six on roll 2\", \"six on roll 3\" mutually exclusive?","What happens to the sum $3\\times\\frac16$ if you ask the same question with 6 rolls?","Use the complement of \"no sixes\"."],"concept":"The addition rule requires disjointness; use the complement for \"at least one\".","misconception":"Adding probabilities of events that can co-occur.","why":"With six rolls the false method would give probability 1, which is clearly absurd.","fam":"axioms:diagnose"},{"id":"AXM-47","topic":"axioms","sub":"diagnose: complement of a product","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A fair coin is tossed twice. A student says: \"The probability of <i>not</i> getting two heads is $1 - \\frac12\\cdot\\frac12 = \\frac34$\" and then writes \"so the probability of getting <i>at most one</i> head is $\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12 = \\frac14$.\" What is the correct probability of at most one head?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.75,"display":"3/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"3/4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.75},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of two heads (or two tails) only. \"At most one head\" is the complement of \"two heads\".","v":0.25},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly one head.","v":0.5},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"At most one head can fail (outcome HH), so the probability is below 1.","v":1}],"solution":["\"At most one head\" is the complement of \"two heads\".","$P = 1 - P(HH) = 1 - \\frac14 = \\frac34$.","The student’s first line is correct and the second line contradicts it."],"hints":["Is \"at most one head\" the same event as \"not two heads\"?","The student’s first line already contains the answer.","Compute $1 - P(HH)$."],"concept":"Complement rule; consistency of reasoning.","misconception":"Using the product $P(A)P(B)$ for a complement.","why":"Listing the outcomes {HH, HT, TH, TT} confirms 3 of 4.","fam":"axioms:diagnose"},{"id":"AXM-48","topic":"axioms","sub":"bounds: Boole’s inequality","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A project can fail through a technical fault ($A$, probability 0.2), a funding shortfall ($B$, probability 0.3) or a regulatory delay ($C$, probability 0.1). Nothing is known about how these events relate. What is the <b>best upper bound</b> on the probability that the project fails through at least one of them that follows from the axioms?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6,"display":"0.6"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6},{"text":"0.006","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three occur if they were independent; the union is at least as large as each event.","v":0.006},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This is a valid bound but not the best one available.","v":1},{"text":"0.4","correct":false,"why":"No rule produces 0.4 here; the union of events cannot be bounded by a quantity smaller than its largest member, but the sum is the available upper bound.","v":0.4}],"solution":["For any events, $P(A\\cup B\\cup C) \\le P(A)+P(B)+P(C)$ (Boole's inequality).","The bound is $0.2+0.3+0.1 = 0.6$; it is attained when the events are mutually exclusive."],"hints":["Without information on overlap, what is the largest value a union can take?","Overlapping outcomes are counted more than once in a sum.","Add the three probabilities."],"concept":"Subadditivity of probability.","misconception":"Multiplying probabilities without any independence information.","why":"Adding probabilities can only over-count overlaps, never under-count.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-49","topic":"axioms","sub":"bounds: smallest possible union","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"$P(A) = 0.6$ and $P(B) = 0.5$, and nothing else is known about how $A$ and $B$ are related. What is the <b>smallest possible</b> value of $P(A\\cup B)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6,"display":"0.6"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"The union contains $A$, so it cannot be smaller than $P(A) = 0.6$.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the product $P(A)P(B)$, which is the intersection only under independence and is not a union.","v":0.3},{"text":"1.1","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(A)+P(B)$, which exceeds 1 and is not even a probability.","v":1.1}],"solution":["$A\\subset A\\cup B$ and $B\\subset A\\cup B$, so $P(A\\cup B)\\ge\\max\\{P(A),P(B)\\} = 0.6$.","The minimum is reached when $B\\subset A$, which is consistent with the given probabilities."],"hints":["Is the union ever smaller than one of its members?","When is the union as small as possible: when $A$ and $B$ overlap as much as they can.","Take the larger of the two probabilities."],"concept":"Lower bound for a union through monotonicity.","misconception":"Confusing the smallest union with the intersection.","why":"Complete overlap makes the union equal to the larger event.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-50","topic":"axioms","sub":"inclusion-exclusion with four events","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Four equally reliable sensors raise an alarm independently of the labelling of the sensors in the following symmetric way: each sensor alone has probability 0.30; each pair of sensors both alarming has probability 0.10; each triple 0.04; all four together 0.01. What is the probability that <b>at least one</b> sensor raises the alarm?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7499999999999999,"display":"0.75"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(\\text{union}) = \\sum P(A_i) - \\sum P(A_iA_j) + \\sum P(A_iA_jA_k) - P(A_1A_2A_3A_4)$.","There are 4 singles, $\\binom42 = 6$ pairs, $\\binom43 = 4$ triples.","$= 1.20-0.60+0.16-0.01 = 0.75$."],"hints":["How many singles, pairs and triples are there among four events?","Signs alternate: plus, minus, plus, minus.","Compute $4(0.30)-6(0.10)+4(0.04)-0.01$."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion with four events.","misconception":"Using the wrong number of pairs or triples.","why":"Each outcome in several events is counted once by the alternating sum.","fam":"axioms:inclusion-exclusion"},{"id":"AXM-51","topic":"axioms","sub":"number theory probability: two divisors","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A whole number is chosen at random from 1 to 100, all equally likely. What is the probability that it is divisible by 2 <b>or</b> by 3?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.67,"display":"0.67"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Multiples of 2: 50. Multiples of 3: 33. Multiples of 6 (both): 16.","$50+33-16 = 67$, so $P = 0.67$."],"hints":["Count multiples of 2, multiples of 3, and multiples of both.","Both means divisible by 6.","Add and subtract the overlap."],"concept":"Addition rule on counted outcomes.","misconception":"Adding $50+33$ without removing the multiples of 6.","why":"Numbers divisible by both are counted in each list.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-52","topic":"axioms","sub":"number theory probability: three divisors","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A whole number is chosen at random from 1 to 100. What is the probability that it is divisible by <b>at least one</b> of 2, 3 and 5?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.74,"display":"0.74"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.74","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.74},{"text":"0.71","correct":false,"why":"The numbers divisible by all three (multiples of 30: 30, 60, 90) must be added back.","v":0.71},{"text":"0.7333","correct":false,"why":"This treats divisibility by 2, 3 and 5 as independent, which is only approximately true in 1 to 100.","v":0.7333333333333334},{"text":"1.03","correct":false,"why":"This sums the three counts; numbers divisible by more than one are counted repeatedly (and the result exceeds 1).","v":1.03}],"solution":["Singles: $50+33+20 = 103$. Pairs (multiples of 6, 10, 15): $16+10+6 = 32$. Triple (multiples of 30): 3.","$103-32+3 = 74$, so $P = 0.74$."],"hints":["Count multiples of each divisor, then of each pair of divisors, then of all three.","Pairs are multiples of 6, 10 and 15; the triple is multiples of 30.","Singles minus pairs plus triple."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion with three events on counted outcomes.","misconception":"Omitting the triple intersection.","why":"Inclusion-exclusion corrects every multiple counting.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-53","topic":"axioms","sub":"geometric probability: product","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two numbers $X$ and $Y$ are chosen independently and uniformly from $(0,1)$. What is the probability that their <b>product is less than $\\tfrac12$</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8465735902799727,"display":"0.8466"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8466","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.8465735902799727},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"The region $xy<\\tfrac12$ includes more than half the square: above the line $x=\\tfrac12$ the curve $y<1/(2x)$ still keeps a large part.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.6931","correct":false,"why":"This is only part of the area (the integral of $1/(2x)$ times 2); the strip $x<\\tfrac12$ contributes area $\\tfrac12$ as well.","v":0.6931471805599453},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is the area of the square $[0,\\tfrac12]^2$, a small part of the region $xy<\\tfrac12$.","v":0.25}],"solution":["For $x\\le\\tfrac12$ every $y\\in(0,1)$ works: area $\\tfrac12$.","For $x>\\tfrac12$ we need $y<\\dfrac{1}{2x}$: area $\\int_{1/2}^{1}\\dfrac{1}{2x}dx = \\tfrac12\\ln2$.","$P = \\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\ln2 \\approx 0.847$."],"hints":["Represent the pair as a point in the unit square and shade $xy<\\tfrac12$.","Split the region at $x=\\tfrac12$.","For $x>\\tfrac12$ the boundary is the curve $y = 1/(2x)$."],"concept":"Geometric probability with a non-linear boundary.","misconception":"Assuming the region is half the square.","why":"The probability equals the area of the region because the distribution is uniform on the unit square.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-54","topic":"axioms","sub":"geometric probability: triangle from a broken stick","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A stick of length 1 is broken at two independent random points. What is the probability that the three pieces can form a <b>triangle</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"1/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.25},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The triangle condition (each piece below $\\tfrac12$) fails on a larger region than this; the feasible area is a quarter of the square.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This is not the area of the feasible region in the unit square.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/8","correct":false,"why":"This is half of the correct feasible area.","v":0.125}],"solution":["Pieces $a, b-a, 1-b$ (with $a<b$) form a triangle iff each is shorter than $\\tfrac12$.","In the $(a,b)$ triangle $0<a<b<1$ (area $\\tfrac12$) the condition defines a smaller triangle of area $\\tfrac18$.","By symmetry $P = \\dfrac{1/8}{1/2} = \\dfrac14$."],"hints":["What condition must three lengths satisfy to form a triangle?","With total length 1, that means every piece is shorter than $\\tfrac12$.","Describe the allowed region of cut points in the unit square."],"concept":"Geometric probability for a classic region.","misconception":"Guessing 1/2 by symmetry.","why":"Triangle inequality becomes three linear constraints on the cut points.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-55","topic":"axioms","sub":"geometric probability: closeness","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two numbers $X$ and $Y$ are chosen independently and uniformly from $(0,1)$. What is the probability that they differ by <b>less than 0.2</b>, i.e. $|X-Y|<0.2$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3599999999999999,"display":"0.36"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["The region $|x-y|\\ge0.2$ consists of two right triangles with legs $0.8$.","Their total area is $2\\cdot\\tfrac12(0.8)^2 = 0.64$.","$P(|X-Y|<0.2) = 1-0.64 = 0.36$."],"hints":["Picture the unit square and the band around the diagonal.","The complement is two triangles in the corners.","Each triangle has legs of length $1-0.2$."],"concept":"Geometric probability using complementary triangles.","misconception":"Using $0.2$ or $0.4$ directly as the probability.","why":"Area of the band equals 1 minus the area of the two corner triangles.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-56","topic":"axioms","sub":"exclusive events with a ratio","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Events $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive with $P(A\\cup B) = 0.9$ and $P(A) = 2P(B)$. Find $P(B)$.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3,"display":"0.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["For exclusive events $P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B) = 3P(B) = 0.9$.","$P(B) = 0.3$."],"hints":["What does mutual exclusivity say about $P(A\\cup B)$?","Write $P(A)$ in terms of $P(B)$.","Solve the linear equation."],"concept":"Additivity of disjoint events.","misconception":"Subtracting an intersection that is zero.","why":"Disjoint events share no outcomes, so probabilities add.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-57","topic":"axioms","sub":"sequences with a restriction: no two consecutive heads","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fair coin is tossed 5 times. What is the probability that <b>no two consecutive tosses are both heads</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.40625,"display":"13/32"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"13/32","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.40625},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"Half of the sequences would be allowed only if the restriction were trivial; many sequences contain adjacent heads.","v":0.5},{"text":"11/32","correct":false,"why":"This miscounts the valid sequences by two; the Fibonacci recursion gives 13.","v":0.34375},{"text":"19/32","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least two consecutive heads occur.","v":0.59375}],"solution":["Count valid sequences: lengths 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 give 2, 3, 5, 8, 13 (each is the sum of the previous two).","$P = 13/32$."],"hints":["Count the sequences of length $n$ with no \"HH\" by looking at how they end.","The counts follow $a_n = a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}$.","Divide $a_5$ by $2^5$."],"concept":"Probability via counting restricted sequences.","misconception":"Assuming independence between the restriction and sequence length.","why":"All $2^5$ sequences are equally likely.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-58","topic":"axioms","sub":"symmetry: ranking","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Six applicants are ranked in a uniformly random order. What is the probability that applicant $X$ is ranked <b>above both</b> applicant $Y$ and applicant $Z$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3333333333333333,"display":"1/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["Only the relative order of $X, Y, Z$ matters, and all $3! = 6$ relative orders are equally likely.","$X$ is first in 2 of them: $P = 2/6 = 1/3$."],"hints":["Does the position of the other three applicants matter?","How many equally likely relative orders do $X, Y, Z$ have?","In how many of them is $X$ first?"],"concept":"Symmetry for random rankings.","misconception":"Counting all six applicants.","why":"Restricting attention to three items keeps the orders equally likely.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-59","topic":"axioms","sub":"bounds: smallest triple intersection","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three events have $P(A) = 0.6$, $P(B) = 0.7$ and $P(C) = 0.8$, with no other information. What is the <b>smallest possible</b> value of $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.09999999999999964,"display":"0.1"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.09999999999999964},{"text":"0.336","correct":false,"why":"This is the value under independence, not the minimum over all dependence structures.","v":0.336},{"text":"0","correct":false,"why":"The three events are so likely that they must overlap: the failures have total probability $0.4+0.3+0.2 = 0.9<1$.","v":0},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is the largest value possible, $\\min P$, not the smallest.","v":0.6}],"solution":["$P((A\\cap B\\cap C)^c) = P(A^c\\cup B^c\\cup C^c) \\le 0.4+0.3+0.2 = 0.9$.","So $P(A\\cap B\\cap C) \\ge 1-0.9 = 0.1$."],"hints":["Work with the complements: each has a small probability.","Apply Boole’s inequality to the union of the complements.","The intersection is the complement of that union."],"concept":"Lower bound for an intersection via complements.","misconception":"Using the independence value.","why":"If failures are unlikely, success of all is forced to have probability at least $1-\\sum P(\\text{failure})$.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-60","topic":"axioms","sub":"statements that must hold","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"For arbitrary events $A$ and $B$ in the same sample space, which statement is <b>always</b> true?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(A\\cap B) \\le \\min\\{P(A), P(B)\\}$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(A\\cap B) \\le \\min\\{P(A), P(B)\\}$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(A\\cup B) \\ge P(A)+P(B)$","correct":false,"why":"The union is at most the sum: overlapping outcomes are counted twice in the sum."},{"text":"$P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$","correct":false,"why":"This holds only for independent events."},{"text":"$P(A)+P(B)\\le1$","correct":false,"why":"This holds only when the events are mutually exclusive (or for small probabilities); for example $P(A) = P(B) = 0.8$ is allowed."}],"solution":["$A\\cap B\\subset A$ and $A\\cap B\\subset B$, so by monotonicity $P(A\\cap B)\\le P(A)$ and $\\le P(B)$.","The other statements fail for suitable events."],"hints":["Test each statement with a simple example, such as $A = B$.","Which statement follows from set inclusion alone?","Monotonicity: a subset is not more probable than its superset."],"concept":"Properties that hold for all events.","misconception":"Applying independence formulas or exclusivity facts universally.","why":"Only facts derived from the axioms hold for every pair of events.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-61","topic":"axioms","sub":"inconsistent probability statements","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Which pair of statements <b>cannot</b> both be true for the same events?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(A) = 0.3$ and $P(A\\cap B) = 0.4$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(A) = 0.3$ and $P(A\\cap B) = 0.4$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(A) = 0.3$ and $P(A\\cup B) = 0.8$","correct":false,"why":"Possible: $P(B)$ could be 0.5 with no overlap."},{"text":"$P(A) = 0.6$ and $P(A^c) = 0.4$","correct":false,"why":"These sum to 1 as required."},{"text":"$P(A) = 0.7$ and $P(B) = 0.6$ with $P(A\\cup B)=1$","correct":false,"why":"Possible: this implies $P(A\\cap B) = 0.3$."}],"solution":["$A\\cap B\\subset A$ forces $P(A\\cap B)\\le P(A)$. A value of 0.4 for the intersection with $P(A)=0.3$ violates this.","The other pairs can be realised."],"hints":["Which statement involves an event and a subset of it?","Can a subset have a larger probability than the whole?","Check the other pairs by finding an example."],"concept":"Consistency of probability statements.","misconception":"Only checking whether the numbers lie between 0 and 1.","why":"Probabilities must respect set inclusion, not just lie in [0, 1].","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-62","topic":"axioms","sub":"digit strings: strictly increasing","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A 3-digit code is generated by choosing each digit independently and uniformly from 0 to 9 (leading zeros allowed). What is the probability that the digits are <b>strictly increasing</b> from left to right?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.12,"display":"0.12"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["A strictly increasing code is determined by its set of three distinct digits: $\\binom{10}{3} = 120$ codes.","$P = 120/1000 = 0.12$."],"hints":["If three distinct digits are chosen, how many orders are increasing?","So count sets of 3 distinct digits.","Divide by $10^3$."],"concept":"Counting increasing sequences by combinations.","misconception":"Using $10\\cdot9\\cdot8$.","why":"Each 3-subset of digits has exactly one increasing arrangement.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-63","topic":"axioms","sub":"complement: the larger of two dice","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair ten-sided dice (faces 1 to 10) are rolled. What is the probability that the <b>larger</b> of the two results is at least 8?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.51,"display":"0.51"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.51","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.51},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that one particular die shows at least 8.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This adds the two probabilities and counts the outcomes where both dice are high twice.","v":0.6},{"text":"0.09","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that both dice show at least 8.","v":0.09}],"solution":["The maximum is below 8 only if both dice are at most 7: $0.7\\times0.7 = 0.49$.","$P(\\max\\ge8) = 1-0.49 = 0.51$."],"hints":["When is the larger result below 8?","Both dice must be at most 7.","Take the complement."],"concept":"Complement for \"at least one is large\".","misconception":"Adding the single-die probabilities.","why":"The maximum exceeds a threshold iff at least one die does.","fam":"axioms:two-event-rules"},{"id":"AXM-64","topic":"axioms","sub":"certain events: pigeonhole in probability","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three people each have a blood-pressure status that is either \"normal\" or \"elevated\". What is the probability that at least two of them have the <b>same</b> status?","data":"","answer":{"value":1,"display":"1"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1","correct":true,"why":"","v":1},{"text":"0.75","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of two particular people matching if the statuses are equally likely; with three people a match is unavoidable.","v":0.75},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"Equal chance does not enter: only two statuses exist, so among three people a match is certain.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.875","correct":false,"why":"This would be the probability that not all three are the same; a match between two is certain.","v":0.875}],"solution":["With only two possible statuses and three people, three different statuses are impossible.","So at least two share a status with certainty: $P = 1$."],"hints":["How many different statuses can three people have at most?","Can all three be different?","What is the probability of an event that cannot fail?"],"concept":"Certain events from a pigeonhole argument.","misconception":"Computing a probability when the event is certain.","why":"The complement \"all different\" is impossible.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-65","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability: poker dice","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Five fair six-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability of a <b>full house</b>: three dice show one value and the other two show a different, matching value?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.038580246913580245,"display":"0.0386"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0006,"options":[],"solution":["Choose the value that appears three times (6 ways) and the value that appears twice (5 ways), then choose which 3 of the 5 dice show the first value: $\\binom53 = 10$.","Favourable: $6\\cdot5\\cdot10 = 300$ of $6^5 = 7776$.","$P = 300/7776 \\approx 0.0386$."],"hints":["Choose the two values involved, in order of role.","Then choose the dice that show the triple value.","Divide by $6^5$."],"concept":"Pattern counting for dice.","misconception":"Not distinguishing the triple value from the pair value.","why":"Each full house is generated once by choosing roles and positions.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-66","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability: product parity","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair dice are rolled. What is the probability that the <b>product</b> of the two numbers is even?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.75,"display":"3/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["The product is odd only if both numbers are odd: $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac12 = \\tfrac14$.","$P(\\text{even}) = 1-\\tfrac14 = \\tfrac34$."],"hints":["When is a product odd?","Both dice must be odd.","Take the complement."],"concept":"Complement with a parity argument.","misconception":"Adding probabilities of \"first even\" and \"second even\".","why":"A product is even iff at least one factor is even.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-67","topic":"axioms","sub":"geometric series: first success on an even trial","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fair coin is tossed repeatedly until the first head appears. What is the probability that the first head occurs on an <b>even-numbered</b> toss?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3333333333333333,"display":"1/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The first toss is a head with probability $\\tfrac12$, so odd tosses are more likely than even ones.","v":0.5},{"text":"2/5","correct":false,"why":"This is not the sum of the geometric series $\\tfrac14+\\tfrac1{16}+\\cdots$.","v":0.4},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is only the probability that the first head is on toss 2.","v":0.25}],"solution":["$P(\\text{first head on toss }2k) = (\\tfrac12)^{2k}$.","$\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\left(\\tfrac14\\right)^k = \\dfrac{1/4}{1-1/4} = \\dfrac13$."],"hints":["Write the probability that the first head is on toss $2k$.","Add over $k=1,2,3,\\dots$.","Use $\\sum r^k = r/(1-r)$ for $r<1$."],"concept":"Countable additivity with a geometric series.","misconception":"Assuming odd and even tosses are equally likely.","why":"The first toss is a head with probability one half, so odd positions carry more mass.","fam":"axioms:special"},{"id":"AXM-68","topic":"axioms","sub":"symmetry: subsets of even size","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A random subset of $\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ is chosen so that all $2^5$ subsets are equally likely (the empty set included). What is the probability that it has an <b>even number</b> of elements?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5,"display":"1/2"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["Toggling the membership of element 1 maps each subset of even size to a subset of odd size and back.","So exactly half the $2^5 = 32$ subsets have even size: $P = 16/32 = 1/2$.","Check: $1+10+5 = 16$."],"hints":["Is there a one-to-one map between even and odd subsets?","Toggle one fixed element.","Check by counting: $\\binom50+\\binom52+\\binom54$."],"concept":"Symmetry argument for counting.","misconception":"Computing from scratch without noticing the pairing.","why":"The toggle is a bijection, so the two classes have the same size.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-69","topic":"axioms","sub":"coprime numbers: probability by inclusion-exclusion","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A whole number $N$ is chosen at random from 1 to 60. What is the probability that $N$ has <b>no common factor</b> with 60 other than 1, i.e. is divisible by none of 2, 3 and 5?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.26666666666666666,"display":"4/15"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"4/15","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.26666666666666666},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of not being divisible by 2 only.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the prime 5; numbers divisible by 5 but not by 2 or 3 are also excluded.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"2/15","correct":false,"why":"This is $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac15$, the probability of being divisible by all three.","v":0.13333333333333333}],"solution":["Divisible by at least one of 2, 3, 5: $30+20+12-10-6-4+2 = 44$.","Coprime to 60: $60-44 = 16$, so $P = 16/60 = 4/15$.","(Equivalently $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac23\\cdot\\tfrac45\\cdot60$.)"],"hints":["\"No common factor with 60\" means not divisible by 2, 3 or 5.","Count numbers divisible by at least one with inclusion-exclusion.","Subtract from 60."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion for divisibility.","misconception":"Excluding only multiples of 2.","why":"The count of coprime residues is $60(1-\\tfrac12)(1-\\tfrac13)(1-\\tfrac15)$.","fam":"axioms:inclusion-exclusion"},{"id":"AXM-70","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability: digit sum","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A two-digit number from 10 to 99 is chosen at random. What is the probability that its digits <b>add up to 9</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.1,"display":"0.1"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Numbers with digit sum 9: 18, 27, 36, 45, 54, 63, 72, 81, 90: 9 numbers.","$P = 9/90 = 0.1$."],"hints":["How many two-digit numbers are there?","List those whose digits sum to 9.","Divide the number of such numbers by the number of two-digit numbers."],"concept":"Counting favourable outcomes.","misconception":"Using 100 outcomes.","why":"All 90 numbers are equally likely.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-71","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability: bit strings","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A random 8-bit string is generated with each bit independently 0 or 1 with equal probability. What is the probability that it has <b>exactly four 1s</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2734375,"display":"0.2734"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Number of strings with four 1s: $\\binom84 = 70$.","$P = 70/256 \\approx 0.2734$."],"hints":["How many strings have their four 1s in specified positions?","How many ways to choose those positions?","Divide by $2^8$."],"concept":"Classical probability with combinations.","misconception":"Using $1/2^4$.","why":"All $2^8$ strings are equally likely.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-72","topic":"axioms","sub":"arrangement probability: repeated letters adjacent","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The five letters A, A, B, B, C are arranged in a random order (all distinguishable arrangements equally likely). What is the probability that the two A's are <b>next to each other</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4,"display":"2/5"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"2/5","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.4},{"text":"1/5","correct":false,"why":"Forming the block $AA$ and arranging four units gives $4!/2! = 12$ arrangements, not $4!/5! $ of all orders.","v":0.2},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This is not the ratio of block arrangements to total distinguishable arrangements.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"3/5","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the A’s are <i>not</i> adjacent.","v":0.6}],"solution":["Distinguishable arrangements: $\\dfrac{5!}{2!\\,2!} = 30$.","With AA as a block: the units AA, B, B, C give $\\dfrac{4!}{2!} = 12$ arrangements.","$P = 12/30 = 2/5$."],"hints":["Count all distinguishable arrangements of the five letters.","Glue the two A’s together and count arrangements of the four units.","Divide the block arrangements by the total number of arrangements."],"concept":"Block method for repeated letters.","misconception":"Using $5!$ for the total.","why":"Blocks reduce the problem to a smaller arrangement count.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"AXM-73","topic":"axioms","sub":"classical probability: two independent draws with ties","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent fair eight-sided dice are rolled. What is the probability that the first die shows a <b>strictly larger</b> number than the second?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4375,"display":"7/16"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"7/16","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.4375},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The two dice show equal numbers with probability $8/64$, so \"first larger\" is less than one half.","v":0.5},{"text":"9/16","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the first die is larger <i>or equal</i>.","v":0.5625},{"text":"1/8","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of a tie.","v":0.125}],"solution":["By symmetry $P(\\text{first}>\\text{second}) = P(\\text{second}>\\text{first})$.","Ties: $8/64$. So $2P = 1-8/64 = 56/64$ and $P = 28/64 = 7/16$."],"hints":["Is \"first larger\" as likely as \"second larger\"?","What is the probability of a tie?","Split the remaining probability equally."],"concept":"Symmetry with ties.","misconception":"Answering one half.","why":"The three events (first larger, second larger, tie) partition the sample space.","fam":"axioms:classical"},{"id":"CND-03","topic":"cond","sub":"symmetry of positions","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Six items are inspected in sequence and exactly 2 are found defective. Every arrangement of the defects among the six positions is equally likely. What is the probability that the defective items are the <b>first and last</b> items inspected?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.06666666666666667,"display":"1/15"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/15","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.06666666666666667},{"text":"1/36","correct":false,"why":"This treats the two defects as independent positions with probability $1/6$ each, ignoring that they must occupy different positions.","v":0.027777777777777776},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that a particular position, such as the first, is defective.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/30","correct":false,"why":"This divides by ordered pairs of positions ($6\\cdot5 = 30$); the two defects are indistinguishable, so only the 15 unordered pairs count.","v":0.03333333333333333}],"solution":["Given exactly two defects, the pair of positions is equally likely to be any of the $\\binom{6}{2} = 15$ two-element subsets.","Only one subset, $\\{1,6\\}$, is favourable.","$P = 1/15$."],"hints":["Think of the pair of defect positions as a random 2-element subset.","How many such subsets are there?","How many equal $\\{1,6\\}$?"],"concept":"Symmetry given the count of defects.","misconception":"Multiplying position probabilities as if independent.","why":"Conditioning on the count makes every placement equally likely.","fam":"cond:symmetry"},{"id":"CND-04","topic":"cond","sub":"at least one: reduced sample space","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent audits each flag a firm with probability $\\tfrac12$. You are told that <b>at least one</b> audit flagged the firm. What is the probability that <b>both</b> flagged it?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3333333333333333,"display":"1/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This would be right if you knew which audit had flagged the firm; \"at least one\" leaves three outcomes.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that both flagged.","v":0.25},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that exactly one audit flagged, given at least one.","v":0.6666666666666666}],"solution":["Outcomes (audit 1, audit 2) with at least one flag: FF, FN, NF. Each is equally likely.","Both flagged: FF only. $P = 1/3$."],"hints":["List the outcomes consistent with \"at least one audit flagged\".","Are those outcomes equally likely?","Count those with both flags."],"concept":"Conditioning on a union.","misconception":"Treating \"at least one\" as \"a specified one\".","why":"Learning only that at least one flagged keeps all three outcomes in play.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-06","topic":"cond","sub":"posterior from a count condition","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three machines produce one item each, independently. Machine 1 makes a defective item with probability 0.1, machine 2 with probability 0.2 and machine 3 with probability 0.3. Exactly <b>one</b> of the three items is found defective. What is the probability that it came from <b>machine 1</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.14070351758793972,"display":"0.1407"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1407","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.14070351758793972},{"text":"0.1667","correct":false,"why":"This weights machines by their defect rates alone and ignores that the other two items were good.","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"0.1","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that machine 1’s item is defective.","v":0.1},{"text":"0.504","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that none of the items is defective, not a posterior.","v":0.504}],"solution":["Exactly one defective item has three disjoint patterns:","only machine 1: $0.1\\cdot0.8\\cdot0.7 = 0.056$; only machine 2: $0.9\\cdot0.2\\cdot0.7 = 0.126$; only machine 3: $0.9\\cdot0.8\\cdot0.3 = 0.216$.","Total $0.398$. $P(\\text{machine 1}\\mid\\text{exactly one}) = 0.056/0.398 \\approx 0.141$."],"hints":["List the three ways exactly one item can be defective.","Compute the probability of each pattern using independence.","Divide the pattern with machine 1 by the sum of the patterns."],"concept":"Conditioning on the total count.","misconception":"Ignoring the good items in the other positions.","why":"The conditioning event is the union of the three patterns.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-08","topic":"cond","sub":"specific card known","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three cards are dealt from a standard deck. Given that the <b>ace of hearts is among the three cards</b>, what is the probability that <b>all three are aces</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.002352941176470588,"display":"0.00235"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.00235","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.002352941176470588},{"text":"0.000181","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that three cards are all aces.","v":0.00018099547511312217},{"text":"0.00226","correct":false,"why":"The two unknown cards come from the 51 cards other than the ace of hearts, not from all 52.","v":0.0022624434389140274},{"text":"0.0588","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that one other specified card is an ace; two more aces are needed.","v":0.058823529411764705}],"solution":["Given the ace of hearts is in the hand, the other two cards form a uniformly random pair from the remaining 51 cards: $\\binom{51}{2} = 1275$ pairs.","Both must be among the 3 remaining aces: $\\binom32 = 3$ pairs.","$P = 3/1275 \\approx 0.00235$."],"hints":["Once the ace of hearts is fixed, how many cards remain to be chosen, and from how many?","How many pairs of the other cards would make all three aces?","Divide favourable pairs by all pairs."],"concept":"Conditioning on a known member of a sample.","misconception":"Not reducing the population after the known card.","why":"The remaining two cards are a random sample from the other 51.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-09","topic":"cond","sub":"specific versus at least one","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A hand of two cards is dealt from a full pack and you are told only that it contains <b>one or more hearts</b>. What is the chance that the hand consists of <b>two hearts</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.13333333333333333,"display":"2/15"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"2/15","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.13333333333333333},{"text":"4/17","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that both are hearts given that a <i>specified</i> card is a heart; \"at least one\" is weaker information.","v":0.23529411764705882},{"text":"1/17","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that both cards are hearts.","v":0.058823529411764705},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that one particular card is a heart.","v":0.25}],"solution":["$P(\\text{both hearts}) = \\dfrac{\\binom{13}{2}}{\\binom{52}{2}} = \\dfrac{78}{1326}$.","$P(\\text{at least one heart}) = 1-\\dfrac{\\binom{39}{2}}{\\binom{52}{2}} = 1-\\dfrac{741}{1326} = \\dfrac{585}{1326}$.","$P = 78/585 = 2/15 \\approx 0.133$."],"hints":["Both hearts is a subset of at least one heart.","Compute $P(\\text{at least one heart})$ via the complement.","Divide $P(\\text{both})$ by $P(\\text{at least one})$."],"concept":"Conditioning on a union versus on a specified event.","misconception":"Treating \"at least one\" as \"the first\".","why":"The conditioning event includes outcomes with only one heart, which dilutes the probability.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-10","topic":"cond","sub":"sequential stages","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A candidate goes through three interview rounds and is eliminated at the first round she fails. $P(\\text{pass round 1}) = 0.8$, $P(\\text{pass round 2}\\mid\\text{pass round 1}) = 0.6$ and $P(\\text{pass round 3}\\mid\\text{pass rounds 1 and 2}) = 0.5$. Given that she was <b>not hired</b> (did not pass all three), what is the probability that she was eliminated <b>after passing at least one round</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.736842105263158,"display":"0.7368"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.7368","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.736842105263158},{"text":"0.4211","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of elimination in round 2 only; elimination in round 3 also follows a passed round.","v":0.42105263157894746},{"text":"0.2632","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of elimination in round 1, which is the complement of what is asked.","v":0.2631578947368421},{"text":"0.56","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of the event; it has not been divided by $P(\\text{not hired})$.","v":0.56}],"solution":["Elimination probabilities: round 1: $0.2$; round 2: $0.8\\cdot0.4 = 0.32$; round 3: $0.8\\cdot0.6\\cdot0.5 = 0.24$. Hired: $0.24$.","$P(\\text{not hired}) = 0.76$.","Eliminated after passing at least one round = round 2 or round 3: $0.32+0.24 = 0.56$.","$P = 0.56/0.76 \\approx 0.737$."],"hints":["List the mutually exclusive ways the process can end and their probabilities.","The condition \"not hired\" is the union of three elimination outcomes.","The event of interest is elimination in round 2 or round 3."],"concept":"Conditioning on a union of stage-wise outcomes.","misconception":"Forgetting to condition on the elimination.","why":"The conditional probability restricts attention to the three elimination outcomes.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-11","topic":"cond","sub":"Pólya urn","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"An urn holds 4 red and 6 blue balls. Each time a ball is drawn, it is returned together with <b>3 more balls of the same colour</b>. What is the probability that exactly <b>2 of the first 3 draws</b> are red?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.24230769230769234,"display":"0.2423"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2423","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.24230769230769234},{"text":"0.288","correct":false,"why":"This is the binomial value with fixed probability 0.4; reinforcement changes the composition after every draw.","v":0.28800000000000003},{"text":"0.0808","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of the single order R, R, B; the pattern can occur in 3 orders, all equally likely.","v":0.08076923076923077},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This corresponds to sampling without replacement; here balls are added, not removed.","v":0.30000000000000004}],"solution":["One order, e.g. R, R, B: $\\dfrac{4}{10}\\cdot\\dfrac{7}{13}\\cdot\\dfrac{6}{16} = \\dfrac{168}{2080}$.","The other two orders (R,B,R and B,R,R) have the same probability because the numerators and denominators are the same numbers in a different order.","$P = 3\\times\\dfrac{168}{2080} = \\dfrac{504}{2080} \\approx 0.242$."],"hints":["Compute the probability of one specific order, e.g. R, R, B.","How does the urn change after each draw?","Is the probability of R, B, R different from R, R, B?"],"concept":"Pólya urn: order does not matter for the probability.","misconception":"Using a binomial model with a fixed success probability.","why":"Exchangeability makes all orders with the same number of reds equally likely.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-12","topic":"cond","sub":"ordering of independent dice","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three independent fair 8-sided dice are rolled in turn. What is the probability that the three results are <b>strictly increasing</b> in the order rolled (first < second < third)?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.109375,"display":"0.1094"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1094","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.109375},{"text":"0.1667","correct":false,"why":"By symmetry all 6 orderings of distinct values are equally likely, but equal values are also possible, so $1/6$ overstates the probability.","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"0.6563","correct":false,"why":"This counts all orderings of three distinct values; only the increasing order is favourable.","v":0.65625},{"text":"0.0156","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three results are equal, not that they are strictly increasing.","v":0.015625}],"solution":["Each of the $8^3 = 512$ triples is equally likely.","A strictly increasing triple is determined by choosing 3 distinct values: $\\binom{8}{3} = 56$.","$P = 56/512 = 7/64 \\approx 0.109$."],"hints":["Each ordered triple of results is equally likely.","How many triples have three distinct values in increasing order?","Choose the set of three values; the order is then forced."],"concept":"Counting ordered outcomes with a monotone constraint.","misconception":"Using $1/3!$ without allowing ties.","why":"Each set of three distinct values appears in exactly one increasing order.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-13","topic":"cond","sub":"multi-stage total probability","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A hire pool has 18 laptops, 11 of which have never been rented. Three batches of 3 laptops are rented out at random one after another, and each batch is returned to the pool after use (so each batch is a uniformly random set of 3, independent of the others). What is the probability that the <b>third batch contains no laptop that was ever rented before</b>, either originally or in the first two batches?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.06286895784391468,"display":"0.0629"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0629","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.06286895784391468},{"text":"0.2022","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the first two batches, which also used laptops.","v":0.20220588235294118},{"text":"0.00827","correct":false,"why":"The first two batches need not be entirely among the never-rented laptops; they only must avoid the third batch.","v":0.008267636166166294},{"text":"0.1127","correct":false,"why":"Only one earlier batch has been taken into account; there are two.","v":0.11274960351787773}],"solution":["Fix the third batch. It must consist of 3 never-rented laptops: $\\dfrac{\\binom{11}{3}}{\\binom{18}{3}} = \\dfrac{165}{816}$.","Given that, each earlier batch must avoid those 3 laptops: probability $\\dfrac{\\binom{15}{3}}{\\binom{18}{3}} = \\dfrac{455}{816}$ each, independently.","$P = \\dfrac{165}{816}\\left(\\dfrac{455}{816}\\right)^2 \\approx 0.0629$."],"hints":["It is easier to start from the third batch and ask what the earlier batches must avoid.","The batches are independent random 3-sets.","Multiply: third batch all new, first batch disjoint from it, second batch disjoint from it."],"concept":"Multi-stage reasoning with independent random subsets.","misconception":"Tracking only the first stage.","why":"Conditioning on the identity of the third batch makes the rest independent.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-14","topic":"cond","sub":"symmetry: given the number of up moves","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A trader’s profit-and-loss is positive on any given day with probability $p$ and negative otherwise, independently across days. In a four-day week exactly <b>two days were profitable</b>. What is the probability that the profitable days were the <b>first two days</b> of the week?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.16666666666666666,"display":"1/6"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that days 1 and 2 are both up when $p = 1/2$, without conditioning on the number of up days.","v":0.25},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that day 1 is up given the condition, not that days 1 and 2 are both up.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/8","correct":false,"why":"The two up days are chosen from $\\binom42 = 6$ equally likely pairs, so this is too small.","v":0.125}],"solution":["Given exactly two up days, every set of two days is equally likely, whatever $p$ is: $\\binom42 = 6$ sets.","Only $\\{1,2\\}$ is favourable: $P = 1/6$."],"hints":["Does the answer depend on $p$ once the number of up days is known?","Every arrangement of two ups among four days has probability $p^2(1-p)^2$.","Divide one arrangement by the number of arrangements."],"concept":"Symmetry given the number of successes in Bernoulli trials.","misconception":"Trying to compute with $p$ instead of using symmetry.","why":"All arrangements with the same count have equal probability.","fam":"cond:symmetry"},{"id":"CND-15","topic":"cond","sub":"nested events","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A component lasts beyond 1000 hours with probability 0.90, beyond 3000 hours with probability 0.60 and beyond 5000 hours with probability 0.25. Given that it <b>fails before 5000 hours</b>, what is the probability that it failed <b>before 3000 hours</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5333333333333333,"display":"8/15"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"8/15","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5333333333333333},{"text":"2/5","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of failing before 3000 hours.","v":0.4},{"text":"3/10","correct":false,"why":"The probabilities of nested events are divided, not multiplied.","v":0.30000000000000004},{"text":"7/12","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of failing between 3000 and 5000 hours given survival to 3000 hours, the reverse conditioning.","v":0.5833333333333334}],"solution":["Failing before 3000 h is contained in failing before 5000 h, so the intersection is the smaller event.","$P(F_{3000}) = 1-0.60 = 0.40$, $P(F_{5000}) = 1-0.25 = 0.75$.","$P(F_{3000}\\mid F_{5000}) = \\dfrac{0.40}{0.75} = \\dfrac{8}{15}$."],"hints":["Is one of the two events contained in the other?","Convert survival probabilities into failure probabilities.","Divide the probability of the smaller event by that of the larger."],"concept":"Conditional probability for nested events.","misconception":"Conditioning in the wrong direction.","why":"If $A\\subset B$ then $P(A\\mid B) = P(A)/P(B)$.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-16","topic":"cond","sub":"optimising a conditional design","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Eight red and eight green tokens must be split between two bags (each bag receiving at least one token). A bag is then chosen with probability $\\tfrac13$ for bag 1 and $\\tfrac23$ for bag 2, and a token is drawn at random from it. How should the tokens be split to maximise the probability of drawing a red token, and what is that probability?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8222222222222222,"display":"37/45"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"37/45","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.8222222222222222},{"text":"29/45","correct":false,"why":"This puts the single red token in the bag that is chosen less often; it should go in the more likely bag.","v":0.6444444444444444},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"An equal split gives $\\tfrac12$ in every bag, which can be improved by concentrating red tokens where they count most.","v":0.5},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This puts all red tokens in bag 2 and all green in bag 1; leaving one red alone in bag 2 and the rest with the greens does better.","v":0.6666666666666666}],"solution":["The total probability is $\\tfrac13\\cdot\\dfrac{r_1}{n_1} + \\tfrac23\\cdot\\dfrac{8-r_1}{n_2}$.","Best: put exactly one red token alone in the more likely bag (bag 2), so it gives red with probability 1, and all remaining tokens (7 red, 8 green) in bag 1: $\\dfrac13\\cdot\\dfrac7{15}$.","$P = \\dfrac23+\\dfrac{7}{45} = \\dfrac{37}{45} \\approx 0.822$."],"hints":["Write the probability of red as a weighted average of the bag proportions.","The bag chosen more often should have the highest possible proportion of red.","What is the highest proportion one bag can have, and what is left for the other?"],"concept":"Optimising a total-probability expression.","misconception":"Balancing the bags rather than concentrating red tokens in the more likely bag.","why":"A weighted average is maximised by placing the larger weight on the larger value.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-17","topic":"cond","sub":"information about a later draw","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two cards are dealt from a standard deck. You are told that the <b>second card is a spade</b> (but are not shown either card). What is the probability that <b>both cards are spades</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.23529411764705882,"display":"4/17"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"4/17","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.23529411764705882},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the first card is a spade without the information; the second card being a spade removes one spade from the others.","v":0.25},{"text":"1/17","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that both are spades.","v":0.058823529411764705},{"text":"6/25","correct":false,"why":"After removing the second card there are 51 cards for the first, not 50.","v":0.24}],"solution":["Given that the second card is a spade, the first card is equally likely to be any of the other 51 cards, 12 of which are spades.","$P = 12/51 = 4/17 \\approx 0.235$."],"hints":["By symmetry, the first card is a random card from those not used as the second.","How many spades and how many cards remain?","Divide the spades left by the cards left."],"concept":"Conditioning on a later draw.","misconception":"Thinking that the later draw cannot affect the earlier card.","why":"In an exchangeable sequence information about any draw updates the others equally.","fam":"cond:symmetry"},{"id":"CND-18","topic":"cond","sub":"Markov dependence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A commuter uses route X or route Y each day. She chooses X on the first day with probability 0.4. On every later day she stays with the previous day's route with probability 0.7 and switches with probability 0.3. What is the probability that she uses <b>X, X, Y, Y</b> on days 1 to 4?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.05879999999999999,"display":"0.0588"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0588","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.05879999999999999},{"text":"0.1372","correct":false,"why":"The change from X to Y on day 3 has probability 0.3, not 0.7.","v":0.1372},{"text":"0.0625","correct":false,"why":"This treats each day as an independent fair choice, ignoring both the initial probability and the persistence.","v":0.0625},{"text":"0.0882","correct":false,"why":"The first-day probability of X is 0.4, not 0.6.","v":0.0882}],"solution":["Multiply along the sequence: day 1 X: 0.4; day 2 stays X: 0.7; day 3 switches to Y: 0.3; day 4 stays Y: 0.7.","$0.4\\times0.7\\times0.3\\times0.7 = 0.0588$."],"hints":["Each day depends only on the previous day. Use the chain rule.","Which transitions occur: stay, switch, stay?","Multiply the first-day probability by the three transition probabilities."],"concept":"Chain rule for a two-state Markov sequence.","misconception":"Treating the days as independent.","why":"Each factor is the conditional probability of the next day given the previous one.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-20","topic":"cond","sub":"envelope puzzle","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four envelopes are on a table: one holds two \\$100 notes, one holds two \\$50 notes, and two hold one \\$100 note and one \\$50 note. An envelope is chosen at random and one of its notes, chosen at random, is drawn out: it is a <b>\\$100 note</b>. What is the probability that the <b>other note in the envelope is also \\$100</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5,"display":"1/2"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/2","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This treats the three kinds of envelope as equally likely, but mixed envelopes are twice as common.","v":0.6666666666666666},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of choosing the two-\\$100 envelope.","v":0.25},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"Seeing a \\$100 note rules out the two-\\$50 envelope; of the three \\$100 notes, two belong to the double-\\$100 envelope.","v":0.3333333333333333}],"solution":["There are 8 equally likely (envelope, note) pairs. Pairs showing \\$100: 2 from the double-\\$100 envelope and 1 from each mixed envelope: 4 pairs.","Of those, the other note is \\$100 in the 2 pairs from the double-\\$100 envelope.","$P = 2/4 = 1/2$."],"hints":["Count the equally likely ways of seeing a \\$100 note.","In how many of them is the other note also \\$100?","Use notes (not envelopes) as the equally likely outcomes."],"concept":"Conditioning on an observed item.","misconception":"Treating envelope types as equally likely.","why":"Observing a \\$100 note is evidence for the envelopes with more \\$100 notes.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-22","topic":"cond","sub":"competing events in a game","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A game uses a fair eight-sided die. On the first roll, 8 wins and 1 loses immediately. Any other first roll (2 to 7) becomes the \"point\": the die is then rolled repeatedly until the point appears (win) or a 1 or an 8 appears (loss), whichever comes first. What is the probability of winning?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.375,"display":"3/8"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"3/8","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.375},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This ignores that rolling an 8 during the point phase is also a loss; only 1 was counted.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/8","correct":false,"why":"This counts only an immediate win on the first roll.","v":0.125},{"text":"7/32","correct":false,"why":"This allows only one further roll; the die is rolled repeatedly until the game ends.","v":0.21875}],"solution":["On the point phase each roll ends the game with probability $3/8$ (point, 1 or 8), and it is a win with probability $1/8$. So $P(\\text{win}\\mid\\text{point phase}) = \\dfrac{1/8}{3/8} = \\dfrac13$.","First roll: 8 wins ($1/8$); 2 to 7 enters the point phase ($6/8$).","$P = \\dfrac18+\\dfrac68\\cdot\\dfrac13 = \\dfrac38$."],"hints":["Split on the first roll: immediate win, immediate loss, or point.","During the point phase only three outcomes end the game. Which of them wins?","Probability of winning in the point phase is the winning share of the ending outcomes."],"concept":"Total probability with a repeated-trial stage.","misconception":"Counting only one extra roll.","why":"Ignoring irrelevant rolls, the ending outcome is equally likely to be any of the three.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-24","topic":"cond","sub":"reduced sample space with a product","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fair four-sided die (faces 1 to 4) and a fair six-sided die are rolled and their <b>product</b> is recorded. Given that the product exceeds 6, what is the probability that it is <b>at least 12</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6666666666666666,"display":"2/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"2/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6666666666666666},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This divides the favourable outcomes by all 24 outcomes and does not use the condition.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of the condition itself, the product exceeding 6.","v":0.5},{"text":"4/5","correct":false,"why":"The denominator should count all outcomes with product above 6, which is 12.","v":0.8}],"solution":["Outcomes with product $>6$: $a=2$: $b=4,5,6$ (3); $a=3$: $b=3,4,5,6$ (4); $a=4$: $b=2,\\dots,6$ (5): 12 outcomes.","Of those, product $\\ge12$: $(2,6),(3,4),(3,5),(3,6),(4,3),(4,4),(4,5),(4,6)$: 8 outcomes.","$P = 8/12 = 2/3$."],"hints":["List the outcomes where the product exceeds 6.","Among those, which have product 12 or more?","Divide the second count by the first."],"concept":"Reduced sample space for non-standard dice.","misconception":"Dividing by 24.","why":"All 24 outcomes are equally likely, so conditioning restricts to the 12.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-25","topic":"cond","sub":"reduced sample space with counts","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four independent tosses of a fair coin are made, and the report says only that the tosses were <b>not all tails</b>. What is the probability that heads came up on <b>three or more</b> of the tosses?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3333333333333333,"display":"1/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"5/16","correct":false,"why":"The denominator should exclude the all-tails outcome that the condition rules out: 15, not 16.","v":0.3125},{"text":"4/15","correct":false,"why":"This counts only outcomes with exactly three heads; four heads also qualifies.","v":0.26666666666666666},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This would be the probability for the even split between \"at most two\" and \"more than two\" heads, which does not apply here.","v":0.5}],"solution":["At least one head: $2^4-1 = 15$ outcomes. At least three heads: $\\binom43+\\binom44 = 5$ outcomes (all inside the 15).","$P = 5/15 = 1/3$."],"hints":["How many outcomes have at least one head?","How many of them have three or more heads?","Divide the second count by the first."],"concept":"Conditioning on a binomial event.","misconception":"Using the full sample space as the denominator.","why":"All 15 outcomes remaining after conditioning are equally likely.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-26","topic":"cond","sub":"reduced sample space with dice","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A pair of fair dice is thrown and the total is known to be <b>four or less</b>. What is the probability that <b>one of the dice, at least, displays a single dot</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8333333333333334,"display":"5/6"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"5/6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.8333333333333334},{"text":"11/36","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that at least one die shows 1.","v":0.3055555555555556},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This counts only the outcomes (1,1), (1,2), (1,3); the reversed pairs (2,1), (3,1) also qualify.","v":0.5},{"text":"5/36","correct":false,"why":"This divides by the whole sample space rather than the six outcomes with a sum of at most 4.","v":0.1388888888888889}],"solution":["Outcomes with sum $\\le4$: (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1), (2,2): 6 outcomes.","With at least one die showing 1: all except (2,2): 5 outcomes.","$P = 5/6$."],"hints":["List the outcomes with sum at most 4.","Which of these have a die showing 1?","Divide the number of qualifying outcomes by the six outcomes."],"concept":"Conditioning on a sum.","misconception":"Missing the reversed order outcomes.","why":"Conditioning restricts to six equally likely outcomes.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-27","topic":"cond","sub":"unknown composition","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A supplier ships crates of three components in which the number of faulty components is equally likely to be 0, 1, 2 or 3. One component is tested and put back, then a component is tested again. If the first test finds a <b>faulty</b> component, what is the probability that the second test also finds a <b>faulty</b> one?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7777777777777778,"display":"7/9"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"7/9","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.7777777777777778},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that the second test is faulty; the first result is informative about the crate.","v":0.5},{"text":"5/6","correct":false,"why":"This is the value for crates of 2 components with 0, 1 or 2 faulty; here there are 3 components.","v":0.8333333333333334},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This would be the probability if the crate were known to hold exactly 2 faulty components.","v":0.6666666666666666}],"solution":["$P(F_1) = \\tfrac14\\left(0+\\tfrac13+\\tfrac23+1\\right) = \\tfrac12$.","$P(F_1\\cap F_2) = \\tfrac14\\left(0+\\tfrac19+\\tfrac49+1\\right) = \\tfrac14\\cdot\\tfrac{14}{9} = \\tfrac{7}{18}$.","$P(F_2\\mid F_1) = \\dfrac{7/18}{1/2} = \\dfrac79$."],"hints":["Condition on the number of faulty components $k$; the two tests are independent given $k$.","Compute $P(W_1)$ and $P(W_1\\cap W_2)$ by averaging over $k$.","Divide the second by the first."],"concept":"Draws that are conditionally independent but unconditionally dependent.","misconception":"Assuming the draws are independent unconditionally.","why":"The first draw updates beliefs about the composition, which raises the chance of another white.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-28","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on a union","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Five cards from a shuffled pack are dealt face down. An observer reveals only that the number of <b>hearts is four or five</b>. What is the probability that <b>all five cards are hearts</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.04411764705882353,"display":"0.0441"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0441","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.04411764705882353},{"text":"0.000495","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of five hearts.","v":0.0004951980792316927},{"text":"0.6429","correct":false,"why":"The fifth card in a four-heart hand is any non-heart: 39 choices, not 1.","v":0.6428571428571429},{"text":"0.1875","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the fifth card is a heart given that the first four are hearts, a different condition.","v":0.1875}],"solution":["Exactly four hearts: $\\binom{13}{4}\\cdot39 = 715\\cdot39 = 27\\,885$. Five hearts: $\\binom{13}{5} = 1287$.","$P(\\text{five}\\mid\\ge4) = \\dfrac{1287}{27885+1287} = \\dfrac{1287}{29172} \\approx 0.0441$."],"hints":["Both events are counts of five-card hands, so no common denominator is needed.","Count hands with exactly four hearts: choose 4 hearts and one non-heart.","Divide five-heart hands by hands with at least four hearts."],"concept":"Conditioning on an \"at least\" event through counts.","misconception":"Forgetting the non-heart card choices in the denominator.","why":"All hands are equally likely, so the ratio of counts is the conditional probability.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-29","topic":"cond","sub":"solving for the intersection","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two events satisfy $P(A)=0.5$ and $P(B)=0.3$. The conditional probability of $A$ given $B$ exceeds the conditional probability of $B$ given $A$ by 0.3. Determine the probability that both events occur.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.22499999999999998,"display":"0.225"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.225","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.22499999999999998},{"text":"0.15","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(A)P(B)$, which would be the intersection under independence; independence is not implied here.","v":0.15},{"text":"0.45","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(B\\mid A) = x/0.5$ evaluated at the correct $x$, not the intersection itself.","v":0.45},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the given value of the difference, not the intersection.","v":0.3}],"solution":["Let $x = P(A\\cap B)$. Then $P(A\\mid B) = x/0.3$ and $P(B\\mid A) = x/0.5$.","$\\dfrac{x}{0.3}-\\dfrac{x}{0.5} = 0.3 \\Rightarrow x\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}-2\\right) = 0.3 \\Rightarrow x = 0.225$."],"hints":["Write both conditional probabilities in terms of $P(A\\cap B)$.","Substitute into the given difference.","Solve the linear equation for $x$."],"concept":"Using the definition of conditional probability algebraically.","misconception":"Assuming independence.","why":"Both conditional probabilities share the numerator $P(A\\cap B)$.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-30","topic":"cond","sub":"joint table","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A random firm is classified by sector (rows) and region (columns). The joint probabilities are:<table class=\"jt\"><tr><th></th><th>North</th><th>South</th><th>East</th><th>West</th></tr><tr><th>Manufacturing</th><td>.10</td><td>.06</td><td>.08</td><td>.04</td></tr><tr><th>Services</th><td>.12</td><td>.15</td><td>.14</td><td>.11</td></tr><tr><th>Agriculture</th><td>.06</td><td>.05</td><td>.03</td><td>.06</td></tr></table>Given that the firm is <b>not located in the South</b>, what is the probability that it is in <b>Manufacturing</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2972972972972973,"display":"0.2973"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2973","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.2972972972972973},{"text":"0.28","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of Manufacturing.","v":0.28},{"text":"0.22","correct":false,"why":"This is the joint probability of Manufacturing and not South; it has not been divided by $P(\\text{not South})$.","v":0.22},{"text":"0.2308","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of Manufacturing given the firm <i>is</i> in the South.","v":0.23076923076923075}],"solution":["$P(\\text{Manufacturing}\\cap\\text{not South}) = 0.10+0.08+0.04 = 0.22$.","$P(\\text{not South}) = 1-(0.06+0.15+0.05) = 0.74$.","$P = 0.22/0.74 \\approx 0.297$."],"hints":["Which cells make up the event \"not South\" and the event \"Manufacturing and not South\"?","The probability of \"not South\" is the complement of the South column total.","Divide the joint by the conditioning probability."],"concept":"Conditional probability from a joint table.","misconception":"Reporting the joint probability as the conditional.","why":"The conditioning event restricts the table to the three non-South columns.","fam":"cond:table"},{"id":"CND-31","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on at least one","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A customer holds a store loyalty card ($L$) with probability 0.35 and a co-branded credit card ($B$) with probability 0.45; the probability of holding both is 0.15. Given that the customer holds <b>at least one</b> of the two cards, what is the probability that the customer holds the <b>credit card</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6923076923076923,"display":"0.6923"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6923","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6923076923076923},{"text":"0.45","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of holding the credit card.","v":0.45},{"text":"0.4615","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of holding the credit card <i>only</i>; customers holding both also hold the credit card.","v":0.46153846153846156},{"text":"0.2308","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of holding both cards given at least one.","v":0.23076923076923075}],"solution":["$P(L\\cup B) = 0.35+0.45-0.15 = 0.65$.","Since $B\\subset L\\cup B$, $P(B\\mid L\\cup B) = 0.45/0.65 \\approx 0.692$."],"hints":["What is the probability of holding at least one of the cards?","Is holding the credit card a subset of holding at least one card?","Divide $P(B)$ by $P(L\\cup B)$."],"concept":"Conditioning on a union.","misconception":"Using the marginal probability.","why":"The credit-card event is entirely inside the conditioning event.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-33","topic":"cond","sub":"two-stage transfers","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Bag 1 holds 5 red and 3 green balls; bag 2 holds 4 red and 4 green balls. A ball is moved at random from bag 1 to bag 2, then a ball is moved at random from bag 2 back to bag 1. What is the probability that bag 1 ends up with <b>more red balls than it started with</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.16666666666666666,"display":"1/6"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"3/8","correct":false,"why":"This is only the probability that the first transferred ball is green; the returning ball must then be red.","v":0.375},{"text":"3/16","correct":false,"why":"After the transfer bag 2 holds 9 balls, so the denominator is 9.","v":0.1875},{"text":"5/9","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that bag 1 returns to its original composition (same colour moved there and back).","v":0.5555555555555556}],"solution":["Bag 1 gains a red ball only if a green ball leaves and a red ball returns.","First transfer green: $3/8$. Bag 2 then has 4 red, 5 green (9 balls). Second transfer red: $4/9$.","$P = \\dfrac38\\cdot\\dfrac49 = \\dfrac16$."],"hints":["How can bag 1 end up with one more red ball than it began with?","After the first transfer, how many balls and how many red balls are in bag 2?","Multiply the two stage probabilities."],"concept":"Two-stage experiment with changing composition.","misconception":"Not updating bag 2 after the first transfer.","why":"The second stage depends on the first only through the colour moved.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-35","topic":"cond","sub":"inferring a joint probability","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"8% of firms have a debt problem ($D$) and 5% have a governance problem ($G$). Among firms that have <b>at least one</b> of the two problems, 20% have <b>both</b>. A firm with a governance problem is selected. What is the probability that it also has a debt problem?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4333333333333334,"display":"0.4333"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4333","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.4333333333333334},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of firms with at least one problem that have both, not the share of governance-problem firms.","v":0.2},{"text":"0.2708","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(G\\mid D)$, the probability of a governance problem given a debt problem.","v":0.27083333333333337},{"text":"0.0217","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(D\\cap G)$; it must be divided by $P(G) = 0.05$.","v":0.02166666666666667}],"solution":["Let $x = P(D\\cap G)$. Then $P(D\\cup G) = 0.08+0.05-x = 0.13-x$ and $x = 0.20(0.13-x)$.","$1.2x = 0.026$, so $x \\approx 0.0217$.","$P(D\\mid G) = x/0.05 \\approx 0.433$."],"hints":["Express $P(D\\cup G)$ in terms of the unknown intersection.","Use \"20% of at least one are both\" as an equation.","Then divide the intersection by $P(G)$."],"concept":"Solving for an intersection from a conditional statement.","misconception":"Mistaking the 20% for a conditional on $G$.","why":"The statement \"20% of those with at least one have both\" is a conditional probability given the union.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-37","topic":"cond","sub":"first to draw a winner","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Seven tickets, 3 of them winning, are drawn alternately without replacement by two players, player 1 first, until a winning ticket is drawn. The player who draws the first winning ticket wins the prize. What is the probability that <b>player 1</b> wins?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6285714285714286,"display":"22/35"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"22/35","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6285714285714286},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The players are not symmetric: player 1 draws first and so is more likely to find the first winning ticket.","v":0.5},{"text":"3/7","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that player 1 wins on the very first draw ($3/7$) only.","v":0.42857142857142855},{"text":"3/5","correct":false,"why":"This counts only the winning tickets at draws 1 and 3; draw 5 is also player 1’s.","v":0.6}],"solution":["The three winning tickets occupy a random 3-subset of the 7 draw positions. The first winning ticket is at position $k$ with probability $\\dfrac{\\binom{7-k}{2}}{\\binom73}$:","$k=1$: 15/35, $k=2$: 10/35, $k=3$: 6/35, $k=4$: 3/35, $k=5$: 1/35.","Player 1 draws at odd positions: $(15+6+1)/35 = 22/35 \\approx 0.629$."],"hints":["Player 1 wins if the first winning ticket is at draw 1, 3 or 5.","The first winning ticket is at position $k$ when the other two winners are among the later positions.","Count subsets: $\\binom{7-k}{2}$ out of $\\binom73 = 35$."],"concept":"Distribution of the first success in sampling without replacement.","misconception":"Assuming equal chances for both players.","why":"The first-draw advantage is built into the positions of the first winner.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-38","topic":"cond","sub":"diagnose: sampling with and without replacement","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A manager picks 2 employees at random (without replacement) from 5 women and 5 men for a training programme. A student computes $P(\\text{both are women}) = \\frac{5}{10}\\times\\frac{5}{10} = \\frac14$. What is the error and the correct value?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"After the first woman is picked only 4 women remain among 9 people: $\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot\\frac{4}{9} = \\frac29$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"After the first woman is picked only 4 women remain among 9 people: $\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot\\frac{4}{9} = \\frac29$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The student should have used $\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac15$","correct":false,"why":"Only the numerator was updated; the number of people left is also 9, not 10."},{"text":"There is no error: each pick is independent of the previous one","correct":false,"why":"Picks without replacement are not independent; the second pick depends on the first."},{"text":"The correct value is $\\frac{5}{10}\\cdot\\frac{5}{9}=\\frac{5}{18}$","correct":false,"why":"The number of women left after one woman is picked is 4, not 5."}],"solution":["$P(W_1) = 5/10$. Given $W_1$, the remaining pool is 4 women among 9: $P(W_2\\mid W_1) = 4/9$.","$P(W_1\\cap W_2) = \\frac{5}{10}\\cdot\\frac49 = \\frac{2}{9}\\approx0.222$."],"hints":["Does the composition of the pool change after the first selection?","What is $P(W_2\\mid W_1)$?","Multiply $P(W_1)$ by that conditional probability."],"concept":"Multiplication rule with dependent draws.","misconception":"Treating draws without replacement as independent.","why":"The student’s answer corresponds to selecting with replacement.","fam":"cond:diagnose"},{"id":"CND-39","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on a detail","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A family has two children; each child is independently a boy or a girl with probability 1/2 and is born on any of 7 weekdays with equal probability. You are told that <b>at least one child is a boy born on a Tuesday</b>. What is the probability that <b>both children are boys</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.48148148148148145,"display":"13/27"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"13/27","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.48148148148148145},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This is the answer if you are told only that \"at least one is a boy\"; the extra detail (Tuesday) makes the two-boys case relatively more likely.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This would be the answer if you were told that a <i>specific</i> (say the older) child is a boy.","v":0.5},{"text":"27/196","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of the <i>conditioning</i> event (at least one Tuesday boy), not the probability of two boys given it.","v":0.1377551020408163}],"solution":["Each child has 14 equally likely (sex, day) types, so 196 equally likely ordered families.","At least one Tuesday boy: $196 - 13^2 = 27$ families.","Both boys with at least one Tuesday boy: boys on 7 days each: $49 - 36 = 13$ families.","$P = 13/27 \\approx 0.481$."],"hints":["Enumerate equally likely (sex, weekday) pairs for each child: how many are there?","Count families with at least one Tuesday boy using the complement of \"no Tuesday boy\".","Among those, count families in which both children are boys."],"concept":"Conditioning on a detail changes the likelihood of the cases: reduced sample space counting.","misconception":"Assuming the extra detail is irrelevant (answer 1/3).","why":"The Tuesday detail makes a two-boy family more likely to be described this way (two chances to have a Tuesday boy).","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-40","topic":"cond","sub":"interpretation: Simpson’s paradox","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Two job-training programmes: employment rates after one year.<table class=\"jt\"><tr><th></th><th>Low-skill entrants</th><th>High-skill entrants</th><th>All</th></tr><tr><th>Programme 1</th><td>20 / 100</td><td>9 / 10</td><td>29 / 110</td></tr><tr><th>Programme 2</th><td>1 / 10</td><td>70 / 100</td><td>71 / 110</td></tr></table>Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Programme 1 has the higher rate within each skill group but the lower rate overall, because it mostly enrols low-skill entrants (Simpson’s paradox)"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Programme 1 has the higher rate within each skill group but the lower rate overall, because it mostly enrols low-skill entrants (Simpson’s paradox)","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Programme 2 is better, because its overall rate (64.5%) is higher than Programme 1’s (26.4%)","correct":false,"why":"The overall rates mix groups with very different baseline employment. Comparing within each skill group is the fair comparison."},{"text":"The data are inconsistent: a programme cannot be better in each group but worse overall","correct":false,"why":"This is possible. The overall rate is a weighted average of group rates with different weights (group sizes)."},{"text":"Programme 1 is better only for low-skill entrants","correct":false,"why":"Programme 1 is also better for high-skill entrants (90% versus 70%)."}],"solution":["Within low-skill: $20\\% > 10\\%$. Within high-skill: $90\\% > 70\\%$. So Programme 1 does better in <i>both</i> groups.","Overall: Programme 1 $= 29/110 = 26.4\\%$ and Programme 2 $= 71/110 = 64.5\\%$.","The overall rate is $\\sum P(\\text{group})\\,P(\\text{employed}\\mid\\text{group})$; Programme 1 puts 91% of its weight on the low-skill group, Programme 2 only 9%."],"hints":["Compare the programmes within each skill group first.","How do the two programmes differ in their group composition?","The overall rate is a weighted average, with weights given by composition."],"concept":"Law of total probability: overall rates depend on the mixing weights (Simpson’s paradox).","misconception":"Comparing aggregate rates when group compositions differ.","why":"Skill is a confounder: it affects both enrolment and employment.","fam":"cond:diagnose"},{"id":"CND-41","topic":"cond","sub":"reading conditional probability in words","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A retailer sends a promotional email. Which probability describes \"among the customers who <b>opened</b> the email, the share who <b>bought</b>\"?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(\\text{bought}\\mid\\text{opened})$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(\\text{bought}\\mid\\text{opened})$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(\\text{opened}\\mid\\text{bought})$","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of buyers who opened the email: the conditioning event is \"bought\"."},{"text":"$P(\\text{bought}\\cap\\text{opened})$","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of <i>all</i> customers who both opened and bought, not restricted to openers."},{"text":"$P(\\text{bought})$","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of all customers who bought, ignoring who opened the email."}],"solution":["\"Among those who opened\" restricts attention to openers: the conditioning event is \"opened\".","The share of them who bought is $P(\\text{bought}\\mid\\text{opened})$."],"hints":["Which group is the denominator: all customers or a subgroup?","\"Among those who ...\" names the subgroup.","The event after \"share who ...\" goes before the bar."],"concept":"Identifying the conditioning event from a verbal description.","misconception":"Swapping the two events.","why":"Conditional probability is a proportion within a subgroup.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-42","topic":"cond","sub":"tree diagram: joint from stage probabilities","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"60% of a firm's components come from supplier A. 3% of supplier A's components are defective. What is the probability that a randomly chosen component came from supplier A <b>and</b> is defective?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.018,"display":"0.018"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Multiply along the branch: $P(A)\\,P(\\text{defective}\\mid A) = 0.60\\times0.03 = 0.018$."],"hints":["The event is \"A and defective\": a path through the tree.","The second branch is conditional on the first.","Multiply the probabilities along the path."],"concept":"Multiplication rule from a tree.","misconception":"Adding the two percentages.","why":"The joint probability is the share of supplier A times the defect rate within that share.","fam":"cond:sequential"},{"id":"CND-43","topic":"cond","sub":"chain rule for three events","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A loan applicant must pass three checks in order. The probability of passing the first is 0.8. Given that the first is passed, the probability of passing the second is 0.7; given that both are passed, the probability of passing the third is 0.5. What is the probability that the applicant passes <b>all three</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.27999999999999997,"display":"0.28"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(C_1C_2C_3) = P(C_1)\\,P(C_2\\mid C_1)\\,P(C_3\\mid C_1C_2) = 0.8\\times0.7\\times0.5 = 0.28$."],"hints":["Each probability is conditional on the earlier checks being passed.","Write the joint as a product of a marginal and conditionals.","Multiply the three numbers."],"concept":"Chain rule for sequential events.","misconception":"Treating the checks as independent with the wrong marginal probabilities.","why":"Each factor is the probability of the next step given the history so far.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-44","topic":"cond","sub":"conditional independence versus independence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Given the state of the economy (boom or recession), two firms' hiring decisions $A$ and $B$ are independent. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$A$ and $B$ can still be dependent unconditionally, because both are driven by the state of the economy"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$A$ and $B$ can still be dependent unconditionally, because both are driven by the state of the economy","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$A$ and $B$ must be independent unconditionally","correct":false,"why":"Conditional independence given a common cause does not carry over: the common driver induces dependence."},{"text":"$A$ and $B$ must be mutually exclusive","correct":false,"why":"Both firms can hire in a boom; nothing makes the events disjoint."},{"text":"$A$ and $B$ are dependent given the state of the economy","correct":false,"why":"The premise says they are independent <i>given</i> the state."}],"solution":["Example: in a boom each firm hires with probability 0.8; in a recession 0.2; boom and recession equally likely.","Then $P(A) = P(B) = 0.5$ but $P(A\\cap B) = 0.5(0.64)+0.5(0.04) = 0.34 \\ne 0.25$.","So the events are dependent unconditionally."],"hints":["Construct a small numerical example with two states.","Compute $P(A\\cap B)$ by conditioning on the state.","Compare with $P(A)P(B)$."],"concept":"Conditional independence does not imply independence.","misconception":"Assuming independence given a variable means independence overall.","why":"A shared driver makes outcomes move together when it is not observed.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-45","topic":"cond","sub":"diagnose: the prosecutor’s fallacy","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A forensic test matches an innocent person's DNA profile with probability 1 in 10,000. A suspect from a city of one million people matches, and the prosecutor says: \"The chance the suspect is innocent is 1 in 10,000.\" What is the error?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"It treats $P(\\text{match}\\mid\\text{innocent})$ as $P(\\text{innocent}\\mid\\text{match})$; about 100 innocent people would match, so the suspect alone is far from certainly guilty"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"It treats $P(\\text{match}\\mid\\text{innocent})$ as $P(\\text{innocent}\\mid\\text{match})$; about 100 innocent people would match, so the suspect alone is far from certainly guilty","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Nothing: a 1 in 10,000 match probability means a 1 in 10,000 chance of innocence","correct":false,"why":"The match probability is conditional on innocence; the probability of innocence given a match is a different conditional."},{"text":"The prosecutor should have used 1 in 1,000,000, the size of the city","correct":false,"why":"The relevant calculation combines the match rate with the number of people who could match, not the population size alone."},{"text":"The suspect must be innocent because 100 people match","correct":false,"why":"A match is still evidence; with about 100 matches the suspect is one of about 101 equally plausible matches if there is no other evidence, so the probability of innocence is high but not certain."}],"solution":["Among 1,000,000 people, about $1{,}000{,}000\\times10^{-4} = 100$ innocent people match, plus the guilty person.","Without other evidence, $P(\\text{innocent}\\mid\\text{match}) \\approx 100/101$, not $10^{-4}$.","The prosecutor transposed the conditional."],"hints":["Which is given: P(match | innocent) or P(innocent | match)?","Count how many innocent people in the city would be expected to match.","Compare with the number of guilty matches."],"concept":"Prosecutor’s fallacy as a transposed conditional.","misconception":"Equating $P(E\\mid H)$ with $P(H\\mid E)$.","why":"The base rate of innocence is very high, so even a rare match produces many innocent matches.","fam":"cond:diagnose"},{"id":"CND-46","topic":"cond","sub":"comparing conditional rates","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"In an A/B test, 120 of the 400 visitors shown the promotion bought, and 90 of the 600 visitors not shown the promotion bought. What is $P(\\text{buy}\\mid\\text{promotion}) - P(\\text{buy}\\mid\\text{no promotion})$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.15,"display":"0.15"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(\\text{buy}\\mid\\text{promo}) = 120/400 = 0.30$.","$P(\\text{buy}\\mid\\text{no promo}) = 90/600 = 0.15$.","Difference $= 0.15$."],"hints":["Each probability is a proportion within its own group.","Which counts form the denominators?","Subtract the two proportions."],"concept":"Conditional probabilities from group counts.","misconception":"Dividing both by the grand total.","why":"The groups have different sizes, so rates must be computed within each group.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-47","topic":"cond","sub":"ordered draws without replacement","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"An urn holds 5 red and 4 blue balls. Three balls are drawn one after another without replacement. What is the probability that they appear in the colour order <b>red, blue, red</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.15873015873015872,"display":"10/63"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["$\\dfrac59\\cdot\\dfrac48\\cdot\\dfrac47 = \\dfrac{80}{504} = \\dfrac{10}{63} \\approx 0.159$.","After one red is removed, 4 of the remaining 8 balls are blue; after one blue is also removed, 4 of the remaining 7 are red."],"hints":["The colour order is fixed, so use the chain rule.","Update the composition after each draw.","Multiply the three conditional probabilities."],"concept":"Chain rule without replacement.","misconception":"Not updating the composition.","why":"Each conditional probability reflects the balls already removed.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-48","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on a sum of two dice","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair dice are rolled and the sum is 7. What is the probability that <b>one of the dice shows a 3</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3333333333333333,"display":"1/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/6","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the first die shows 3 given nothing else; both orders (3,4) and (4,3) contain a 3.","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"11/36","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that at least one die shows a 3.","v":0.3055555555555556},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"Of the six outcomes with sum 7, only (3,4) and (4,3) contain a 3.","v":0.5}],"solution":["Outcomes with sum 7: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1): six equally likely.","Those containing a 3: (3,4) and (4,3).","$P = 2/6 = 1/3$."],"hints":["List the outcomes with sum 7.","Which of them contain a 3?","Divide the number containing a 3 by the six outcomes."],"concept":"Reduced sample space.","misconception":"Using all 36 outcomes in the denominator.","why":"The six sum-7 outcomes are equally likely.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-49","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on the maximum","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair dice are rolled and the larger of the two numbers is 4. What is the probability that the <b>smaller</b> number is 2?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2857142857142857,"display":"2/7"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"2/7","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.2857142857142857},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"The seven outcomes with maximum 4 are not four equally likely smaller values: the smaller value 4 (outcome (4,4)) occurs once, the others twice.","v":0.25},{"text":"1/7","correct":false,"why":"The smaller number 2 arises from two ordered outcomes, (2,4) and (4,2).","v":0.14285714285714285},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"There are seven outcomes with maximum 4, not six.","v":0.3333333333333333}],"solution":["Outcomes with maximum 4: $(4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4),(1,4),(2,4),(3,4)$: seven outcomes.","Smaller number 2: $(4,2)$ and $(2,4)$.","$P = 2/7$."],"hints":["List all ordered outcomes whose larger value is 4.","The pair (4,4) occurs once; the others twice.","Count those with smaller value 2."],"concept":"Conditioning on an order statistic.","misconception":"Treating the four possible smaller values as equally likely.","why":"Ordered outcomes, not values, are equally likely.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-50","topic":"cond","sub":"sampling without replacement: exactly one","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A batch of 20 invoices contains 6 with errors. Two invoices are checked at random without replacement. What is the probability that <b>exactly one</b> has an error?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4421052631578947,"display":"0.4421"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0006,"options":[],"solution":["Error then clean: $\\tfrac{6}{20}\\cdot\\tfrac{14}{19}$; clean then error: $\\tfrac{14}{20}\\cdot\\tfrac{6}{19}$.","$P = 2\\cdot\\dfrac{84}{380} = \\dfrac{168}{380} \\approx 0.442$."],"hints":["There are two orders in which exactly one error occurs.","Update the counts after the first invoice.","Add the two orders."],"concept":"Exactly one in two draws without replacement.","misconception":"Using sampling with replacement.","why":"The two orders are disjoint and equally likely.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-51","topic":"cond","sub":"identity for a conditional union","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"For events $A$, $B$ and $C$ with $P(C)>0$, which expression equals $P(A\\cup B\\mid C)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(A\\mid C)+P(B\\mid C)-P(A\\cap B\\mid C)$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(A\\mid C)+P(B\\mid C)-P(A\\cap B\\mid C)$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(A\\mid C)+P(B\\mid C)$","correct":false,"why":"This double counts outcomes in $A\\cap B$ (within $C$), unless $A$ and $B$ are disjoint given $C$."},{"text":"$P(A\\mid C)\\,P(B\\mid C)$","correct":false,"why":"This is a product, valid for the intersection only under conditional independence."},{"text":"$P(A\\cup B)/P(C)$","correct":false,"why":"The numerator should be $P((A\\cup B)\\cap C)$, not $P(A\\cup B)$."}],"solution":["Conditional on $C$, $P(\\cdot\\mid C)$ is itself a probability measure, so the addition rule holds:","$P(A\\cup B\\mid C) = P(A\\mid C)+P(B\\mid C)-P(A\\cap B\\mid C)$."],"hints":["Is $P(\\cdot\\mid C)$ a probability measure?","Which rules does a probability measure satisfy?","Apply the addition rule within $C$."],"concept":"Conditional probability is a probability measure.","misconception":"Applying rules for unconditional events with the wrong denominator.","why":"Conditioning only changes the sample space, not the axioms.","fam":"cond:information"},{"id":"CND-52","topic":"cond","sub":"ballot problem","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"In an election candidate $A$ receives 3 votes and candidate $B$ receives 2. The votes are counted in a uniformly random order. What is the probability that $A$ is <b>strictly ahead</b> of $B$ throughout the count?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2,"display":"1/5"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["There are $\\binom52 = 10$ equally likely orders of 3 A's and 2 B's.","A is strictly ahead throughout only for AAABB and AABAB: 2 orders.","$P = 2/10 = 1/5$ (the ballot theorem gives $(3-2)/(3+2)$)."],"hints":["Count the equally likely orders of the votes.","The first vote must be for $A$; list the orders that keep $A$ strictly ahead.","Check against $(a-b)/(a+b)$."],"concept":"Ballot problem.","misconception":"Assuming the order of votes is irrelevant.","why":"The ballot theorem counts paths staying above the diagonal.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-53","topic":"cond","sub":"geometric waiting time conditioned on parity","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fair coin is tossed until the first head. Given that the first head occurred on an <b>even-numbered</b> toss, what is the probability that it occurred on <b>toss 2</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.75,"display":"3/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"3/4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.75},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of the first head on toss 2.","v":0.25},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The first head is on toss 2 with probability $\\tfrac14$ out of a total of $\\tfrac13$ for even tosses, which is more than half.","v":0.5},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This does not equal the ratio $\\tfrac14\\big/\\tfrac13$.","v":0.6666666666666666}],"solution":["$P(\\text{first head on toss }2) = \\tfrac14$.","$P(\\text{even}) = \\tfrac14+\\tfrac1{16}+\\cdots = \\tfrac13$.","$P = \\dfrac{1/4}{1/3} = \\dfrac34$."],"hints":["Write $P(\\text{toss }2\\cap\\text{even})$ and $P(\\text{even})$.","The event \"toss 2\" is contained in \"even\".","Sum the geometric series for $P(\\text{even})$."],"concept":"Conditioning with a geometric distribution.","misconception":"Forgetting to condition.","why":"Earlier tosses carry more mass than later ones.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-54","topic":"cond","sub":"equal conditional probabilities imply independence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"$P(A\\mid B) = P(A\\mid B^c) = 0.4$ and $P(B)$ is strictly between 0 and 1. Which conclusion is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$A$ and $B$ are independent, and $P(A) = 0.4$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$A$ and $B$ are independent, and $P(A) = 0.4$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive","correct":false,"why":"Exclusive events have $P(A\\mid B) = 0$, not 0.4."},{"text":"$P(A) = 0.8$","correct":false,"why":"By total probability $P(A) = 0.4P(B)+0.4P(B^c) = 0.4$."},{"text":"Nothing can be concluded about $P(A)$","correct":false,"why":"Total probability determines $P(A)$ because both conditional probabilities are given and equal."}],"solution":["$P(A) = P(A\\mid B)P(B)+P(A\\mid B^c)P(B^c) = 0.4\\,[P(B)+P(B^c)] = 0.4$.","Since $P(A\\mid B) = P(A)$, the events are independent."],"hints":["Use the law of total probability for $P(A)$.","What is $P(B)+P(B^c)$?","Compare $P(A\\mid B)$ with $P(A)$."],"concept":"Independence characterised through conditional probabilities.","misconception":"Failing to use total probability.","why":"If $A$ is equally likely whether or not $B$ occurs, $B$ carries no information about $A$.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-55","topic":"cond","sub":"reversing a conditional from three numbers","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$P(A) = 0.5$, $P(B) = 0.25$ and $P(A\\mid B) = 0.6$. What is $P(B\\mid A)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3,"display":"0.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(A\\cap B) = P(A\\mid B)P(B) = 0.6\\times0.25 = 0.15$.","$P(B\\mid A) = P(A\\cap B)/P(A) = 0.15/0.5 = 0.30$."],"hints":["Both conditional probabilities share the same intersection.","Find $P(A\\cap B)$ from the given conditional.","Divide by $P(A)$."],"concept":"Reversing a conditional with the joint probability.","misconception":"Assuming $P(B\\mid A) = P(A\\mid B)$.","why":"The intersection is the common link between the two conditionals.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-56","topic":"cond","sub":"when conditional probability is undefined","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"For which situation is $P(A\\mid B) = P(A\\cap B)/P(B)$ <b>not defined</b> by this formula?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(B) = 0$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(B) = 0$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(A) = 0$","correct":false,"why":"Then $P(A\\cap B) = 0$ and $P(A\\mid B) = 0$, which is well defined when $P(B)>0$."},{"text":"$P(A\\cap B) = 0$","correct":false,"why":"This gives $P(A\\mid B) = 0$, a perfectly valid value."},{"text":"$A\\subset B$","correct":false,"why":"Then $P(A\\mid B) = P(A)/P(B)$, which is defined."}],"solution":["The formula divides by $P(B)$, so it requires $P(B)>0$.","When $P(B) = 0$ the conditional probability is not defined by this ratio."],"hints":["Which quantity is in the denominator?","What happens when it is zero?","Check that the other cases still have $P(B)>0$."],"concept":"Domain of the definition of conditional probability.","misconception":"Mixing up zero numerator with zero denominator.","why":"You cannot condition on an event that has no probability mass.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-57","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on parity of a sum","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair dice are rolled and the sum is even. What is the probability that <b>both</b> dice show odd numbers?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5,"display":"1/2"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["An even sum arises from (odd, odd) or (even, even): $9+9 = 18$ outcomes.","Both odd: 9 outcomes. $P = 9/18 = 1/2$."],"hints":["In which two ways can a sum be even?","How many outcomes does each way contain?","Divide the both-odd outcomes by all outcomes with an even sum."],"concept":"Reduced sample space with parity.","misconception":"Using the unconditional probability of both odd (1/4).","why":"The two ways of getting an even sum are equally likely.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-58","topic":"cond","sub":"interpreting two conditional probabilities","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A retailer finds $P(\\text{received a discount}\\mid\\text{repeat customer}) = 0.7$ and $P(\\text{repeat customer}\\mid\\text{received a discount}) = 0.2$. Which statement is a correct reading?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Most repeat customers received a discount, but only a minority of discount recipients are repeat customers"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Most repeat customers received a discount, but only a minority of discount recipients are repeat customers","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Most discount recipients are repeat customers","correct":false,"why":"That would be $P(\\text{repeat}\\mid\\text{discount})>0.5$; it equals 0.2."},{"text":"The two probabilities must be equal because both describe the same pairs of events","correct":false,"why":"They have different denominators (repeat customers vs discount recipients) and generally differ."},{"text":"A repeat customer has a 20% chance of receiving a discount","correct":false,"why":"That mixes up the direction: 0.7 is the chance for a repeat customer."}],"solution":["0.7 is the share of repeat customers (the conditioning group) who got a discount.","0.2 is the share of discount recipients who are repeat customers.","So most repeat customers got a discount, but repeat customers are only 20% of those receiving discounts."],"hints":["Which group is the denominator of each probability?","Read each as \"among ..., the share who ...\".","Compare 0.7 and 0.2 with one half."],"concept":"Direction of conditioning in a business reading.","misconception":"Swapping the denominators.","why":"Different denominators make the two conditionals independent facts.","fam":"cond:diagnose"},{"id":"CND-59","topic":"cond","sub":"continuous conditioning: uniform","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A number $X$ is uniform on $(0,1)$. Given that $X>0.3$, what is the probability that $X>0.5$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7142857142857143,"display":"5/7"},"fmt":"p","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$P(X>0.5\\mid X>0.3) = \\dfrac{P(X>0.5)}{P(X>0.3)} = \\dfrac{0.5}{0.7} = \\dfrac57 \\approx 0.714$."],"hints":["$\\{X>0.5\\}$ is inside $\\{X>0.3\\}$.","For a uniform variable probability is length.","Divide the lengths."],"concept":"Conditioning a uniform variable on a half-line.","misconception":"Answering 0.5.","why":"The conditional distribution is uniform on the remaining interval.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-60","topic":"cond","sub":"conditional expectation from a reduced space","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A fair six-sided die is rolled and shows an <b>even</b> number. What is the expected value of the roll given this information?","data":"","answer":{"value":4,"display":"4"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Given even, the outcomes 2, 4, 6 are equally likely.","$E = (2+4+6)/3 = 4$."],"hints":["Which outcomes remain possible?","Are they equally likely?","Average the remaining outcomes."],"concept":"Conditional expectation on a reduced sample space.","misconception":"Using the unconditional mean 3.5.","why":"The conditional distribution is uniform on the even faces.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-61","topic":"cond","sub":"memorylessness of a geometric waiting time","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A salesperson makes a sale on each independent call with probability 0.2. She has made 3 calls without a sale. What is the probability that she makes a sale within the <b>next 2 calls</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3599999999999999,"display":"0.36"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["The calls are independent, so past failures do not matter.","$P(\\text{sale within 2}) = 1-0.8^2 = 0.36$.","Check: $1-P(\\text{no sale in 5})/P(\\text{no sale in 3}) = 1-0.8^2$."],"hints":["Do three earlier failures change the next call?","Compute the probability of no sale in two calls.","Take the complement."],"concept":"Memorylessness.","misconception":"The gambler’s fallacy.","why":"Independent trials ignore the past.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-62","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on distinct values","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three numbers are drawn one after another, with replacement, from $\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$. Given that all three are different, what is the probability that they are in <b>increasing</b> order?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.16666666666666666,"display":"1/6"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"5/54","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of an increasing triple; the condition raises it.","v":0.09259259259259259},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"There are $3! = 6$ equally likely orders of three distinct values, not 3.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"Only one of the six orders is increasing.","v":0.5}],"solution":["Given three distinct values, all $3! = 6$ orderings are equally likely by symmetry.","One of them is increasing: $P = 1/6$."],"hints":["Given the set of three distinct values, how many orders are possible?","Are they equally likely?","How many are increasing?"],"concept":"Symmetry given distinct values.","misconception":"Not conditioning.","why":"Conditioning on distinctness leaves every ordering of a set equally likely.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-63","topic":"cond","sub":"symmetry: different colours","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"An urn holds 5 red and 3 blue balls. Two balls are drawn without replacement and have <b>different colours</b>. What is the probability that the <b>first</b> ball drawn was red?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5,"display":"1/2"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/2","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5},{"text":"5/8","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that the first ball is red; the condition removes the bias.","v":0.625},{"text":"3/7","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(\\text{second blue}\\mid\\text{first red})$, a different quantity.","v":0.42857142857142855},{"text":"15/56","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(\\text{red then blue})$, not divided by $P(\\text{different colours})$.","v":0.26785714285714285}],"solution":["$P(RB) = \\tfrac58\\cdot\\tfrac37 = \\tfrac{15}{56}$ and $P(BR) = \\tfrac38\\cdot\\tfrac57 = \\tfrac{15}{56}$.","The two orders are equally likely, so $P(\\text{first red}\\mid\\text{different}) = \\tfrac12$."],"hints":["Compute the probabilities of red-then-blue and blue-then-red.","Are they equal?","Condition on their union."],"concept":"Symmetry in sampling without replacement.","misconception":"Reporting the unconditional probability.","why":"Both orders of a given pair of colours are equally likely.","fam":"cond:symmetry"},{"id":"CND-64","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on at least two of three","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Three independent events each occur with probability $\\tfrac12$. Given that <b>at least two</b> occur, what is the probability that <b>all three</b> occur?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"1/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["$P(\\text{all three}) = 1/8$. $P(\\text{at least two}) = (3+1)/8 = 1/2$.","$P = (1/8)/(1/2) = 1/4$."],"hints":["How many of the 8 equally likely outcomes have at least two events?","How many have all three?","Divide the second count by the first."],"concept":"Conditioning on a count of successes.","misconception":"Using the unconditional probability 1/8 of all three occurring.","why":"Of the 4 outcomes with at least two occurrences, exactly one has three.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-65","topic":"cond","sub":"geometric conditioning on a region","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A point $(X,Y)$ is chosen uniformly in the unit square. Given that $X+Y>1$, what is the probability that $X>\\tfrac12$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.75,"display":"3/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["The region $X+Y>1$ is a triangle of area $\\tfrac12$.","Within it, $X>\\tfrac12$ leaves the part $\\{x>\\tfrac12,\\ y>1-x\\}$ of area $\\int_{1/2}^1 x\\,dx = \\tfrac38$.","$P = \\dfrac{3/8}{1/2} = \\dfrac34$."],"hints":["Draw the unit square and shade $x+y>1$.","Find the area of the part with $x>\\tfrac12$.","Divide by the area of the triangle."],"concept":"Conditioning in a geometric setting.","misconception":"Using the unconditional $\\tfrac12$.","why":"Probability is area ratio under the uniform distribution.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"CND-66","topic":"cond","sub":"conditioning on arrival order","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent arrival times $A$ and $B$ are uniform on $(0,1)$. Given that $B>0.4$, what is the probability that $A<B$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7,"display":"0.7"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(A<B,\\ B>0.4) = \\int_{0.4}^{1} b\\,db = \\dfrac{1-0.16}{2} = 0.42$.","$P(B>0.4) = 0.6$.","$P = 0.42/0.6 = 0.7$."],"hints":["Condition on the value of $B$: $P(A<b) = b$.","Integrate over $b>0.4$.","Divide by $P(B>0.4)$."],"concept":"Conditional probability with two continuous variables.","misconception":"Answering one half.","why":"Larger values of $B$ make $A<B$ more likely.","fam":"cond:reduced-space"},{"id":"IND-01","topic":"indep","sub":"independence and complements","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Suppose $A$ and $B$ are independent events and neither is certain nor impossible. Select the one conclusion that <b>must hold</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$A^c$ and $B$ are independent"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$A^c$ and $B$ are independent","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive","correct":false,"why":"Independent events with positive probabilities have $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B) > 0$, so they can occur together."},{"text":"$P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B)$","correct":false,"why":"This holds only for mutually exclusive events; here $P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)-P(A)P(B)$."},{"text":"$P(A\\mid B) < P(A)$","correct":false,"why":"For independent events $P(A\\mid B) = P(A)$ exactly, not smaller."}],"solution":["$P(A^c\\cap B) = P(B)-P(A\\cap B) = P(B)-P(A)P(B) = (1-P(A))P(B) = P(A^c)P(B)$.","So $A^c$ and $B$ are independent. The other statements are false for independent events with positive probability."],"hints":["Test each statement against the definition $P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)$.","Could independent events with positive probabilities be mutually exclusive?","Write $P(A^c\\cap B)$ as $P(B) - P(A\\cap B)$."],"concept":"Independence is preserved under complements.","misconception":"Confusing independence with exclusivity.","why":"Learning that $A$ did not occur says no more about $B$ than learning that it did.","fam":"indep:definition"},{"id":"IND-02","topic":"indep","sub":"ranking probabilities of repeated trials","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A compliance officer compares three scenarios and wants them ordered by probability, <b>largest first</b>. (a) Six independent audits, each passed with probability 0.9, are all passed. (b) A fair coin shows at least one head in two tosses. (c) At least two of three independent checks, each succeeding with probability 0.4, succeed.","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"(b), then (a), then (c): 0.75 > 0.531 > 0.352"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"(b), then (a), then (c): 0.75 > 0.531 > 0.352","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"(a), then (b), then (c)","correct":false,"why":"Six trials at 0.9 succeed together with probability $0.9^6 \\approx 0.531$, which is less than 0.75."},{"text":"(b), then (c), then (a)","correct":false,"why":"$P(c) = 3(0.4)^2(0.6)+0.4^3 = 0.352$, which is smaller than $0.9^6 \\approx 0.531$."},{"text":"(c), then (a), then (b)","correct":false,"why":"The ranking is reversed: (b) has probability 0.75, the largest."}],"solution":["(a) $0.9^6 = 0.531$. (b) $1-0.5^2 = 0.75$. (c) $3(0.4)^2(0.6) + (0.4)^3 = 0.288 + 0.064 = 0.352$.","Order: (b) $0.75 >$ (a) $0.531 >$ (c) $0.352$."],"hints":["Compute each probability with an appropriate formula.","(a) is a product of independent probabilities; (b) uses the complement; (c) is a binomial tail.","Compare the three numbers."],"concept":"Probabilities of repeated independent trials.","misconception":"Judging by intuition about how many trials are involved.","why":"The three events use three different structural tools: product, complement and binomial sum.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-03","topic":"indep","sub":"gambler’s fallacy","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A trader's trades are independent and each is profitable with probability 0.5. After seven profitable trades in a row she says: \"I am due a loss, so the next trade is much more likely to lose.\" Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Given independence, the next trade is profitable with probability 0.5, whatever the previous results"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Given independence, the next trade is profitable with probability 0.5, whatever the previous results","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The probability of a loss is higher because eight profitable trades in a row are rare","correct":false,"why":"The chance of eight in a row is small <i>before</i> any trade is made; given seven, the eighth is still 50:50."},{"text":"The probability of a profit is higher because she is on a winning streak","correct":false,"why":"This is the \"hot hand\" version of the same error; independence means the streak carries no information."},{"text":"The probability depends on the number of trades remaining in the year","correct":false,"why":"Independent trades are unaffected by how many remain."}],"solution":["By independence $P(\\text{8th profitable}\\mid\\text{first 7 profitable}) = \\dfrac{0.5^8}{0.5^7} = 0.5$.","The streak is unlikely in advance, but past results do not change the probability of the next independent trade."],"hints":["What does independence say about the effect of past trades on the next one?","Compute $P(\\text{8 profits})/P(\\text{7 profits})$.","Distinguish the probability of the whole streak from the probability of one more success."],"concept":"Independence and the gambler’s fallacy.","misconception":"Believing past results make a reversal more likely (\"due\").","why":"The unconditional probability of the whole run is small, but the next trial is a fresh independent event.","fam":"indep:diagnose"},{"id":"IND-04","topic":"indep","sub":"independence in a table","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Firms are classified by export status (exporter $E$ / non-exporter $N$) and size (large $L$ / small $S$). It is known that $P(E\\cap L) = 0.15$ and $P(E\\cap S) = 0.25$. What value must $P(N\\cap S)$ take for export status and size to be <b>independent</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.375,"display":"0.375"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.375","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.375},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(E\\cap S)$, not the missing cell; the cells must be chosen to make the joint probabilities factor.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.24","correct":false,"why":"This multiplies $P(N)$ by $P(E)$, which are complementary events and cannot be independent.","v":0.24},{"text":"0.225","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(N\\cap L) = 0.225$, the neighbouring cell, not $P(N\\cap S)$.","v":0.22499999999999995}],"solution":["$P(E) = 0.15+0.25 = 0.40$. Independence needs $P(E\\cap L) = P(E)P(L)$, so $P(L) = 0.15/0.40 = 0.375$.","Then $P(N\\cap L) = 0.375-0.15 = 0.225$ and $P(N\\cap S) = 1-0.15-0.25-0.225 = 0.375$.","(Check: $P(N)P(S) = 0.6\\times0.625 = 0.375$.)"],"hints":["Find $P(E)$ from the two given cells.","Use $P(E\\cap L)=P(E)P(L)$ to determine $P(L)$.","The four cells sum to 1."],"concept":"Using independence to complete a joint table.","misconception":"Filling the missing cell without using the product rule.","why":"Independence ties each cell to the row and column totals.","fam":"indep:definition"},{"id":"IND-05","topic":"indep","sub":"conditioning independent events","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two machines are independently in working order on a given day with probabilities 0.9 and 0.7. Given that <b>at least one machine is down</b>, what is the probability that machine 1 is down?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2702702702702703,"display":"0.2703"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2703","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.2702702702702703},{"text":"0.1","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that machine 1 is down.","v":0.1},{"text":"0.3333","correct":false,"why":"This is the odds-like ratio of the two down probabilities; it does not use the union.","v":0.33333333333333337},{"text":"0.03","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that both machines are down.","v":0.03}],"solution":["$P(\\text{at least one down}) = 1-P(\\text{both up}) = 1-0.9\\cdot0.7 = 0.37$.","Machine 1 down implies at least one down, so $P(M_1\\text{ down}\\mid\\text{at least one down}) = \\dfrac{0.1}{0.37} \\approx 0.270$."],"hints":["What is the probability that at least one machine is down?","Is \"machine 1 down\" contained in \"at least one down\"?","Divide the probability of machine 1 being down by that of the union."],"concept":"Conditioning an independent pair on a union.","misconception":"Assuming independence means the condition has no effect.","why":"The condition about the union is informative about each part even though the machines are independent.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-06","topic":"indep","sub":"redundancy","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A data-centre power system has three independent backup generators. During an outage each fails to start with probability 0.05. What is the probability that <b>all three fail to start</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.00012500000000000003,"display":"0.000125"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.000125","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.00012500000000000003},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"This is the failure probability of a single generator; redundancy lowers the risk.","v":0.05},{"text":"0.15","correct":false,"why":"This adds the probabilities, which does not describe all failing together and can exceed 1 with more units.","v":0.15000000000000002},{"text":"0.8574","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three start successfully.","v":0.8573749999999999}],"solution":["The generators fail independently, so $P(\\text{all fail}) = 0.05^3 = 0.000125$."],"hints":["All three must fail; are the failures independent?","Multiply the three failure probabilities.","Compare with a single generator’s 0.05."],"concept":"Multiplication rule for independent events.","misconception":"Adding failure probabilities.","why":"Independent failures multiply, so each extra backup cuts the risk sharply.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-07","topic":"indep","sub":"conditioning on one trial","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Six employees independently own an electric vehicle with probability 0.3 each. It is known that <b>a particular employee, Kim, owns one</b>. What is the probability that <b>at least three</b> of the six employees own an electric vehicle?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.47178000000000014,"display":"0.4718"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4718","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.47178000000000014},{"text":"0.2557","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of at least three owners among six; Kim’s ownership is known and counts as one.","v":0.25569000000000025},{"text":"0.1631","correct":false,"why":"This requires at least three among the <i>other five</i>, but Kim already provides one: two more are needed.","v":0.16308000000000022},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is a single employee’s ownership probability, not the probability for the group.","v":0.3}],"solution":["Kim contributes one owner. By independence the other five are still independent with $p=0.3$. We need at least two more owners among them.","$P = 1 - 0.7^5 - 5(0.3)(0.7)^4 = 1 - 0.16807 - 0.36015 = 0.4718$."],"hints":["How many more owners are needed once Kim is known to be one?","The other five employees are unaffected by Kim.","Use the complement of \"none or exactly one among the five\"."],"concept":"Conditioning on one trial of an independent set.","misconception":"Recomputing the six-person probability or ignoring Kim’s contribution.","why":"Independence makes the remaining five trials unchanged.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-08","topic":"indep","sub":"conditioning on at least one","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Eight independent sensors each trigger an alarm with probability 0.2 during a test. Given that <b>at least one</b> sensor triggered, what is the probability that <b>at least three</b> triggered?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.24402240617184798,"display":"0.244"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.244","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.24402240617184798},{"text":"0.2031","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of at least three triggers; it has not been divided by $P(\\ge1)$.","v":0.2030822399999997},{"text":"0.4032","correct":false,"why":"This is the conditional probability of <i>exactly one</i> trigger.","v":0.40318805004168107},{"text":"0.148","correct":false,"why":"This conditions on one particular sensor having triggered, which is not the information given.","v":0.14803199999999966}],"solution":["$P(\\ge1) = 1-0.8^8 = 0.8322$.","$P(\\ge3) = 1-\\left[0.8^8 + 8(0.2)(0.8)^7 + 28(0.2)^2(0.8)^6\\right] = 1-[0.1678+0.3355+0.2936] = 0.2031$.","Since \"$\\ge3$\" is inside \"$\\ge1$\", $P(\\ge3\\mid\\ge1) = 0.2031/0.8322 \\approx 0.244$."],"hints":["Both events are tail events of the same binomial count.","The event \"at least three\" is contained in \"at least one\".","Divide the two tail probabilities."],"concept":"Conditioning on a tail event of a binomial count.","misconception":"Treating the condition as fixing one specific trial.","why":"Conditioning removes only the all-quiet outcome from the sample space.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-09","topic":"indep","sub":"at least one in n trials","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Each email attachment independently contains malware with probability 0.02. How many attachments must be scanned for the probability that <b>at least one</b> contains malware to be at least 0.9?","data":"","answer":{"value":114,"display":"114"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"114","correct":true,"why":"","v":114},{"text":"45","correct":false,"why":"This divides the target probability by the per-item probability, which ignores the complement structure; probabilities do not add.","v":45},{"text":"113","correct":false,"why":"Rounding down gives a probability slightly below 0.9; round up.","v":113},{"text":"100","correct":false,"why":"At $n=100$ the probability is $1-0.98^{100} \\approx 0.867$, still short of 0.9.","v":100}],"solution":["$P(\\text{at least one}) = 1-0.98^n \\ge 0.9 \\iff 0.98^n \\le 0.1 \\iff n \\ge \\dfrac{\\ln 0.1}{\\ln 0.98} \\approx 113.97$.","The smallest integer is $n = 114$."],"hints":["Express the probability of at least one as $1-(1-p)^n$.","Rearrange the inequality and take logarithms.","Dividing by $\\ln0.98<0$ reverses the inequality; then round up."],"concept":"Solving for $n$ in \"at least one\" probabilities.","misconception":"Adding probabilities or rounding the wrong way.","why":"Each attachment multiplies the survival probability of the \"no malware\" event by 0.98.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-11","topic":"indep","sub":"inclusion-exclusion with replacement","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A box holds 40 red, 35 blue and 25 green counters. Eight counters are drawn <b>with replacement</b>. What is the probability that <b>all three colours</b> appear?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8521222500000001,"display":"0.8521"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8521","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.8521222500000001},{"text":"0.8512","correct":false,"why":"Outcomes missing two colours (all draws one colour) are subtracted twice in this sum; add them back.","v":0.851226443671875},{"text":"0.8566","correct":false,"why":"This multiplies the three events \"colour appears\" as if they were independent; they are negatively dependent because the draws are shared.","v":0.856579622720203},{"text":"0.8829","correct":false,"why":"This assumes the three colours are equally likely, but the proportions are 0.40, 0.35 and 0.25.","v":0.88294467306813}],"solution":["Let $M_c$ be \"colour $c$ missing\". $P(M_{\\text{red}}) = 0.6^8$, $P(M_{\\text{blue}}) = 0.65^8$, $P(M_{\\text{green}}) = 0.75^8$.","Two colours missing means all eight draws are the third colour: $P(M_aM_b) = p_c^{\\,8}$; all three missing is impossible.","$P(\\text{some missing}) = \\sum P(M_c) - \\sum P(M_aM_b) = 0.1488-0.0009$.","$P(\\text{all appear}) = 1-0.1488+0.0009 \\approx 0.8521$."],"hints":["Use the complement: some colour is missing.","Apply inclusion-exclusion to the three events \"colour $c$ missing\".","If two colours are missing, all draws have the remaining colour."],"concept":"Inclusion-exclusion with independent draws.","misconception":"Subtracting overlapping missing-colour events twice.","why":"The alternating sum counts each outcome with missing colours exactly once.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-13","topic":"indep","sub":"at least one but not all","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"On a given day the three production lines of a plant are each up independently, with probabilities 0.9, 0.8 and 0.6. Find the probability that <b>some line is up but the plant is not fully operational</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5599999999999999,"display":"0.56"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.56","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5599999999999999},{"text":"0.992","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least one runs; the case of all three running must be excluded.","v":0.992},{"text":"0.432","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three run.","v":0.43200000000000005},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts a constant that has no meaning here; probabilities of independent events are combined through products.","v":0.30000000000000027}],"solution":["$P(\\text{none running}) = 0.1\\cdot0.2\\cdot0.4 = 0.008$.","$P(\\text{all running}) = 0.9\\cdot0.8\\cdot0.6 = 0.432$.","The event is the complement of these two disjoint cases: $1-0.008-0.432 = 0.560$."],"hints":["Which two extreme outcomes must be excluded?","Compute each by multiplying independent probabilities.","Subtract their sum from 1."],"concept":"Complement of a union of extreme cases.","misconception":"Excluding only the \"none\" case.","why":"The outcomes none and all are disjoint, so their probabilities add.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-15","topic":"indep","sub":"solving from conditional probabilities","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The conditional probabilities of two events satisfy $P(A\\mid B) = 0.5$ and $P(B\\mid A) = 0.25$, and the probability that at least one of them occurs is 0.7. Determine the unconditional probability of $A$.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.56,"display":"0.56"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.56","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.56},{"text":"0.28","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(B) = 2x$ where $x = P(A\\cap B) = 0.14$, not $P(A)$.","v":0.28},{"text":"0.14","correct":false,"why":"This is the intersection $P(A\\cap B)$.","v":0.14},{"text":"0.35","correct":false,"why":"This scales $P(A\\cup B)$ by a ratio of conditional probabilities, which has no basis; $P(A)$ must be found from the intersection.","v":0.35}],"solution":["Let $x = P(A\\cap B)$. Then $P(B) = x/P(A\\mid B) = 2x$ and $P(A) = x/P(B\\mid A) = 4x$.","$P(A\\cup B) = 4x + 2x - x = 5x = 0.7$, so $x = 0.14$.","$P(A) = 4x = 0.56$."],"hints":["Express $P(A)$ and $P(B)$ in terms of the intersection $x$.","Insert them into the addition rule for the union.","Solve for $x$ then compute $P(A)$."],"concept":"Combining conditional probabilities with the addition rule.","misconception":"Mixing up which marginal each conditional gives.","why":"Each conditional probability expresses a marginal as a multiple of the intersection.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-16","topic":"indep","sub":"independent events and unions","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Events $A$, $B$ and $C$ are mutually independent with $P(A) = 0.5$, $P(A\\cup B) = 0.7$ and $P(A\\cup C) = 0.8$. What is $P(A\\cup B\\cup C)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.88,"display":"0.88"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.88","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.88},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This treats the union as $P(A\\cup B)+P(A\\cup C)-P(A)$, which double counts $B$ and $C$ overlaps.","v":1},{"text":"0.97","correct":false,"why":"The numbers 0.3 and 0.2 are $P((A\\cup B)^c)$ and $P((A\\cup C)^c)$, not $P(B^c)$ and $P(C^c)$.","v":0.97},{"text":"1.5","correct":false,"why":"This sums the three probabilities without the overlap corrections.","v":1.5}],"solution":["$1-P(A\\cup B) = P(A^c)P(B^c) = 0.5\\,P(B^c) = 0.3$, so $P(B^c) = 0.6$ and $P(B) = 0.4$.","$0.5\\,P(C^c) = 0.2$, so $P(C^c) = 0.4$, $P(C) = 0.6$.","$P(A\\cup B\\cup C) = 1 - 0.5\\cdot0.6\\cdot0.4 = 0.88$."],"hints":["Use complements: $P((A\\cup B)^c) = P(A^c)P(B^c)$.","Solve for $P(B)$ and $P(C)$.","The union of independent events is the complement of the product of the complements."],"concept":"Union of independent events via complements.","misconception":"Treating $P(A\\cup B)$ as $P(B)$.","why":"Independence makes $P(\\text{none occurs})$ a simple product.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-17","topic":"indep","sub":"sum of independent non-uniform variables","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent spinners show 1, 2 or 3. Spinner 1: $P(1)=0.5$, $P(2)=0.3$, $P(3)=0.2$. Spinner 2: $P(1)=0.2$, $P(2)=0.5$, $P(3)=0.3$. What is the probability that the <b>sum is 4</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.33999999999999997,"display":"0.34"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.34","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.33999999999999997},{"text":"0.3333","correct":false,"why":"This treats the nine outcomes as equally likely.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"The pair (3, 1) also gives a sum of 4.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.37","correct":false,"why":"The probabilities have been assigned to the wrong spinner: spinner 1 has $P(1)=0.5$, spinner 2 has $P(3)=0.3$.","v":0.37}],"solution":["Sum 4 occurs for $(1,3)$, $(2,2)$, $(3,1)$:","$0.5\\cdot0.3 + 0.3\\cdot0.5 + 0.2\\cdot0.2 = 0.15+0.15+0.04 = 0.34$."],"hints":["List the pairs of results with sum 4.","The spinners are independent, so multiply probabilities within a pair.","The pairs are disjoint: add."],"concept":"Distribution of a sum of independent discrete variables.","misconception":"Assuming equal probabilities for all pairs.","why":"Each pair of results contributes the product of its marginal probabilities.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-18","topic":"indep","sub":"none of three independent events","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three unrelated promotions each lead to a sale, independently, with probabilities 0.6, 0.5 and 0.2. Find the chance that the customer buys <b>at least once</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.84,"display":"0.84"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.84","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.84},{"text":"1.3","correct":false,"why":"This sum exceeds 1; events that can co-occur are not added.","v":1.3},{"text":"0.16","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that none occurs.","v":0.16000000000000003},{"text":"0.06","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three occur.","v":0.06}],"solution":["$P(\\text{none}) = (1-0.6)(1-0.5)(1-0.2) = 0.16$.","$P(\\text{at least one}) = 1-0.16 = 0.84$."],"hints":["Use the complement of \"none occurs\".","Complements of independent events are independent.","Multiply the three non-occurrence probabilities."],"concept":"Complement for the union of independent events.","misconception":"Adding probabilities.","why":"The union has one failing case: none of the events occurs.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-21","topic":"indep","sub":"repeated trials: solving for p","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A bidder wins each tender independently with the same probability. The probability that she wins <b>at least one of two</b> tenders is 0.51. What is the probability that she wins at least one of <b>six</b> tenders?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.882351,"display":"0.8824"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8824","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.882351},{"text":"1.53","correct":false,"why":"Probabilities of \"at least one\" do not scale linearly with the number of tenders; the result exceeds 1.","v":1.53},{"text":"0.8673","correct":false,"why":"This uses 0.51, the success probability of a pair, as if it were the failure probability of a pair.","v":0.8673489999999999},{"text":"0.2601","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that she wins at least one in each of two pairs, not at least one of six.","v":0.2601}],"solution":["Let $p$ be the win probability. $1-(1-p)^2 = 0.51$ gives $(1-p)^2 = 0.49$, so $1-p = 0.7$ and $p = 0.3$.","$P(\\text{at least one of six}) = 1-0.7^6 = 1-0.1176 = 0.8824$."],"hints":["Express \"at least one of two\" using $p$.","Solve for the failure probability $1-p$.","Use the complement for six tenders."],"concept":"Backing out a trial probability.","misconception":"Scaling the probability linearly.","why":"Only the failure probability multiplies across independent trials.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-22","topic":"indep","sub":"first to succeed among several players","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four players $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ take turns, in that order and repeatedly, rolling a fair die. The first player to roll a six wins. What is the probability that <b>player $C$</b> wins?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.22354694485842033,"display":"0.2235"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2235","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.22354694485842033},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"The first players have an advantage because they roll before the others; the four players are not equally likely to win.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.1157","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that $C$ wins in the first round only; later rounds also contribute.","v":0.11574074074074076},{"text":"0.1667","correct":false,"why":"This is $C$’s chance of rolling a six on any one turn, not of winning the contest.","v":0.16666666666666666}],"solution":["$C$ wins in round $r$ if the four players all fail in the previous $r-1$ rounds, $A$ and $B$ fail, and $C$ rolls a six: $(5/6)^{4(r-1)}(5/6)^2(1/6)$.","Sum: $P(C) = \\dfrac{(5/6)^2(1/6)}{1-(5/6)^4} = \\dfrac{25/216}{671/1296} = \\dfrac{150}{671} \\approx 0.2235$."],"hints":["Write the probability that $C$ wins in the first round.","What is the probability that nobody wins in one full round of four rolls?","Sum the geometric series with that ratio."],"concept":"Geometric series for turn-taking contests.","misconception":"Assuming equal chances for all players.","why":"Each unsuccessful full round returns the game to its starting state.","fam":"indep:racing"},{"id":"IND-24","topic":"indep","sub":"smallest n for a target probability","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"In each trial a sensor fires with probability 0.2 and, independently, an alarm relay engages with probability 0.5; a trial counts as a success only if <b>both</b> happen. Trials are independent. What is the smallest number of trials for which the probability of <b>at least one success</b> exceeds 0.95?","data":"","answer":{"value":29,"display":"29"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"29","correct":true,"why":"","v":29},{"text":"28","correct":false,"why":"At $n=28$ the probability is $1-0.9^{28} \\approx 0.948$, just below 0.95.","v":28},{"text":"10","correct":false,"why":"This is $1/p$, the expected number of trials until the first success, not the number needed for 95% confidence.","v":10},{"text":"15","correct":false,"why":"At $n=15$ the probability is $1-0.9^{15} \\approx 0.794$.","v":15}],"solution":["Per-trial success: $0.2\\times0.5 = 0.1$.","$1-0.9^n > 0.95 \\iff 0.9^n < 0.05 \\iff n > \\dfrac{\\ln0.05}{\\ln0.9} \\approx 28.43$.","Smallest integer: $n=29$."],"hints":["First find the success probability of one trial.","Set up $1-(1-p)^n$ and compare with 0.95.","Take logarithms and round up."],"concept":"Number of independent trials needed.","misconception":"Using the expected waiting time as the answer.","why":"The failure probability shrinks by a factor 0.9 per trial.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-25","topic":"indep","sub":"race between two outcomes","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two dice are thrown again and again. Find the chance that a throw totalling <b>5</b> occurs earlier than any throw totalling <b>10</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5714285714285714,"display":"4/7"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"4/7","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5714285714285714},{"text":"1/9","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of a 5 on a single roll; the game continues until either total appears.","v":0.1111111111111111},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The totals 5 and 10 are not equally likely: 5 can be made in four ways, 10 in three.","v":0.5},{"text":"3/7","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that 10 appears before 5.","v":0.42857142857142855}],"solution":["Only rolls with total 5 (4 of 36) or total 10 (3 of 36) end the contest; other rolls are ignored.","$P(5\\text{ first}) = \\dfrac{4/36}{4/36+3/36} = \\dfrac47$."],"hints":["Which rolls end the contest, and which can be ignored?","Among the decisive rolls, compare the number of ways of making each total.","Divide the favourable ways by the total number of decisive ways."],"concept":"Competing outcomes in repeated trials.","misconception":"Counting rolls that do not decide the contest.","why":"Conditional on a decisive roll, the two totals are in proportion to their probabilities.","fam":"indep:racing"},{"id":"IND-28","topic":"indep","sub":"matching distribution","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Customers independently choose one of three subscription plans: Basic with probability 0.5, Plus with 0.3 and Premium with 0.2. What is the probability that <b>three</b> randomly chosen customers all pick the <b>same plan</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.16,"display":"0.16"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.16","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.16},{"text":"0.38","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that <i>two</i> customers choose the same plan.","v":0.38},{"text":"0.18","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three choose <i>different</i> plans.","v":0.18},{"text":"0.1111","correct":false,"why":"This assumes the three plans are equally likely.","v":0.11111111111111108}],"solution":["The event is a union of three disjoint events: all Basic, all Plus, all Premium.","$P = 0.5^3+0.3^3+0.2^3 = 0.125+0.027+0.008 = 0.160$."],"hints":["\"All the same\" splits into one event per plan.","For a given plan, the three choices are independent.","Add the three products."],"concept":"Probability of agreement among independent draws.","misconception":"Assuming equal plan probabilities.","why":"The three agreement events are disjoint.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-29","topic":"indep","sub":"solving for independent probabilities","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two components $A$ and $B$ fail independently on any given day. The probability that <b>neither</b> fails is 0.56, and the probability that <b>$A$ fails but $B$ does not</b> is 0.14. What is the probability that <b>both</b> fail?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.06,"display":"0.06"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.06","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.06},{"text":"0.14","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that only $A$ fails.","v":0.14},{"text":"0.44","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least one component fails.","v":0.44},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(A)+P(B)$, not the probability that both fail.","v":0.5}],"solution":["$P(A\\text{ fails},B\\text{ works}) + P(\\text{neither fails}) = P(B\\text{ works})$, so $P(B\\text{ works}) = 0.14+0.56 = 0.70$ and $P(B) = 0.30$.","$P(A\\text{ works}) = 0.56/0.70 = 0.80$, so $P(A) = 0.20$.","$P(\\text{both}) = 0.20\\times0.30 = 0.06$."],"hints":["The two given events are the two ways in which $B$ can work. What do they add up to?","Use independence to get $P(A\\text{ works})$.","Multiply the two failure probabilities."],"concept":"Recovering marginal probabilities from joint ones under independence.","misconception":"Not using independence to separate the marginals.","why":"Splitting on $A$ shows $B$’s marginal; independence gives $A$’s.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-30","topic":"indep","sub":"dependent reviewers","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Each of two reviewers detects a given error with probability 0.8. In 5% of cases <b>neither</b> reviewer detects it. What is the probability that <b>only the first</b> reviewer detects it? (Detections are not assumed to be independent.)","data":"","answer":{"value":0.1499999999999999,"display":"0.15"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.15","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.1499999999999999},{"text":"0.16","correct":false,"why":"This assumes independence; the \"neither detects\" probability of 0.05 differs from $0.2\\times0.2 = 0.04$.","v":0.16000000000000003},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the first reviewer misses the error.","v":0.2},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that neither detects it.","v":0.05}],"solution":["$P(\\text{at least one detects}) = 1-0.05 = 0.95$.","$P(\\text{both detect}) = 0.8+0.8-0.95 = 0.65$.","$P(\\text{only first}) = 0.8-0.65 = 0.15$.","(Under independence $P(\\text{both})$ would be $0.64$; the reviewers are slightly positively dependent.)"],"hints":["Use the addition rule to find the probability that both detect the error.","The union of detections is the complement of \"neither detects\".","\"Only the first\" is the first reviewer’s event minus the intersection."],"concept":"Using the addition rule when independence is not available.","misconception":"Assuming independence automatically.","why":"The given marginals and union determine the intersection without further assumptions.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-31","topic":"indep","sub":"association versus independence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"For events $A$ and $B$, $P(A) = 0.5$, $P(B) = 0.4$ and $P(A\\cup B) = 0.75$. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"They are negatively associated: $P(A\\cap B) = 0.15 < P(A)P(B) = 0.20$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"They are negatively associated: $P(A\\cap B) = 0.15 < P(A)P(B) = 0.20$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"They are independent: $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$","correct":false,"why":"The intersection computed from the union is 0.15, not 0.20."},{"text":"They are positively associated: $P(A\\cap B) > P(A)P(B)$","correct":false,"why":"Positive association needs an intersection above 0.20; here it is 0.15."},{"text":"They are mutually exclusive: $P(A\\cap B) = 0$","correct":false,"why":"Then $P(A\\cup B)$ would be 0.9, not 0.75."}],"solution":["$P(A\\cap B) = 0.5+0.4-0.75 = 0.15$.","Independence would give $0.5\\times0.4 = 0.20$. Since $0.15<0.20$, $P(A\\mid B) = 0.375 < P(A)$: the events are negatively associated."],"hints":["Find $P(A\\cap B)$ from the union.","Compare it with $P(A)P(B)$.","Below the product means one event makes the other less likely."],"concept":"Classifying dependence from marginals and a union.","misconception":"Concluding independence without checking the product.","why":"The sign of $P(A\\cap B)-P(A)P(B)$ indicates the direction of association.","fam":"indep:definition"},{"id":"IND-32","topic":"indep","sub":"at least two of three independent events","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three independent screening systems flag a transaction with probabilities $\\tfrac15$, $\\tfrac14$ and $\\tfrac12$. What is the probability that <b>at least two</b> of them flag it?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.225,"display":"0.225"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.225","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.225},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of <i>exactly</i> two flags; all three also qualifies.","v":0.2},{"text":"0.025","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that all three flag the transaction.","v":0.025},{"text":"0.475","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly one flag.","v":0.4750000000000001}],"solution":["Exactly two: $0.2\\cdot0.25\\cdot0.5 + 0.2\\cdot0.75\\cdot0.5 + 0.8\\cdot0.25\\cdot0.5 = 0.025+0.075+0.10 = 0.20$.","All three: $0.2\\cdot0.25\\cdot0.5 = 0.025$.","At least two: $0.225$."],"hints":["Separate \"exactly two\" and \"all three\".","For each pattern multiply the three independent probabilities.","Add the disjoint patterns."],"concept":"Probabilities of counts for non-identical independent trials.","misconception":"Omitting the all-three case.","why":"The patterns are disjoint and exhaust the event.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-34","topic":"indep","sub":"non-transitive dice","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three six-sided dice are labelled $A$: 2, 2, 4, 4, 9, 9; $B$: 1, 1, 6, 6, 8, 8; $C$: 3, 3, 5, 5, 7, 7. Two players each choose a die and roll once, independently; the higher number wins. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Each die beats the next with probability 5/9 ($A$ beats $B$, $B$ beats $C$, $C$ beats $A$), so no die is best"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Each die beats the next with probability 5/9 ($A$ beats $B$, $B$ beats $C$, $C$ beats $A$), so no die is best","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$A$ is best: it contains the largest number, 9","correct":false,"why":"A single large face does not make a die better in head-to-head comparisons; $C$ beats $A$ with probability 5/9."},{"text":"All three dice are equally good because their average face is 5","correct":false,"why":"Equal averages do not make the head-to-head probabilities equal; each die beats another with probability 5/9."},{"text":"$B$ is best because it beats $A$","correct":false,"why":"In fact $A$ beats $B$ with probability 5/9 (not the reverse)."}],"solution":["$P(A>B) = \\tfrac13\\left(\\tfrac13+\\tfrac13+1\\right)= \\tfrac59$ (face 2 beats B's 1s, 4 beats B's 1s, 9 beats all).","$P(B>C) = \\tfrac13\\left(0+\\tfrac23+1\\right) = \\tfrac59$.","$P(C>A) = \\tfrac13\\left(\\tfrac13+\\tfrac23+\\tfrac23\\right) = \\tfrac59$.","The \"beats\" relation is cyclic: no die is best."],"hints":["Compare two dice at a time: $P(X>Y)$ for each pair.","For each face of $X$, count how often it beats a face of $Y$.","See whether the three results form a cycle."],"concept":"Non-transitivity of \"is likely to beat\".","misconception":"Assuming that the die with the highest face or mean is best.","why":"Probabilistic dominance is not transitive.","fam":"indep:definition"},{"id":"IND-35","topic":"indep","sub":"nested events and dependence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Events $A \\subset B$ with $0 < P(A) < P(B) < 1$. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(A\\mid B) = P(A)/P(B) > P(A)$, so $A$ and $B$ are dependent"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(A\\mid B) = P(A)/P(B) > P(A)$, so $A$ and $B$ are dependent","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$A$ and $B$ are independent because $A$ is part of $B$","correct":false,"why":"Being contained makes them dependent: knowing $B$ occurred makes $A$ more likely."},{"text":"$P(A\\mid B) = P(B\\mid A)$","correct":false,"why":"$P(B\\mid A) = 1$ because $A\\subset B$, while $P(A\\mid B) < 1$."},{"text":"$P(A\\mid B) < P(A)$","correct":false,"why":"Dividing by $P(B) < 1$ increases the probability: $P(A\\mid B) = P(A)/P(B) > P(A)$."}],"solution":["Since $A\\subset B$, $A\\cap B = A$ and $P(A\\mid B) = P(A)/P(B)$, which exceeds $P(A)$ because $P(B)<1$.","Independence would require $P(A\\mid B) = P(A)$, i.e. $P(B)=1$, which is excluded."],"hints":["What is $A\\cap B$ when $A\\subset B$?","Write $P(A\\mid B)$ and compare with $P(A)$.","Independence would need $P(B) = 1$."],"concept":"Subset events are dependent unless trivial.","misconception":"Thinking containment implies independence.","why":"Knowing $B$ occurred makes the smaller event $A$ more likely.","fam":"indep:definition"},{"id":"IND-36","topic":"indep","sub":"no repeated colour in a row","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A biased coin lands heads with probability 0.3. It is tossed six times, independently. What is the probability that <b>no two consecutive tosses show the same face</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.018521999999999993,"display":"0.0185"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0185","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.018521999999999993},{"text":"0.00926","correct":false,"why":"This is only one of the two alternating patterns (starting with H or with T).","v":0.009260999999999997},{"text":"0.0313","correct":false,"why":"This is the value for a fair coin.","v":0.03125},{"text":"0.1852","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly three heads in any order, not just alternating sequences.","v":0.18521999999999994}],"solution":["Only two sequences alternate: HTHTHT and THTHTH. Each has three heads and three tails: probability $0.3^3\\,0.7^3 = 0.009261$.","$P = 2\\times0.009261 = 0.0185$."],"hints":["How many sequences of six tosses have no two equal neighbours?","Each such sequence contains how many heads and tails?","Add the probabilities of these sequences."],"concept":"Probability of a pattern under independence.","misconception":"Counting every arrangement with three heads.","why":"Alternation forces the entire sequence once the first toss is fixed.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-38","topic":"indep","sub":"diagnose: exclusive vs independent","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A student writes: \"$P(A) = 0.3$ and $P(B) = 0.4$. The events are mutually exclusive, so they do not affect each other, i.e. they are independent. Hence $P(A\\cap B) = 0.3\\times0.4 = 0.12$.\" Where is the error?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Mutually exclusive events with positive probabilities are dependent; here $P(A\\cap B) = 0$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Mutually exclusive events with positive probabilities are dependent; here $P(A\\cap B) = 0$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The multiplication is wrong: it should be $0.3 + 0.4 - 0.12 = 0.58$","correct":false,"why":"That expression is $P(A\\cup B)$ under independence, not the intersection. The error is earlier: exclusivity does not give independence."},{"text":"Nothing is wrong: exclusive events are always independent","correct":false,"why":"If $A$ occurs then $B$ certainly does not, so knowing $A$ changes $P(B)$ from 0.4 to 0: dependence."},{"text":"The events must have the same probability to be independent","correct":false,"why":"Independence concerns $P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)$, not equal marginals."}],"solution":["Mutually exclusive means $A\\cap B = \\emptyset$, so $P(A\\cap B) = 0$.","Independence would require $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B) = 0.12 \\ne 0$.","So these events cannot be independent. Disjointness is the strongest kind of dependence: one event rules out the other."],"hints":["Compare what exclusivity and independence each say about $P(A\\cap B)$.","What is $P(B\\mid A)$ if $A$ and $B$ cannot occur together?","The student has used the independence formula after (wrongly) deducing independence."],"concept":"Distinguishing mutually exclusive from independent events.","misconception":"\"They don’t overlap, so they don’t interact.\"","why":"Exclusive events carry information about each other: occurrence of one excludes the other.","fam":"indep:diagnose"},{"id":"IND-39","topic":"indep","sub":"diagnose: P(A|B)=P(B|A)","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A student claims: \"If $P(A\\mid B) = P(B\\mid A)$ then $A$ and $B$ are independent.\" Which statement about the claim is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"False: the equality holds whenever $P(A) = P(B)$, even for dependent events (for example, $A = B$ with positive probability)"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"False: the equality holds whenever $P(A) = P(B)$, even for dependent events (for example, $A = B$ with positive probability)","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"True: the two conditional probabilities being equal means the events do not influence each other","correct":false,"why":"Equality of the two conditionals only means $P(A\\cap B)/P(B) = P(A\\cap B)/P(A)$, i.e. $P(A)=P(B)$ when the intersection is non-empty."},{"text":"True, but only if $P(A) + P(B) = 1$","correct":false,"why":"No such condition makes the claim true."},{"text":"False: the two conditional probabilities can never be equal for dependent events","correct":false,"why":"They can be equal: take $A = B$, both conditionals equal 1."}],"solution":["$P(A\\mid B) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(B)}$ and $P(B\\mid A) = \\dfrac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}$.","When $P(A\\cap B) > 0$ these are equal exactly when $P(A) = P(B)$ — which says nothing about independence.","Counterexample: $A = B$ with $0<P(A)<1$. Then both conditionals equal 1, yet $P(A\\cap B) = P(A) \\neq P(A)^2$."],"hints":["Write both conditional probabilities using $P(A\\cap B)$.","When are two fractions with the same numerator equal?","Find an example with $A = B$."],"concept":"Conditional probabilities and independence are different conditions.","misconception":"Equating symmetric-looking conditions with independence.","why":"Independence needs $P(A\\mid B)=P(A)$, comparing a conditional with a marginal.","fam":"indep:diagnose"},{"id":"IND-40","topic":"indep","sub":"pairwise versus mutual independence","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Three events are <b>pairwise</b> independent: each pair satisfies $P(A_i\\cap A_j) = P(A_i)P(A_j)$. Does it follow that the three events are mutually independent, i.e. that $P(A_1\\cap A_2\\cap A_3) = P(A_1)P(A_2)P(A_3)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"No: pairwise independence does not guarantee the three-way product rule"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"No: pairwise independence does not guarantee the three-way product rule","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Yes: independence of every pair is the same as mutual independence","correct":false,"why":"There are examples (two fair coins and the event \"same face\") where all pairs are independent but the triple product fails."},{"text":"Yes, but only if the events have equal probabilities","correct":false,"why":"Equal probabilities do not repair the failure; the standard counterexample has all probabilities equal to 1/2."},{"text":"No: pairwise independent events must be mutually exclusive","correct":false,"why":"Independent events with positive probabilities cannot be mutually exclusive."}],"solution":["Mutual independence requires the product rule for every subset, including the triple.","Counterexample: toss two fair coins; $A$ = first is heads, $B$ = second is heads, $C$ = both show the same face.","Each pair is independent, but $P(A\\cap B\\cap C) = \\tfrac14 \\ne \\tfrac18$."],"hints":["List the product conditions that mutual independence requires.","Pairs are only some of them.","Try two fair coins with a third event defined from both."],"concept":"Pairwise versus mutual independence.","misconception":"Assuming pairwise independence is enough.","why":"Mutual independence is a stronger, joint condition.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-41","topic":"indep","sub":"pairwise independent but not mutually independent","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair coins are tossed. Let $A$ = \"first is heads\", $B$ = \"second is heads\" and $C$ = \"both coins show the same face\". What is $P(A\\cap B\\cap C)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"1/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["$A\\cap B$ is \"two heads\", which already implies \"same face\": $A\\cap B\\cap C = A\\cap B$.","$P = 1/4$.","(The product $P(A)P(B)P(C) = 1/8$, so the events are not mutually independent even though each pair is independent.)"],"hints":["Does $A\\cap B$ force $C$?","Then $A\\cap B\\cap C = A\\cap B$.","What is the probability of two heads?"],"concept":"The triple intersection can differ from the product.","misconception":"Multiplying three marginal probabilities.","why":"The events are dependent as a triple through the shared tosses.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-42","topic":"indep","sub":"reliability: series-parallel network","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A system has two parallel branches. Each branch consists of two components in <b>series</b>. Every component works independently with probability 0.9, and the system works if <b>at least one branch</b> works. What is the probability that the system works?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.9639,"display":"0.9639"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["A branch works with probability $0.9\\times0.9 = 0.81$.","Both branches fail with probability $(1-0.81)^2 = 0.0361$.","System reliability $= 1-0.0361 = 0.9639$."],"hints":["Find the reliability of one series branch.","The branches are in parallel: use the complement of \"both fail\".","Combine the branch and complement results."],"concept":"Series and parallel reliability.","misconception":"Mixing up series and parallel formulas.","why":"Series multiplies reliabilities; parallel multiplies failure probabilities.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-43","topic":"indep","sub":"reliability: parallel block in series","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A payment system needs a gateway (works with probability 0.95) <b>and</b> at least one of three identical servers (each works with probability 0.7), all independent. What is the probability that the system works?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.9243499999999999,"display":"0.9243"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0006,"options":[],"solution":["At least one server works: $1-0.3^3 = 0.973$.","The gateway is in series with that block: $0.95\\times0.973 = 0.92435$."],"hints":["Treat the three servers as one parallel block.","Find the block reliability using the complement.","The block and the gateway are in series: multiply."],"concept":"Parallel block in series with another component.","misconception":"Multiplying all reliabilities.","why":"Redundancy helps only the block it is applied to.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-44","topic":"indep","sub":"reliability: 2-out-of-3 with different components","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A control system works if at least <b>2 of its 3</b> sensors work. The sensors work independently with probabilities 0.9, 0.8 and 0.7. What is the probability that the system works?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.902,"display":"0.902"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Exactly two work: $0.9(0.8)(0.3)+0.9(0.2)(0.7)+0.1(0.8)(0.7) = 0.216+0.126+0.056 = 0.398$.","All three: $0.9(0.8)(0.7) = 0.504$.","Total $0.902$."],"hints":["Separate \"exactly two work\" from \"all three work\".","With different probabilities each pattern must be written out.","Add the four patterns."],"concept":"Majority system with unequal component reliabilities.","misconception":"Using a binomial formula with a single $p$.","why":"Non-identical components require an explicit sum over patterns.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-45","topic":"indep","sub":"testing independence of two events from a model","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair dice are rolled. Which pair of events is <b>independent</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"\"the sum is 7\" and \"the first die shows 3\""},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"\"the sum is 7\" and \"the first die shows 3\"","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"\"the sum is 8\" and \"the first die shows 3\"","correct":false,"why":"$P(\\text{both}) = 1/36$ but $P(\\text{sum 8})P(\\text{first 3}) = \\tfrac{5}{36}\\cdot\\tfrac16 = \\tfrac{5}{216}$."},{"text":"\"the sum is 12\" and \"the first die shows 6\"","correct":false,"why":"A sum of 12 forces the first die to be 6: $P(\\text{both}) = 1/36 \\ne \\tfrac1{36}\\cdot\\tfrac16$."},{"text":"\"the sum is 2\" and \"the first die shows 1\"","correct":false,"why":"A sum of 2 forces the first die to be 1, so the events are dependent."}],"solution":["$P(\\text{sum }7) = 6/36 = 1/6$, $P(\\text{first }3) = 1/6$, $P(\\text{both}) = P((3,4)) = 1/36 = \\tfrac16\\cdot\\tfrac16$.","The product rule holds. For the other pairs it fails."],"hints":["Compute the three probabilities for each pair.","Test $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$.","A sum of 7 can be completed from any first value."],"concept":"Checking independence by the product rule.","misconception":"Judging independence by intuition.","why":"Whatever the first die shows, exactly one value of the second die gives a sum of 7.","fam":"indep:definition"},{"id":"IND-46","topic":"indep","sub":"independence of successive draws","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two cards are drawn from a standard deck. In which scenario are the events \"first card is a heart\" and \"second card is a heart\" <b>independent</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"The first card is replaced and the deck reshuffled before the second draw"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"The first card is replaced and the deck reshuffled before the second draw","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The first card is kept and the second drawn from the remaining 51","correct":false,"why":"Without replacement $P(\\text{second heart}\\mid\\text{first heart}) = 12/51 \\ne 13/52$."},{"text":"Both cards are drawn at once from the full deck","correct":false,"why":"Drawing both at once is equivalent to drawing without replacement."},{"text":"In every scenario, because the deck is shuffled","correct":false,"why":"Shuffling randomises order but does not remove the dependence caused by removing a card."}],"solution":["With replacement and reshuffling, the second draw has the same distribution regardless of the first: $P = 13/52$ in both cases.","Without replacement, a heart first leaves 12 hearts among 51 cards."],"hints":["Does the first draw change the composition for the second draw?","Compare $P(\\text{second heart}\\mid\\text{first heart})$ with $P(\\text{second heart})$.","Which scenario restores the original deck?"],"concept":"Independence under sampling with replacement.","misconception":"Assuming random draws are independent.","why":"Replacement keeps the sampling distribution fixed.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-47","topic":"indep","sub":"exactly one of two independent events","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Server 1 is up with probability 0.9 and server 2 with probability 0.7, independently. What is the probability that <b>exactly one</b> server is up?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.34,"display":"0.34"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Server 1 up, 2 down: $0.9\\times0.3 = 0.27$.","Server 1 down, 2 up: $0.1\\times0.7 = 0.07$.","Total $0.34$."],"hints":["Two disjoint patterns give exactly one up.","Use independence within each pattern.","Add the two products."],"concept":"Exactly one of two independent events.","misconception":"Adding the two up-probabilities.","why":"The patterns are disjoint and each is a product of marginals.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-48","topic":"indep","sub":"common-cause failure","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two machines share a power supply. In a given month a power shock occurs with probability 0.05 and then <b>both</b> machines fail. Otherwise each machine fails independently with probability 0.06. What is the probability that <b>both</b> fail?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.05342,"display":"0.0534"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Condition on the shock: $P(\\text{both}) = 0.05\\cdot1 + 0.95\\cdot(0.06)^2 = 0.05+0.00342 = 0.0534$.","An independence assumption would give a far smaller value."],"hints":["Condition on whether a shock occurs.","Given a shock both fail; otherwise the machines fail independently.","Combine with the law of total probability."],"concept":"Common-cause failures make failures positively dependent.","misconception":"Assuming independence and squaring the failure probability.","why":"A shared cause raises the chance of simultaneous failure well above the independent value.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-49","topic":"indep","sub":"first success on a given trial","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Each independent call to a prospect ends in a sale with probability 0.3. What is the probability that the <b>first sale occurs on the fourth call</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.10289999999999998,"display":"0.1029"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["The first three calls must fail and the fourth succeed: $0.7^3\\times0.3 = 0.1029$."],"hints":["What must happen on each of the first three calls?","And on the fourth?","Multiply the independent probabilities."],"concept":"Geometric probability for independent trials.","misconception":"Using $0.3^4$ or $4\\times0.3$.","why":"The pattern failure, failure, failure, success has this probability.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-50","topic":"indep","sub":"records among independent values","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A stock's weekly return is independent and identically distributed (continuous) each week. What is the probability that week 5's return is the <b>highest of the first 5 weeks</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2,"display":"1/5"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["By symmetry each of the 5 values is equally likely to be the largest.","$P = 1/5$."],"hints":["Are the five returns exchangeable?","Which of the five is the largest, and how likely is each?","Ties have probability zero for a continuous distribution."],"concept":"Symmetry for iid continuous variables.","misconception":"Trying to integrate with a specific distribution.","why":"All five positions are equally likely to hold the maximum.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-51","topic":"indep","sub":"functions of independent variables","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"$X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables. For which pair is independence <b>guaranteed</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$X^2$ and $Y^3$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$X^2$ and $Y^3$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$X$ and $X+Y$","correct":false,"why":"$X+Y$ contains $X$, so the two are generally dependent."},{"text":"$X+Y$ and $X-Y$","correct":false,"why":"These are dependent in general (they are uncorrelated only if $\\mathrm{Var}(X)=\\mathrm{Var}(Y)$, and even then not independent in general)."},{"text":"$X$ and $\\max(X,Y)$","correct":false,"why":"The maximum depends on $X$ directly."}],"solution":["Functions of different independent variables are independent: $g(X)$ and $h(Y)$ are independent for any functions $g, h$.","The other pairs share $X$."],"hints":["Which pair uses separate variables only?","Functions of one variable alone cannot link the two.","Check whether each other pair depends on both $X$ and $Y$."],"concept":"Independence is preserved under separate transformations.","misconception":"Believing that sums and differences of independent variables are independent.","why":"Different variables are fed into different functions, so no information is shared.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-52","topic":"indep","sub":"runs: no three consecutive heads","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A fair coin is tossed 10 times. What is the probability that there is <b>no run of three or more consecutive heads</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4921875,"display":"0.4922"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Let $a_n$ be the number of valid sequences of length $n$. A valid sequence ends in T, HT or HHT (preceded by a valid sequence), so $a_n = a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}+a_{n-3}$ with $a_1=2$, $a_2=4$, $a_3=7$.","Continuing: 13, 24, 44, 81, 149, 274, 504.","$P = 504/1024 \\approx 0.492$."],"hints":["Classify valid sequences by how they end: T, HT or HHT.","This yields a recursion with three terms.","Compute $a_{10}$ and divide by $2^{10}$."],"concept":"Runs and tribonacci-type recursion.","misconception":"Assuming independence of position windows.","why":"Every sequence of tosses is equally likely; counting valid ones gives the probability.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-54","topic":"indep","sub":"complements of independent events","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$A$ and $B$ are independent with $P(A) = 0.3$ and $P(B) = 0.6$. What is the probability that <b>neither</b> occurs?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.27999999999999997,"display":"0.28"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["The complements are independent: $P(A^c\\cap B^c) = 0.7\\times0.4 = 0.28$.","Check: $1-P(A\\cup B) = 1-0.72 = 0.28$."],"hints":["Complements of independent events are independent.","Find each complement’s probability.","Multiply the two complement probabilities."],"concept":"Independence is preserved under complements.","misconception":"Using $1-0.3-0.6$.","why":"Neither occurring is a conjunction of two independent complements.","fam":"indep:definition"},{"id":"IND-55","topic":"indep","sub":"equal values of independent variables","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two employees independently pick a lunch day uniformly from the 12 working days of a fortnight (Monday of week 1 to Friday of week 2, minus two holidays). What is the probability that they pick the <b>same day</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.08333333333333333,"display":"1/12"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["Whatever the first picks, the second matches with probability $1/12$.","$P = 1/12$."],"hints":["Condition on the first employee’s choice.","The second is independent of it.","How likely is it to pick that day?"],"concept":"Probability of a match between independent uniform choices.","misconception":"Counting pairs as if unordered.","why":"The first choice is arbitrary; only the second must hit it.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-56","topic":"indep","sub":"disjoint random subsets","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two analysts each choose a random subset of the four stocks $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$, independently and uniformly among all 16 subsets. What is the probability that their chosen subsets are <b>disjoint</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.31640625,"display":"0.3164"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Consider each stock separately: it can be in neither subset, only the first, or only the second ($3$ of the $4$ equally likely combinations); being in both would violate disjointness.","Independent stocks: $(3/4)^4 = 81/256 \\approx 0.316$."],"hints":["Look at one stock at a time: in how many of the 4 combinations is it not in both?","The choices for different stocks are independent.","Raise to the fourth power."],"concept":"Independence across elements of random sets.","misconception":"Counting subsets of fixed size.","why":"Each stock is in a subset independently with probability 1/2.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-57","topic":"indep","sub":"redundancy design: number of parallel units","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A backup system uses $n$ identical units in parallel, each working independently with probability 0.8. What is the <b>smallest $n$</b> for which the probability that at least one unit works is <b>at least 0.999</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":5,"display":"5"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Need $1-0.2^n \\ge 0.999$, i.e. $0.2^n \\le 0.001$.","$n \\ge \\dfrac{\\ln0.001}{\\ln0.2} \\approx 4.29$, so $n = 5$.","Check: $0.2^4 = 0.0016$ (too large), $0.2^5 = 0.00032$."],"hints":["Write the system reliability as $1-(1-r)^n$.","Solve the inequality using logarithms.","Round up to an integer."],"concept":"Number of redundant units for a reliability target.","misconception":"Rounding down.","why":"Each extra unit multiplies the failure probability by 0.2.","fam":"indep:repeated-trials"},{"id":"IND-58","topic":"indep","sub":"comparing two designs","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Design 1 uses a single component that works with probability 0.99. Design 2 uses two <b>independent</b> components in parallel, each working with probability 0.9. Which design is more reliable?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"They are equally reliable: both work with probability 0.99"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"They are equally reliable: both work with probability 0.99","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Design 1, because one good component beats two worse ones","correct":false,"why":"Two independent parallel units of reliability 0.9 give $1-0.1^2 = 0.99$."},{"text":"Design 2: it works with probability 0.999","correct":false,"why":"Design 2 fails with probability $0.1^2 = 0.01$, so it works with probability 0.99, not 0.999."},{"text":"Cannot be compared without more information","correct":false,"why":"Both reliabilities can be computed directly."}],"solution":["Design 2 fails only if both units fail: $0.1\\times0.1 = 0.01$, so it works with probability $0.99$.","Design 1 works with probability $0.99$. The two are equally reliable."],"hints":["Compute the reliability of each design.","A parallel pair fails only if both fail.","Compare the two numbers."],"concept":"Parallel redundancy replaces component quality.","misconception":"Judging by the best single component.","why":"Redundancy can substitute for reliability.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-59","topic":"indep","sub":"larger of two independent uniforms","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent numbers are chosen uniformly from $(0,1)$. What is the probability that the <b>larger</b> exceeds 0.9?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.18999999999999995,"display":"0.19"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["The maximum is at most 0.9 only if both are: $0.9\\times0.9 = 0.81$.","$P(\\max>0.9) = 0.19$."],"hints":["When is the maximum not above 0.9?","Both numbers must be below 0.9.","Take the complement."],"concept":"Maximum of independent variables via the complement.","misconception":"Doubling 0.1.","why":"Both variables must be small for the maximum to be small.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"IND-61","topic":"indep","sub":"streaks: no two consecutive rainy days","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Each of 5 consecutive days is rainy with probability 0.3, independently. What is the probability that there are <b>two consecutive rainy days</b> at some point?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.27332999999999996,"display":"0.2733"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0006,"options":[],"solution":["Let $f_n$ be the probability of no two consecutive rainy days in $n$ days. $f_n = 0.7f_{n-1}+0.21f_{n-2}$ (dry day, or wet day followed by a forced dry day).","$f_1 = 1$, $f_2 = 0.91$, $f_3 = 0.847$, $f_4 = 0.784$, $f_5 = 0.7267$.","Answer: $1-0.7267 = 0.2733$."],"hints":["Use the complement: no two consecutive rainy days.","Condition on whether day 1 is rainy or dry.","Build up the probabilities for $n=1,\\dots,5$."],"concept":"Pattern probability by recursion.","misconception":"Treating pairs of days as independent windows.","why":"Overlapping windows are dependent, so a recursion is needed.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-62","topic":"indep","sub":"diagnose: physical separation versus independence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A student argues: \"Sales on Monday and sales on Tuesday are independent because they happen on different days and nothing physically connects them.\" What is wrong with the argument?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Independence is about information: if Monday’s sales tell you anything about Tuesday’s (through demand, season, promotion), the events are dependent"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Independence is about information: if Monday’s sales tell you anything about Tuesday’s (through demand, season, promotion), the events are dependent","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Nothing: events on different days are always independent","correct":false,"why":"Different time points can be strongly related through shared demand or trends."},{"text":"Different days make the events mutually exclusive","correct":false,"why":"Both days’ sales can be high at the same time; exclusivity is a different notion."},{"text":"Independence is only defined for events on the same day","correct":false,"why":"Independence is defined for any two events on the same probability space."}],"solution":["Independence means $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$, equivalently that learning $A$ does not change the probability of $B$.","Day-to-day sales usually share drivers (seasonality, promotions, the economy), so one day carries information about the next."],"hints":["What does independence say about learning one event’s outcome?","Would a very high Monday change your expectation for Tuesday?","Physical separation is not the criterion."],"concept":"Independence is a statement about information, not separation.","misconception":"Equating \"unrelated in space or time\" with independence.","why":"Shared drivers induce dependence between separated events.","fam":"indep:diagnose"},{"id":"IND-63","topic":"indep","sub":"AND versus OR alarm logic","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent sensors each give a false alarm during a night with probabilities 0.05 and 0.02. The system sounds the alarm if <b>either</b> sensor triggers. What is the probability of a false system alarm in a night?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.06900000000000006,"display":"0.069"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["No false alarm requires both sensors quiet: $0.95\\times0.98 = 0.931$.","False alarm: $1-0.931 = 0.069$.","(If both had to trigger the probability would be $0.05\\times0.02 = 0.001$.)"],"hints":["\"Either triggers\" is a union.","Use the complement: both quiet.","Multiply the independent quiet probabilities."],"concept":"Union of independent events.","misconception":"Adding the two probabilities.","why":"The complement of \"either\" is \"neither\", a product of complements.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-64","topic":"indep","sub":"which consequences follow from independence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"$A$ and $B$ are independent events with $0<P(A)<1$ and $0<P(B)<1$. Which of the following is <b>not</b> a consequence of independence?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(A^c\\cap B) = P(A^c)P(B)$","correct":false,"why":"This is a consequence: complements of independent events are independent."},{"text":"$P(A\\mid B) = P(A)$","correct":false,"why":"This is the defining property of independence in terms of conditional probability."},{"text":"$P(B\\mid A^c) = P(B)$","correct":false,"why":"This is a consequence: $B$ is independent of $A^c$ too."}],"solution":["The addition formula $P(A\\cup B) = P(A)+P(B)$ holds only when $P(A\\cap B) = 0$. For independent events with positive probabilities $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B) > 0$, so it fails.","The other statements follow from independence."],"hints":["Which statement is really about mutually exclusive events?","What is $P(A\\cap B)$ under independence?","Check each of the other statements against the product rule."],"concept":"Consequences of independence.","misconception":"Using the exclusive-events addition rule for independent events.","why":"Independence puts probability on the intersection; exclusivity removes it.","fam":"indep:combining"},{"id":"IND-65","topic":"indep","sub":"independent trials: at least once in repeated attempts with learning","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A job seeker applies to firms one after another. Because her application improves, the success probability of attempt $k$ is $0.1k$ ($k = 1,2,3,4$), independently across attempts. What is the probability that she gets <b>at least one</b> offer in the four attempts?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6976,"display":"0.6976"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6976","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6976},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This adds the probabilities; they would sum to 1.0 which is not the probability of at least one offer.","v":1},{"text":"0.3024","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of <i>no</i> offer.","v":0.3024},{"text":"0.6836","correct":false,"why":"This uses the average success probability $0.25$ for every attempt; the probabilities differ by attempt.","v":0.68359375}],"solution":["No offer in any attempt: $0.9\\times0.8\\times0.7\\times0.6 = 0.3024$.","At least one offer: $1-0.3024 = 0.6976$."],"hints":["Use the complement: no offer at all.","The attempts are independent but have different probabilities.","Multiply the four failure probabilities."],"concept":"Complement for independent non-identical trials.","misconception":"Using a common probability.","why":"Independence lets the failure probabilities multiply even when they differ.","fam":"indep:conditioning"},{"id":"BAY-01","topic":"bayes","sub":"total probability","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A lender's loan book is split into prime borrowers (50%), near-prime (30%) and subprime (20%). The probability of default within a year is 0.02, 0.08 and 0.25 respectively. What is the probability that a randomly chosen loan defaults within a year?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.084,"display":"0.084"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.084","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.084},{"text":"0.1167","correct":false,"why":"This is the unweighted average of the three default rates; the segments have different sizes.","v":0.11666666666666665},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is the default rate of the largest-risk segment only.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.01","correct":false,"why":"This is the contribution of the prime segment only; the other segments must be added.","v":0.01}],"solution":["Condition on the segment and add the weighted probabilities:","$P(D) = 0.5(0.02) + 0.3(0.08) + 0.2(0.25) = 0.010+0.024+0.050 = 0.084$."],"hints":["Which events partition the loan book?","Each segment contributes its share times its default rate.","Add the three contributions."],"concept":"Law of total probability.","misconception":"Averaging the segment rates without weights.","why":"The segments are disjoint and cover all loans, so contributions add.","fam":"bayes:total-probability"},{"id":"BAY-02","topic":"bayes","sub":"total probability over stages","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"60% of an online retailer's orders are shipped to home addresses and 40% to pickup points. Home orders are returned with probability 0.15 and pickup orders with probability 0.05. A returned item is refunded in full with probability 0.9 for home orders and 0.7 for pickup orders. What is the probability that a randomly chosen order ends in a <b>full refund</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.095,"display":"0.095"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.095","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.095},{"text":"0.11","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that an order is returned; not every return is fully refunded.","v":0.11},{"text":"0.17","correct":false,"why":"This is an unweighted sum of the branch probabilities; each branch must be multiplied by its share of orders.","v":0.17},{"text":"0.82","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of a full refund <i>given a return</i> using the order mix, not the probability for a randomly chosen order.","v":0.8200000000000001}],"solution":["Follow the tree. Home: $0.6\\times0.15\\times0.9 = 0.081$. Pickup: $0.4\\times0.05\\times0.7 = 0.014$.","The branches are disjoint: $0.081+0.014 = 0.095$."],"hints":["Draw the tree with delivery type first, then return, then refund.","Multiply along each branch that ends in a full refund.","Add the branch probabilities."],"concept":"Total probability through a multi-stage tree.","misconception":"Not multiplying along the whole branch.","why":"Each full-refund path corresponds to a unique branch.","fam":"bayes:total-probability"},{"id":"BAY-04","topic":"bayes","sub":"reverse total probability","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Overall, 90% of invoices are paid on time. 25% of invoices are flagged \"express\", and 80% of express invoices are paid on time. What is the probability that a <b>non-express</b> invoice is paid on time?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.9333333333333332,"display":"0.9333"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.9333","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.9333333333333332},{"text":"0.9","correct":false,"why":"This is the overall on-time rate; the express invoices are paid less reliably, so the others must be higher.","v":0.9},{"text":"0.1","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts rates directly rather than removing the express contribution from the total and rescaling.","v":0.09999999999999998},{"text":"0.1333","correct":false,"why":"The express invoices contribute $0.25\\times0.8$ to the total, not $0.8$.","v":0.1333333333333333}],"solution":["Total probability: $0.9 = 0.25(0.8) + 0.75\\,x$, where $x$ is the non-express on-time rate.","$0.75x = 0.9-0.2 = 0.7$, so $x = 0.9333$."],"hints":["Write the total probability of being on time as a weighted sum over the two invoice types.","The unknown is the non-express conditional probability.","Solve the linear equation."],"concept":"Using total probability backwards.","misconception":"Subtracting percentages without weighting.","why":"The overall rate is a weighted average of the two group rates.","fam":"bayes:total-probability"},{"id":"BAY-05","topic":"bayes","sub":"reversing a conditional","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"40% of households own a bicycle. Of the households that own a bicycle, 30% also own a car. Overall 50% of households own a car. What is the probability that a household owns a bicycle <b>given that it owns a car</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.24,"display":"0.24"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.24","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.24},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(\\text{car}\\mid\\text{bicycle})$, the conditional in the opposite direction.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.4","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of owning a bicycle.","v":0.4},{"text":"0.12","correct":false,"why":"This is the joint probability of owning both; it must be divided by $P(\\text{car})$.","v":0.12}],"solution":["$P(\\text{bicycle}\\cap\\text{car}) = P(\\text{car}\\mid\\text{bicycle})P(\\text{bicycle}) = 0.3\\times0.4 = 0.12$.","$P(\\text{bicycle}\\mid\\text{car}) = 0.12/0.50 = 0.24$."],"hints":["Which conditional probability is given and which is asked for?","Find the joint probability first.","Divide by the probability of the conditioning event."],"concept":"Reversing a conditional.","misconception":"Equating $P(A\\mid B)$ and $P(B\\mid A)$.","why":"Both conditionals share the same joint probability and differ only in the denominator.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-06","topic":"bayes","sub":"partition and posterior","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A streaming service's users are students (25%), professionals (45%) and retirees (30%). In a given month 60% of students, 30% of professionals and 50% of retirees stream a live event. A user who streamed the event is chosen. What is the probability that the user is a <b>professional</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3103448275862069,"display":"0.3103"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3103","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3103448275862069},{"text":"0.45","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior share of professionals; professionals are less likely than average to stream.","v":0.45},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the streaming rate of professionals, $P(\\text{stream}\\mid\\text{professional})$.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.135","correct":false,"why":"This is the joint probability of being a professional who streams; divide by $P(\\text{stream})$.","v":0.135}],"solution":["$P(\\text{stream}) = 0.25(0.6)+0.45(0.3)+0.30(0.5) = 0.15+0.135+0.15 = 0.435$.","$P(\\text{professional}\\mid\\text{stream}) = \\dfrac{0.135}{0.435} \\approx 0.310$."],"hints":["Compute the probability of streaming from the three segments.","Compute the joint probability for professionals.","Divide the joint by the total."],"concept":"Posterior over a partition.","misconception":"Using the prior or the likelihood as the answer.","why":"The posterior share is each segment’s contribution to the total evidence.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-09","topic":"bayes","sub":"transfer between boxes","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Box 1 holds 3 red and 2 green balls; box 2 holds 1 red and 3 green balls. Two balls are taken from box 1 at random (without replacement) and placed in box 2; then one ball is drawn from box 2 and is <b>red</b>. What is the probability that both transferred balls were red?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4090909090909091,"display":"0.4091"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4091","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.4090909090909091},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability that both transferred balls are red.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of drawing red from box 2 after two red balls were added; it is a likelihood, not the posterior.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.15","correct":false,"why":"This is the joint probability of the transfer and the red draw; it must be divided by $P(\\text{red})$.","v":0.15}],"solution":["Transfer outcomes: RR with prob $\\binom32/\\binom52 = 0.3$; RG: $3\\cdot2/10 = 0.6$; GG: $1/10 = 0.1$.","Box 2 then has 6 balls with 3, 2, 1 red balls respectively, so $P(\\text{red}\\mid\\cdot) = 1/2,\\ 1/3,\\ 1/6$.","$P(\\text{red}) = 0.15+0.20+0.0167 = 0.3667$.","$P(RR\\mid\\text{red}) = 0.15/0.3667 \\approx 0.409$."],"hints":["List the three possible transfers and their probabilities.","For each transfer, what is the composition of box 2 and the probability of drawing red?","Apply Bayes’ theorem to the transfer RR."],"concept":"Bayes’ theorem with a hidden intermediate stage.","misconception":"Ignoring that the transfer composition is uncertain.","why":"The red draw is more likely after a red-heavy transfer.","fam":"bayes:information"},{"id":"BAY-10","topic":"bayes","sub":"what information was received","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent credit checks each pass an applicant with probability $\\tfrac12$. Analyst $X$ is told \"the <b>first</b> check passed\". Analyst $Y$ is told \"<b>at least one</b> check passed\". What probability does each assign to <b>both</b> checks having passed?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$X$: $\\tfrac12$ and $Y$: $\\tfrac13$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$X$: $\\tfrac12$ and $Y$: $\\tfrac13$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Both assign $\\tfrac13$","correct":false,"why":"Knowing which check passed (\"the first\") leaves only two outcomes, so $X$’s probability is $\\tfrac12$."},{"text":"Both assign $\\tfrac12$","correct":false,"why":"\"At least one passed\" keeps three outcomes (PP, PF, FP), so $Y$’s probability is $\\tfrac13$."},{"text":"$X$: $\\tfrac13$ and $Y$: $\\tfrac12$","correct":false,"why":"The two probabilities are the wrong way round: the more specific information gives the higher probability."}],"solution":["$X$: given the first passed, the second is independent: $P(\\text{both}) = \\tfrac12$.","$Y$: outcomes with at least one pass are PP, PF, FP, equally likely: $P(\\text{both}) = \\tfrac13$.","The same facts, reported differently, carry different information."],"hints":["List the outcomes consistent with each analyst’s information.","Are the remaining outcomes equally likely?","Count those with both checks passed."],"concept":"The information received determines the conditioning event.","misconception":"Treating \"a check passed\" and \"the first check passed\" as the same.","why":"\"At least one\" is weaker information than a specific check.","fam":"bayes:information"},{"id":"BAY-12","topic":"bayes","sub":"base rates","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Card fraud affects 4% of transactions made abroad and 0.5% of transactions made at home. The bank processes <b>three times as many</b> transactions at home as abroad. A transaction known to be fraudulent is chosen at random. What is the probability that it was made <b>abroad</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7272727272727273,"display":"0.7273"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.7273","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.7272727272727273},{"text":"0.8889","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the change in the mix and still treats the locations as equally common.","v":0.888888888888889},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability of abroad.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.01","correct":false,"why":"This is the joint probability of abroad and fraud; divide by $P(\\text{fraud})$.","v":0.01}],"solution":["Priors: abroad $0.25$, home $0.75$.","$P(\\text{abroad}\\mid\\text{fraud}) = \\dfrac{0.25(0.04)}{0.25(0.04)+0.75(0.005)} = \\dfrac{0.01}{0.01375} \\approx 0.727$."],"hints":["Convert \"three times as many\" into prior probabilities.","Weight the fraud rates by the new priors.","Compare with the equal-prior answer."],"concept":"Effect of the base rate on the posterior.","misconception":"Using the equal-prior answer.","why":"A smaller prior for the high-risk group lowers its posterior share.","fam":"bayes:testing"},{"id":"BAY-13","topic":"bayes","sub":"which container","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Bag $X$ holds 2 red and 8 blue chips; bag $Y$ holds 6 red and 4 blue chips. A bag is chosen at random and a chip is drawn: it is <b>red</b>. What is the probability that the chip came from bag $X$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"0.25"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.25","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.25},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability of bag $X$; the red chip favours bag $Y$.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the proportion of red chips in bag $X$, not the posterior.","v":0.2},{"text":"0.75","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the chip came from bag $Y$.","v":0.75}],"solution":["$P(X\\mid\\text{red}) = \\dfrac{0.5(0.2)}{0.5(0.2)+0.5(0.6)} = \\dfrac{0.1}{0.4} = 0.25$."],"hints":["Both bags are equally likely a priori.","What is the probability of red from each bag?","Divide bag $X$’s contribution by the total."],"concept":"Posterior for a two-state partition.","misconception":"Reporting the proportion of red chips.","why":"Observing red shifts belief towards the red-rich bag.","fam":"bayes:information"},{"id":"BAY-15","topic":"bayes","sub":"which source","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Reports come from team $A$ (70% of reports) or team $B$ (30%). Team $A$ files a report late with probability 0.2 and team $B$ with probability 0.5. A <b>late</b> report arrives. What is the probability that it came from team $B$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5172413793103449,"display":"0.5172"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.5172","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5172413793103449},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior share of reports from team $B$.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is team $B$’s lateness rate, not the probability that a late report is from $B$.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.14","correct":false,"why":"This is the joint probability of team $A$ and late.","v":0.13999999999999999}],"solution":["$P(\\text{late}) = 0.7(0.2)+0.3(0.5) = 0.14+0.15 = 0.29$.","$P(B\\mid\\text{late}) = 0.15/0.29 \\approx 0.517$."],"hints":["Find the total probability that a report is late.","Find the joint probability of team $B$ and late.","Divide the joint probability by the total probability of the evidence."],"concept":"Bayes with unequal reliability.","misconception":"Reporting the prior.","why":"Team $B$ is a minority of reports but much more often late, which raises its posterior share.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-16","topic":"bayes","sub":"weights from sizes","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Departments $X$, $Y$ and $Z$ have 40, 60 and 100 employees. 25%, 50% and 10% of them respectively are on flexible contracts. One employee on a flexible contract is chosen at random. What is the probability that this employee works in department $Z$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2,"display":"0.2"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.2},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of all employees in $Z$; flexible contracts are rarer there.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.1","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of $Z$ employees on flexible contracts.","v":0.1},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"The denominator omits department $Z$’s own flexible employees.","v":0.25}],"solution":["Flexible employees: $X$: $40(0.25) = 10$; $Y$: $60(0.5) = 30$; $Z$: $100(0.1) = 10$. Total 50.","$P(Z) = 10/50 = 0.20$."],"hints":["Count the flexible employees in each department.","A random flexible employee is equally likely to be any of them.","Divide $Z$’s count by the total."],"concept":"Posterior from counts.","misconception":"Using the department’s share of all employees.","why":"With equally likely individuals, the posterior is a ratio of counts.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-17","topic":"bayes","sub":"sequential evidence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A box holds a fair die and a die that always shows 6. One die is chosen at random and rolled twice; both rolls show 6. What is the probability that the <b>always-6 die</b> was chosen?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.972972972972973,"display":"0.973"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.973","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.972972972972973},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability; two sixes are far more likely from the loaded die.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.8571","correct":false,"why":"This uses only one roll as evidence; two sixes were observed, which has probability $1/36$ for the fair die.","v":0.8571428571428571},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"Two sixes do not rule out the fair die; it produces them with probability $1/36$.","v":1}],"solution":["$P(\\text{two 6s}\\mid\\text{loaded}) = 1$, $P(\\text{two 6s}\\mid\\text{fair}) = 1/36$.","$P(\\text{loaded}\\mid\\text{two 6s}) = \\dfrac{0.5\\cdot1}{0.5\\cdot1+0.5\\cdot\\frac1{36}} = \\dfrac{36}{37} \\approx 0.973$."],"hints":["How likely is the evidence \"two sixes\" under each die?","Both dice are equally likely a priori.","Apply Bayes’ theorem."],"concept":"Posterior updating with repeated independent evidence.","misconception":"Using one roll rather than both.","why":"Each additional six multiplies the odds of the loaded die by 6.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-18","topic":"bayes","sub":"impossible evidence","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A switch is either a standard one (equally likely to be \"up\" or \"down\" on each independent test) or a stuck one that always shows \"up\". One switch is chosen at random and tested three times, showing up, up, down. What is the probability that the switch is the <b>standard</b> one?","data":"","answer":{"value":1,"display":"1"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"1","correct":true,"why":"","v":1},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior; the final \"down\" is impossible for the stuck switch.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.125","correct":false,"why":"This is the likelihood of the observed sequence under the standard switch, not a posterior.","v":0.125},{"text":"0","correct":false,"why":"The stuck switch can never show \"down\", so the observation rules it out entirely.","v":0}],"solution":["$P(\\text{up, up, down}\\mid\\text{stuck}) = 0$, since it never shows \"down\".","The observation can only come from the standard switch: posterior $= 1$."],"hints":["Can the stuck switch produce a \"down\"?","What is the likelihood of the observation under each hypothesis?","What does Bayes’ theorem give when one likelihood is zero?"],"concept":"Zero-likelihood evidence eliminates a hypothesis.","misconception":"Letting the prior dominate.","why":"The posterior of any hypothesis with zero likelihood is zero.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-20","topic":"bayes","sub":"three hypotheses","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fund has three analysts: a strong one who calls market direction correctly 90% of the time, an average one (60%) and a weak one (50%). One is chosen at random and makes a call, which turns out to be <b>correct</b>. What is the probability that the strong analyst made the call?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.45,"display":"0.45"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.45","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.45},{"text":"0.3333","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability of the strong analyst.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"0.9","correct":false,"why":"This is the strong analyst’s accuracy, not the posterior.","v":0.9},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"The weak analyst’s contribution (0.5) was left out of the total.","v":0.6}],"solution":["With equal priors the posterior is proportional to the likelihoods: $\\dfrac{0.9}{0.9+0.6+0.5} = \\dfrac{0.9}{2.0} = 0.45$."],"hints":["The three priors are equal, so they cancel.","Sum the three accuracies.","Divide the strong analyst’s accuracy by the sum."],"concept":"Posterior over three hypotheses.","misconception":"Omitting a hypothesis from the total.","why":"Equal priors make the posterior proportional to the likelihoods.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-21","topic":"bayes","sub":"host reveals information","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A prize is behind one of three doors, equally likely. You choose door 1. The host, who knows where the prize is and always opens a different door that does not hide the prize (choosing at random if he has a choice), opens door 3. What is the probability that the prize is behind <b>door 2</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6666666666666666,"display":"2/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"2/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6666666666666666},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This ignores how the host chose: if the prize were behind door 1 he could open either door, so opening door 3 is weaker evidence for door 1.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior for door 1 (or any door); the host’s action is informative.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"Door 3 being empty does not make door 2 certain; the prize may still be behind door 1.","v":1}],"solution":["$P(\\text{open 3}\\mid\\text{prize 1}) = \\tfrac12$ (random choice), $P(\\text{open 3}\\mid\\text{prize 2}) = 1$ (forced), $P(\\text{open 3}\\mid\\text{prize 3}) = 0$.","$P(\\text{prize 2}\\mid\\text{open 3}) = \\dfrac{\\frac13\\cdot1}{\\frac13\\cdot\\frac12+\\frac13\\cdot1} = \\dfrac23$."],"hints":["What is the probability that the host opens door 3 under each prize location?","He cannot open your door or the prize door.","Apply Bayes’ theorem with equal priors."],"concept":"Posterior depends on the mechanism that produced the evidence.","misconception":"Treating the opened door as a random observation.","why":"When the prize is behind door 2 the host’s action is forced; when behind door 1 it is a coin toss.","fam":"bayes:information"},{"id":"BAY-22","topic":"bayes","sub":"many hypotheses","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Eight machines are numbered $1,\\dots,8$; machine $i$ produces a defective item with probability $i/20$. One machine is chosen at random and produces a defective item. What is the probability that it was machine <b>8</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2222222222222222,"display":"2/9"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"2/9","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.2222222222222222},{"text":"1/8","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability of machine 8.","v":0.125},{"text":"2/5","correct":false,"why":"This is machine 8’s defect rate, not the posterior.","v":0.4},{"text":"2/7","correct":false,"why":"The total weight is $1+\\dots+8 = 36$, not 28.","v":0.2857142857142857}],"solution":["With equal priors the posterior is proportional to $i/20$, so $P(8\\mid D) = \\dfrac{8}{1+2+\\cdots+8} = \\dfrac{8}{36} = \\dfrac29 \\approx 0.222$."],"hints":["The priors are equal, so they cancel in Bayes’ theorem.","Sum the likelihoods $i/20$ over the eight machines.","Divide machine 8’s likelihood by the sum."],"concept":"Posterior over many equally likely hypotheses.","misconception":"Misadding the series.","why":"The posterior is proportional to the likelihood when the priors are equal.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-23","topic":"bayes","sub":"screening with a prior","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"An auditor's initial suspicion that a filing is fraudulent is 30%. Red flags appear in 60% of fraudulent filings and in 10% of honest ones. A filing shows <b>red flags</b>. What is the probability that it is fraudulent?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.72,"display":"0.72"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.72","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.72},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of red flags given fraud, the reverse conditional.","v":0.6},{"text":"0.3","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior suspicion, which the red flags should increase.","v":0.3},{"text":"0.18","correct":false,"why":"This is the joint probability of fraud and red flags; divide by $P(\\text{flags})$.","v":0.18}],"solution":["$P(\\text{flags}) = 0.3(0.6)+0.7(0.1) = 0.18+0.07 = 0.25$.","$P(\\text{fraud}\\mid\\text{flags}) = 0.18/0.25 = 0.72$."],"hints":["Compute the total probability of red flags.","Then the joint of fraud and red flags.","Divide the joint probability by the total probability of the evidence."],"concept":"Bayes with a subjective prior.","misconception":"Confusing the likelihood with the posterior.","why":"Red flags are six times as likely under fraud, but the lower prior tempers the update.","fam":"bayes:testing"},{"id":"BAY-25","topic":"bayes","sub":"sequential evidence","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A candidate is eventually hired with probability 0.5. If hired, a call arrives on Monday with probability 0.3 and, independently, on Tuesday with probability 0.4. If not hired, calls (to say so) arrive with probability 0.1 on Monday and 0.2 on Tuesday, independently. <b>No call has arrived by the end of Tuesday.</b> What is the probability that the candidate is hired?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.36842105263157887,"display":"0.3684"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3684","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.36842105263157887},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior; the absence of calls is evidence against being hired.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.42","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of no call given hired, a likelihood.","v":0.42},{"text":"0.4375","correct":false,"why":"This uses only Monday’s silence; Tuesday’s silence is also observed.","v":0.43749999999999994}],"solution":["Likelihood of no call by Tuesday: hired $0.7\\times0.6 = 0.42$; not hired $0.9\\times0.8 = 0.72$.","$P(\\text{hired}\\mid\\text{no call}) = \\dfrac{0.5(0.42)}{0.5(0.42)+0.5(0.72)} = \\dfrac{0.42}{1.14} \\approx 0.368$."],"hints":["Evidence over two days is conditionally independent given the state.","Multiply the no-call probabilities for each state.","Apply Bayes’ theorem to the combined evidence."],"concept":"Updating on a sequence of conditionally independent observations.","misconception":"Updating with only one day’s evidence.","why":"Silence on both days is less likely if hired than if not.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-26","topic":"bayes","sub":"recessive trait","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A recessive condition shows only in people with two copies ($aa$) of a gene. Anna shows the dominant trait and her brother has the condition ($aa$); their parents both show the dominant trait. Anna marries a man who is a carrier ($Aa$) with probability $\\tfrac1{50}$ and otherwise has no copy of $a$. What is the probability that their first child has the condition?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.003333333333333333,"display":"0.00333"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.00333","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.003333333333333333},{"text":"0.005","correct":false,"why":"This assumes Anna is a carrier for sure; she is a carrier only with probability $2/3$ (her parents are both $Aa$, and she is not $aa$).","v":0.005},{"text":"0.1667","correct":false,"why":"This assumes the husband is certainly a carrier; he is one with probability $1/50$.","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"0.0025","correct":false,"why":"Given that Anna shows the dominant trait, $P(Aa) = 2/3$, not $1/2$.","v":0.0025}],"solution":["Both parents must be $Aa$ (their son is $aa$). Given Anna is not $aa$, $P(Aa) = \\dfrac{2/4}{3/4} = \\dfrac23$.","The child is $aa$ only if both parents are carriers and both pass on $a$: $\\dfrac23\\cdot\\dfrac1{50}\\cdot\\dfrac14 = \\dfrac{1}{300} \\approx 0.0033$."],"hints":["What genotypes can Anna’s parents have?","Given Anna is not $aa$, find the probability she is $Aa$.","A child needs $a$ from each parent: each carrier passes it with probability $1/2$."],"concept":"Conditional probability in a genetic setting.","misconception":"Using the unconditional probability of Anna’s genotype.","why":"Her phenotype removes the $aa$ case, leaving $Aa$ and $AA$ in ratio 2:1.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-27","topic":"bayes","sub":"agreement of two judges","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two referees independently classify a paper correctly with probability 0.7 each (a wrong classification is the opposite verdict). Given that the two referees <b>agree</b> with each other, what is the probability that their common verdict is <b>correct</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8448275862068965,"display":"0.8448"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8448","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.8448275862068965},{"text":"0.7","correct":false,"why":"This is the accuracy of one referee; agreement of two is stronger evidence.","v":0.7},{"text":"0.49","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that both are correct, not divided by the probability of agreement.","v":0.49},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"Agreement can occur in two ways (both right, both wrong), which are not equally likely.","v":0.5}],"solution":["Agree and correct: $0.7^2 = 0.49$. Agree and wrong: $0.3^2 = 0.09$.","$P(\\text{correct}\\mid\\text{agree}) = \\dfrac{0.49}{0.58} \\approx 0.845$."],"hints":["In how many ways can the two referees agree?","Compute the probability of each way.","Divide the correct case by the total probability of agreement."],"concept":"Posterior given agreement.","misconception":"Taking the single-referee accuracy as the answer.","why":"Two independent signals that agree are more reliable than either alone.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-31","topic":"bayes","sub":"noisy message","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"An event is true with probability 0.6. A sensor reports it correctly with probability 0.7; otherwise it reports \"true\" or \"false\" with equal probability. The sensor reports <b>\"true\"</b>. What is the probability that the event is in fact true?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.894736842105263,"display":"0.8947"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8947","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.894736842105263},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability that the event is true.","v":0.6},{"text":"0.7","correct":false,"why":"This is the accuracy of the reporting rule, but the random report can also say \"true\" for a false event.","v":0.7},{"text":"0.85","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(\\text{report true}\\mid\\text{true})$, a likelihood, not the posterior.","v":0.85}],"solution":["$P(\\text{report true}\\mid\\text{true}) = 0.7+0.3(0.5) = 0.85$; $P(\\text{report true}\\mid\\text{false}) = 0.3(0.5) = 0.15$.","$P(\\text{true}\\mid\\text{report true}) = \\dfrac{0.6(0.85)}{0.6(0.85)+0.4(0.15)} = \\dfrac{0.51}{0.57} \\approx 0.895$."],"hints":["Find the probability that the sensor says \"true\" in each state.","Include the case in which the sensor guesses.","Apply Bayes’ theorem with prior 0.6."],"concept":"Posterior through a noisy channel.","misconception":"Ignoring guessing.","why":"The reported value is a mixture of a correct report and a random report.","fam":"bayes:testing"},{"id":"BAY-32","topic":"bayes","sub":"common cause","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two parts come from the same batch. The batch is of good quality with probability 0.7 and then each part is defective independently with probability 0.02; otherwise (probability 0.3) each part is defective independently with probability 0.15. The first part is found to be <b>defective</b>. What is the probability that the second part is also defective?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.11915254237288134,"display":"0.1192"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1192","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.11915254237288134},{"text":"0.059","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that the second part is defective; the first part’s defect is informative about the batch.","v":0.059},{"text":"0.0225","correct":false,"why":"This treats the batch as certainly poor.","v":0.0225},{"text":"0.15","correct":false,"why":"This is the defect rate for a poor batch, but the batch might be good.","v":0.15}],"solution":["$P(\\text{poor}\\mid D_1) = \\dfrac{0.3(0.15)}{0.3(0.15)+0.7(0.02)} = \\dfrac{0.045}{0.059} \\approx 0.763$.","$P(D_2\\mid D_1) = 0.763(0.15)+0.237(0.02) \\approx 0.119$."],"hints":["The parts are independent only given the batch quality.","Update the probability that the batch is poor after the first defect.","Use total probability for the second part with the updated weights."],"concept":"Conditional independence and updating through a common cause.","misconception":"Treating the two parts as independent unconditionally.","why":"The first defect is evidence for a poor batch, raising the second part’s defect probability.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-33","topic":"bayes","sub":"partition and posterior","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A subscription service has monthly (50%), annual (30%) and family (20%) plans. Renewal rates are 0.6, 0.8 and 0.9 respectively. A subscriber who <b>did not renew</b> is chosen. What is the probability that this subscriber was on the <b>monthly</b> plan?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7142857142857143,"display":"0.7143"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.7143","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.7142857142857143},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior share of monthly subscribers.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.4","correct":false,"why":"This is the non-renewal rate of monthly subscribers.","v":0.4},{"text":"0.4167","correct":false,"why":"This is the share of monthly plans among those who <i>did</i> renew.","v":0.41666666666666663}],"solution":["Non-renewal rates: $0.4, 0.2, 0.1$.","$P(\\text{non-renewal}) = 0.5(0.4)+0.3(0.2)+0.2(0.1) = 0.28$.","$P(\\text{monthly}\\mid\\text{non-renewal}) = 0.20/0.28 \\approx 0.714$."],"hints":["Use non-renewal probabilities, not renewal probabilities.","Weight by the plan shares.","Divide the monthly term by the total."],"concept":"Posterior based on a complementary event.","misconception":"Using renewal rates.","why":"The evidence is non-renewal, so the likelihoods are complementary.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-34","topic":"bayes","sub":"rare conditions","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"One transaction in 20,000 is fraudulent. A screening rule flags 98% of fraudulent transactions and 2% of legitimate ones. A transaction is flagged. What is the probability that it is <b>fraudulent</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.0024441340782122905,"display":"0.00244"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.00244","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.0024441340782122905},{"text":"0.98","correct":false,"why":"This is the detection rate, $P(\\text{flag}\\mid\\text{fraud})$, not the posterior.","v":0.98},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"The base rate is tiny, so most flags are false alarms.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.02","correct":false,"why":"This is the false-flag rate for legitimate transactions.","v":0.02}],"solution":["$P(\\text{flag}) = 0.00005(0.98)+0.99995(0.02) = 0.000049+0.019999 = 0.020048$.","$P(\\text{fraud}\\mid\\text{flag}) = 0.000049/0.020048 \\approx 0.0024$.","About 1 flagged transaction in 400 is fraudulent."],"hints":["Think of 20,000 transactions: how many are fraudulent, and how many are flagged in total?","False alarms come from the 19,999 legitimate ones.","Compare true flags to all flags."],"concept":"Base-rate fallacy in screening.","misconception":"Equating sensitivity with the posterior.","why":"Even a small false-positive rate produces many false alarms when the condition is rare.","fam":"bayes:testing"},{"id":"BAY-36","topic":"bayes","sub":"predictive probability","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A box holds five strategies whose success probabilities are $0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9$. One strategy is chosen at random and tried once: it <b>succeeds</b>. If the same strategy is tried again, what is the probability that it succeeds again?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6599999999999999,"display":"0.66"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.66","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6599999999999999},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional success probability; the first success is informative about which strategy was chosen.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.9","correct":false,"why":"This assumes the best strategy was chosen.","v":0.9},{"text":"0.7","correct":false,"why":"This is the posterior weight of the 0.7 strategy; the predictive probability is a weighted average of all five.","v":0.7}],"solution":["Posterior weights are proportional to $p_i$: $0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9$ (sum 2.5).","$P(\\text{second success}\\mid\\text{first success}) = \\dfrac{\\sum p_i^2}{\\sum p_i} = \\dfrac{1.65}{2.5} = 0.66$."],"hints":["After one success, the weights of the strategies are proportional to $p_i$.","Average the success probability with those weights.","The numerator is $\\sum p_i^2$."],"concept":"Predictive probability after updating.","misconception":"Using the prior mean 0.5.","why":"A success shifts weight towards high-probability strategies.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-39","topic":"bayes","sub":"Poisson likelihoods","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A bank branch receives complaints at a Poisson rate of 6 per month. A new training programme reduces the rate to 4 per month in 60% of branches and has no effect in the other 40%. A branch that completed the programme has <b>3 complaints</b> in a month. What is the probability that the programme was effective for this branch?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7665746545193762,"display":"0.7666"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.7666","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.7665746545193762},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability that the programme works.","v":0.6},{"text":"0.1954","correct":false,"why":"This is the Poisson probability of 3 complaints when the rate is 4: a likelihood.","v":0.19536681481316456},{"text":"0.6865","correct":false,"why":"This uses equal priors for the two rates; the prior probability that the programme works is 0.6.","v":0.6864564835986605}],"solution":["$P(3\\mid\\lambda=4) = e^{-4}4^3/3! = 0.1954$; $P(3\\mid\\lambda=6) = e^{-6}6^3/3! = 0.0892$.","$P(\\text{effective}\\mid3) = \\dfrac{0.6(0.1954)}{0.6(0.1954)+0.4(0.0892)} = \\dfrac{0.1172}{0.1529} \\approx 0.767$."],"hints":["What is the probability of exactly 3 complaints at each rate?","Weight by 0.6 and 0.4.","Divide the effective term by the sum."],"concept":"Posterior with discrete Poisson likelihoods.","misconception":"Using the prior.","why":"A count of 3 is more probable when the rate is 4.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-41","topic":"bayes","sub":"random number of trials","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A buyer rolls a fair die and then inspects that many items from a delivery (as many as the die shows). Each item is defective independently with probability 0.1. What is the probability that <b>no inspected item</b> is defective?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7028385,"display":"0.7028"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.7028","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.7028385},{"text":"0.6916","correct":false,"why":"This uses the average number of items (3.5) as if it were fixed; the probability must be averaged over the die outcomes.","v":0.6915901242788246},{"text":"0.9","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability for a single item only.","v":0.9},{"text":"0.4686","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least one item is defective when 6 items are inspected.","v":0.46855899999999995}],"solution":["Condition on the die: $P(\\text{none}\\mid n) = 0.9^n$.","$P = \\tfrac16(0.9+0.81+0.729+0.6561+0.5905+0.5314) = \\dfrac{4.2170}{6} \\approx 0.703$."],"hints":["Condition on the number of items inspected.","Each case has probability $0.9^n$.","Average over the six equally likely values of $n$."],"concept":"Total probability with a random sample size.","misconception":"Plugging in the mean sample size.","why":"The probability is an average of a convex function, not the function at the mean.","fam":"bayes:total-probability"},{"id":"BAY-44","topic":"bayes","sub":"transfer between purses","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Purse 1 holds 5 ten-pound notes and 3 twenty-pound notes; purse 2 holds 2 ten-pound and 6 twenty-pound notes. One note is moved at random from purse 1 to purse 2, then a note is drawn at random from purse 2. What is the probability that the note drawn is a <b>twenty-pound</b> note?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7083333333333333,"display":"17/24"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"17/24","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.7083333333333333},{"text":"3/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability for purse 2 before any note is added.","v":0.75},{"text":"3/8","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the moved note was a twenty.","v":0.375},{"text":"51/64","correct":false,"why":"After the transfer purse 2 holds 9 notes, so the denominators are 9.","v":0.796875}],"solution":["Moved a ten (prob $5/8$): purse 2 has 2 tens, 6 twenties, 1 more ten: 9 notes with 6 twenties, so $P(20) = 6/9$.","Moved a twenty ($3/8$): 7 twenties of 9.","$P = \\dfrac58\\cdot\\dfrac69+\\dfrac38\\cdot\\dfrac79 = \\dfrac{51}{72} = \\dfrac{17}{24} \\approx 0.708$."],"hints":["Condition on the denomination moved.","Update the composition of purse 2.","Combine with the law of total probability."],"concept":"Total probability after a transfer.","misconception":"Using the original composition.","why":"The moved note changes the numerator and denominator of purse 2’s proportion.","fam":"bayes:information"},{"id":"BAY-46","topic":"bayes","sub":"rare event screening in counts","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"0.1% of accounts are used for illicit transfers. A monitoring rule flags 95% of illicit accounts and 2% of legitimate accounts. Out of every <b>1,000 flagged accounts</b>, about how many are actually illicit?","data":"","answer":{"value":45.38939321548017,"display":"45.3894"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"45.3894","correct":true,"why":"","v":45.38939321548017},{"text":"950","correct":false,"why":"This confuses the detection rate (95% of illicit accounts flagged) with the share of flagged accounts that are illicit.","v":950},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of illicit accounts per 1,000 accounts overall, not per 1,000 flagged.","v":1},{"text":"20","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of legitimate accounts flagged per 1,000 legitimate accounts.","v":20}],"solution":["Take 1,000,000 accounts: 1,000 illicit, 950 flagged; 999,000 legitimate, $0.02\\times999\\,000 = 19\\,980$ flagged.","Flagged total: $20\\,930$; illicit share $950/20\\,930 \\approx 4.5\\%$.","Per 1,000 flagged: about $45$."],"hints":["Use a large imaginary population and compute counts.","How many legitimate accounts are flagged?","Take the ratio of flagged illicit to all flagged and scale to 1,000."],"concept":"Natural-frequency version of Bayes’ theorem.","misconception":"Reading detection rate as precision.","why":"False flags from the huge legitimate group swamp the true detections.","fam":"bayes:testing"},{"id":"BAY-48","topic":"bayes","sub":"relative rates","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"30% of drivers in a portfolio are novices, 50% are intermediate and 20% are experienced. Accident rates of novice and intermediate drivers are four and two times that of experienced drivers. A driver who had an accident is chosen. What is the probability that the driver is <b>experienced</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.08333333333333333,"display":"0.0833"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0833","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.08333333333333333},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior share of experienced drivers; they have fewer accidents.","v":0.2},{"text":"0.1429","correct":false,"why":"This is the ratio of the experienced relative rate to the sum of the three relative rates, ignoring the portfolio shares.","v":0.14285714285714285},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is a ratio of relative rates that ignores the shares of drivers.","v":0.25}],"solution":["With relative rates 4, 2, 1 and shares 0.3, 0.5, 0.2: weights $1.2, 1.0, 0.2$ (sum 2.4).","$P(\\text{experienced}\\mid\\text{accident}) = 0.2/2.4 \\approx 0.083$."],"hints":["Assign the experienced group rate 1 and the others 4 and 2.","Multiply by shares.","Divide the experienced weight by the total."],"concept":"Bayes with relative rates.","misconception":"Ignoring group sizes.","why":"Only ratios of accident rates matter.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-50","topic":"bayes","sub":"sampling evidence","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A shipment comes from supplier $A$ (35% of shipments, 4% of units defective) or supplier $B$ (65% of shipments, 1.5% defective). An inspector tests 30 units from one shipment and finds <b>3 defective</b>. What is the probability that the shipment came from supplier $A$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.836076864416994,"display":"0.8361"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8361","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.836076864416994},{"text":"0.35","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability that the shipment is from supplier $A$; three defectives in 30 are more likely from $A$.","v":0.35},{"text":"0.04","correct":false,"why":"This is supplier $A$’s defect rate.","v":0.04},{"text":"0.5895","correct":false,"why":"This uses only the defect rate as evidence and ignores the observed count of 3 in 30.","v":0.5894736842105263}],"solution":["The binomial coefficient cancels. $A$: $0.35(0.04)^3(0.96)^{27} = 0.35\\times6.4\\times10^{-5}\\times0.3324 = 7.44\\times10^{-6}$.","$B$: $0.65(0.015)^3(0.985)^{27} = 0.65\\times3.375\\times10^{-6}\\times0.6645 = 1.46\\times10^{-6}$.","$P(A\\mid3) = \\dfrac{7.44}{7.44+1.46} \\approx 0.836$."],"hints":["Find the probability of 3 defectives in 30 under each supplier.","The binomial coefficient is common to both and cancels.","Weight by 0.35 and 0.65 and normalise."],"concept":"Bayes with binomial likelihoods.","misconception":"Using only the defect rate.","why":"The sample count carries much more information than the prior.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-51","topic":"bayes","sub":"diagnose: transposed conditional","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A screening test detects a condition in 90% of people who have it and gives a false alarm for 10% of people who do not. Only 1% of the population has the condition. A news article says: \"If you test positive, there is a 90% chance you have it.\" What is the error?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"It treats $P(\\text{positive}\\mid\\text{condition}) = 0.9$ as if it were $P(\\text{condition}\\mid\\text{positive})$; with a 1% base rate the latter is only about 8%"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"It treats $P(\\text{positive}\\mid\\text{condition}) = 0.9$ as if it were $P(\\text{condition}\\mid\\text{positive})$; with a 1% base rate the latter is only about 8%","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Nothing: a test with 90% sensitivity gives a 90% chance of the condition after a positive result","correct":false,"why":"Sensitivity is the probability of a positive result <i>given</i> the condition, not of the condition given a positive result."},{"text":"The article should have used the false-alarm rate: 10%","correct":false,"why":"The false-alarm rate is a different conditional probability and is also not the answer."},{"text":"The article is right because the test is accurate in both directions","correct":false,"why":"Accuracy in one direction does not carry over: it depends on the base rate."}],"solution":["Per 1000 people: 10 have the condition, 9 test positive; 990 do not, about 99 test positive.","$P(\\text{condition}\\mid+) = \\dfrac{9}{9+99} \\approx 0.083$.","The article transposed the conditional."],"hints":["Which probability does \"detects the condition in 90%\" describe: positive given condition, or condition given positive?","Consider 1000 people and count true and false positives.","Apply Bayes’ theorem with the 1% prior."],"concept":"Reversing a conditional probability requires the prior (Bayes).","misconception":"The base-rate fallacy.","why":"The 99 false positives from the healthy majority dwarf the 9 true positives.","fam":"bayes:diagnose"},{"id":"BAY-52","topic":"bayes","sub":"diagnose: ignoring prior proportions","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Plant A produces 10% of a firm’s output with 5% defective; plant B produces 90% with 1% defective. A defective item is found. A student argues: \"A is five times more defective than B, so $P(A\\mid\\text{defective}) = 5/(5+1) = 0.83$.\" What is the correct probability that the item came from plant A?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.35714285714285715,"display":"0.3571"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.002,"options":[{"text":"0.3571","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.35714285714285715},{"text":"0.8333","correct":false,"why":"This uses only the likelihood ratio and ignores that plant B produces nine times as many items.","v":0.8333333333333334},{"text":"0.1","correct":false,"why":"This is the prior probability of plant A; its higher defect rate raises it.","v":0.1},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"This is the defect rate of plant A, $P(\\text{defective}\\mid A)$.","v":0.05}],"solution":["$P(A\\cap D) = 0.1(0.05) = 0.005$; $P(B\\cap D) = 0.9(0.01) = 0.009$.","$P(A\\mid D) = 0.005/0.014 \\approx 0.357$.","Posterior odds = prior odds $(1:9)$ $\\times$ likelihood ratio $(5:1) = 5:9$."],"hints":["The student has accounted for the defect rates but not for something else. What?","Compare the number of defective items from each plant per 1000 items produced.","Posterior odds = prior odds $\\times$ likelihood ratio."],"concept":"Bayes’ theorem needs both priors and likelihoods.","misconception":"Using the likelihood ratio as if it were the posterior.","why":"Plant B makes up for its lower defect rate with sheer volume.","fam":"bayes:diagnose"},{"id":"BAY-53","topic":"bayes","sub":"interpretation: reversing a survey statistic","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"In a labour-force survey, 80% of the unemployed respondents are under 35. A commentator concludes: \"So 80% of workers under 35 are unemployed.\" Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"The commentator transposed the conditional: $P(\\text{under 35}\\mid\\text{unemployed}) = 0.8$ says nothing about $P(\\text{unemployed}\\mid\\text{under 35})$ without the base rates"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"The commentator transposed the conditional: $P(\\text{under 35}\\mid\\text{unemployed}) = 0.8$ says nothing about $P(\\text{unemployed}\\mid\\text{under 35})$ without the base rates","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The conclusion is correct because the same people are being described","correct":false,"why":"The two statements have different denominators (the unemployed versus the under-35s)."},{"text":"The conclusion is wrong, and the true figure must be 20%","correct":false,"why":"There is no way to derive 20% from the information."},{"text":"The conclusion is correct if the survey is random","correct":false,"why":"Random sampling does not turn one conditional probability into its reverse."}],"solution":["$P(\\text{U35}\\mid\\text{Unemp}) = 0.8$ and $P(\\text{Unemp}\\mid\\text{U35}) = \\dfrac{P(\\text{U35}\\mid\\text{Unemp})\\,P(\\text{Unemp})}{P(\\text{U35})}$.","If, say, 7% of workers are unemployed and 40% are under 35, the unemployment rate among the under-35s is $0.8\\times0.07/0.40 = 14\\%$."],"hints":["Which group forms the denominator of each percentage?","Write both statements as conditional probabilities.","The two are related by Bayes’ formula using $P(\\text{Unemp})$ and $P(\\text{U35})$."],"concept":"Conditional probability is not symmetric (prosecutor’s fallacy in surveys).","misconception":"Transposing a conditional.","why":"The unemployed are a small group, so most under-35s are employed even if most unemployed are young.","fam":"bayes:diagnose"},{"id":"BAY-54","topic":"bayes","sub":"diagnose: simple vs weighted average","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"An online shop gets 90% of its visitors from mobile, where 2% buy, and 10% from desktop, where 8% buy. A student computes the overall conversion rate as $(2\\% + 8\\%)/2 = 5\\%$. What is the correct overall rate?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.026000000000000002,"display":"0.026"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.026","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.026000000000000002},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"The simple average treats the two channels as equally important; the overall rate weights each by its share of visitors.","v":0.05},{"text":"0.08","correct":false,"why":"This is the desktop rate only.","v":0.08},{"text":"0.02","correct":false,"why":"This is the mobile rate only; desktop visitors also contribute.","v":0.02}],"solution":["By the law of total probability: $P(\\text{buy}) = 0.9(0.02)+0.1(0.08) = 0.018+0.008 = 0.026$, i.e. 2.6%."],"hints":["Are the two groups of visitors of equal size?","The overall rate is an average of group rates weighted by group sizes.","$0.9\\times0.02+0.1\\times0.08$."],"concept":"Law of total probability: weighted average of conditional probabilities.","misconception":"Using an unweighted average.","why":"The result must lie between 2% and 8%, close to 2% because mobile dominates.","fam":"bayes:diagnose"},{"id":"BAY-55","topic":"bayes","sub":"natural frequencies","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Out of every 1000 employees screened for a rare skill gap, 20 truly have it. The screen flags 18 of those 20 and also flags 49 of the 980 who do not. Among employees who are flagged, what proportion truly have the gap?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.26865671641791045,"display":"0.2687"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Flagged in total: $18+49 = 67$.","Of these 18 truly have the gap: $18/67 \\approx 0.269$."],"hints":["Count everyone who is flagged, true or false.","How many of the flagged truly have the gap?","Divide the second count by the first."],"concept":"Posterior from counts of flagged people.","misconception":"Using 18/20 as the answer.","why":"Most flagged people are false positives because the gap is rare.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-56","topic":"bayes","sub":"odds form","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Before reviewing a transaction, an auditor puts the chance it is fraudulent at 1%. A rule fires on 90% of fraudulent transactions and 6% of legitimate ones. Using odds, what is the probability that a transaction is fraudulent given the rule fires?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.13157894736842107,"display":"0.1316"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["Prior odds $=0.01/0.99 \\approx 0.0101$. Likelihood ratio $=0.9/0.06 = 15$.","Posterior odds $\\approx 0.1515$, so probability $=0.1515/1.1515 \\approx 0.1316$."],"hints":["Convert the prior probability to odds.","Multiply by the ratio of the two firing rates.","Convert the posterior odds back to a probability."],"concept":"Posterior odds equal prior odds times the likelihood ratio.","misconception":"Reporting the odds as a probability.","why":"A likelihood ratio of 15 only lifts a 1% prior to about 13%.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-57","topic":"bayes","sub":"sequential updating with two tests","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A supplier is \"weak\" with probability 0.10. A weak supplier misses a delivery date with probability 0.5, a good one with probability 0.1. Given the supplier misses two consecutive dates (independent given type), what is the probability that it is weak?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7352941176470588,"display":"0.7353"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Likelihood of two misses: weak $0.5^2 = 0.25$, good $0.1^2 = 0.01$.","Posterior $=0.1(0.25)/(0.1(0.25)+0.9(0.01)) = 0.025/0.034 \\approx 0.735$."],"hints":["Find the chance of two misses under each supplier type.","Misses are independent given the type, so multiply.","Apply Bayes’ rule with the 0.10 prior."],"concept":"Updating on two observations at once.","misconception":"Using a single miss probability.","why":"Repeated evidence multiplies the likelihood ratio.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-58","topic":"bayes","sub":"second look after a first update","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Using the supplier model above (weak with prior 0.10; miss probability 0.5 if weak, 0.1 if good), the supplier has just missed one date. What is the probability that it misses the <b>next</b> date as well?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.24285714285714285,"display":"0.2429"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["After one miss: $P(\\text{weak}) = 0.05/0.14 \\approx 0.357$.","Next miss: $0.357(0.5)+0.643(0.1) \\approx 0.243$."],"hints":["First update the probability of \"weak\" after one miss.","Use that as the new prior for the next date.","Combine with the law of total probability."],"concept":"Posterior predictive probability.","misconception":"Using the original 0.10 prior for the next date.","why":"The next-date probability mixes both types with updated weights.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-59","topic":"bayes","sub":"three hypotheses","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Orders come from Region A (50%), B (30%) or C (20%). Late-delivery rates are 4%, 10% and 12% respectively. A late order is selected. From which region is it most likely to have come?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Region B (posterior about 0.41)"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Region B (posterior about 0.41)","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Region A, because it has the largest share of orders","correct":false,"why":"A’s posterior is $0.02/0.07 \\approx 0.286$, less than B’s 0.429."},{"text":"Region C, because its late rate is highest","correct":false,"why":"C’s posterior is $0.03/0.07 \\approx 0.286$, below B."},{"text":"All regions are equally likely","correct":false,"why":"The prior shares and late rates differ, so the posteriors differ."}],"solution":["Joint (late and region): A $0.5(0.04)=0.02$, B $0.3(0.10)=0.03$, C $0.2(0.12)=0.024$; total $0.074$.","Posteriors: A 0.27, B 0.41, C 0.32."],"hints":["Multiply each prior by its late rate.","Normalise by the total.","Compare the three posteriors."],"concept":"Posterior over a three-way partition.","misconception":"Picking by prior share or by likelihood alone.","why":"Prior and likelihood both matter.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-60","topic":"bayes","sub":"false-positive paradox diagnostic","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A manager says: \"The credit model is 95% accurate in both directions, so a flagged applicant has a 95% chance of defaulting.\" Defaults are 2% of applicants. What is the flaw?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"It ignores the base rate: with 2% defaults, most flagged applicants are non-defaulters"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"It ignores the base rate: with 2% defaults, most flagged applicants are non-defaulters","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Nothing: 95% accuracy means 95% of flagged applicants default","correct":false,"why":"This confuses $P(\\text{flag}\\mid\\text{default})$ with $P(\\text{default}\\mid\\text{flag})$."},{"text":"Accuracy cannot be the same in both directions","correct":false,"why":"A model can have equal sensitivity and specificity; the error is elsewhere."},{"text":"The model should flag more applicants","correct":false,"why":"More flags would not change the base-rate issue."}],"solution":["Per 10 000 applicants: 200 defaulters, 190 flagged; 9 800 non-defaulters, 490 flagged.","Flagged who default: $190/680 \\approx 0.28$."],"hints":["Think about 10 000 applicants.","Count flagged defaulters and flagged non-defaulters.","Compare the two counts."],"concept":"Base-rate neglect.","misconception":"Confusing sensitivity with posterior probability.","why":"A small base rate dilutes the flagged group with false positives.","fam":"bayes:diagnose"},{"id":"BAY-61","topic":"bayes","sub":"screening threshold","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A screen detects 80% of true cases. The prevalence is 5%. What specificity (to 3 decimals) is needed so that 50% of positive results are true cases?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.9578947368421052,"display":"0.9579"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["For PPV 0.5, false positives must equal true positives: $0.95(1-\\text{spec}) = 0.05(0.8)=0.04$.","$1-\\text{spec} = 0.0421$, specificity $\\approx 0.958$."],"hints":["PPV of 0.5 means true positives equal false positives.","Write the true-positive rate across the whole population.","Solve for the false-positive rate among non-cases."],"concept":"Working backwards from a target positive predictive value.","misconception":"Setting specificity equal to 0.5.","why":"Rare conditions demand very high specificity.","fam":"bayes:testing"},{"id":"BAY-62","topic":"bayes","sub":"which factory produced it","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Plant 1 makes 70% of chips and has a defect rate of 2%; Plant 2 makes 30% with a defect rate of 5%. A defective chip is found. What is the probability it came from Plant 2?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5172413793103449,"display":"0.5172"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(\\text{defect}) = 0.014+0.015 = 0.029$.","$P(\\text{Plant 2}\\mid\\text{defect}) = 0.015/0.029 \\approx 0.517$."],"hints":["Find the overall defect rate first.","What part of it comes from Plant 2?","Divide the favourable count by the total."],"concept":"Basic Bayes with two sources.","misconception":"Answering 0.30.","why":"The higher defect rate lifts Plant 2 above its production share.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-63","topic":"bayes","sub":"inverse probability from a table","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Of 400 survey respondents, 160 are renters. 56 renters and 36 non-renters said they plan to switch banks. Given that someone plans to switch, what is the probability they are a renter?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6086956521739131,"display":"0.6087"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Plan to switch: $56+36 = 92$.","Renters among them: $56/92 \\approx 0.609$."],"hints":["How many respondents plan to switch in total?","How many of them rent?","Divide the second by the first."],"concept":"Reversing the conditioning in a table.","misconception":"Using 56/160.","why":"56/160 is the switching rate among renters, not the share of switchers who rent.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-64","topic":"bayes","sub":"discrete prior on a coin","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A coin is one of three types with equal prior probability: $P(\\text{H})=0.3$, $0.5$ or $0.8$. It is tossed three times and shows 3 heads. What is the probability it is the $0.8$ coin?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7710843373493975,"display":"0.7711"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Likelihoods: $0.027, 0.125, 0.512$.","Posterior $=0.512/0.664 \\approx 0.771$."],"hints":["Compute the probability of three heads for each coin.","Equal priors cancel.","Divide the third likelihood by the sum."],"concept":"Posterior over a discrete parameter.","misconception":"Using the single-toss probability 0.8.","why":"Three heads in a row strongly favours the heads-heavy coin.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-65","topic":"bayes","sub":"randomised response","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"To ask a sensitive question, each respondent secretly tosses a fair coin: heads means answer \"Yes\" regardless; tails means answer truthfully. 62% of responses are \"Yes\". Estimate the proportion who truly have the trait.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.24,"display":"0.24"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.01,"options":[],"solution":["$P(\\text{Yes}) = 0.5 + 0.5p = 0.62$.","$p = 0.24$."],"hints":["Write $P(\\text{Yes})$ by conditioning on the coin.","Heads gives Yes for sure.","Solve $0.5+0.5p = 0.62$."],"concept":"Inverting total probability to estimate a hidden proportion.","misconception":"Reporting 0.62 directly.","why":"Half of the Yes answers are noise from the coin.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-66","topic":"bayes","sub":"two independent alarms","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A server is compromised with probability 0.02. Alarm X fires with probability 0.7 if compromised and 0.05 otherwise; Alarm Y fires with probability 0.6 if compromised and 0.1 otherwise (independent given the state). Both alarms fire. What is the probability the server is compromised?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.631578947368421,"display":"0.6316"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Both fire: compromised $0.7(0.6)=0.42$, clean $0.05(0.1)=0.005$.","Posterior $=0.0084/(0.0084+0.0049) \\approx 0.632$."],"hints":["Multiply the two firing rates within each state.","Weight by the priors.","Normalise by the sum of the weighted terms."],"concept":"Combining independent pieces of evidence.","misconception":"Averaging the two alarm rates.","why":"Given the state, the alarms multiply.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-67","topic":"bayes","sub":"one alarm fires, other silent","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Using the alarms from the previous setting (compromised prior 0.02; X fires with 0.7 vs 0.05; Y fires with 0.6 vs 0.1), Alarm X fires but Alarm Y stays <b>silent</b>. What is the probability the server is compromised?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.11267605633802817,"display":"0.1127"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["Compromised: $0.7(0.4)=0.28$; clean: $0.05(0.9)=0.045$.","Posterior $=0.0056/(0.0056+0.0441) \\approx 0.113$."],"hints":["A silent alarm uses the complement of its firing rate.","Multiply within each state.","Apply Bayes with the prior."],"concept":"Negative evidence matters too.","misconception":"Ignoring the silent alarm.","why":"A silent alarm lowers the posterior relative to X-only evidence.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-68","topic":"bayes","sub":"identifying the mistaken conditional","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A test has $P(\\text{positive}\\mid\\text{condition}) = 0.9$. Which statement correctly describes this number?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Among people with the condition, 90% test positive"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Among people with the condition, 90% test positive","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Among people who test positive, 90% have the condition","correct":false,"why":"This reverses the conditioning; that quantity is the positive predictive value."},{"text":"90% of the population has the condition","correct":false,"why":"It is not a population share."},{"text":"The test is correct 90% of the time overall","correct":false,"why":"Overall accuracy also depends on the specificity and prevalence."}],"solution":["$P(A\\mid B)$ restricts attention to cases where $B$ holds. Here that is people with the condition."],"hints":["Which group is conditioned on?","Read the bar as \"among those who\".","Match with the options."],"concept":"Reading conditional probability notation.","misconception":"Transposing the conditional.","why":"The group named after the bar is the group being described.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-69","topic":"bayes","sub":"prosecution-style evidence","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A record matches a suspect on a feature shared by 1 in 5 000 people. The pool of equally plausible people is 20 000, and exactly one is the source. Assuming a source always matches, what is the probability the matched suspect is the source?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.20000800032001279,"display":"0.2"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["Expected other matches $\\approx 19999/5000 \\approx 4$.","Posterior $=1/(1+4.0) \\approx 0.20$."],"hints":["Estimate how many innocent people also match.","The source is one of the matched group.","Divide 1 by the size of that group."],"concept":"Match probability is not the posterior.","misconception":"Answering 0.9998.","why":"A rare feature still matches several people in a big pool.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-70","topic":"bayes","sub":"which urn after two draws","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Urn 1 has 3 red and 2 blue balls; Urn 2 has 1 red and 4 blue. An urn is chosen at random and two balls are drawn without replacement: both are red. What is the probability the urn was Urn 1?","data":"","answer":{"value":1,"display":"1"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Urn 2 has only one red ball, so two reds are impossible.","The posterior for Urn 1 is 1."],"hints":["Can Urn 2 supply two red balls?","If not, what is its likelihood?","What follows for the posterior?"],"concept":"Zero likelihood eliminates a hypothesis.","misconception":"Using the prior 0.5.","why":"The evidence is impossible under Urn 2.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-71","topic":"bayes","sub":"sampling for quality","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A lot is \"good\" (2% defective) with probability 0.8 or \"bad\" (10% defective) with probability 0.2. Two items are inspected independently and both are defective. What is the probability the lot is bad?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8620689655172414,"display":"0.8621"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Likelihoods: bad $0.01$, good $0.0004$.","Posterior bad $=0.002/(0.002+0.00032) \\approx 0.862$."],"hints":["Compute the chance of two defectives under each lot type.","Weight by the prior.","Normalise by the sum of the weighted terms."],"concept":"Inference about batch quality.","misconception":"Adding the defect rates.","why":"Two defectives are much more probable from a bad lot.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-72","topic":"bayes","sub":"accuracy versus predictive value comparison","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Test P has sensitivity 0.99 and specificity 0.90. Test Q has sensitivity 0.80 and specificity 0.99. Prevalence is 1%. Which test has the higher probability of a true case given a positive result?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Test Q (PPV about 0.45 against about 0.09 for P)"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Test Q (PPV about 0.45 against about 0.09 for P)","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Test P, because its sensitivity is higher","correct":false,"why":"P’s PPV is $0.0099/(0.0099+0.099)\\approx0.091$."},{"text":"They are equal because prevalence is the same","correct":false,"why":"The error rates differ, so PPVs differ."},{"text":"Cannot be determined without the sample size","correct":false,"why":"PPV depends on sensitivity, specificity and prevalence only."}],"solution":["P: $0.0099/(0.0099+0.099) \\approx 0.091$.","Q: $0.008/(0.008+0.0099) \\approx 0.447$."],"hints":["Compute true and false positives for each test.","Compare the shares that are true.","Specificity matters more at low prevalence."],"concept":"PPV depends strongly on specificity when prevalence is low.","misconception":"Choosing by sensitivity.","why":"Most positives at low prevalence come from the healthy group.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-73","topic":"bayes","sub":"game-show style conditioning","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A prize is behind one of 4 doors. You pick door 1. The host, who knows where the prize is, opens one of the <b>other</b> doors that does not hold the prize (chosen at random among valid ones) and shows nothing. What is the probability the prize is behind a particular <b>other unopened</b> door?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.375,"display":"3/8"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["Prize behind your door: $1/4$. Behind one of the other three: $3/4$, shared equally among the two unopened ones: $3/8$ each."],"hints":["Your door keeps 1/4.","The other 3/4 is now concentrated on the unopened doors.","Split equally between the two remaining others."],"concept":"Host knowledge changes the posterior.","misconception":"Answering 1/3.","why":"Opening a door reveals information because the host avoids the prize.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-74","topic":"bayes","sub":"prior sensitivity","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A signal appears with probability 0.8 when a stock is \"up-trending\" and 0.3 otherwise. How large must the prior probability of up-trending be for the posterior after seeing the signal to equal 0.5?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2727272727272727,"display":"0.2727"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["Need $p(0.8) = (1-p)(0.3)$.","$p = 0.3/1.1 \\approx 0.273$."],"hints":["Posterior 0.5 means the two joint terms are equal.","Write the equation: prior times 0.8 equals complement times 0.3.","Solve the linear equation for the prior probability."],"concept":"Solving for the prior that gives a target posterior.","misconception":"Setting the prior to 0.5.","why":"Equal joint terms give a 50% posterior.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-75","topic":"bayes","sub":"insurance claim risk class","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Drivers are low-risk (60%), medium (30%) or high-risk (10%), with annual claim probabilities 0.05, 0.15 and 0.40. A driver files a claim this year. What is the probability the driver is high-risk?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3478260869565218,"display":"0.3478"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Joint: $0.03, 0.045, 0.04$; total $0.115$.","High-risk: $0.04/0.115 \\approx 0.348$."],"hints":["Multiply each class share by its claim rate.","Sum for the total claim probability.","Take the high-risk share."],"concept":"Posterior over risk classes.","misconception":"Answering 0.40.","why":"High-risk drivers are rare but over-represented among claimants.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-76","topic":"bayes","sub":"which statement increases belief","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"When does observing event $E$ <b>increase</b> the probability of hypothesis $H$?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"When $P(E\\mid H) > P(E\\mid H^c)$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"When $P(E\\mid H) > P(E\\mid H^c)$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"When $P(E\\mid H)$ is large, whatever $P(E\\mid H^c)$ is","correct":false,"why":"If $E$ is just as likely without $H$, observing it changes nothing."},{"text":"When $P(H)$ is large","correct":false,"why":"A large prior does not by itself mean the evidence raises it."},{"text":"When $E$ is rare","correct":false,"why":"Rarity alone does not point toward $H$."}],"solution":["$P(H\\mid E) > P(H)$ exactly when $P(E\\mid H) > P(E)$, which is equivalent to $P(E\\mid H) > P(E\\mid H^c)$."],"hints":["Compare the evidence rates with and without $H$.","If they are equal, nothing changes.","Pick the option comparing them."],"concept":"Direction of a Bayesian update.","misconception":"Judging by $P(E\\mid H)$ alone.","why":"Evidence helps only if it is more likely under $H$ than under its complement.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-77","topic":"bayes","sub":"tree reading","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A tree has first branches \"Online order\" (0.65) and \"In-store order\" (0.35). Online orders are returned with probability 0.12; in-store with 0.04. Given an order was returned, what is the probability it was online?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8478260869565217,"display":"0.8478"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Returned: online $0.078$, in-store $0.014$.","Online share $=0.078/0.092 \\approx 0.848$."],"hints":["Multiply along each path ending in \"returned\".","Add the two paths.","Divide the online path by the total."],"concept":"Reading a reversed probability from a tree.","misconception":"Answering 0.65 or 0.12.","why":"Online orders dominate returns by both volume and rate.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-78","topic":"bayes","sub":"majority of tests","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A component is defective with probability 0.1. Each of 3 independent checks flags a defective one with probability 0.8 and a sound one with probability 0.15. Exactly 2 of the 3 checks flag the component. What is the probability that it is defective?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4264889629321117,"display":"0.4265"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["Likelihoods: defective $3(0.64)(0.2)=0.384$; sound $3(0.0225)(0.85)=0.0574$.","Weighted: $0.1(0.384)=0.0384$ and $0.9(0.0574)=0.0516$.","Posterior $=0.0384/0.09 \\approx 0.427$."],"hints":["Write the binomial probability of exactly 2 flags under each state.","Weight by the prior.","Normalise by the sum of the weighted terms."],"concept":"Binomial likelihood in a posterior.","misconception":"Omitting the number of arrangements.","why":"The counting factor cancels only if applied to both states.","fam":"bayes:testing"},{"id":"BAY-79","topic":"bayes","sub":"conditioning on a sum","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A die is chosen at random: a fair die, or a loaded die that shows 6 with probability 1/2 (other faces equally likely). It is rolled and shows 6. What is the probability that it is the loaded die?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.75,"display":"3/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["$P(6) = 0.25+1/12 = 1/3$.","Posterior $=0.25/(1/3) = 3/4$."],"hints":["Find the probability of a 6 under each die.","Weight by 1/2 each.","Divide the favourable count by the total."],"concept":"Posterior for a loaded die.","misconception":"Answering 1/2.","why":"A 6 is three times as likely under the loaded die.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-80","topic":"bayes","sub":"misreading a likelihood ratio","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A forecasting signal has likelihood ratio 4 in favour of a recession. A student says \"so a recession is now 4 times as likely, i.e. probability rises from 10% to 40%\". What is the correct reading?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"The <i>odds</i> are multiplied by 4: 1:9 becomes 4:9, a probability of about 31%"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"The <i>odds</i> are multiplied by 4: 1:9 becomes 4:9, a probability of about 31%","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The student is right: probability is multiplied by 4","correct":false,"why":"Only the odds are multiplied; probabilities saturate at 1."},{"text":"The probability becomes 4%","correct":false,"why":"A ratio above 1 raises, not lowers, the probability."},{"text":"The probability becomes 50%","correct":false,"why":"That would need a larger likelihood ratio."}],"solution":["Prior odds $=0.1/0.9 = 1/9$. Posterior odds $=4/9$.","Probability $=4/13 \\approx 0.31$."],"hints":["Convert 10% to odds.","Multiply the odds by 4.","Convert back to a probability."],"concept":"Likelihood ratios act on odds.","misconception":"Multiplying probabilities by the ratio.","why":"Odds, not probabilities, scale by the likelihood ratio.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-81","topic":"bayes","sub":"two-stage screening","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Prevalence is 2%. Stage 1 has sensitivity 0.9 and false-positive rate 0.10. Only those who test positive are retested with an independent Stage 2 (sensitivity 0.95, false-positive rate 0.02). What fraction of those who test positive at <b>both</b> stages truly have the condition?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8971668415529905,"display":"0.8972"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Both positive: cases $0.02(0.855)=0.0171$; non-cases $0.98(0.002)=0.00196$.","Posterior $\\approx 0.897$."],"hints":["Multiply the two stage rates for each group.","Weight by prevalence.","Normalise by the sum of the weighted terms."],"concept":"Sequential screening raises predictive value.","misconception":"Using the Stage 2 PPV with the original prevalence.","why":"Independent second tests multiply the evidence.","fam":"bayes:total-probability"},{"id":"BAY-82","topic":"bayes","sub":"sampling from two populations","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A survey draws a person from a city in which 55% are under 40. Under-40s own a car with probability 0.35; others with probability 0.70. A sampled person owns a car. What is the probability the person is under 40?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3793103448275862,"display":"0.3793"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Joint: $0.1925$ and $0.315$.","Posterior $=0.1925/0.5075 \\approx 0.379$."],"hints":["Multiply each group share by its ownership rate.","Add to get overall ownership.","Take the under-40 portion."],"concept":"Inference about group membership.","misconception":"Answering 0.55.","why":"Car ownership is more common among the older group, lowering the under-40 share.","fam":"bayes:updating"},{"id":"BAY-83","topic":"bayes","sub":"impossibility of a high posterior","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A condition has prevalence 0.1%. A test detects every case (sensitivity 1) and has false-positive rate 5%. Which is the best description of $P(\\text{condition}\\mid\\text{positive})$?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"About 2%: perfect sensitivity cannot offset a 5% false-positive rate at 0.1% prevalence"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"About 2%: perfect sensitivity cannot offset a 5% false-positive rate at 0.1% prevalence","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"100%, because every case is detected","correct":false,"why":"Detecting all cases says nothing about the false positives."},{"text":"95%, the complement of the false-positive rate","correct":false,"why":"That is the specificity, not the PPV."},{"text":"50%, because the result is either right or wrong","correct":false,"why":"Outcomes need not be equally likely."}],"solution":["Per 100 000: 100 cases flagged, $99\\,900\\times0.05 = 4\\,995$ false positives.","PPV $=100/5\\,095 \\approx 0.0196$."],"hints":["Take 100 000 people.","Count true and false positives.","Divide true by all positives."],"concept":"Low prevalence drives PPV.","misconception":"Equating PPV with sensitivity or specificity.","why":"False positives from the large healthy group dominate.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"BAY-84","topic":"bayes","sub":"prior from a hierarchy","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A fund manager is \"skilled\" with probability 0.2. Skilled managers beat the market in a given year with probability 0.65; unskilled with 0.45. A manager beat the market in two consecutive independent years. What is the probability that the manager beats it in a <b>third</b> year?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5185598377281948,"display":"0.5186"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["Posterior skilled $=0.0845/(0.0845+0.162)\\approx0.343$.","Next year $=0.343(0.65)+0.657(0.45)\\approx0.519$."],"hints":["Update the probability of \"skilled\" after two wins.","Use it as the weight for the next year.","Mix the two win rates."],"concept":"Predicting after learning about hidden quality.","misconception":"Using the prior mix 0.49.","why":"Two wins nudge the belief toward skill, raising the prediction slightly.","fam":"bayes:partition"},{"id":"RVX-01","topic":"rv","sub":"probability mass function","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A fair four-sided die (faces 1 to 4) and a fair five-sided die (faces 1 to 5) are rolled, and $X$ is the <b>larger</b> of the two results (either one if they tie). What is $P(X = 4)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.35,"display":"7/20"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"7/20","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.35},{"text":"1/5","correct":false,"why":"This counts only the outcomes where the four-sided die shows 4; a 4 on the five-sided die with a smaller value on the other die also gives a maximum of 4.","v":0.2},{"text":"4/5","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X\\le4)$, the probability that the maximum does not exceed 4.","v":0.8},{"text":"1/4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the four-sided die shows 4, not the probability that the maximum is 4.","v":0.25}],"solution":["Maximum at most 4: both dice at most 4: $4\\times4 = 16$ outcomes. Maximum at most 3: $3\\times3 = 9$ outcomes.","$P(X=4) = \\dfrac{16-9}{20} = \\dfrac{7}{20}$."],"hints":["Count the outcomes where the maximum equals 4 exactly.","Use $P(X\\le4)-P(X\\le3)$.","The sample space has $4\\times5 = 20$ equally likely outcomes."],"concept":"Probability mass function of a maximum.","misconception":"Counting only one die.","why":"The event $\\{X=4\\}$ is the difference of two nested \"at most\" events.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-02","topic":"rv","sub":"rank of the best of a group","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two senior and two junior analysts are ranked by a panel; all $4! = 24$ rankings are equally likely. Let $X$ be the <b>rank of the best-placed junior</b> (rank 1 is best). What is $P(X = 2)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3333333333333333,"display":"1/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X=1)$, the probability that a junior is top ranked.","v":0.5},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the second-ranked person is a junior given a senior is first; it must be multiplied by the probability that a senior is first.","v":0.6666666666666666},{"text":"1/6","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X=3)$: both seniors fill ranks 1 and 2.","v":0.16666666666666666}],"solution":["$X=2$ means rank 1 is a senior and rank 2 is a junior.","$P = \\dfrac24\\cdot\\dfrac23 = \\dfrac13$."],"hints":["What must be true of ranks 1 and 2 for the best junior to be second?","Multiply conditional probabilities for the first two ranks.","After a senior is first, 2 juniors remain among 3 people."],"concept":"Distribution of the position of the first success in random order.","misconception":"Omitting the conditioning on the first rank.","why":"The event is a specific sequence of types in the first two positions.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-03","topic":"rv","sub":"mass function of a sum","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A broker makes two independent trades. The first is profitable with probability 0.5, the second with probability 0.4. A profitable trade yields \\$100 or \\$200 with equal probability; an unprofitable trade yields \\$0. Let $X$ be the total gain. What is $P(X = 200)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3,"display":"0.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3},{"text":"0.05","correct":false,"why":"This is only the case in which both trades are profitable with \\$100 each.","v":0.05},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This includes the single-profit cases only; both trades yielding \\$100 also gives \\$200.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that both trades are profitable, which includes totals of 200, 300 and 400.","v":0.2}],"solution":["Total \\$200 can arise in three disjoint ways:","first only, \\$200: $0.5\\cdot0.5\\cdot0.6 = 0.15$;","second only, \\$200: $0.5\\cdot0.5\\cdot0.4 = 0.10$;","both, \\$100 each: $(0.5\\cdot0.5)(0.4\\cdot0.5) = 0.05$.","$P(X=200) = 0.30$."],"hints":["List the ways in which the two gains can sum to \\$200.","Include the cases in which one trade is unprofitable.","Multiply independent probabilities within each case and add the cases."],"concept":"Distribution of a sum of independent discrete gains.","misconception":"Missing some combinations of gains.","why":"The three cases are disjoint, so their probabilities add.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-04","topic":"rv","sub":"size-biased sampling","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A university has three lecture sections with 20, 30 and 70 students. A student is chosen at random from all 120 students and $X$ is the size of that student's section. What is $E[X]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":51.666666666666664,"display":"51.6667"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"51.6667","correct":true,"why":"","v":51.666666666666664},{"text":"40","correct":false,"why":"This averages over sections (a randomly chosen section), not over students.","v":40},{"text":"70","correct":false,"why":"This is the size of the largest section; students are spread over all three sections.","v":70},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This divides the number of students by the number of students and has no relation to the average section size seen by a student.","v":1}],"solution":["$P(X=20) = 20/120$, $P(X=30) = 30/120$, $P(X=70) = 70/120$.","$E[X] = \\dfrac{20^2+30^2+70^2}{120} = \\dfrac{6200}{120} \\approx 51.7$."],"hints":["Which is chosen at random: a section or a student?","A section is selected with probability proportional to its size.","Weight each section size by its share of students."],"concept":"Size-biased expectation.","misconception":"Averaging section sizes with equal weights.","why":"Large sections hold more students, so a random student is more likely to be in one.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-06","topic":"rv","sub":"insurance pricing","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"An insurer pays \\$80,000 if a claim occurs within a year; the claim probability is 0.015. The premium $P$ is set so that the insurer's expected profit equals <b>20% of the premium</b>. What premium should be charged?","data":"","answer":{"value":1500,"display":"1,500"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"1,500","correct":true,"why":"","v":1500},{"text":"1,200","correct":false,"why":"This premium covers the expected payout only and leaves zero expected profit.","v":1200},{"text":"1,440","correct":false,"why":"This adds 20% of the expected payout as profit, but the profit must be 20% of the <i>premium</i>.","v":1440},{"text":"960","correct":false,"why":"This reduces the expected payout, which gives an expected loss.","v":960}],"solution":["Expected profit $= P - 0.015(80000) = P-1200$. Set this equal to $0.2P$: $0.8P = 1200$, so $P = 1500$."],"hints":["Write the expected profit in terms of the premium.","The target is 20% of the premium, not of the claim cost.","Solve the linear equation."],"concept":"Expected value pricing.","misconception":"Taking a percentage of the wrong base.","why":"The premium appears on both sides of the profit equation.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-07","topic":"rv","sub":"indicator method","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A committee of 5 is chosen at random from 12 employees, 4 of whom are women. What is the expected number of women on the committee?","data":"","answer":{"value":1.6666666666666667,"display":"1.6667"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"1.6667","correct":true,"why":"","v":1.6666666666666667},{"text":"0.3333","correct":false,"why":"This is the proportion of women, not the expected count for a committee of 5.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1.8182","correct":false,"why":"The probability that a given member is a woman is $4/12$, not $4/11$.","v":1.8181818181818181},{"text":"2.5","correct":false,"why":"This assumes half the committee are women.","v":2.5}],"solution":["Each of the 5 seats is held by a woman with probability $4/12$ (by symmetry).","$E = 5\\times\\dfrac{4}{12} = \\dfrac{5}{3} \\approx 1.67$."],"hints":["Use an indicator for each committee member.","What is the probability that a given committee member is a woman?","Add the expectations."],"concept":"Expectation of a hypergeometric count by linearity.","misconception":"Using a binomial formula with the wrong probability.","why":"Each position is equally likely to be any employee.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-08","topic":"rv","sub":"expected value of a betting strategy","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A gambler bets on an event of probability 0.45 at even odds. Strategy: stake \\$1; if it wins, stop. If it loses, stake \\$2; if that wins, stop. If that loses, stake \\$4 and then stop whatever happens. Let $X$ be the net gain. What is $E[X]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":-0.3310000000000004,"display":"-0.331"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"-0.331","correct":true,"why":"","v":-0.3310000000000004},{"text":"0","correct":false,"why":"Even odds would give zero expectation only if the event had probability 0.5.","v":0},{"text":"0.6673","correct":false,"why":"The final loss is \\$7 (1+2+4), not \\$1.","v":0.66725},{"text":"-0.1","correct":false,"why":"This is the expectation of a single \\$1 bet; the strategy has several stages.","v":-0.10000000000000003}],"solution":["Wins: stage 1: $+1$ with prob $0.45$; stage 2: $-1+2 = +1$ with prob $0.55\\cdot0.45$; stage 3: $-1-2+4 = +1$ with prob $0.55^2\\cdot0.45$. All three lost: $-1-2-4 = -7$ with prob $0.55^3 = 0.1664$.","$E[X] = (0.45+0.2475+0.1361)(1) + 0.1664(-7) = 0.8336-1.1647 = -0.331$."],"hints":["Enumerate the four outcomes of the strategy and the gain in each.","Compute the probability of each branch.","Weight the gains and add."],"concept":"Expectation over a decision tree.","misconception":"Assuming a staking system beats an unfavourable game.","why":"Frequent small wins are offset by rare large losses; expectation stays negative.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-09","topic":"rv","sub":"expected search effort","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A prize is hidden in one of 12 boxes, each equally likely. Boxes are opened one at a time in a fixed order until the prize is found. What is the <b>expected number of boxes opened</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":6.5,"display":"6.5"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"6.5","correct":true,"why":"","v":6.5},{"text":"6","correct":false,"why":"This is the midpoint of 0 to 12; the first box is box number 1, so the mean is $(1+12)/2$.","v":6},{"text":"12","correct":false,"why":"This is the worst case, when the prize is in the last box.","v":12},{"text":"0.0833","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the prize is in any particular box.","v":0.08333333333333333}],"solution":["The number of boxes opened equals the position $K$ of the prize, uniform on $1,\\dots,12$.","$E[K] = \\dfrac{1+2+\\cdots+12}{12} = \\dfrac{78}{12} = 6.5$."],"hints":["What is the distribution of the position of the prize?","Use the sum $1+2+\\cdots+n = n(n+1)/2$.","Divide the sum of the positions by 12."],"concept":"Expectation of a discrete uniform variable.","misconception":"Using $n/2$ for a variable starting at 1.","why":"The mean of a discrete uniform variable on $1..n$ is $(n+1)/2$.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-10","topic":"rv","sub":"group testing","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Sixty blood samples are split into ten batches of six and each batch is mixed and tested once. A mixed batch tests positive if any of its samples is positive, and each sample is independently positive with probability 0.05. Positive batches are then re-tested sample by sample (six more tests); negative batches need nothing more. What is the <b>expected total number of tests</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":25.89448656250001,"display":"25.8945"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"25.8945","correct":true,"why":"","v":25.89448656250001},{"text":"10","correct":false,"why":"This counts only the pooled tests; positive pools require 6 additional tests.","v":10},{"text":"60","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of tests with no pooling.","v":60},{"text":"28","correct":false,"why":"The probability that a pool is positive is $1-0.95^6$, not $6\\times0.05$ scaled again.","v":28.000000000000004}],"solution":["Each pool needs 1 test, plus 6 more if positive. $P(\\text{positive}) = 1-0.95^6 \\approx 0.2649$.","Expected tests per pool: $1+6(0.2649) = 2.589$.","Ten pools: $25.9$ tests."],"hints":["How many tests does a single pool need in each case?","What is the probability that a pool of 6 is positive?","Multiply the expected tests per pool by 10."],"concept":"Expected cost of a conditional procedure.","misconception":"Using $np$ for the positive probability.","why":"Pooling pays off when positives are rare.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-11","topic":"rv","sub":"newsvendor decision","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A bakery bakes $k$ loaves each morning at a cost of \\$2 each and sells them at \\$5; unsold loaves are worthless. Daily demand is equally likely to be 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6 loaves. How many loaves should it bake to <b>maximise expected profit</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":4,"display":"4"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"4","correct":true,"why":"","v":4},{"text":"3","correct":false,"why":"At $k=3$ the expected profit is 6.5, below the 7.0 obtained with 4 loaves.","v":3},{"text":"6","correct":false,"why":"Baking for the maximum demand wastes loaves on low-demand days: the expected profit is 5.5, below the optimum.","v":6},{"text":"2","correct":false,"why":"At $k=2$ the expected profit is about 5.17, well below the optimum.","v":2}],"solution":["Profit for $k$ loaves and demand $d$ is $5\\min(d,k)-2k$. Averaging over $d=1,\\dots,6$ gives expected profits $k=1$: 3.00; $k=2$: 5.17; $k=3$: 6.50; $k=4$: 7.00; $k=5$: 6.67; $k=6$: 5.50.","Equivalently, loaf number $k$ sells with probability $(7-k)/6$ and is worth baking while $5(7-k)/6 > 2$, i.e. $k\\le4$.","Bake 4 loaves."],"hints":["Compute the expected profit for each order quantity $k$.","Think about the marginal loaf: probability it sells times \\$5 minus its \\$2 cost.","Order while the marginal expected gain is positive."],"concept":"Optimising expected profit over a discrete decision.","misconception":"Ordering for the average or maximum demand.","why":"The optimum balances the cost of unsold loaves against forgone sales.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-12","topic":"rv","sub":"expected winnings","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A box holds 4 red and 6 blue marbles. Two are drawn without replacement. You win \\$3 if they are the <b>same colour</b> and lose \\$2 if they differ. What is your expected winning?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.33333333333333326,"display":"0.3333"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3333","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.33333333333333326},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"The probability of the same colour is $21/45$, not 0.52.","v":0.6000000000000001},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This averages the two prizes with equal weights; the outcomes are not equally likely.","v":0.5},{"text":"1.4","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the loss on the differing-colour outcomes.","v":1.4}],"solution":["$P(\\text{same}) = \\dfrac{\\binom42+\\binom62}{\\binom{10}2} = \\dfrac{6+15}{45} = \\dfrac{21}{45}$; $P(\\text{differ}) = \\dfrac{24}{45}$.","$E = 3\\cdot\\dfrac{21}{45} - 2\\cdot\\dfrac{24}{45} = \\dfrac{15}{45} = \\dfrac13$."],"hints":["Find the probability of two marbles of the same colour.","The complement is \"different colours\".","Weight the gain and the loss by these probabilities."],"concept":"Expected value of a two-outcome game.","misconception":"Using equal weights.","why":"Expected value weights each payoff by its probability.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-13","topic":"rv","sub":"variance of a population value","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A bank has four branches with 12, 18, 30 and 40 staff. One branch is chosen at random (each with probability $\\tfrac14$) and $Y$ is its staff count. What is $\\mathrm{Var}(Y)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":117,"display":"117"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"117","correct":true,"why":"","v":117},{"text":"10.8167","correct":false,"why":"This is the standard deviation, not the variance.","v":10.816653826391969},{"text":"742","correct":false,"why":"This is $E[Y^2]$; the square of the mean must be subtracted.","v":742},{"text":"156","correct":false,"why":"This divides by 3 as for a sample variance; here the four branches are the whole distribution.","v":156}],"solution":["$E[Y] = (12+18+30+40)/4 = 25$.","$E[Y^2] = (144+324+900+1600)/4 = 742$.","$\\mathrm{Var}(Y) = 742-625 = 117$."],"hints":["Find the mean of $Y$ first.","Then compute $E[Y^2]$.","Use $\\mathrm{Var}(Y) = E[Y^2]-(E[Y])^2$."],"concept":"Variance from a pmf.","misconception":"Reporting $E[Y^2]$ or the standard deviation.","why":"The shortcut formula reduces variance to two expectations.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-14","topic":"rv","sub":"expectation of a quadratic","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A random variable $X$ has $E[X] = 2$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 3$. Find $E\\big[(X-1)(X+3)\\big]$.","data":"","answer":{"value":8,"display":"8"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"8","correct":true,"why":"","v":8},{"text":"5","correct":false,"why":"This substitutes the mean into the product; $E[g(X)]\\ne g(E[X])$ for a quadratic.","v":5},{"text":"4","correct":false,"why":"The linear term $2X$ was dropped when expanding $(X-1)(X+3) = X^2+2X-3$.","v":4},{"text":"14","correct":false,"why":"The constant term has the wrong sign: $(X-1)(X+3)$ expands with $-3$, not $+3$.","v":14}],"solution":["$(X-1)(X+3) = X^2+2X-3$.","$E[X^2] = \\mathrm{Var}(X)+(E[X])^2 = 3+4 = 7$.","$E = 7+2(2)-3 = 8$."],"hints":["Expand the product.","Find $E[X^2]$ from the variance and the mean.","Use linearity of expectation on the expanded form."],"concept":"Using $E[X^2] = \\mathrm{Var}(X)+(E[X])^2$.","misconception":"Plugging the mean into a nonlinear expression.","why":"Variance is exactly the gap between $E[X^2]$ and $(E[X])^2$.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-15","topic":"rv","sub":"expected value with two sources","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"In a promotion a customer rolls a fair six-sided die and, separately, spins a fair two-sided token. The prize in dollars is <b>three times</b> the die value if the token lands on gold and <b>half</b> the die value if it lands on silver. What is the expected prize?","data":"","answer":{"value":6.125,"display":"6.125"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"6.125","correct":true,"why":"","v":6.125},{"text":"3.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the expected die value alone, without the multipliers.","v":3.5},{"text":"12.25","correct":false,"why":"The multipliers must be weighted by the coin probabilities $\\frac12$ each.","v":12.25},{"text":"1.75","correct":false,"why":"The expected multiplier is 1.75; it still has to be multiplied by the expected die value 3.5.","v":1.75}],"solution":["The coin and die are independent: $E[\\text{winnings}] = E[\\text{multiplier}]\\,E[\\text{die}] = (0.5\\cdot3+0.5\\cdot0.5)(3.5) = 1.75\\times3.5 = 6.125$."],"hints":["Find the expected multiplier from the coin.","The die value has expectation 3.5.","Independence lets you multiply the two expectations."],"concept":"Expectation of a product of independent variables.","misconception":"Ignoring one of the two sources of randomness.","why":"For independent factors $E[XY] = E[X]E[Y]$.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-16","topic":"rv","sub":"indicator method","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two reviewers each independently pick 4 of 15 papers at random. What is the <b>expected number of papers picked by both</b> reviewers?","data":"","answer":{"value":1.0666666666666667,"display":"1.0667"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"1.0667","correct":true,"why":"","v":1.0666666666666667},{"text":"0.2667","correct":false,"why":"This is one reviewer’s probability of picking a given paper, not the expected size of the overlap.","v":0.26666666666666666},{"text":"4","correct":false,"why":"This is the expected number of papers picked by one reviewer.","v":4},{"text":"0.0711","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that one particular paper is picked by both reviewers; the expected count sums it over all 15 papers.","v":0.07111111111111111}],"solution":["For a given paper, $P(\\text{picked by both}) = \\dfrac4{15}\\cdot\\dfrac4{15}$ by independence.","Summing 15 indicators: $15\\cdot\\dfrac{16}{225} = \\dfrac{16}{15} \\approx 1.07$."],"hints":["Define an indicator for each paper.","The two reviewers choose independently: multiply their selection probabilities.","Add over the 15 papers."],"concept":"Linearity with indicators.","misconception":"Counting papers rather than using probabilities.","why":"Linearity avoids any need for the joint distribution of the count.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-17","topic":"rv","sub":"expected number of distinct categories","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"25 colleagues each independently pick one of the 12 calendar months at random for a holiday. What is the <b>expected number of different months</b> that are chosen?","data":"","answer":{"value":10.637089856448267,"display":"10.6371"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"10.6371","correct":true,"why":"","v":10.637089856448267},{"text":"12","correct":false,"why":"With 25 people it is very likely, but not certain, that every month appears; the expectation is slightly below 12.","v":12},{"text":"1.3629","correct":false,"why":"This is the expected number of months that nobody chooses.","v":1.3629101435517332},{"text":"2.0833","correct":false,"why":"This is the expected number of people per month, not the number of months chosen.","v":2.0833333333333335}],"solution":["For each month define an indicator that at least one person chooses it. $P = 1-(11/12)^{25} \\approx 0.8866$.","By linearity $E = 12\\times0.8866 \\approx 10.64$."],"hints":["Define an indicator for each month.","The probability that nobody picks a given month is $(11/12)^{25}$.","Add the 12 expectations."],"concept":"Expected number of occupied categories via indicators.","misconception":"Trying to find the full distribution.","why":"Linearity makes the dependence between months irrelevant.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-18","topic":"rv","sub":"collecting all types","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A cereal brand includes one of 5 different collectible cards in every box, each type equally likely and independently of earlier boxes. What is the <b>expected number of boxes</b> needed to complete the set?","data":"","answer":{"value":11.416666666666666,"display":"11.4167"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"11.4167","correct":true,"why":"","v":11.416666666666666},{"text":"5","correct":false,"why":"This is the number of different cards; repeats are expected before the set is complete.","v":5},{"text":"10.4167","correct":false,"why":"The last card needs an expected $5/1 = 5$ boxes, which was left out of the sum.","v":10.416666666666664},{"text":"25","correct":false,"why":"This is $5^2$; the expected number is smaller because later stages need fewer cards.","v":25}],"solution":["After $k$ distinct cards, each box yields a new one with probability $(5-k)/5$, so the wait is geometric with mean $5/(5-k)$.","Sum over $k=0,\\dots,4$: $5(1+\\tfrac12+\\tfrac13+\\tfrac14+\\tfrac15) = 11.42$."],"hints":["Split the collection into stages: waiting for the first new card, the second, and so on.","Each stage is a geometric waiting time.","Add the expected waiting times."],"concept":"Coupon-collector expectation.","misconception":"Assuming the number of boxes equals the number of cards.","why":"The final cards take longest because few new types remain.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-19","topic":"rv","sub":"variance of a sum","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Twelve independent rolls are made of a fair die with faces numbered 1 to 4, and the face values are added. Find the <b>variance of this total</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":15,"display":"15"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"15","correct":true,"why":"","v":15},{"text":"1.25","correct":false,"why":"This is the variance of one roll; the 12 independent variances add.","v":1.25},{"text":"90","correct":false,"why":"This is the sum of second moments, $12E[X^2]$, not the variance.","v":90},{"text":"180","correct":false,"why":"This would be the variance of 12 times a single roll (perfect dependence), not of a sum of 12 independent rolls.","v":180}],"solution":["One roll: $E[X]=2.5$, $E[X^2] = (1+4+9+16)/4 = 7.5$, so $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 7.5-6.25 = 1.25$.","For independent rolls variances add: $12\\times1.25 = 15$."],"hints":["Find the variance of one roll first.","Are the rolls independent?","Add 12 equal variances."],"concept":"Variance of a sum of independent variables.","misconception":"Using $n^2$ times the variance.","why":"Independence removes covariances so only variances remain.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-20","topic":"rv","sub":"density normalisation","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A continuous variable has density $f(x) = c\\,(4-x^2)$ for $-2<x<2$ and 0 elsewhere. What is $c$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.09375,"display":"0.0938"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0938","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.09375},{"text":"0.1875","correct":false,"why":"This integrates only over $(0,2)$, which is half the support.","v":0.1875},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This would normalise a constant density on $(-2,2)$; the $x^2$ term is ignored.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.0469","correct":false,"why":"The integral is $32/3$, not $64/3$.","v":0.046875}],"solution":["$\\int_{-2}^{2}(4-x^2)\\,dx = \\left[4x-\\dfrac{x^3}{3}\\right]_{-2}^{2} = 2\\left(8-\\dfrac83\\right) = \\dfrac{32}{3}$.","Setting $c\\cdot\\dfrac{32}{3} = 1$ gives $c = \\dfrac{3}{32} = 0.09375$."],"hints":["A density must integrate to 1 over its support.","Integrate $4-x^2$ from $-2$ to $2$.","Divide 1 by the integral to solve for $c$."],"concept":"Normalising a density.","misconception":"Integrating over only half the support.","why":"The constant $c$ scales the area under the curve to 1.","fam":"rv:density"},{"id":"RVX-21","topic":"rv","sub":"existence of moments","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A variable has density $f(x) = 2/x^3$ for $x>1$ and 0 otherwise. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$E[X] = 2$ is finite, but $E[X^2]$ is infinite, so $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$ does not exist"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$E[X] = 2$ is finite, but $E[X^2]$ is infinite, so $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$ does not exist","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Both $E[X]$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$ are finite","correct":false,"why":"$E[X^2] = \\int_1^\\infty 2/x\\,dx$ diverges logarithmically."},{"text":"$E[X]$ does not exist","correct":false,"why":"$E[X] = \\int_1^\\infty 2/x^2\\,dx = 2$, which is finite."},{"text":"$E[X]=1$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(X)=1$","correct":false,"why":"The mean of this density is 2, and the variance is not finite."}],"solution":["$\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{x^3}dx = 1$, so it is a density.","$E[X] = \\int_1^\\infty x\\cdot\\dfrac{2}{x^3}dx = \\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{x^2}dx = 2$.","$E[X^2] = \\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{x}dx = \\infty$, so the variance does not exist."],"hints":["Compute $E[X]$ by integrating $x f(x)$.","Then check $E[X^2]$ by integrating $x^2 f(x)$.","Does $\\int_1^\\infty 1/x\\,dx$ converge?"],"concept":"Mean may exist while variance does not.","misconception":"Assuming that a finite mean implies a finite variance.","why":"The heavier the tail, the lower the highest moment that exists.","fam":"rv:density"},{"id":"RVX-22","topic":"rv","sub":"solving for density parameters","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A waiting time $X$ (in minutes) is described by a linear density $f(x) = a+bx$ on $0\\le x\\le2$ and zero elsewhere, and its mean is known to be $1.2$ minutes. Find the probability that the wait exceeds one minute.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6499999999999999,"display":"0.65"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.65","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6499999999999999},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This would hold for a symmetric density; here the mean 1.2 exceeds 1 but the density is not constant.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.35","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X<1) = a + b/2$, the complement of the probability asked for.","v":0.35},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is $E[X]/2$, not a tail probability.","v":0.6}],"solution":["Normalisation: $\\int_0^2(a+bx)dx = 2a+2b = 1$.","Mean: $\\int_0^2 x(a+bx)dx = 2a+\\tfrac83 b = 1.2$.","Subtract twice the first from... solving gives $b = 0.3$, $a = 0.2$.","$P(X>1) = \\int_1^2(a+bx)dx = a + 1.5b = 0.65$."],"hints":["Use two conditions: total probability 1 and the given mean.","Solve the two linear equations for $a$ and $b$.","Integrate the density from 1 to 2."],"concept":"Determining a density from moment conditions.","misconception":"Forgetting one of the two conditions.","why":"Two unknown parameters need two conditions.","fam":"rv:density"},{"id":"RVX-23","topic":"rv","sub":"expected lifetime","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The lifetime (in years) of a component has density $f(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}\\,e^{-x/2}$ for $x\\ge0$. What is its expected lifetime?","data":"","answer":{"value":4,"display":"4"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"4","correct":true,"why":"","v":4},{"text":"2","correct":false,"why":"This is the mean of the density $x e^{-x}$, which has a different scale.","v":2},{"text":"8","correct":false,"why":"This is $2\\times$ the mean; the scale parameter is 2 and the shape is 2, giving a mean of 4.","v":8},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This is not obtained from the integral of $x f(x)$.","v":1}],"solution":["$E[X] = \\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{x^2}{4}e^{-x/2}dx$. Put $x = 2u$: $= \\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot8\\int_0^\\infty u^2e^{-u}du = 2\\cdot2! = 4$.","(This is a gamma distribution with shape 2 and scale 2: mean $2\\times2 = 4$.)"],"hints":["Set up $\\int_0^\\infty x f(x)\\,dx$.","Substitute $x = 2u$ to reach a standard gamma integral.","Use $\\int_0^\\infty u^n e^{-u}du = n!$."],"concept":"Mean of a continuous distribution by integration.","misconception":"Mis-scaling after substitution.","why":"Rescaling the variable multiplies the mean by the scale.","fam":"rv:density"},{"id":"RVX-25","topic":"rv","sub":"decision under expectation","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Option $A$ pays \\$0, \\$3 or \\$12 with probabilities 0.5, 0.3 and 0.2. Option $B$ pays \\$0, \\$1 or \\$15 with probabilities 0.6, 0.25 and 0.15. Which option has the higher expected payoff?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Option $A$: expected payoff \\$3.30 versus \\$2.50"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Option $A$: expected payoff \\$3.30 versus \\$2.50","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Option $B$: it offers the largest single prize, \\$15","correct":false,"why":"The expected payoff of $B$ is $0.25+2.25 = \\$2.50$, below $A$’s \\$3.30."},{"text":"They are equal, since both have a 50% to 60% chance of paying nothing","correct":false,"why":"The chance of paying nothing is different, and so are the expected payoffs."},{"text":"Option $A$: expected payoff \\$4.00 versus \\$2.50","correct":false,"why":"The payoff of $A$ is $0.9+2.4 = \\$3.30$, not \\$4.00."}],"solution":["$E_A = 0.3(3)+0.2(12) = 0.9+2.4 = 3.30$.","$E_B = 0.25(1)+0.15(15) = 0.25+2.25 = 2.50$.","Option $A$ has the higher expected payoff."],"hints":["Compute the expected payoff of each option separately.","A zero payoff contributes nothing to the expectation.","Compare the two values."],"concept":"Using expected value to compare options.","misconception":"Choosing on the maximum prize.","why":"Expectation summarises each distribution with a single number weighted by probability.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-26","topic":"rv","sub":"expected count","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A call centre expects 340 calls on a given day. 15% of calls are complaints and 20% of complaints are escalated to a manager. About how many calls are expected to be escalated?","data":"","answer":{"value":10.200000000000001,"display":"10.2"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"10.2","correct":true,"why":"","v":10.200000000000001},{"text":"51","correct":false,"why":"This is the expected number of complaints, not the escalated ones.","v":51},{"text":"68","correct":false,"why":"The 20% applies only to complaints, not to all calls.","v":68},{"text":"119","correct":false,"why":"The proportions are applied in sequence (multiplied), not added.","v":118.99999999999999}],"solution":["Complaints: $340\\times0.15 = 51$. Escalated: $51\\times0.20 = 10.2$."],"hints":["How many of the calls are complaints?","The escalation rate applies to complaints only.","Multiply the proportions."],"concept":"Expected counts from chained proportions.","misconception":"Adding proportions.","why":"Each proportion is a conditional rate applied in turn.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-27","topic":"rv","sub":"reading a pmf","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The number $X$ of defects found on a circuit board has pmf $p(0)=0.30$, $p(1)=0.30$, $p(2)=0.20$, $p(3)=0.12$, $p(4)=0.08$. What is $P(1\\le X\\le3)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.62,"display":"0.62"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.62","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.62},{"text":"0.92","correct":false,"why":"The value $X=0$ (probability 0.30) is not in the interval.","v":0.9199999999999999},{"text":"0.2","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(1<X<3) = P(X=2)$; the end values 1 and 3 are included.","v":0.2},{"text":"0.7","correct":false,"why":"The value $X=4$ lies outside the interval.","v":0.7}],"solution":["$P(1\\le X\\le3) = p(1)+p(2)+p(3) = 0.30+0.20+0.12 = 0.62$."],"hints":["Which values of $X$ satisfy $1\\le X\\le3$?","The end points are included.","Add their probabilities."],"concept":"Probabilities of events defined by a discrete variable.","misconception":"Excluding or including the wrong end points.","why":"A discrete event’s probability is the sum of the pmf over its values.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-28","topic":"rv","sub":"valid pmf","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Which of the following is a <b>valid</b> probability mass function?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$p(x) = (6-x)/15$ for $x = 1,2,3,4,5$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$p(x) = (6-x)/15$ for $x = 1,2,3,4,5$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$p(x) = x/10$ for $x = 1,2,3,4,5$","correct":false,"why":"The probabilities sum to $15/10 = 1.5 > 1$."},{"text":"$p(x) = (x-2)/6$ for $x = 1,2,3,4$","correct":false,"why":"$p(1) = -1/6$ is negative."},{"text":"$p(x) = x^2/50$ for $x = 1,2,3,4,5$","correct":false,"why":"The probabilities sum to $55/50 > 1$."}],"solution":["A pmf needs $p(x)\\ge0$ and $\\sum p(x) = 1$.","$(6-x)/15$ for $x=1,\\dots,5$ gives $5,4,3,2,1$ over 15: all non-negative and summing to $15/15 = 1$.","The other candidates violate non-negativity or sum to more than 1."],"hints":["Check non-negativity for each candidate.","Then check whether the probabilities sum to 1.","Only one candidate satisfies both."],"concept":"Conditions for a pmf.","misconception":"Checking only one condition.","why":"Probabilities must be non-negative and total 1.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-31","topic":"rv","sub":"deductible pricing","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The annual loss from a fire is \\$0, \\$2,000 or \\$8,000 with probabilities 0.85, 0.10 and 0.05. An insurer pays the loss in excess of a \\$1,000 deductible. What premium gives the insurer an <b>expected profit of \\$150</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":600,"display":"600"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"600","correct":true,"why":"","v":600},{"text":"750","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the deductible and prices as if the insurer paid the whole loss.","v":750},{"text":"450","correct":false,"why":"This is the expected payout; the target profit of \\$150 has not been added.","v":450},{"text":"300","correct":false,"why":"The profit must be added to, not subtracted from, the expected payout.","v":300}],"solution":["Payout: \\$1,000 with probability 0.10 and \\$7,000 with probability 0.05.","Expected payout $= 100+350 = 450$.","Premium $= 450+150 = 600$."],"hints":["What does the insurer pay in each loss case after the deductible?","Compute the expected payout.","Add the required expected profit."],"concept":"Expected value of a policy payout.","misconception":"Ignoring the deductible.","why":"Expected profit is premium minus expected payout.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-33","topic":"rv","sub":"linear transformation of a variance","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A warehouse holds 100 units and ships them in crates of 4. The number of crates $X$ ordered has variance $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = 2.25$. What is the variance of the number of units <b>left</b> after the order, $Y = 100-4X$?","data":"","answer":{"value":36,"display":"36"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"36","correct":true,"why":"","v":36},{"text":"9","correct":false,"why":"The coefficient must be squared when scaling a variance: $a^2\\mathrm{Var}(X)$.","v":9},{"text":"2","correct":false,"why":"Multiplying by 4 scales the variance by 16; only the constant 100 leaves it unchanged.","v":2.25},{"text":"216","correct":false,"why":"The constant 100 does not multiply the variance; it has no effect on it.","v":216}],"solution":["$\\mathrm{Var}(a+bX) = b^2\\mathrm{Var}(X)$.","$\\mathrm{Var}(100-4X) = (-4)^2(2.25) = 36$."],"hints":["Which part of $100-4X$ affects the variance?","The coefficient is squared.","Multiply $(-4)^2$ by 2.25."],"concept":"Variance of a linear transformation.","misconception":"Not squaring the coefficient.","why":"Shifts change location, not spread; scalings change spread quadratically.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-34","topic":"rv","sub":"expected maximum","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair four-sided dice (faces 1 to 4) are rolled and $M$ is the <b>larger</b> of the two results. What is $E[M]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":3.125,"display":"3.125"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"3.125","correct":true,"why":"","v":3.125},{"text":"2.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the mean of a single die; the maximum of two is larger on average.","v":2.5},{"text":"1.875","correct":false,"why":"This is $E[\\min]$, the expected smaller value.","v":1.875},{"text":"4","correct":false,"why":"This is the largest possible value of the maximum, not its expectation.","v":4}],"solution":["$P(M=k) = \\dfrac{k^2-(k-1)^2}{16} = \\dfrac{2k-1}{16}$ for $k=1,\\dots,4$: $\\tfrac1{16},\\tfrac3{16},\\tfrac5{16},\\tfrac7{16}$.","$E[M] = \\dfrac{1+6+15+28}{16} = \\dfrac{50}{16} = 3.125$."],"hints":["Find $P(M\\le k)$ for each $k$ and difference them.","Write the pmf of $M$.","Compute $\\sum k\\,P(M=k)$."],"concept":"Expectation of a maximum.","misconception":"Assuming the maximum has the same mean as one die.","why":"Taking a maximum shifts probability towards large values.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-35","topic":"rv","sub":"sum of two discrete variables","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"$X$ is uniform on $\\{1,3,5\\}$ and $Y$ is uniform on $\\{0,2,4\\}$, independently. What is $P(X+Y\\ge7)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3333333333333333,"display":"1/3"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/3","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X+Y\\ge5)$, which includes the sums 5.","v":0.6666666666666666},{"text":"1/9","correct":false,"why":"This is only the sum 9; the sums 7 also qualify.","v":0.1111111111111111},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"There are 9 equally likely pairs; 3 give a sum of at least 7.","v":0.5}],"solution":["The nine pairs are equally likely. Sums $\\ge7$: $(3,4)\\to7$, $(5,2)\\to7$, $(5,4)\\to9$: 3 pairs.","$P = 3/9 = 1/3$."],"hints":["How many equally likely pairs are there?","List the pairs with sum at least 7.","Divide the favourable count by the nine equally likely pairs."],"concept":"Probability of an event defined by a sum.","misconception":"Missing pairs.","why":"Independence and uniformity make the pairs equally likely.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-36","topic":"rv","sub":"telescoping pmf","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The number of failures in a week has pmf $f(x) = \\dfrac{2}{(x+2)(x+3)}$ for $x = 0,1,2,\\dots$ Find $P(X\\ge3\\mid X\\ge1)$.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6000000000000001,"display":"3/5"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"3/5","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6000000000000001},{"text":"2/5","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X\\ge3)$, which has not been divided by $P(X\\ge1)$.","v":0.4},{"text":"2/3","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X\\ge1)$, the probability of the conditioning event.","v":0.6666666666666666},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"There is no reason for the conditional probability to be one half.","v":0.5}],"solution":["Write $f(x) = 2\\left[\\dfrac1{x+2}-\\dfrac1{x+3}\\right]$. The tail sum telescopes: $P(X\\ge k) = \\dfrac{2}{k+2}$.","$P(X\\ge3) = 2/5$ and $P(X\\ge1) = 2/3$.","$P(X\\ge3\\mid X\\ge1) = \\dfrac{2/5}{2/3} = \\dfrac35$."],"hints":["Use partial fractions on $f(x)$.","Sum the tail $\\sum_{x\\ge k}f(x)$: most terms cancel.","Since $\\{X\\ge3\\}\\subset\\{X\\ge1\\}$, divide the two tail probabilities."],"concept":"Tail probabilities by telescoping.","misconception":"Not dividing by the conditioning probability.","why":"The partial-fraction form turns the infinite sum into one remaining term.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-38","topic":"rv","sub":"diagnose: expectation of a nonlinear function","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A firm’s monthly demand $X$ is equally likely to be 10 or 30 units. Revenue is $R = X^2$ (in thousands). A student says: \"$E[X]=20$, so $E[R] = 20^2 = 400$.\" What is the error and the correct expected revenue?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$E[X^2] \\neq (E[X])^2$: $E[R] = \\tfrac12(100)+\\tfrac12(900) = 500$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$E[X^2] \\neq (E[X])^2$: $E[R] = \\tfrac12(100)+\\tfrac12(900) = 500$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"There is no error: $E[R] = (E[X])^2 = 400$","correct":false,"why":"For a nonlinear function, $E[g(X)] \\neq g(E[X])$ in general; here $E[X^2] = \\text{Var}(X) + (E[X])^2 = 100 + 400 = 500$."},{"text":"The correct value is $E[R] = 2E[X] = 40$","correct":false,"why":"Squaring is not linear; doubling is not the same as squaring."},{"text":"The correct value is $\\sqrt{400}=20$","correct":false,"why":"Taking a square root reverses a square on the mean but does not give $E[X^2]$."}],"solution":["$E[X^2] = \\frac12(10^2)+\\frac12(30^2) = 50+450 = 500$.","Also $E[X^2] = \\mathrm{Var}(X)+(E[X])^2 = 100 + 400$. The difference from 400 is the variance."],"hints":["Is squaring a linear operation?","Compute $E[X^2]$ directly from the two possible values.","How is $E[X^2]$ related to $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$ and $(E[X])^2$?"],"concept":"Expectation of a function of a random variable; variance as the gap.","misconception":"Plugging the mean into a nonlinear function.","why":"Variability raises the expected value of a convex function.","fam":"rv:diagnose"},{"id":"RVX-39","topic":"rv","sub":"expected value from a table","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Daily demand for a bakery’s special loaf has the distribution: 0 loaves (0.1), 10 (0.3), 20 (0.4), 30 (0.2). What is the expected daily demand?","data":"","answer":{"value":17,"display":"17"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$E = 0(0.1)+10(0.3)+20(0.4)+30(0.2) = 3+8+6 = 17$ loaves."],"hints":["Multiply each demand by its probability.","Do not average the four demand values equally.","Add the four products."],"concept":"Expectation as a probability-weighted average.","misconception":"Taking the plain average of the values.","why":"Likelier values carry more weight.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-40","topic":"rv","sub":"variance from a table","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Using the demand distribution 0 (0.1), 10 (0.3), 20 (0.4), 30 (0.2), what is the variance of daily demand?","data":"","answer":{"value":81,"display":"81"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$E[X^2] = 0+30+160+180 = 370$.","$\\mathrm{Var} = 370-17^2 = 81$."],"hints":["Compute $E[X^2]$ with the same probabilities.","Subtract the square of the mean.","The mean is 17."],"concept":"Variance via $E[X^2]-(E[X])^2$.","misconception":"Squaring the expected value of $X$ only.","why":"Variance is the mean square minus the squared mean.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-41","topic":"rv","sub":"linear transformation of mean and variance","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Weekly cost $C$ (in thousands) has mean 12 and standard deviation 3. Cost in rupees is $Y = 1000\\,C + 5000$. What is the standard deviation of $Y$?","data":"","answer":{"value":3000,"display":"3000"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Adding a constant does not change spread; scaling by 1000 multiplies the standard deviation by 1000.","$\\mathrm{SD}(Y) = 3000$."],"hints":["Does adding 5000 change variability?","How does multiplying by 1000 change the SD?","Combine the effect of the shift and the scale."],"concept":"Shifts do not change spread; scales multiply it.","misconception":"Adding 5000 to the SD.","why":"The SD scales by the absolute value of the multiplier.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-42","topic":"rv","sub":"indicator sum","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A shop has 8 customers waiting. Each independently buys with probability 0.35. Each purchase earns ₹120. What is the expected revenue?","data":"","answer":{"value":336,"display":"336"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Expected number of purchases $=8(0.35)=2.8$.","Revenue $=2.8\\times120 = 336$."],"hints":["Write the count of purchases as a sum of indicators.","Expectation of a sum is the sum of expectations.","Multiply by the revenue per purchase."],"concept":"Linearity of expectation.","misconception":"Computing a full distribution unnecessarily.","why":"Each customer contributes $0.35\\times120$ on average.","fam":"rv:indicators"},{"id":"RVX-43","topic":"rv","sub":"expected number of matches","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Ten employees each draw a name at random from a hat (without replacement) in a gift swap. What is the expected number of employees who draw their own name?","data":"","answer":{"value":1,"display":"1"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Each person draws their own name with probability $1/10$.","By linearity (dependence does not matter) the expected count is $10\\times1/10 = 1$."],"hints":["Define an indicator for each person.","What is each indicator’s probability?","Linearity does not need independence."],"concept":"Linearity holds under dependence.","misconception":"Trying to compute the full derangement distribution.","why":"The answer 1 does not depend on the number of people.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-44","topic":"rv","sub":"variance of sum of independent","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent project costs have standard deviations 4 and 3 (in lakhs). What is the standard deviation of the total cost?","data":"","answer":{"value":5,"display":"5"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Variances add: $16+9=25$.","SD $=5$ (not $4+3=7$)."],"hints":["Variances, not standard deviations, add for independent variables.","Square each SD first.","Take the square root of the sum."],"concept":"Additivity of variance.","misconception":"Adding standard deviations.","why":"Independent variability combines as a Pythagorean sum.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-45","topic":"rv","sub":"covariance effect","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Returns $X$ and $Y$ have $\\mathrm{Var}(X)=0.04$, $\\mathrm{Var}(Y)=0.09$ and correlation $\\rho=-0.5$. What is the variance of the portfolio $X+Y$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.07,"display":"0.07"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.0005,"options":[],"solution":["$\\mathrm{Cov} = \\rho\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y = -0.5(0.2)(0.3) = -0.03$.","$\\mathrm{Var}(X+Y) = 0.04+0.09+2(-0.03) = 0.07$."],"hints":["Find the covariance from the correlation and SDs.","Use $\\mathrm{Var}(X+Y)$ with the covariance term.","Negative correlation reduces the total."],"concept":"Diversification lowers variance.","misconception":"Ignoring the covariance term.","why":"Negative covariance subtracts from the sum of variances.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-46","topic":"rv","sub":"expected value of a bet","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A raffle ticket costs ₹50. There is one prize of ₹5 000, two of ₹500 and the rest nothing; 200 tickets are sold. What is the expected net gain of one ticket?","data":"","answer":{"value":-20,"display":"-20"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Expected prize $=(5000+2\\cdot500)/200 = 30$.","Net $=30-50 = -20$."],"hints":["Total prize money divided by tickets gives the expected prize.","Subtract the ticket price.","A negative value means an expected loss."],"concept":"Expected net gain.","misconception":"Ignoring the ticket price.","why":"The expected prize is below the ticket price.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-47","topic":"rv","sub":"newsvendor-type decision","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A vendor buys papers at ₹4 and sells at ₹7; unsold ones are worth nothing. Demand is 40 with probability 0.4 and 60 with probability 0.6. Which stock level (40 or 60) gives the higher expected profit?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"60 papers (expected profit ₹132 against ₹120 for 40)"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"60 papers (expected profit ₹132 against ₹120 for 40)","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"40 papers, because it never leaves unsold stock","correct":false,"why":"Stock 40 gives a certain 120, which is lower than 132."},{"text":"They give the same expected profit","correct":false,"why":"Calculate: 120 against 132."},{"text":"Neither: profit is negative either way","correct":false,"why":"Both profits are positive."}],"solution":["Stock 40: always sells 40, profit $40\\times3 = 120$.","Stock 60: demand 40 gives $280-240 = 40$; demand 60 gives $180$.","Expected $0.4(40)+0.6(180) = 124$."],"hints":["Compute profit for each demand under each stock level.","Weight by the demand probabilities.","Compare the expected profits."],"concept":"Choosing an order quantity by expected profit.","misconception":"Choosing the safe option without computing.","why":"The extra upside outweighs the cost of unsold stock.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-48","topic":"rv","sub":"tail sum for expectation","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A non-negative integer variable $N$ has $P(N\\ge1)=0.9$, $P(N\\ge2)=0.5$, $P(N\\ge3)=0.2$ and $P(N\\ge4)=0$. What is $E[N]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":1.5999999999999999,"display":"1.6"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$E[N] = \\sum_{k\\ge1}P(N\\ge k) = 0.9+0.5+0.2 = 1.6$.","Check by the pmf: $P(N{=}0)=0.1$, $1\\!:0.4$, $2\\!:0.3$, $3\\!:0.2$."],"hints":["For non-negative integers, add up the tail probabilities.","Start the sum at $k=1$.","Add the three given values."],"concept":"Tail-sum formula.","misconception":"Adding the pmf values.","why":"Each $k$ contributes $P(N\\ge k)$.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-49","topic":"rv","sub":"conditional expectation by total expectation","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Customers are Retail (70%) with mean spend ₹800, or Corporate (30%) with mean spend ₹4 000. What is the overall mean spend per customer?","data":"","answer":{"value":1760,"display":"1760"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$E = 0.7(800)+0.3(4000) = 560+1200 = 1760$."],"hints":["Condition on the customer type.","Weight each mean by the type share.","Add the two weighted means together."],"concept":"Law of total expectation.","misconception":"Averaging the two means equally.","why":"The rarer group has a large effect because of its high mean.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-50","topic":"rv","sub":"variance by conditioning","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A variable is $N(0,\\,1)$ with probability 0.5 and $N(4,\\,1)$ with probability 0.5 (mean, variance). What is its overall variance?","data":"","answer":{"value":5,"display":"5"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Average within-group variance $=1$.","Mean $=2$; between-group variance $=0.5(4)+0.5(4)=4$.","Total $=1+4 = 5$."],"hints":["Average the within-group variances.","Add the variance of the group means.","Group means are 0 and 4 with mean 2."],"concept":"Total variance = within + between.","misconception":"Reporting only the within-group variance.","why":"Mixtures are more spread out than their components.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-51","topic":"rv","sub":"LOTUS on a squared variable","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$X$ takes the values $-1, 0, 2$ with probabilities $0.3, 0.2, 0.5$. What is $E[X^2]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":2.3,"display":"2.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$E[X^2] = (-1)^2(0.3)+0+2^2(0.5) = 0.3+2 = 2.3$."],"hints":["Square each value first, then weight.","Do not square $E[X]$.","Add the weighted squares."],"concept":"Expectation of a function of $X$.","misconception":"Computing $(E[X])^2$.","why":"$E[X^2]\\ne(E[X])^2$ unless the variance is zero.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-52","topic":"rv","sub":"standard deviation interpretation","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Two investments have the same mean annual return of 8%. Investment A has standard deviation 2%, B has 10%. What does this tell us?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"B’s returns are more spread out, so B is riskier in the sense of variability"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"B’s returns are more spread out, so B is riskier in the sense of variability","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"A has the higher average return","correct":false,"why":"Both have the same mean of 8%."},{"text":"B always returns more than A","correct":false,"why":"Spread does not mean B is always higher."},{"text":"The standard deviation says nothing about risk","correct":false,"why":"It is a standard measure of variability."}],"solution":["The mean describes the centre, the SD describes spread.","Same mean with a larger SD means more variability."],"hints":["Which number measures centre?","Which number measures spread?","Compare the SDs."],"concept":"Mean versus spread.","misconception":"Equating a high SD with a high mean.","why":"SD measures variability, not level.","fam":"rv:diagnose"},{"id":"RVX-53","topic":"rv","sub":"probability from a pmf with unknown constant","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A variable takes the values 1, 2, 3, 4 with probabilities proportional to $k$, $2k$, $3k$, $4k$ for a constant $c$ such that $P(X=k)=ck$. What is $E[X]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":3,"display":"3"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Total $c(1+2+3+4)=1$, so $c=0.1$.","$E=0.1(1+4+9+16) = 3$."],"hints":["Find $c$ so that the probabilities sum to 1.","Then weight each value.","Compute $0.1\\times(1+4+9+16)$."],"concept":"Normalising a pmf.","misconception":"Forgetting to find $c$.","why":"The pmf must sum to 1.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-54","topic":"rv","sub":"median vs mean","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Monthly claims are mostly small, but a few are very large. Which relationship is typical?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"The mean exceeds the median"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"The mean exceeds the median","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The mean is below the median","correct":false,"why":"Large claims pull the mean upward, not downward."},{"text":"The mean equals the median","correct":false,"why":"That holds for symmetric distributions."},{"text":"The mode is the largest value","correct":false,"why":"The mode is the most common value, here a small one."}],"solution":["Right skew pulls the mean toward the tail while the median stays near the bulk.","So mean $>$ median."],"hints":["Which measure is sensitive to extreme values?","Large claims pull in which direction?","Compare the two measures."],"concept":"Skewness and the mean.","misconception":"Assuming mean and median coincide.","why":"Heavy right tails raise the mean more than the median.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-55","topic":"rv","sub":"risk-neutral comparison","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Project A pays ₹100 000 with probability 0.6 and loses ₹20 000 otherwise. Project B pays ₹50 000 for certain. By how much does the expected payoff of A exceed that of B?","data":"","answer":{"value":2000,"display":"2000"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$E[A]=60\\,000-8\\,000 = 52\\,000$.","Difference from B: $2\\,000$."],"hints":["Compute the expected payoff of A.","B is certain, so its expectation is 50 000.","Take the difference."],"concept":"Comparing expected payoffs.","misconception":"Forgetting the loss branch.","why":"The loss has probability 0.4.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-56","topic":"rv","sub":"standardising","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Exam scores have mean 62 and standard deviation 8. A student scores 78. How many standard deviations above the mean is that?","data":"","answer":{"value":2,"display":"2"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$z = (78-62)/8 = 2$."],"hints":["Subtract the mean from the score.","Divide by the standard deviation.","Interpret in SD units."],"concept":"Standardisation.","misconception":"Dividing by the mean.","why":"A z-score of 2 means 2 SDs above the mean.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-57","topic":"rv","sub":"geometric mean waiting","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Each loan application is approved with probability 0.25, independently. What is the expected number of applications needed until the first approval (counting the successful one)?","data":"","answer":{"value":4,"display":"4"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["For a geometric waiting time with success probability $p$, $E = 1/p = 4$."],"hints":["This is a geometric waiting time.","Recall the mean of a geometric variable.","Take the reciprocal of $p$."],"concept":"Mean of a geometric variable.","misconception":"Using $p$ itself.","why":"Rarer success means longer waits.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-58","topic":"rv","sub":"maximum of two dice","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two fair dice are rolled. What is the expected value of the larger of the two numbers?","data":"","answer":{"value":4.472222222222222,"display":"4.4722"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(\\max = m) = (2m-1)/36$.","$E = \\sum m(2m-1)/36 = (1+6+15+28+45+66)/36 = 161/36 \\approx 4.47$."],"hints":["Count the ordered pairs for which the maximum is $m$.","There are $2m-1$ such pairs.","Weight and add."],"concept":"Expectation of an order statistic.","misconception":"Answering 3.5 or 4.","why":"The maximum is above the single-die mean of 3.5.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-59","topic":"rv","sub":"variance of a Bernoulli sum","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Of 50 independent customers, each defaults with probability 0.08. What is the variance of the number of defaults?","data":"","answer":{"value":3.68,"display":"3.68"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Each indicator has variance $0.08\\times0.92 = 0.0736$.","Independent sum: $50\\times0.0736 = 3.68$."],"hints":["Variance of one Bernoulli indicator is $p(1-p)$.","Independent variances add.","Multiply by 50."],"concept":"Variance of a count.","misconception":"Using $np$ as the variance.","why":"Variance is $np(1-p)$.","fam":"rv:variance"},{"id":"RVX-60","topic":"rv","sub":"Chebyshev bound","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Daily sales have mean 500 and standard deviation 40, with an unknown distribution. What is the Chebyshev <b>upper bound</b> on the probability that sales differ from the mean by 100 or more?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.16,"display":"0.16"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(|X-\\mu|\\ge k\\sigma) \\le 1/k^2$ with $k = 100/40 = 2.5$.","Bound $=1/6.25 = 0.16$."],"hints":["Express 100 in standard deviations.","Chebyshev: bound $=1/k^2$.","Compute $1/2.5^2$."],"concept":"Chebyshev bound.","misconception":"Using $1/k$.","why":"Chebyshev is conservative but needs no distribution.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-61","topic":"rv","sub":"expectation does not imply a typical outcome","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A lottery has expected winnings of ₹250 per ticket. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Over many tickets the average winning per ticket tends toward ₹250, though a single ticket will rarely pay exactly that"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Over many tickets the average winning per ticket tends toward ₹250, though a single ticket will rarely pay exactly that","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"A single ticket will usually pay about ₹250","correct":false,"why":"Individual outcomes can be far from the mean."},{"text":"Every ticket pays exactly ₹250","correct":false,"why":"The mean is not a guaranteed payment."},{"text":"You win ₹250 at least half the time","correct":false,"why":"The mean is not the median or a threshold."}],"solution":["Expectation is a long-run average, not a typical single outcome.","It may not even be a possible value."],"hints":["Think of the long-run average.","Is the expected value always attainable?","Pick the statement about many tickets."],"concept":"Meaning of expected value.","misconception":"Reading the mean as a typical single outcome.","why":"The mean summarises many repetitions.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"RVX-62","topic":"rv","sub":"expected cost with fixed and variable parts","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A call centre has a fixed cost of ₹5 000 per day plus ₹20 per call. Calls per day have mean 300 and standard deviation 30. What are the mean and standard deviation of daily cost? Give the <b>standard deviation</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":600,"display":"600"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Cost $=5000+20N$.","$E = 11\\,000$; $\\mathrm{SD} = 20\\times30 = 600$."],"hints":["Write cost as a linear function of calls.","The fixed cost does not affect spread.","Multiply the SD by 20."],"concept":"Linear cost transformation.","misconception":"Adding the fixed cost to the SD.","why":"Only the variable part scales the spread.","fam":"rv:decisions"},{"id":"RVX-63","topic":"rv","sub":"expected maximum of sensors","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent components each last 1, 2 or 3 months with equal probability. The system fails when the <b>first</b> component fails. What is the expected lifetime of the system in months?","data":"","answer":{"value":1.5555555555555556,"display":"1.5556"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(\\min=1)=5/9$, $P(\\min=2)=3/9$, $P(\\min=3)=1/9$.","$E = (5+6+3)/9 = 14/9 \\approx 1.56$."],"hints":["Count the pairs for each possible minimum.","Weight each minimum by its probability.","Add the three terms."],"concept":"Expectation of a minimum.","misconception":"Answering the single-component mean of 2.","why":"The first failure is earlier than a typical component.","fam":"rv:pmf-expectation"},{"id":"DST-01","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A call-centre agent resolves each call independently with probability 0.7. What is the probability that <b>exactly 3 of the next 5 calls</b> are resolved?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.30870000000000003,"display":"0.3087"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3087","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.30870000000000003},{"text":"0.0309","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of one specific arrangement of three resolved and two unresolved calls; there are $\\binom53 = 10$ arrangements.","v":0.03086999999999999},{"text":"0.1323","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly 2 resolved calls.","v":0.13230000000000006},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is the observed proportion, not a probability.","v":0.6}],"solution":["The number resolved is binomial$(5, 0.7)$.","$P(X=3) = \\binom53(0.7)^3(0.3)^2 = 10\\times0.343\\times0.09 = 0.3087$."],"hints":["Identify $n$, $p$ and the number of successes.","Count the arrangements of 3 successes among 5 calls.","Multiply the count by $p^3(1-p)^2$."],"concept":"Binomial probability mass function.","misconception":"Omitting the binomial coefficient.","why":"Each arrangement is equally likely and mutually exclusive.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-03","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial tail as evidence","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A trader claims she can predict the daily direction of an index. Over 12 days she is right on <b>9 days</b>. If she were guessing (probability 0.5 each day), what is the probability of doing <b>at least this well</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.072998046875,"display":"0.073"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.073","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.072998046875},{"text":"0.0537","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly 9 correct, not at least 9.","v":0.0537109375},{"text":"0.1938","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least 8 correct.","v":0.19384765625},{"text":"0.75","correct":false,"why":"This is the success proportion, not a probability under guessing.","v":0.75}],"solution":["$P(X\\ge9) = \\dfrac{\\binom{12}9+\\binom{12}{10}+\\binom{12}{11}+\\binom{12}{12}}{2^{12}} = \\dfrac{220+66+12+1}{4096} \\approx 0.073$."],"hints":["Under guessing the number correct is binomial$(12, 0.5)$.","\"At least this well\" means 9, 10, 11 or 12 correct.","Sum the four probabilities."],"concept":"Tail probability under a null model.","misconception":"Using the exact probability of 9.","why":"The tail probability measures how surprising the result is under guessing.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-04","topic":"dist","sub":"majority decoding","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A sensor sends each status bit 7 times. Each copy is received wrongly with probability 0.25, independently, and the receiver takes the majority. What is the probability that a bit is <b>decoded wrongly</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.070556640625,"display":"0.0706"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0706","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.070556640625},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"Repeating the bit and taking a majority reduces the error probability below a single copy’s 0.25.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.2436","correct":false,"why":"A majority of 7 needs at least 4 wrong copies; 3 wrong copies still gives the right majority.","v":0.24359130859375},{"text":"0.0577","correct":false,"why":"This counts only exactly 4 wrong copies; 5, 6 or 7 wrong copies also give a wrong decision.","v":0.05767822265625}],"solution":["The majority is wrong if at least 4 of the 7 copies are wrong.","$P = \\sum_{k=4}^{7}\\binom7k(0.25)^k(0.75)^{7-k} \\approx 0.0577+0.0115+0.0013+0.0001 = 0.0706$."],"hints":["How many wrong copies are needed for the majority to be wrong?","The number of wrong copies is binomial$(7, 0.25)$.","Sum the tail from 4 to 7."],"concept":"Majority rule via a binomial tail.","misconception":"Using only the exact count.","why":"Redundancy converts per-copy error into a smaller tail probability.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-05","topic":"dist","sub":"comparing reliability designs","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A module works with probability $p$, independently of others. A triple-module system works if <b>at least 2 of 3</b> modules work. For which values of $p$ is the triple system <b>more reliable than a single module</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$p > \\tfrac12$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$p > \\tfrac12$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$p < \\tfrac12$","correct":false,"why":"For $p<\\tfrac12$ the triple system is less reliable: $3p^2-2p^3 < p$."},{"text":"For all $p$","correct":false,"why":"The system is better only when modules are more likely to work than not."},{"text":"$p > \\tfrac23$","correct":false,"why":"The crossing point is 1/2: $3p^2-2p^3 - p = p(1-p)(2p-1)$."}],"solution":["$P(\\text{system works}) = 3p^2(1-p)+p^3 = 3p^2-2p^3$.","$3p^2-2p^3 > p \\iff 3p-2p^2 > 1 \\iff 2p^2-3p+1 < 0 \\iff (2p-1)(p-1) < 0$, i.e. $\\tfrac12 < p < 1$."],"hints":["Write the system reliability as a function of $p$.","Set up the inequality \"system reliability > $p$\".","Factor the quadratic."],"concept":"Comparing a redundant system with a single unit.","misconception":"Assuming redundancy always helps.","why":"Majority rule amplifies whichever side of 1/2 the units are on.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-06","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Customers arrive at a service counter as a Poisson process with a mean of 1.5 arrivals per minute. What is the probability that <b>at least 3</b> customers arrive in a given minute?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.19115316946194194,"display":"0.1912"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1912","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.19115316946194194},{"text":"0.1255","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of <i>exactly</i> 3 arrivals.","v":0.12551071508349176},{"text":"0.4422","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least 2 arrivals.","v":0.44217459962892547},{"text":"0.8745","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts only the probability of exactly 3; the terms for 0, 1 and 2 are the ones to remove.","v":0.8744892849165082}],"solution":["$P(X=0) = e^{-1.5} = 0.2231$, $P(X=1) = 0.3347$, $P(X=2) = 0.2510$.","$P(X\\ge3) = 1-0.8088 = 0.1912$."],"hints":["Use the complement of \"0, 1 or 2 arrivals\".","Evaluate $e^{-\\lambda}\\lambda^k/k!$ with $\\lambda = 1.5$.","Subtract the three terms from 1."],"concept":"Poisson tail via the complement.","misconception":"Computing only a single Poisson term.","why":"The infinite upper tail is easier as 1 minus a finite sum.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-07","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson approximation to rare events","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A system generates 12,000 independent five-digit codes. Each code consists of one digit repeated five times (such as 33333) with probability $10/10^5 = 10^{-4}$. Using a Poisson approximation, what is the probability that <b>at least one</b> of the 12,000 codes is of this type?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6988057880877979,"display":"0.6988"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6988","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6988057880877979},{"text":"1.2","correct":false,"why":"A probability cannot exceed 1; 1.2 is the expected number of such codes, not a probability.","v":1.2},{"text":"0.3012","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that none of the codes is of this type.","v":0.3011942119122021},{"text":"0.3614","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of <i>exactly one</i> such code.","v":0.3614330542946425}],"solution":["The number of such codes is approximately Poisson with $\\lambda = 12\\,000\\times10^{-4} = 1.2$.","$P(\\ge1) = 1-e^{-1.2} \\approx 0.699$."],"hints":["Find the expected number of such codes.","For rare independent events the count is approximately Poisson.","Use the complement: one minus the probability of zero such codes."],"concept":"Poisson approximation for a rare event over many trials.","misconception":"Reporting the expected count as a probability.","why":"$\\lambda = np$ summarises the rarity and the number of trials.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-09","topic":"dist","sub":"conditional Poisson","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Weekly outages at a data centre follow a Poisson law with mean 2. This week is known not to have been outage-free. Find the chance that it saw <b>three or more</b> outages.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.37392942900133735,"display":"0.3739"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3739","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.37392942900133735},{"text":"0.3233","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability of three or more outages; it must be divided by $P(\\ge1)$.","v":0.3233235838169365},{"text":"0.687","correct":false,"why":"This is the conditional probability of <i>two or more</i>.","v":0.6869647145006686},{"text":"0.2087","correct":false,"why":"This is the conditional probability of exactly 3.","v":0.20869019033288758}],"solution":["$P(X\\ge1) = 1-e^{-2} = 0.8647$. $P(X\\ge3) = 1-e^{-2}(1+2+2) = 0.3233$.","$P(X\\ge3\\mid X\\ge1) = 0.3233/0.8647 \\approx 0.374$."],"hints":["Find $P(X\\ge3)$ and $P(X\\ge1)$.","$\\{X\\ge3\\}\\subset\\{X\\ge1\\}$.","Divide the larger tail probability by the smaller one."],"concept":"Conditioning a Poisson variable on a tail event.","misconception":"Not dividing by the conditioning probability.","why":"Conditioning removes the zero-outage outcome.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-10","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson approximation","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A factory's items are independently defective with probability 0.004. In a batch of 400 items, use the Poisson approximation to find the probability of <b>at least 2</b> defective items.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.47506905321389603,"display":"0.4751"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4751","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.47506905321389603},{"text":"0.323","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly one defective item.","v":0.3230344287914486},{"text":"0.7981","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least one defective item.","v":0.7981034820053446},{"text":"0.2166","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least 3 defective items.","v":0.21664151018073718}],"solution":["$\\lambda = 400\\times0.004 = 1.6$.","$P(X\\ge2) = 1-e^{-1.6}(1+1.6) = 1-0.2019\\times2.6 = 0.475$."],"hints":["Find $\\lambda = np$.","Use the complement of \"0 or 1\".","Compute $e^{-1.6}$."],"concept":"Poisson approximation to a binomial tail.","misconception":"Miscounting the excluded terms.","why":"For small $p$ and large $n$ the binomial is close to Poisson.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-11","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson process","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Orders arrive at a warehouse as a Poisson process at a rate of 3 per hour. What is the probability that <b>no order</b> arrives in the next 20 minutes?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.36787944117144233,"display":"0.3679"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3679","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.36787944117144233},{"text":"0.0498","correct":false,"why":"This uses a full hour rather than 20 minutes.","v":0.049787068367863944},{"text":"0.6321","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that at least one order arrives.","v":0.6321205588285577},{"text":"2.06e-9","correct":false,"why":"This treats the number of minutes as the mean number of arrivals.","v":2.061153622438558e-9}],"solution":["In 20 minutes the mean number of orders is $3\\times\\tfrac13 = 1$.","$P(\\text{none}) = e^{-1} \\approx 0.368$."],"hints":["Convert the rate to the 20-minute interval.","The count in the interval is Poisson with that mean.","Evaluate $P(0) = e^{-\\lambda}$."],"concept":"Poisson process counts over an interval.","misconception":"Not rescaling the rate to the interval.","why":"The mean count is rate times time.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-12","topic":"dist","sub":"series of games","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"In a best-of-five playoff, one franchise is the stronger side and takes any single game with probability 0.65, independently of the other games. Find the chance that it is the franchise that wins the playoff.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.764830625,"display":"0.7648"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.7648","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.764830625},{"text":"0.65","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of winning a single game; the series has several games.","v":0.65},{"text":"0.3364","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly 3 wins in 5 games; 4 or 5 wins also give the series.","v":0.33641562499999994},{"text":"0.2746","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of winning 3 straight games only; the series can last longer.","v":0.27462500000000006}],"solution":["Imagine all five games are played. The stronger team wins the series iff it wins at least 3.","$P = \\binom53(0.65)^3(0.35)^2+\\binom54(0.65)^4(0.35)+(0.65)^5 = 0.3364+0.3124+0.1160 = 0.7648$."],"hints":["Pretend all five games are played regardless of the result.","The series is won exactly when at least 3 of the 5 games are won.","Sum the binomial terms for 3, 4 and 5 wins."],"concept":"Series probability via a binomial tail.","misconception":"Considering only the games actually played.","why":"Playing \"extra\" games after the series is decided does not change who has more wins.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-13","topic":"dist","sub":"expected duration","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two teams of equal strength meet in a best-of-five series. Find the <b>average number of games</b> such a series lasts.","data":"","answer":{"value":4.125,"display":"4.125"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"4.125","correct":true,"why":"","v":4.125},{"text":"4","correct":false,"why":"The series is not equally likely to end after 4 games; 3-0 and 4-1 outcomes have different probabilities.","v":4},{"text":"5","correct":false,"why":"This is the maximum length of the series.","v":5},{"text":"4.25","correct":false,"why":"The probabilities of lengths 3, 4, 5 are $0.25, 0.375, 0.375$; a 4-game series is as likely as a 5-game one.","v":4.25}],"solution":["The series ends in 3 games with probability $2(\\tfrac12)^3 = 0.25$.","In 4 games: $2\\binom32(\\tfrac12)^4 = 0.375$. In 5 games: $2\\binom42(\\tfrac12)^5 = 0.375$.","$E = 3(0.25)+4(0.375)+5(0.375) = 4.125$."],"hints":["What are the possible lengths of the series?","For length $n$, the winner takes the last game and 2 of the previous $n-1$.","Multiply by 2 for either team and weight each length."],"concept":"Expected length of a first-to-$k$ series.","misconception":"Assuming lengths are equally likely.","why":"Longer series require both teams to win games, which is more probable.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-14","topic":"dist","sub":"negative binomial","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A recruiter needs 3 candidates to accept an offer. Each candidate accepts independently with probability 0.4. What is the probability that the <b>third acceptance comes on exactly the 7th offer</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.12441600000000001,"display":"0.1244"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1244","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.12441600000000001},{"text":"0.2903","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly 3 acceptances in 7 offers; the 7th offer must itself be an acceptance.","v":0.29030400000000006},{"text":"0.1161","correct":false,"why":"This counts too many arrangements; the last offer is fixed as an acceptance.","v":0.11612160000000003},{"text":"0.00829","correct":false,"why":"This is one specific arrangement; the two earlier acceptances can be placed in $\\binom62 = 15$ ways.","v":0.008294400000000002}],"solution":["The 7th offer must be the 3rd acceptance, so exactly 2 acceptances occur among the first 6 offers.","$P = \\binom62(0.4)^2(0.6)^4\\times0.4 = 15\\times0.16\\times0.1296\\times0.4 \\approx 0.1244$."],"hints":["What must happen on offer 7?","In how many ways can 2 acceptances be placed among the first 6 offers?","Multiply by the probability of one arrangement."],"concept":"Negative binomial probability.","misconception":"Using the plain binomial formula.","why":"Fixing the last trial as a success makes the count of arrangements $\\binom{n-1}{r-1}$.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-16","topic":"dist","sub":"sum of Poisson variables","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Defects on three independent panels are Poisson with means 0.4, 1.1 and 0.8. What is the probability that the three panels together have <b>no defects at all</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.10025884372280375,"display":"0.1003"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1003","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.10025884372280375},{"text":"1.4525","correct":false,"why":"This adds probabilities that should be multiplied for independent panels (and exceeds 1).","v":1.4525200938509404},{"text":"0.4493","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that only the third panel has no defects.","v":0.44932896411722156},{"text":"0.8997","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least one defect.","v":0.8997411562771962}],"solution":["The total number of defects is Poisson with mean $0.4+1.1+0.8 = 2.3$.","$P(0) = e^{-2.3} \\approx 0.1003$."],"hints":["The sum of independent Poisson variables is Poisson.","Add the three means to get the mean of the total.","Evaluate $e^{-\\lambda}$."],"concept":"Sum of independent Poisson variables.","misconception":"Adding probabilities.","why":"Independent zero-defect events multiply: $e^{-0.4}e^{-1.1}e^{-0.8}$.","fam":"dist:clt"},{"id":"DST-17","topic":"dist","sub":"continuous tail probability","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The delay $X$ (in days) of a shipment has density $f(x) = 3/x^4$ for $x>1$ and 0 otherwise. What is $P(X>2)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.125,"display":"0.125"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.125","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.125},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X>2)$ for the density $2/x^3$; the integral of $3/x^4$ is $1/x^3$.","v":0.25},{"text":"0.1875","correct":false,"why":"This is $f(2)$, the density at 2, not the tail probability.","v":0.1875},{"text":"0.875","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(1<X<2)$, the complementary probability.","v":0.875}],"solution":["$P(X>2) = \\int_2^\\infty 3x^{-4}dx = \\left[-x^{-3}\\right]_2^\\infty = \\dfrac{1}{8}$."],"hints":["Integrate the density from 2 to infinity.","An antiderivative of $3x^{-4}$ is $-x^{-3}$.","Evaluate at the limits."],"concept":"Tail probability from a density.","misconception":"Evaluating the density instead of integrating.","why":"Probability is area under the density.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-19","topic":"dist","sub":"normal probability","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Monthly sales are normal with mean 250 and standard deviation 40 (units). What is the probability that sales exceed 300?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.10564983896265612,"display":"0.1056"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1056","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.10564983896265612},{"text":"0.8944","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(X<300)$, the complementary probability.","v":0.8943501610373439},{"text":"0.3773","correct":false,"why":"The standard deviation is 40, not 160: $z = 50/40$.","v":0.37733034626444617},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"The mean is 250, so 300 is above the mean and the probability is below one half.","v":0.5}],"solution":["$z = \\dfrac{300-250}{40} = 1.25$.","$P(X>300) = 1-\\Phi(1.25) = 1-0.8944 = 0.1056$."],"hints":["Standardise 300.","Look up $\\Phi(1.25)$.","Take the upper tail."],"concept":"Normal upper-tail probability.","misconception":"Reporting the lower tail.","why":"The $z$-score measures how many standard deviations above the mean 300 lies.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-20","topic":"dist","sub":"normal and geometric","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The annual peak level of a river is normal with mean 5.0 m and standard deviation 0.8 m, independently from year to year. A flood occurs when the peak exceeds 6.5 m. What is the probability that <b>no flood occurs in the next 20 years</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5393681620729962,"display":"0.5394"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.5394","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.5393681620729962},{"text":"0.0304","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of a flood in a single year.","v":0.030396297157620156},{"text":"0.4606","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least one flood in 20 years.","v":0.4606318379270038},{"text":"0.6079","correct":false,"why":"This adds the yearly flood probabilities, which is the expected number of floods, not a probability of none.","v":0.6079259431524031}],"solution":["$P(\\text{flood in a year}) = 1-\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{6.5-5.0}{0.8}\\right) = 1-\\Phi(1.875) = 0.0304$.","The years are independent: $P(\\text{none in 20}) = (1-0.0304)^{20} \\approx 0.537$."],"hints":["Find the probability of a flood in one year using the normal distribution.","Years are independent.","Raise the no-flood probability to the 20th power."],"concept":"Combining a normal tail with independent trials.","misconception":"Adding yearly probabilities.","why":"No flood in 20 years requires no flood in each year.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-21","topic":"dist","sub":"normal quantile","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Delivery times are normal with mean 45 minutes and standard deviation 6 minutes. Find the time $t$ such that <b>95%</b> of deliveries are completed within $t$ minutes.","data":"","answer":{"value":54.8694,"display":"54.8694"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"54.8694","correct":true,"why":"","v":54.8694},{"text":"56.76","correct":false,"why":"The value 1.96 is the 97.5% point; the 95% point is 1.645.","v":56.76},{"text":"52.68","correct":false,"why":"The value 1.28 is the 90% point.","v":52.68},{"text":"51","correct":false,"why":"One standard deviation above the mean covers only about 84% of deliveries.","v":51}],"solution":["The 95% point of the standard normal is $z = 1.645$.","$t = 45+1.645\\times6 \\approx 54.9$ minutes."],"hints":["Find $z$ with $\\Phi(z) = 0.95$.","Convert back with $t = \\mu + z\\sigma$.","Round to a sensible number of decimals."],"concept":"Using the normal quantile.","misconception":"Using the 97.5% point for a one-sided 95%.","why":"A one-sided 95% limit uses $z = 1.645$.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-22","topic":"dist","sub":"normal approximation to binomial","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A market-research firm knows that 40% of buyers prefer option A. Among 150 independently sampled buyers, approximately what is the probability that <b>55 to 65 of them (both counts included)</b> prefer A? Use the normal approximation with a continuity correction.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6406827166030551,"display":"0.6407"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6407","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6406827166030551},{"text":"0.5953","correct":false,"why":"Without the continuity correction the interval is too narrow; use 54.5 and 65.5.","v":0.5953433508289963},{"text":"0.1214","correct":false,"why":"The standard deviation is $\\sqrt{150(0.4)(0.6)} = 6$, not the variance 36.","v":0.12142651194979409},{"text":"0.9199","correct":false,"why":"The window is 55 to 65, not 50 to 70.","v":0.919881772949275}],"solution":["$\\mu = 150(0.4) = 60$, $\\sigma = \\sqrt{150(0.4)(0.6)} = 6$.","$P(55\\le X\\le65) \\approx \\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{65.5-60}{6}\\right)-\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{54.5-60}{6}\\right) = 2\\Phi(0.917)-1 \\approx 0.640$."],"hints":["Find the mean and standard deviation of the binomial count.","Apply the continuity correction: use 54.5 and 65.5.","Standardise and subtract the cumulative probabilities."],"concept":"Normal approximation with continuity correction.","misconception":"Using the variance as the standard deviation.","why":"The continuity correction accounts for approximating a discrete distribution by a continuous one.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-23","topic":"dist","sub":"two-sided normal probability","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Bolt diameters are normal with mean 12.00 mm and standard deviation 0.04 mm. Bolts must be within $\\pm0.06$ mm of 12.00 mm. What proportion of bolts is <b>within tolerance</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8663855425594584,"display":"0.8664"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.8664","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.8663855425594584},{"text":"0.1336","correct":false,"why":"This is the proportion outside the tolerance (defective).","v":0.13361445744054157},{"text":"0.9332","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of being below the upper limit; the lower limit must also be respected.","v":0.9331927712797292},{"text":"0.0478","correct":false,"why":"The deviation must be standardised: $z = 0.06/0.04 = 1.5$.","v":0.047844506633004436}],"solution":["$z = \\pm\\dfrac{0.06}{0.04} = \\pm1.5$.","$P = \\Phi(1.5)-\\Phi(-1.5) = 2(0.9332)-1 = 0.8664$."],"hints":["Express the tolerance limits as $z$-scores.","By symmetry $\\Phi(-z) = 1-\\Phi(z)$.","Compute $2\\Phi(1.5)-1$."],"concept":"Probability within a symmetric interval.","misconception":"Forgetting to standardise.","why":"The central region between $\\pm z$ has probability $2\\Phi(z)-1$.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-25","topic":"dist","sub":"error probabilities of a test","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A tester suspects a coin favours heads. He tosses it 400 times and will announce \"biased\" once heads reach <b>220 or more</b>. Suppose the coin is in fact fair. What is the chance he announces \"biased\"? Use a normal approximation with continuity correction.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.025587989770653463,"display":"0.0256"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0256","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.025587989770653463},{"text":"0.0228","correct":false,"why":"Without the continuity correction the boundary is at 220 rather than 219.5.","v":0.022750062014251893},{"text":"0.4227","correct":false,"why":"The standard deviation is $\\sqrt{400\\cdot0.25} = 10$, not 100.","v":0.4226964917132092},{"text":"0.9744","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of fewer than 220 heads, the correct decision.","v":0.9744120102293465}],"solution":["For a fair coin, $\\mu = 200$ and $\\sigma = \\sqrt{400(0.5)(0.5)} = 10$.","$P(X\\ge220) \\approx 1-\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{219.5-200}{10}\\right) = 1-\\Phi(1.95) \\approx 0.0256$."],"hints":["Under the fair-coin assumption, find $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$.","The rejection region starts at 220; use 219.5.","Take the upper tail."],"concept":"Type I error probability through the normal approximation.","misconception":"Reporting the correct-decision probability.","why":"The probability of a false alarm is the probability of the rejection region under the null.","fam":"dist:diagnose"},{"id":"DST-26","topic":"dist","sub":"normal approximation to binomial","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"8% of items from a line are non-conforming, independently. Use the normal approximation (with continuity correction) to find the probability that a batch of 300 contains <b>at most 15</b> non-conforming items.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.0352316512261864,"display":"0.0352"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0352","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.0352316512261864},{"text":"0.0277","correct":false,"why":"The continuity correction uses 15.5 for an \"at most 15\" event.","v":0.027725326697493568},{"text":"0.9648","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of more than 15 non-conforming items.","v":0.9647683487738136},{"text":"0.3501","correct":false,"why":"The standard deviation is the square root of the variance 22.08.","v":0.3501321802362262}],"solution":["$\\mu = 300(0.08) = 24$, $\\sigma^2 = 300(0.08)(0.92) = 22.08$, $\\sigma = 4.70$.","$P(X\\le15) \\approx \\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{15.5-24}{4.70}\\right) = \\Phi(-1.81) \\approx 0.035$."],"hints":["Find the mean and standard deviation of the count.","For \"at most 15\" use 15.5.","Standardise and read $\\Phi$."],"concept":"Normal approximation lower tail.","misconception":"Omitting the continuity correction.","why":"A count of at most 15 corresponds to the continuous region below 15.5.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-27","topic":"dist","sub":"exponential memorylessness","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The length of a phone call is exponential with mean 4 minutes. A call has already been going for <b>over 6 minutes</b>. What is the chance it continues for <b>at least another 9 minutes</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.10539922456186433,"display":"0.1054"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1054","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.10539922456186433},{"text":"0.0235","correct":false,"why":"This is the unconditional probability that the call lasts more than 15 minutes.","v":0.02351774585600911},{"text":"0.2231","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of having lasted more than 6 minutes.","v":0.22313016014842982},{"text":"0.8946","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that it ends within the next 9 minutes.","v":0.8946007754381357}],"solution":["By the memoryless property the remaining duration is again exponential with mean 4.","$P(\\text{at least 9 more}) = e^{-9/4} \\approx 0.105$.","Check: $e^{-15/4}/e^{-6/4} = e^{-9/4}$."],"hints":["What does the memoryless property say about the remaining time?","The rate is $1/4$ per minute.","Evaluate $e^{-\\lambda t}$ for $t = 9$."],"concept":"Memorylessness of the exponential distribution.","misconception":"Thinking the elapsed time matters.","why":"The exponential distribution forgets its past.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-28","topic":"dist","sub":"uniform lifetime: conditional survival","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The total lifetime of a battery is uniformly distributed between 0 and 60 hours. It has already lasted 20 hours. What is the probability that it lasts <b>at least 25 more hours</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.375,"display":"0.375"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.375","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.375},{"text":"0.5353","correct":false,"why":"This is the answer for an exponential lifetime; for a uniform lifetime the age matters.","v":0.5352614285189903},{"text":"0.5833","correct":false,"why":"This ignores the information that the battery has already lasted 20 hours.","v":0.5833333333333334},{"text":"0.625","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the battery fails within the next 25 hours.","v":0.625}],"solution":["Given $T>20$, $T$ is uniform on $(20,60)$, a length of 40.","$P(T\\ge45\\mid T>20) = \\dfrac{60-45}{40} = \\dfrac{15}{40} = 0.375$."],"hints":["What is the conditional distribution of $T$ given $T>20$?","Lasting 25 more hours means $T\\ge45$.","Divide the favourable length by the remaining length."],"concept":"Conditional survival for a uniform lifetime.","misconception":"Applying the memoryless property where it does not hold.","why":"A uniform lifetime has an increasing failure rate.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-29","topic":"dist","sub":"Chebyshev","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A random variable has mean 50 and variance 25. What lower bound does Chebyshev's inequality give for $P(40<X<60)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.75,"display":"0.75"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.75","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.75},{"text":"-1.5","correct":false,"why":"The bound is $\\sigma^2/\\varepsilon^2 = 25/100$; here $\\varepsilon = 10$, not $\\sqrt{10}$.","v":-1.5},{"text":"0.95","correct":false,"why":"The 95% figure comes from a normal distribution, which is not assumed.","v":0.95},{"text":"0.25","correct":false,"why":"This is the upper bound on the probability of being at least 10 away from the mean.","v":0.25}],"solution":["$P(|X-50|\\ge10) \\le \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{10^2} = \\dfrac{25}{100} = 0.25$.","So $P(40<X<60) \\ge 1-0.25 = 0.75$."],"hints":["Express the event as $|X-\\mu|<10$.","Apply $P(|X-\\mu|\\ge\\varepsilon)\\le\\sigma^2/\\varepsilon^2$.","Take the complement."],"concept":"Chebyshev bound.","misconception":"Using normal-distribution values.","why":"Chebyshev holds for any distribution with finite variance.","fam":"dist:bounds"},{"id":"DST-30","topic":"dist","sub":"Markov","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"The waiting time at a clinic is non-negative with a mean of 12 minutes. What is the best bound that Markov's inequality gives for the probability that a patient waits <b>at least 30 minutes</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4,"display":"0.4"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.4},{"text":"2.5","correct":false,"why":"This inverts the ratio; Markov’s bound is $E[X]/a$.","v":2.5},{"text":"0.6","correct":false,"why":"This is a lower bound for waiting less than 30 minutes, not an upper bound for waiting at least 30.","v":0.6},{"text":"0.0133","correct":false,"why":"The bound is $E[X]/a$ with no further division.","v":0.013333333333333334}],"solution":["For a non-negative variable, $P(X\\ge a) \\le E[X]/a$.","$P(X\\ge30) \\le 12/30 = 0.4$."],"hints":["Is the variable non-negative with a known mean?","Markov: $P(X\\ge a)\\le E[X]/a$.","Substitute the mean and the threshold into the bound."],"concept":"Markov’s inequality.","misconception":"Inverting the ratio.","why":"A non-negative variable with a small mean cannot be large too often.","fam":"dist:bounds"},{"id":"DST-31","topic":"dist","sub":"Chebyshev sample size","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Daily revenue has a standard deviation of 8 (thousand) and an unknown mean. How many independent days of data are needed so that, by Chebyshev's inequality, the sample mean is within 3 of the true mean with probability <b>at least 0.95</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":143,"display":"143"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"143","correct":true,"why":"","v":143},{"text":"142","correct":false,"why":"Rounding down gives a bound slightly above 0.05; the sample size must be rounded up.","v":142},{"text":"427","correct":false,"why":"The deviation must be squared: $\\sigma^2/(n\\varepsilon^2)$.","v":427},{"text":"28","correct":false,"why":"This is the central-limit-theorem answer; Chebyshev is more conservative.","v":28}],"solution":["$P(|\\bar X-\\mu|\\ge3) \\le \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n\\cdot3^2} = \\dfrac{64}{9n}$.","Require $\\dfrac{64}{9n} \\le 0.05$, so $n \\ge \\dfrac{64}{0.45} = 142.2$.","$n = 143$."],"hints":["The variance of the sample mean is $\\sigma^2/n$.","Apply Chebyshev with $\\varepsilon = 3$.","Solve $64/(9n)\\le0.05$ and round up."],"concept":"Sample size from Chebyshev’s inequality.","misconception":"Using $\\varepsilon$ instead of $\\varepsilon^2$.","why":"Chebyshev needs no distributional assumption but gives larger samples than the CLT.","fam":"dist:bounds"},{"id":"DST-32","topic":"dist","sub":"central limit theorem for Poisson sums","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Thirty independent random counts each have a Poisson distribution with mean 2. Approximate the probability that their <b>total exceeds 70</b>, using the normal approximation with a continuity correction.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.08762218212366724,"display":"0.0876"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0876","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.08762218212366724},{"text":"0.0984","correct":false,"why":"For \"greater than 70\" with integers the boundary is 70.5, not 70.","v":0.09835286643476349},{"text":"0.4305","correct":false,"why":"The standard deviation is $\\sqrt{60}\\approx7.75$, not 60.","v":0.43053981859031265},{"text":"0","correct":false,"why":"The mean and variance of the sum are $30\\times2 = 60$.","v":0}],"solution":["The sum has mean $30\\times2 = 60$ and variance $60$ (so $\\sigma = 7.75$).","$P(S>70) = P(S\\ge71) \\approx 1-\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{70.5-60}{7.75}\\right) = 1-\\Phi(1.355) \\approx 0.088$."],"hints":["Find the mean and variance of the sum.","$S>70$ means $S\\ge71$; use 70.5.","Standardise and take the upper tail."],"concept":"CLT with continuity correction.","misconception":"Using the wrong boundary or variance.","why":"A sum of independent Poisson variables is itself Poisson; the CLT approximates it by a normal.","fam":"dist:clt"},{"id":"DST-33","topic":"dist","sub":"central limit theorem for rounding errors","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"100 weights are each rounded to the nearest gram, and the rounding errors are independent and uniform on $(-0.5, 0.5)$. Approximate the probability that the total rounding error exceeds 2 grams <b>in absolute value</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.48842216943309724,"display":"0.4884"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.4884","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.48842216943309724},{"text":"0.2442","correct":false,"why":"This is a one-sided tail; \"in absolute value\" needs both tails.","v":0.24421108471654862},{"text":"0.8103","correct":false,"why":"The standard deviation of the sum is $\\sqrt{100/12} \\approx 2.89$, not $100/12$.","v":0.8103303499623684},{"text":"4.29e-12","correct":false,"why":"The sum of 100 errors has variance $100/12$, not $1/12$.","v":4.28879154412698e-12}],"solution":["Each error has variance $1/12$, so the sum has variance $100/12 = 8.33$ and $\\sigma = 2.89$.","$P(|S|>2) \\approx 2\\left[1-\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{2}{2.89}\\right)\\right] = 2(1-0.7557) \\approx 0.489$."],"hints":["The variance of a uniform variable on $(a,b)$ is $(b-a)^2/12$.","Add 100 variances and take the square root.","Include both tails."],"concept":"CLT for a sum of uniform errors.","misconception":"Using one tail.","why":"Independent rounding errors accumulate like a random walk with standard deviation growing as $\\sqrt n$.","fam":"dist:diagnose"},{"id":"DST-34","topic":"dist","sub":"central limit theorem for a running total","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Rolls of a fair four-faced die (faces 1 to 4) are added up one by one, stopping the moment the total passes 120. Estimate the chance that the stopping takes <b>50 or more rolls</b>.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3991489772767204,"display":"0.3991"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3991","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.3991489772767204},{"text":"0.6009","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that fewer than 50 rolls are needed; the event asked for needs the total after 49 rolls to be at most 120.","v":0.6008510227232796},{"text":"0.2846","correct":false,"why":"At least 50 rolls are needed iff the total after <i>49</i> rolls is still at most 120.","v":0.2846067838139715},{"text":"0.3747","correct":false,"why":"The continuity correction uses 120.5 for the event \"total at most 120\".","v":0.37469715709789175}],"solution":["At least 50 rolls are needed iff $S_{49} \\le 120$.","$S_{49}$ has mean $49(2.5) = 122.5$ and variance $49(1.25) = 61.25$ ($\\sigma = 7.83$).","$P(S_{49}\\le120) \\approx \\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{120.5-122.5}{7.83}\\right) = \\Phi(-0.256) \\approx 0.399$."],"hints":["Translate \"at least 50 rolls needed\" into a statement about the sum of 49 rolls.","Find the mean and variance of $S_{49}$.","Apply the normal approximation with a continuity correction."],"concept":"Relating a stopping count to a partial sum.","misconception":"Using $S_{50}$ instead of $S_{49}$.","why":"The number of rolls exceeds 49 exactly when the first 49 rolls have not yet passed the threshold.","fam":"dist:clt"},{"id":"DST-35","topic":"dist","sub":"CLT for stock levels","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A part with a lifetime of mean 80 hours and standard deviation 20 hours is swapped for a new one whenever it fails. How many spares should be kept so that, with probability at least <b>0.95</b>, the machine can run 1,500 hours? Use the central limit theorem.","data":"","answer":{"value":21,"display":"21"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"21","correct":true,"why":"","v":21},{"text":"19","correct":false,"why":"This is the number needed on average; a 95% guarantee needs a safety margin.","v":19},{"text":"20","correct":false,"why":"At $n=20$ the probability of lasting 1,500 hours is only about 0.89.","v":20},{"text":"30","correct":false,"why":"This is more than necessary: $n=21$ already gives a probability above 0.95.","v":30}],"solution":["With $n$ parts the total lifetime has mean $80n$ and standard deviation $20\\sqrt n$.","Require $P(S_n\\ge1500) \\ge 0.95 \\iff \\dfrac{80n-1500}{20\\sqrt n} \\ge 1.645$.","Testing: $n=20$: $z = 100/89.4 = 1.12$; $n=21$: $z = 180/91.65 = 1.96$, so $n = 21$."],"hints":["The total lifetime of $n$ parts has mean $80n$ and sd $20\\sqrt n$.","Set up $P(S_n\\ge1500)\\ge0.95$ with $z = 1.645$.","Try integer values of $n$ near $1500/80$."],"concept":"Choosing a stock size with the CLT.","misconception":"Ignoring variability.","why":"Safety stock covers the variability of the total lifetime.","fam":"dist:clt"},{"id":"DST-37","topic":"dist","sub":"random walk","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A price moves up one unit with probability 0.4 and down one unit with probability 0.6 each day, independently. What is the probability that after <b>six days</b> the price is back at its starting level?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.27648,"display":"0.2765"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2765","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.27648},{"text":"0.0138","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of one specific order of three ups and three downs; there are $\\binom63 = 20$.","v":0.013824000000000001},{"text":"0.3125","correct":false,"why":"This uses probability $1/2$ for each move; here up has probability 0.4.","v":0.3125},{"text":"0.4","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of a single up move.","v":0.4}],"solution":["Returning to the start requires 3 ups and 3 downs in 6 days.","$P = \\binom63(0.4)^3(0.6)^3 = 20\\times0.064\\times0.216 = 0.2765$."],"hints":["How many up moves are needed to end at zero net change?","The number of up moves is binomial.","Compute $P(X=3)$."],"concept":"Binomial model for a random walk.","misconception":"Using probability 1/2.","why":"Net change is the number of ups minus the number of downs.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-39","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial tail","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Ten fair dice are rolled. What is the probability that <b>at least three</b> of them show a 6?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.2247732021287403,"display":"0.2248"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.2248","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.2247732021287403},{"text":"0.155","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly three sixes.","v":0.15504535957425192},{"text":"0.5155","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least two sixes.","v":0.5154832513304626},{"text":"0.8385","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of at least one six.","v":0.8384944171101543}],"solution":["$P(X\\le2) = (5/6)^{10}+10(1/6)(5/6)^{9}+45(1/6)^2(5/6)^{8} = 0.1615+0.3230+0.2907 = 0.7752$.","$P(X\\ge3) = 0.2248$."],"hints":["Use the complement of 0, 1 or 2 sixes.","Compute the three binomial terms with $n=10$, $p=1/6$.","Subtract from 1."],"concept":"Binomial tail via the complement.","misconception":"Using only one term.","why":"The upper tail has many terms; the lower tail only three.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-41","topic":"dist","sub":"design with a probability target","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"How large should the on-call roster be so that, with probability at least <b>0.90</b>, three or more of the rostered nurses can come in? Each rostered nurse is independently available with probability 0.7.","data":"","answer":{"value":6,"display":"6"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"6","correct":true,"why":"","v":6},{"text":"5","correct":false,"why":"With 5 nurses $P(\\ge3) = 0.837$, below 0.90.","v":5},{"text":"3","correct":false,"why":"With exactly 3 nurses all must be available: $0.7^3 = 0.343$.","v":3},{"text":"7","correct":false,"why":"This is more than needed: with 6 nurses the probability is already about 0.930.","v":7}],"solution":["$n=4$: $P(\\ge3) = 4(0.7)^3(0.3)+0.7^4 = 0.652$.","$n=5$: $0.837$.","$n=6$: $P(\\ge3) = 1-[0.3^6+6(0.7)(0.3)^5+15(0.49)(0.3)^4] = 1-0.0705 = 0.930$.","The smallest pool is 6."],"hints":["For each pool size $n$ compute $P(X\\ge3)$ with $p=0.7$.","Use the complement: $1-P(X\\le2)$.","Increase $n$ until the probability reaches 0.90."],"concept":"Sizing a pool with a binomial reliability target.","misconception":"Using the expected availability only.","why":"More on-call staff raise the chance that at least three are free.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-42","topic":"dist","sub":"comparing events with uniform numbers","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Ten numbers are drawn independently and uniformly from $(0,1)$. Which is more likely: (a) <b>exactly five</b> of them exceed $\\tfrac12$, or (b) <b>at least two</b> of them exceed 0.9?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"(b): about 0.264 compared with 0.246"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"(b): about 0.264 compared with 0.246","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"(a): about 0.264 compared with 0.246","correct":false,"why":"The values are swapped: $P(a) = 252/1024 = 0.246$."},{"text":"They are equally likely","correct":false,"why":"The two probabilities are 0.246 and 0.264."},{"text":"(a), because five of ten is the most likely count","correct":false,"why":"It is the most likely count for event (a), but event (b) has probability 0.264, slightly higher."}],"solution":["Each number exceeds $\\tfrac12$ with probability $\\tfrac12$: $P(a) = \\binom{10}5/2^{10} = 252/1024 = 0.246$.","Each exceeds 0.9 with probability 0.1: $P(b) = 1-0.9^{10}-10(0.1)(0.9)^9 = 1-0.3487-0.3874 = 0.264$."],"hints":["Turn each number into a success/failure for the event in question.","Event (a) has $p = 1/2$; event (b) has $p = 0.1$.","Compute the two binomial probabilities."],"concept":"Binomial reasoning for events defined by uniform variables.","misconception":"Choosing by intuition.","why":"Each number independently exceeds the cutoff with probability $1-$cutoff.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-45","topic":"dist","sub":"normal approximation to a tail count","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A cinema has 9,000 members. Each member's birthday month is equally likely to be any of the 12 months, independently. What is the approximate probability that <b>at least 780</b> members have a July birthday? (Use the normal approximation with continuity correction.)","data":"","answer":{"value":0.13027637447306484,"display":"0.1303"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.1303","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.13027637447306484},{"text":"0.1263","correct":false,"why":"For \"at least 780\" the continuity-corrected boundary is 779.5.","v":0.12627971029450324},{"text":"0.8697","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of fewer than 780 July birthdays.","v":0.8697236255269352},{"text":"0.4843","correct":false,"why":"The standard deviation is $\\sqrt{687.5} \\approx 26.2$, not 750.","v":0.4843122532399372}],"solution":["The number of July birthdays is binomial$(9000, 1/12)$: $\\mu = 750$, $\\sigma^2 = 9000\\cdot\\tfrac1{12}\\cdot\\tfrac{11}{12} = 687.5$, $\\sigma = 26.2$.","$P(X\\ge780) \\approx 1-\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{779.5-750}{26.2}\\right) = 1-\\Phi(1.125) \\approx 0.130$."],"hints":["Identify the binomial parameters.","For \"at least 780\" use the boundary 779.5.","Standardise and take the upper tail."],"concept":"Upper-tail normal approximation.","misconception":"Using the wrong standard deviation.","why":"Large $n$ with moderate $np$ makes the normal approximation accurate.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-47","topic":"dist","sub":"geometric","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A firm is inspected in any given year with probability 0.15, independently across years. What is the probability that its <b>first inspection occurs in year 4 or later</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6141249999999999,"display":"0.6141"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6141","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6141249999999999},{"text":"0.522","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of no inspection in years 1 to 4; \"year 4 or later\" needs only years 1 to 3 to be clear.","v":0.5220062499999999},{"text":"0.0921","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that the first inspection occurs exactly in year 4.","v":0.09211874999999999},{"text":"0.3859","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of an inspection within the first three years.","v":0.3858750000000001}],"solution":["The first inspection is in year 4 or later iff there is no inspection in years 1, 2 and 3.","$P = 0.85^3 = 0.6141$."],"hints":["What must happen in the first three years?","Years are independent.","Multiply the three no-inspection probabilities."],"concept":"Geometric survival probability.","misconception":"Including year 4 in the no-inspection run.","why":"$P(T\\ge k) = (1-p)^{k-1}$.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-48","topic":"dist","sub":"geometric spread","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Each application succeeds independently with probability 0.2, and applications continue until the first success. What is the <b>standard deviation</b> of the number of applications?","data":"","answer":{"value":4.472135954999579,"display":"4.4721"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"4.4721","correct":true,"why":"","v":4.472135954999579},{"text":"5","correct":false,"why":"This is the mean number of applications, 5.","v":5},{"text":"20","correct":false,"why":"This is the variance $(1-p)/p^2 = 20$, not the standard deviation.","v":20},{"text":"0.4","correct":false,"why":"This is the standard deviation of a single Bernoulli trial.","v":0.4}],"solution":["For a geometric variable with success probability $p$: $\\mathrm{Var} = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2} = \\dfrac{0.8}{0.04} = 20$.","Standard deviation $= \\sqrt{20} \\approx 4.47$."],"hints":["Recall the variance of a geometric variable.","Substitute $p = 0.2$.","Take the square root."],"concept":"Variance and standard deviation of a geometric variable.","misconception":"Reporting the mean or the variance.","why":"Waiting times for rare successes are very variable.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-51","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial standard deviation","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"30% of website visitors click an advertisement, independently. For 40 visitors, what is the <b>standard deviation</b> of the number who click?","data":"","answer":{"value":2.8982753492378874,"display":"2.8983"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"2.8983","correct":true,"why":"","v":2.8982753492378874},{"text":"8.4","correct":false,"why":"This is the variance $np(1-p) = 8.4$, not the standard deviation.","v":8.399999999999999},{"text":"12","correct":false,"why":"This is the mean number of clicks, 12.","v":12},{"text":"3.4641","correct":false,"why":"This omits the factor $(1-p)$ in the variance.","v":3.4641016151377544}],"solution":["$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = np(1-p) = 40(0.3)(0.7) = 8.4$.","$\\mathrm{SD} = \\sqrt{8.4} \\approx 2.90$."],"hints":["Recall the variance of a binomial variable.","Substitute $n=40$, $p=0.3$.","Take the square root."],"concept":"Binomial variance.","misconception":"Reporting variance.","why":"Variance is largest when $p$ is near $1/2$.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-52","topic":"dist","sub":"compound probabilities","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"15% of online orders are returned, and 60% of returned items are restocked (the rest are scrapped). Among 12 independent orders, what is the probability that <b>exactly 3</b> are returned <b>and restocked</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.06863138134481549,"display":"0.0686"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0686","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.06863138134481549},{"text":"0.172","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that exactly 3 orders are returned, not returned and restocked.","v":0.17197558261527826},{"text":"0.0125","correct":false,"why":"This applies the restocking probability directly to all orders.","v":0.012457082880000005},{"text":"0.000354","correct":false,"why":"The probabilities should be multiplied (returned and then restocked), not added.","v":0.0003540515899658203}],"solution":["An order is \"returned and restocked\" with probability $0.15\\times0.6 = 0.09$.","The number of such orders is binomial$(12, 0.09)$.","$P(X=3) = \\binom{12}{3}(0.09)^3(0.91)^9 \\approx 0.0686$."],"hints":["What is the probability that a single order is returned and restocked?","Treat that event as one Bernoulli trial.","Use the binomial pmf with $n=12$."],"concept":"Binomial model with a compound event.","misconception":"Adding probabilities.","why":"Conditional steps multiply along a branch.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-53","topic":"dist","sub":"system reliability","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A circuit board works only if all 3 of its independent components work, each with probability 0.95. Among 8 independent boards, what is the probability that <b>at least 7</b> work?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6805698399335117,"display":"0.6806"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6806","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6805698399335117},{"text":"0.9428","correct":false,"why":"This uses the probability of a single component working for the whole board.","v":0.9427553497265624},{"text":"0.3886","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly 7 working boards; 8 working also qualifies.","v":0.38858081559473934},{"text":"0.8574","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that one board works.","v":0.8573749999999999}],"solution":["A board works with probability $0.95^3 = 0.8574$.","$P(X\\ge7) = 8(0.8574)^7(0.1426)+(0.8574)^8 = 0.3873+0.2921 = 0.680$."],"hints":["Find the probability that one board works.","The number of working boards is binomial$(8, 0.8574)$.","Add the terms for 7 and 8."],"concept":"Two-level reliability model.","misconception":"Using the component probability for boards.","why":"Reliability multiplies within a series system and then enters a binomial count.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-54","topic":"dist","sub":"error probability of a decision rule","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A buyer samples 25 items from a shipment and rejects the supplier's claim that \"at least 90% conform\" if <b>at most 20</b> sampled items conform. If exactly 90% of the shipment actually conforms, what is the probability that the claim is wrongly rejected? (Use the binomial distribution with $p=0.9$.)","data":"","answer":{"value":0.09799362119546465,"display":"0.098"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.098","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.09799362119546465},{"text":"0.902","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that 21 or more conform, i.e. the claim is not rejected.","v":0.9020063788045354},{"text":"0.0334","correct":false,"why":"The rejection region includes 20 conforming items; 20 must be included.","v":0.03339994461149522},{"text":"0.0646","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of exactly 20 conforming items; the rejection region is $\\le 20$.","v":0.06459367658396943}],"solution":["The number conforming is binomial$(25, 0.9)$. The claim is rejected when $X\\le20$.","$P(X\\le20) = 1-P(X\\ge21) \\approx 1-0.9020 = 0.098$."],"hints":["What is the distribution of the number of conforming items when $p = 0.9$?","The rejection region is $X\\le20$.","Use the complement of $X\\ge21$."],"concept":"Probability of a wrong decision.","misconception":"Using the complement incorrectly.","why":"A decision rule has a false-rejection probability determined by the sampling distribution under the claim.","fam":"dist:diagnose"},{"id":"DST-55","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial: exactly k","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A call-centre agent resolves 70% of calls on the first contact, independently. Out of 6 calls, what is the probability that exactly 4 are resolved on the first contact?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.324135,"display":"0.3241"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$P = \\binom64(0.7)^4(0.3)^2 = 15(0.2401)(0.09) \\approx 0.324$."],"hints":["Identify $n$, $p$ and $k$.","Count arrangements with $\\binom{6}{4}$.","Multiply by $0.7^4\\times0.3^2$."],"concept":"Binomial probability mass function.","misconception":"Omitting the number of arrangements.","why":"The binomial coefficient counts the ways to place the 4 successes.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-56","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial: at least one","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A marketing email is opened with probability 0.15 by each recipient independently. If it is sent to 10 recipients, what is the probability that at least one opens it?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8031255956592774,"display":"0.8031"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$1-0.85^{10} \\approx 1-0.1969 = 0.803$."],"hints":["Use the complement: nobody opens it.","Raise 0.85 to the tenth power.","Subtract from 1."],"concept":"At least one via the complement.","misconception":"Multiplying $10\\times0.15$.","why":"10 recipients do not guarantee an opening: $10\\times0.15=1.5>1$ is not a probability.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-57","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial: tail with a cutoff","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A quality plan accepts a batch of 20 items if at most 1 is defective. Each item is defective independently with probability 0.05. What is the probability the batch is accepted?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7358395249438492,"display":"0.7358"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$P(0)+P(1) = 0.95^{20}+20(0.05)(0.95)^{19} \\approx 0.3585+0.3774 = 0.736$."],"hints":["\"At most 1\" means 0 or 1 defectives.","Compute each binomial term.","Add the two probabilities together."],"concept":"Cumulative binomial probability.","misconception":"Using only $P(1)$.","why":"Acceptance includes the no-defect case.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-58","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial: mean and SD","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A bank approves 40% of loan applications independently. Among 50 applications, what is the standard deviation of the number approved? (Give to 2 decimals.)","data":"","answer":{"value":3.4641016151377544,"display":"3.4641"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.01,"options":[],"solution":["$\\mathrm{Var} = np(1-p) = 50(0.4)(0.6) = 12$.","$\\mathrm{SD} = \\sqrt{12} \\approx 3.46$."],"hints":["Variance of a binomial is $np(1-p)$.","Compute the variance first.","Take the square root."],"concept":"Spread of a binomial count.","misconception":"Reporting the variance or $np$.","why":"The SD is the square root of the variance.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-59","topic":"dist","sub":"binomial: most likely count","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Each of 25 independent trials succeeds with probability 0.4. What is the most likely number of successes?","data":"","answer":{"value":10,"display":"10"},"fmt":"i","options":[],"solution":["Mode $=\\lfloor(n+1)p\\rfloor = \\lfloor26\\times0.4\\rfloor = 10$.","Here $np = 10$ exactly."],"hints":["The mode is near $np$.","Use $\\lfloor(n+1)p\\rfloor$.","Compute $26\\times0.4$."],"concept":"Mode of a binomial.","misconception":"Assuming the mode is always 0.","why":"Probabilities rise until the count passes $np$.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-60","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson: exactly","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Customer complaints arrive at an average rate of 3 per day (Poisson). What is the probability of exactly 2 complaints on a given day?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.22404180765538775,"display":"0.224"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$P = e^{-3}3^2/2! = 4.5e^{-3} \\approx 0.224$."],"hints":["Use $e^{-\\lambda}\\lambda^k/k!$.","Set $\\lambda=3$ and $k=2$.","Evaluate the resulting expression numerically."],"concept":"Poisson pmf.","misconception":"Forgetting the factorial.","why":"The factorial arises from unordered arrivals.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-61","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson: rate rescaling","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A help desk receives tickets at a rate of 12 per hour (Poisson). What is the probability of no tickets in a 10-minute period?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.1353352832366127,"display":"0.1353"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["For 10 minutes $\\lambda = 12\\times\\tfrac{10}{60} = 2$.","$P(0) = e^{-2} \\approx 0.135$."],"hints":["Rescale the rate to the 10-minute window.","Ten minutes is one sixth of an hour, so scale 12 accordingly.","The zero-count probability is $e^{-\\lambda}$."],"concept":"Poisson rate scales with time.","misconception":"Using $\\lambda = 12$.","why":"The Poisson parameter is the mean count in the window.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-62","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson: at least","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A server logs an average of 1.5 failures per week (Poisson). What is the probability of at least 2 failures in a week?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.44217459962892547,"display":"0.4422"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$1-P(0)-P(1) = 1-e^{-1.5}(1+1.5) = 1-0.5578 = 0.442$."],"hints":["Use the complement of 0 or 1 failures.","Compute $P(0)$ and $P(1)$.","Subtract their sum from 1."],"concept":"Poisson tail via the complement.","misconception":"Using only $P(2)$.","why":"\"At least 2\" includes all larger counts.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-63","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson: sum of independent","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Online orders arrive as Poisson with mean 4 per hour and phone orders as Poisson with mean 2 per hour, independently. What is the probability of exactly 5 orders in total in one hour?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.16062314104798003,"display":"0.1606"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["The sum is Poisson with mean 6.","$P(5)=e^{-6}6^5/120 \\approx 0.161$."],"hints":["Independent Poisson variables add.","What is the mean of the total?","Apply the pmf with $\\lambda=6$."],"concept":"Sum of Poisson variables.","misconception":"Multiplying the means.","why":"The combined stream is Poisson with the sum of rates.","fam":"dist:clt"},{"id":"DST-64","topic":"dist","sub":"Poisson approximation to binomial","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A bank processes 500 transactions a day, each independently fraudulent with probability 0.004. Use the Poisson approximation to estimate the probability of no fraudulent transaction.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.1353352832366127,"display":"0.1353"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$\\lambda = np = 2$.","$P(0) \\approx e^{-2} \\approx 0.135$ (exact binomial 0.134)."],"hints":["Compute $\\lambda = np$.","Zero events has probability $e^{-\\lambda}$.","Evaluate $e^{-2}$."],"concept":"Poisson approximation.","misconception":"Using $\\lambda = p$.","why":"Many trials, small $p$: use $\\lambda=np$.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-65","topic":"dist","sub":"geometric: at most","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Each sales pitch succeeds with probability 0.2, independently. What is the probability that the first success occurs within the first 3 pitches?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4879999999999999,"display":"0.488"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$1-0.8^3 = 0.488$."],"hints":["Use the complement: no success in 3 pitches.","That is $0.8^3$.","Subtract from 1."],"concept":"Geometric cumulative probability.","misconception":"Using $3\\times0.2$.","why":"The failures must happen three times in a row.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-66","topic":"dist","sub":"geometric: memorylessness","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Sales pitches succeed independently with probability 0.2 each. You have already made 5 unsuccessful pitches. What is the probability that you need at least 3 more pitches to get the first success?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$0.8^2 = 0.64$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$0.8^2 = 0.64$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$0.8^7$","correct":false,"why":"That would include the five failures already observed; the geometric law is memoryless."},{"text":"$0.8^5$","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of 5 failures, which has already occurred."},{"text":"$1-0.8^2$","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of success within 2 more pitches."}],"solution":["At least 3 more pitches means the next 2 fail: $0.8^2 = 0.64$.","Past failures do not matter."],"hints":["At least 3 more means the next two must fail.","Does the past matter?","Compute the probability of two failures."],"concept":"Memoryless property.","misconception":"Including the past failures.","why":"Independent trials have no memory.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-67","topic":"dist","sub":"negative binomial","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Each interview leads to an offer with probability 0.3, independently. What is the probability that the <b>second</b> offer comes on the 5th interview?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.12347999999999996,"display":"0.1235"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["Exactly 1 offer in the first 4, then an offer on the 5th:","$\\binom41(0.3)(0.7)^3\\times0.3 = 4(0.09)(0.343) \\approx 0.1235$."],"hints":["One offer must come in the first four interviews.","The fifth must be an offer.","Multiply the binomial term by 0.3."],"concept":"Waiting time for the second success.","misconception":"Using $\\binom{5}{2}$.","why":"The last trial is fixed as a success.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-68","topic":"dist","sub":"hypergeometric","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A box has 12 laptops, 3 of which are faulty. An auditor tests 4 chosen at random without replacement. What is the probability that exactly one is faulty?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.509090909090909,"display":"0.5091"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$\\binom31\\binom93/\\binom{12}{4} = 3(84)/495 \\approx 0.509$."],"hints":["Count ways to choose one faulty and three good.","Divide by all ways to choose 4 of 12.","Evaluate the resulting expression numerically."],"concept":"Hypergeometric distribution.","misconception":"Using binomial probabilities.","why":"Sampling without replacement changes the probabilities.","fam":"dist:waiting"},{"id":"DST-69","topic":"dist","sub":"uniform: interval","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A delivery arrives at a time uniformly distributed between 2 pm and 6 pm. What is the probability it arrives between 3:30 pm and 5 pm?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.375,"display":"0.375"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Interval length 1.5 out of 4 hours: $1.5/4 = 0.375$."],"hints":["Probability is proportional to length.","Total length is 4 hours.","Divide the sub-interval length by 4."],"concept":"Uniform distribution.","misconception":"Using the midpoint.","why":"Equal-length intervals are equally likely.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-70","topic":"dist","sub":"uniform: conditional","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A delivery arrives at a time uniformly distributed between 2 pm and 6 pm. It has not arrived by 4 pm. What is the probability it arrives before 5 pm?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5,"display":"0.5"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Given it is after 4 pm, the time is uniform on $[4,6]$.","$P = 1/2$."],"hints":["Condition on being after 4 pm.","The remaining distribution is uniform on 4 pm to 6 pm.","What fraction lies before 5 pm?"],"concept":"Conditional uniform distribution.","misconception":"Using the unconditional answer.","why":"Conditioning rescales the uniform window.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-71","topic":"dist","sub":"exponential: survival","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A server’s time to failure is exponential with mean 200 hours. What is the probability it lasts more than 300 hours?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.22313016014842982,"display":"0.2231"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$P(T>300)=e^{-300/200} = e^{-1.5} \\approx 0.223$."],"hints":["The survival function is $e^{-t/\\text{mean}}$.","Compute $t/\\text{mean}$.","Evaluate the exponential."],"concept":"Exponential survival function.","misconception":"Using $1-e^{-1.5}$.","why":"\"Lasts more than\" is the survival probability.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-72","topic":"dist","sub":"exponential: memoryless residual life","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"The server above has already run 100 hours without failure. What is the probability it lasts at least another 50 hours?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.7788007830714049,"display":"0.7788"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["By memorylessness $P(T>150\\mid T>100) = P(T>50) = e^{-0.25} \\approx 0.779$."],"hints":["Use the conditional probability definition.","Memorylessness removes the past.","Evaluate $e^{-50/200}$."],"concept":"Memorylessness of the exponential.","misconception":"Using $e^{-150/200}$.","why":"The remaining life has the original distribution.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-73","topic":"dist","sub":"exponential: comparing two clocks","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent machines fail after exponential times with rates 2 and 3 per year. What is the probability that the first machine fails before the second?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4,"display":"0.4"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P = \\lambda_1/(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2) = 2/5 = 0.4$."],"hints":["Competing exponentials: use a rate ratio.","Rate of machine 1 over the total rate.","Compute $2/(2+3)$."],"concept":"Race between exponential clocks.","misconception":"Answering 1/2.","why":"The faster clock is more likely to ring first.","fam":"dist:continuous"},{"id":"DST-74","topic":"dist","sub":"normal: probability between","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Monthly electricity use of a household is normal with mean 300 kWh and standard deviation 40 kWh. What is the probability use lies between 260 and 380 kWh? (Use standard normal tables.)","data":"","answer":{"value":0.8185946732803799,"display":"0.8186"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.003,"options":[],"solution":["$z$ from $-1$ to $2$.","$\\Phi(2)-\\Phi(-1) = 0.9772-0.1587 = 0.8185$."],"hints":["Standardise each endpoint.","Look up the standard normal cdf at each $z$.","Subtract the lower cdf value from the upper."],"concept":"Normal interval probability.","misconception":"Adding probabilities.","why":"Interval probability is a cdf difference.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-75","topic":"dist","sub":"normal: quantile","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Exam scores are normal with mean 55 and standard deviation 10. What score is exceeded by only the top 10% of students? (Use $z_{0.90}\\approx1.28$.)","data":"","answer":{"value":67.8,"display":"67.8"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.2,"options":[],"solution":["$x = \\mu+z\\sigma = 55+1.28(10) = 67.8$."],"hints":["Top 10% means the 90th percentile.","Use $z\\approx1.28$.","Convert back with $\\mu+z\\sigma$."],"concept":"Normal quantiles.","misconception":"Using $z$ directly.","why":"Quantile means cut-off value.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-76","topic":"dist","sub":"normal: sum of independent","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A commuter’s walking time is $N(12,\\,2^2)$ minutes and bus time is independently $N(25,\\,3^2)$ minutes. What is the probability the total journey exceeds 43 minutes? (Use tables.)","data":"","answer":{"value":0.04804614524496964,"display":"0.048"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.004,"options":[],"solution":["Total $\\sim N(37,\\,13)$, SD $\\approx3.606$.","$z = 6/3.606 \\approx 1.66$.","$P = 1-0.9515 \\approx 0.048$."],"hints":["Add the means and the variances.","Standardise 43 using the combined SD.","Use the upper tail."],"concept":"Sum of independent normals.","misconception":"Adding the SDs.","why":"Variances add, so SD = $\\sqrt{13}$.","fam":"dist:clt"},{"id":"DST-77","topic":"dist","sub":"central limit theorem: sample mean","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Individual basket values have mean ₹900 and standard deviation ₹300 (distribution unknown). For a random sample of 100 baskets, approximately what is the probability that the sample mean exceeds ₹960?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.02275006288725634,"display":"0.0228"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.002,"options":[],"solution":["SD of the mean $=300/\\sqrt{100}=30$.","$z = 60/30 = 2$.","$P \\approx 1-0.9772 = 0.0228$."],"hints":["Find the standard deviation of the sample mean.","Standardise 960.","Use the upper tail of the normal."],"concept":"CLT approximation.","misconception":"Dividing by 100 instead of $\\sqrt{100}$.","why":"Averages are much less variable than single baskets.","fam":"dist:clt"},{"id":"DST-78","topic":"dist","sub":"normal approximation to binomial","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A fair coin is tossed 100 times. Using the normal approximation with continuity correction, what is the approximate probability of at least 60 heads?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.028716493915579067,"display":"0.0287"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.003,"options":[],"solution":["$\\mu=50$, $\\sigma=5$. $P(X\\ge60)\\approx P(Z>(59.5-50)/5)=P(Z>1.9)\\approx0.0287$."],"hints":["Compute mean and standard deviation.","Use 59.5 as the cut-off.","Take the upper-tail probability."],"concept":"Continuity-corrected normal approximation.","misconception":"Using 60 without correction.","why":"The correction bridges discrete and continuous.","fam":"dist:normal"},{"id":"DST-79","topic":"dist","sub":"choosing a distribution","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Which distribution is the most natural model for \"the number of customers who buy among 30 independent visitors, each buying with the same probability\"?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Binomial"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Binomial","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Poisson","correct":false,"why":"There is a fixed number of trials; the Poisson counts events without a fixed number of trials."},{"text":"Geometric","correct":false,"why":"The geometric models the waiting time to a first success, not a count in fixed trials."},{"text":"Uniform","correct":false,"why":"The count is not equally likely over all values."}],"solution":["Fixed number of independent trials with a common success probability: binomial."],"hints":["Is the number of trials fixed?","Are the trials independent with the same $p$?","Match with the distribution."],"concept":"Recognising binomial settings.","misconception":"Using Poisson by default.","why":"Fixed $n$ and constant $p$ identify the binomial.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"DST-80","topic":"dist","sub":"choosing between Poisson and binomial","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Emails arrive in clusters: a busy hour often follows another busy hour. A manager models hourly counts as Poisson. Which assumption is most likely violated?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Independence of counts in different intervals"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Independence of counts in different intervals","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"The count must be non-negative","correct":false,"why":"Counts are non-negative in any case."},{"text":"The rate must be positive","correct":false,"why":"A positive rate is not the issue."},{"text":"The mean must be an integer","correct":false,"why":"Poisson means need not be integers."}],"solution":["Poisson models assume events occur independently at a constant rate.","Clustering breaks independence between intervals."],"hints":["What does Poisson assume about different intervals?","Does clustering conflict with that?","Choose the violated assumption."],"concept":"Checking model assumptions.","misconception":"Assuming Poisson fits any count data.","why":"Clustering induces dependence.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-81","topic":"dist","sub":"comparing variance of binomial and Poisson","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"A count has mean 4 and variance 3.2. Which standard distribution is consistent with these two moments?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"Binomial with $n=20$, $p=0.2$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"Binomial with $n=20$, $p=0.2$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Poisson with $\\lambda=4$","correct":false,"why":"A Poisson variable has variance equal to its mean, 4, not 3.2."},{"text":"Geometric with $p=0.25$","correct":false,"why":"A geometric variable with mean 4 has variance 12."},{"text":"Binomial with $n=8$, $p=0.5$","correct":false,"why":"This has mean 4 but variance 2."}],"solution":["Binomial: $np=4$, $np(1-p)=3.2$ gives $1-p=0.8$, $p=0.2$, $n=20$."],"hints":["Write the mean and variance formulas.","Divide the variance by the mean.","The ratio gives $1-p$."],"concept":"Matching moments to a distribution.","misconception":"Assuming equal mean and variance.","why":"Under-dispersion points to a binomial.","fam":"dist:poisson"},{"id":"DST-82","topic":"dist","sub":"probability integral reading","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"A continuous variable has density $f(x)$ with $f(3)=0.8$. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"The probability of being near 3 is about $0.8\\times$ the interval width, so $P(X=3)=0$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"The probability of being near 3 is about $0.8\\times$ the interval width, so $P(X=3)=0$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$P(X=3)=0.8$","correct":false,"why":"A density value is not a probability; for continuous variables $P(X=3)=0$."},{"text":"$P(X\\le3)=0.8$","correct":false,"why":"That would be a cdf value."},{"text":"$f(3)$ must be at most 1","correct":false,"why":"Density values can exceed 1 when the spread is small."}],"solution":["Density times a small width approximates probability.","Exact points have probability zero."],"hints":["Is a density a probability?","What is $P(X=x)$ for continuous $X$?","Think about intervals."],"concept":"Density versus probability.","misconception":"Reading density as probability.","why":"Probabilities are areas under the density.","fam":"dist:binomial"},{"id":"JNT-01","topic":"joint","sub":"dependence without replacement","diff":1,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two employees are chosen at random, one after the other without replacement, from a team of 9 that includes 4 managers. Let $X_i = 1$ if the $i$th chosen employee is a manager. What is $P(X_1 = 1, X_2 = 1)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.16666666666666666,"display":"1/6"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"16/81","correct":false,"why":"This treats the two choices as independent (with replacement); without replacement the second probability is 3/8.","v":0.19753086419753085},{"text":"4/9","correct":false,"why":"This is the marginal probability $P(X_1 = 1)$.","v":0.4444444444444444},{"text":"3/8","correct":false,"why":"This is the conditional probability $P(X_2 = 1\\mid X_1 = 1)$.","v":0.375}],"solution":["$P(X_1=1,X_2=1) = P(X_1=1)\\,P(X_2=1\\mid X_1=1) = \\dfrac49\\cdot\\dfrac38 = \\dfrac16$."],"hints":["The joint probability is a product of a marginal and a conditional.","After one manager is removed, how many managers remain among how many?","Multiply the marginal by the conditional probability."],"concept":"Joint probability of dependent indicators.","misconception":"Multiplying the marginals.","why":"Sampling without replacement makes the indicators dependent.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-02","topic":"joint","sub":"independence check","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two cards are drawn from the three cards numbered 1, 2, 3, one after the other without replacement. Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ be the numbers drawn. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$X_1$ and $X_2$ are dependent: $P(X_2 = 1\\mid X_1 = 1) = 0 \\ne P(X_2 = 1) = \\tfrac13$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$X_1$ and $X_2$ are dependent: $P(X_2 = 1\\mid X_1 = 1) = 0 \\ne P(X_2 = 1) = \\tfrac13$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent because each card is equally likely at each draw","correct":false,"why":"Equal marginal probabilities do not imply independence; the joint probability of (1, 1) is 0, not $1/9$."},{"text":"They are independent because the draws are random","correct":false,"why":"Randomness does not imply independence; the second draw depends on which card remains."},{"text":"They are dependent only if more than three cards are used","correct":false,"why":"Dependence arises whenever sampling is without replacement, whatever the size."}],"solution":["$P(X_1=1,X_2=1) = 0$ since a card cannot be drawn twice, but $P(X_1=1)P(X_2=1) = \\tfrac13\\cdot\\tfrac13 = \\tfrac19 \\ne 0$.","The joint probability does not factorise, so they are dependent."],"hints":["Compute $P(X_1 = 1, X_2 = 1)$.","Compare it with $P(X_1=1)P(X_2=1)$.","Independence requires the joint to equal the product for every pair of values."],"concept":"Testing independence of two random variables.","misconception":"Checking only the marginal distributions.","why":"One counterexample to the product rule is enough to prove dependence.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-03","topic":"joint","sub":"expectation from a density","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Random variables $X$ and $Y$ share the joint density $f(x,y)=\\tfrac65(x+y^2)$ over the unit square (zero elsewhere). Compute the mean of $X$.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.6,"display":"0.6"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.6","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.6},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is the mean of a uniform variable on (0,1); the density puts more weight on larger $x$.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.4","correct":false,"why":"This is $E[Y]$ or a different moment; for $X$ integrate $x f(x,y)$.","v":0.4},{"text":"0.8","correct":false,"why":"This integrates $x^2 + xy^2$ incorrectly; the second term contributes $\\tfrac12\\cdot\\tfrac13$.","v":0.7999999999999999}],"solution":["$f_X(x) = \\int_0^1\\tfrac65(x+y^2)dy = \\tfrac65\\left(x+\\tfrac13\\right)$.","$E[X] = \\int_0^1 x\\cdot\\tfrac65\\left(x+\\tfrac13\\right)dx = \\tfrac65\\left(\\tfrac13+\\tfrac16\\right) = \\tfrac65\\cdot\\tfrac12 = 0.6$."],"hints":["Find the marginal density of $X$ by integrating out $y$.","Then compute $\\int x f_X(x)\\,dx$.","Or integrate $x f(x,y)$ over the square."],"concept":"Expectation from a joint density.","misconception":"Integrating $f$ without the factor $x$.","why":"The marginal density of $X$ is $f_X(x) = \\int f(x,y)\\,dy$.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-04","topic":"joint","sub":"competing exponentials","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two independent waiting times $X$ and $Y$ are exponential with rates 1 and 3 respectively. What is $P(X<Y)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"1/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.25},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"This would hold for equal rates; $Y$ has the larger rate and so tends to be shorter.","v":0.5},{"text":"3/4","correct":false,"why":"This is $P(Y<X)$, the complementary probability.","v":0.75},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This ratio of rates (1 to 3) is not the probability; the denominator is the sum of the rates.","v":0.3333333333333333}],"solution":["$P(X<Y) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-3x}\\cdot1\\cdot e^{-x}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{1+3} = \\dfrac14$.","(In general $P(X<Y) = \\lambda_X/(\\lambda_X+\\lambda_Y)$.)"],"hints":["Condition on the value of $X$: $P(Y>x) = e^{-3x}$.","Integrate against the density of $X$.","The result is a ratio of rates."],"concept":"Race between independent exponentials.","misconception":"Assuming one half.","why":"The probability that one exponential is first is proportional to its rate.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-05","topic":"joint","sub":"multinomial","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"In a survey, 50% of respondents prefer brand A, 30% brand B and 20% brand C. Among 6 independently chosen respondents, what is the probability that exactly <b>3 prefer A, 2 prefer B and 1 prefers C</b>?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.13499999999999998,"display":"0.135"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.135","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.13499999999999998},{"text":"0.00225","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of one specific order of preferences; there are $6!/(3!2!1!) = 60$ orders.","v":0.00225},{"text":"0.045","correct":false,"why":"The number of arrangements is $\\dfrac{6!}{3!\\,2!\\,1!} = 60$, not $\\binom63 = 20$.","v":0.045},{"text":"0.0823","correct":false,"why":"This assumes the three brands are equally preferred.","v":0.08230452674897117}],"solution":["$P = \\dfrac{6!}{3!\\,2!\\,1!}(0.5)^3(0.3)^2(0.2)^1 = 60\\times0.125\\times0.09\\times0.2 = 0.135$."],"hints":["Count the arrangements of 3 A’s, 2 B’s and 1 C among 6 respondents.","Compute the probability of one arrangement.","Multiply the marginal by the conditional probability."],"concept":"Multinomial probability.","misconception":"Omitting the multinomial coefficient.","why":"Each arrangement has the same probability; the coefficient counts them.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-06","topic":"joint","sub":"geometric probability of meeting","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Two colleagues arrive independently at a café, each at a time uniformly distributed over one hour. Each waits up to <b>10 minutes</b> for the other. What is the probability that they meet?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.30555555555555547,"display":"0.3056"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.3056","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.30555555555555547},{"text":"0.1667","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability that one particular colleague arrives within 10 minutes of a fixed time; both arrival times are random.","v":0.16666666666666666},{"text":"0.0278","correct":false,"why":"This is the area of a small square; the meeting region is a diagonal band.","v":0.027777777777777776},{"text":"0.3333","correct":false,"why":"This treats the full waiting window (10 minutes either side) as a probability; the meeting region is a band within the square.","v":0.3333333333333333}],"solution":["Let $X, Y$ be the arrival times in minutes, uniform on $(0,60)$ and independent. They meet iff $|X-Y|\\le10$.","The complement consists of two triangles with legs 50: area $2\\cdot\\tfrac12\\cdot50^2 = 2500$ of 3600.","$P = 1-\\dfrac{2500}{3600} = \\dfrac{11}{36} \\approx 0.306$."],"hints":["Represent the pair of arrival times as a point in a $60\\times60$ square.","The meeting region is a band around the diagonal.","Use the complement: two triangles."],"concept":"Geometric probability for two uniform variables.","misconception":"Using the marginal rather than the joint region.","why":"Independent uniforms make the pair uniform on the square.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-07","topic":"joint","sub":"symmetry of ranks","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Four independent random variables $X_1,\\dots,X_4$ have the same continuous distribution. What is the probability that $X_3$ is the <b>second smallest</b> of the four?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"1/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.25},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"There are four possible ranks for $X_3$, each equally likely.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/24","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of one specific ordering of all four variables.","v":0.041666666666666664},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"The rank of $X_3$ takes four values, not three.","v":0.3333333333333333}],"solution":["All $4!$ orderings of the four values are equally likely, so the rank of $X_3$ is uniform on $\\{1,2,3,4\\}$.","$P(\\text{rank }2) = 1/4$."],"hints":["Are the four variables exchangeable?","How many possible ranks can $X_3$ have?","Are they equally likely?"],"concept":"Symmetry for i.i.d. continuous variables.","misconception":"Trying to integrate.","why":"Ties have probability zero, so every ranking is equally likely.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-08","topic":"joint","sub":"dependence from a density","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"On the unit square the pair $(X,Y)$ has density $\\tfrac32(x^2+y^2)$. Find the probability that the sum of the two coordinates is below 1.","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"1/4"},"fmt":"p","options":[{"text":"1/4","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.25},{"text":"1/2","correct":false,"why":"The area of the triangle is $\\tfrac12$ but the density is not uniform; it puts more weight near the far corner.","v":0.5},{"text":"1/3","correct":false,"why":"This is the probability of $X<Y$... of the form $P(X+Y<1)$ under $f = x+y$, a different density.","v":0.3333333333333333},{"text":"1/8","correct":false,"why":"This is half of the correct value; the double integral was divided twice.","v":0.125}],"solution":["$P = \\int_0^1\\int_0^{1-x}\\tfrac32(x^2+y^2)\\,dy\\,dx = \\tfrac32\\int_0^1\\left[x^2(1-x)+\\tfrac{(1-x)^3}{3}\\right]dx = \\tfrac32\\left(\\tfrac1{12}+\\tfrac1{12}\\right) = \\tfrac14$."],"hints":["Describe the region $x+y<1$ in the unit square.","Set up the double integral with $y$ from 0 to $1-x$.","Evaluate the inner integral, then the outer."],"concept":"Probability from a joint density over a non-rectangular region.","misconception":"Using area instead of integrating the density.","why":"The density is larger away from the origin, so the near-origin triangle has less than half the mass.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-09","topic":"joint","sub":"independence from a factorised density","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"$X$ and $Y$ have joint density $f(x,y) = 120\\,x^2(1-x)^2\\,y^3$ for $0<x<1$, $0<y<1$ (which factorises as $30x^2(1-x)^2\\times4y^3$). What is $\\mathrm{Var}(X)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.03571428571428571,"display":"0.0357"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"0.0357","correct":true,"why":"","v":0.03571428571428571},{"text":"0.0833","correct":false,"why":"This is the variance of a uniform variable on $(0,1)$; the density here is concentrated around the middle.","v":0.08333333333333333},{"text":"0.5","correct":false,"why":"This is $E[X]$, not the variance.","v":0.5},{"text":"0.2857","correct":false,"why":"This is $E[X^2] = 2/7$, which is not the variance; subtract $(E[X])^2$.","v":0.2857142857142857}],"solution":["The density factorises, so $X$ has density $30x^2(1-x)^2$ on $(0,1)$ (symmetric about $\\tfrac12$).","$E[X] = \\tfrac12$.","$E[X^2] = \\int_0^1 30x^4(1-x)^2dx = 30\\left(\\tfrac15-\\tfrac26+\\tfrac17\\right) = \\tfrac27$.","$\\mathrm{Var}(X) = \\tfrac27-\\tfrac14 = \\tfrac1{28} \\approx 0.0357$."],"hints":["A factorised joint density means independence; read off the density of $X$.","Compute $E[X]$ and $E[X^2]$.","Use $\\mathrm{Var} = E[X^2]-(E[X])^2$."],"concept":"Variance from a marginal density.","misconception":"Using the joint density without factoring.","why":"Independence allows each variable to be treated separately.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-10","topic":"joint","sub":"variance of a difference","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"$R$ and $C$ are independent random variables with the same mean, with $\\mathrm{Var}(R) = 4$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(C) = 9$. What is $E\\big[(R-C)^2\\big]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":13,"display":"13"},"fmt":"i","options":[{"text":"13","correct":true,"why":"","v":13},{"text":"-5","correct":false,"why":"Variances of independent variables add even for a difference.","v":-5},{"text":"1","correct":false,"why":"This subtracts standard deviations; the correct combination adds variances.","v":1},{"text":"36","correct":false,"why":"This multiplies the variances; they should be added.","v":36}],"solution":["Since $E[R-C] = 0$, $E[(R-C)^2] = \\mathrm{Var}(R-C) = \\mathrm{Var}(R)+\\mathrm{Var}(C) = 4+9 = 13$."],"hints":["What is the mean of $R-C$?","Then the expected square equals the variance.","Variances of independent variables add."],"concept":"Variance of a difference.","misconception":"Subtracting variances.","why":"Subtracting an independent variable adds its variability.","fam":"joint:moments"},{"id":"JNT-12","topic":"joint","sub":"marginal from a joint table","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$X$ is the number of items ordered (0, 1 or 2) and $Y$ indicates express delivery (0 or 1). The joint probabilities are: $(0,0)$ 0.20, $(0,1)$ 0.10, $(1,0)$ 0.15, $(1,1)$ 0.25, $(2,0)$ 0.10, $(2,1)$ 0.20. What is $P(X=1)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4,"display":"0.4"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Sum the row: $P(X=1) = 0.15+0.25 = 0.40$."],"hints":["A marginal sums the joint probabilities over the other variable.","Which cells have $X=1$?","Add those probabilities together to get the marginal."],"concept":"Marginal distribution from a joint table.","misconception":"Using a single cell.","why":"Marginalise over all values of $Y$.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-13","topic":"joint","sub":"conditional distribution from a joint table","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Using the joint probabilities $(0,0)$ 0.20, $(0,1)$ 0.10, $(1,0)$ 0.15, $(1,1)$ 0.25, $(2,0)$ 0.10, $(2,1)$ 0.20 for $(X,Y)$, what is $P(X=2\\mid Y=1)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.36363636363636365,"display":"0.3636"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(Y=1) = 0.10+0.25+0.20 = 0.55$.","$P(X=2\\mid Y=1) = 0.20/0.55 \\approx 0.364$."],"hints":["Find $P(Y=1)$ by summing the column.","Take the joint probability for $X=2$, $Y=1$.","Divide the second by the first."],"concept":"Conditional from a joint table.","misconception":"Dividing by the row total.","why":"Condition on the column total.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-14","topic":"joint","sub":"independence check","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"For the joint probabilities $(0,0)$ 0.20, $(0,1)$ 0.10, $(1,0)$ 0.15, $(1,1)$ 0.25, $(2,0)$ 0.10, $(2,1)$ 0.20, are $X$ and $Y$ independent?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"No: for example $P(X=0,Y=0)=0.20 \\ne P(X=0)P(Y=0)=0.30\\times0.45$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"No: for example $P(X=0,Y=0)=0.20 \\ne P(X=0)P(Y=0)=0.30\\times0.45$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"Yes, because all probabilities are positive","correct":false,"why":"Positivity does not imply independence."},{"text":"Yes, because the probabilities sum to 1","correct":false,"why":"Every joint pmf sums to 1."},{"text":"Yes, since $P(X=0,Y=0) = P(X=0)P(Y=0)$","correct":false,"why":"The product is 0.135, not 0.20."}],"solution":["$P(X=0)=0.30$ and $P(Y=0)=0.45$. Product $=0.135 \\ne 0.20$.","One failure is enough to rule out independence."],"hints":["Find the two marginals for the chosen cell.","Multiply the two marginal probabilities together.","Compare with the joint cell."],"concept":"Testing independence from a table.","misconception":"Checking only the sums.","why":"Independence requires all cells to factorise.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-15","topic":"joint","sub":"expectation of a sum from the joint pmf","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"With the same joint probabilities for $(X,Y)$, what is $E[X+Y]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":1.55,"display":"1.55"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$E[X] = 0.35\\cdot... $ compute: $E[X] = 0(0.30)+1(0.40)+2(0.30) = 1.0$.","$E[Y] = 0.55$.","$E[X+Y] = 1.55$."],"hints":["Linearity: $E[X+Y]=E[X]+E[Y]$.","Find each marginal mean.","Add the two marginal means together."],"concept":"Linearity of expectation.","misconception":"Multiplying the means.","why":"Linearity holds with or without independence.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-16","topic":"joint","sub":"covariance from a table","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"With the same joint probabilities, what is $\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.09999999999999998,"display":"0.1"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$E[XY] = 1(0.25)+2(0.20) = 0.65$.","$E[X]E[Y] = 1.0\\times0.55$.","$\\mathrm{Cov} = 0.65-0.55 = 0.10$."],"hints":["Use $E[XY]-E[X]E[Y]$.","Only cells with $Y=1$ contribute to $E[XY]$.","Subtract the product of means."],"concept":"Covariance via $E[XY]$.","misconception":"Using $E[X]E[Y]$ alone.","why":"Positive covariance suggests orders and express delivery move together.","fam":"joint:moments"},{"id":"JNT-17","topic":"joint","sub":"probability of a region","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"With the same joint probabilities, what is $P(X\\ge1,\\;Y=0)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"0.25"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Cells $(1,0)$ and $(2,0)$: $0.15+0.10 = 0.25$."],"hints":["Which cells satisfy both conditions?","List those cells explicitly before adding.","Add the probabilities."],"concept":"Probability of a region in the table.","misconception":"Including the cells with $Y=1$.","why":"Both conditions must hold at once.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-18","topic":"joint","sub":"uniform on a triangle","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$(X,Y)$ is uniformly distributed on the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$. What is $P(X+Y\\le1)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.25,"display":"0.25"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Area of the triangle $=2$.","The region $x+y\\le1$ has area $0.5$.","$P = 0.5/2 = 0.25$."],"hints":["Probability equals area ratio.","Find the area of the whole triangle.","Find the area of the smaller triangle."],"concept":"Geometric probability for a joint uniform.","misconception":"Using lengths instead of areas.","why":"A triangle scaled by 1/2 has a quarter of the area.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-19","topic":"joint","sub":"marginal from a joint density","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$(X,Y)$ has joint density $f(x,y)=x+y$ on the unit square. What is $P(X\\le0.5)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.375,"display":"0.375"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["Marginal $f_X(x) = \\int_0^1(x+y)\\,dy = x+\\tfrac12$.","$P(X\\le0.5)=\\int_0^{0.5}(x+0.5)dx = 0.125+0.25 = 0.375$."],"hints":["Integrate out $y$ to get the marginal density of $X$.","The marginal is $x+\\tfrac12$.","Integrate from 0 to 0.5."],"concept":"Marginal density.","misconception":"Integrating only over $x$.","why":"A marginal integrates over the other variable.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-20","topic":"joint","sub":"independence from density factorisation","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":false,"stem":"Which joint density on the unit square describes <b>independent</b> $X$ and $Y$?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$f(x,y)=4xy$"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$f(x,y)=4xy$","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$f(x,y)=x+y$","correct":false,"why":"This does not factor into a function of $x$ times a function of $y$."},{"text":"$f(x,y)=2(1-x)(1-y)\\cdot2\\,$ restricted to $x+y\\le1$","correct":false,"why":"The restricted support (a triangle) links the variables."},{"text":"$f(x,y)=2$ on the triangle $x+y\\le1$","correct":false,"why":"The triangular support makes $X$ and $Y$ dependent."}],"solution":["Independence needs a product form on a rectangular support.","$4xy$ factors as $(2x)(2y)$ on $[0,1]^2$."],"hints":["Look for a product of a function of $x$ and a function of $y$.","Check the support is a rectangle.","Pick the matching density."],"concept":"Factorisation criterion.","misconception":"Ignoring the support.","why":"A non-rectangular support forces dependence.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-21","topic":"joint","sub":"sum of independent dice: joint reading","diff":1,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$X$ and $Y$ are the results of two fair dice. What is $P(X>Y)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.4166666666666667,"display":"5/12"},"fmt":"p","options":[],"solution":["Ties have probability $6/36$; by symmetry the rest splits equally.","$P = (1-1/6)/2 = 5/12 = 15/36$."],"hints":["Find the probability of a tie.","The remaining outcomes split symmetrically.","Halve the non-tie probability."],"concept":"Symmetry in a joint distribution.","misconception":"Answering 1/2.","why":"Ties take away some probability.","fam":"joint:moments"},{"id":"JNT-23","topic":"joint","sub":"correlation sign interpretation","diff":2,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Returns on two stocks have correlation $-0.8$. Which interpretation is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"When one is above its average the other tends to be below its average"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"When one is above its average the other tends to be below its average","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"When one rises, the other always falls by 80% as much","correct":false,"why":"Correlation is a tendency, not a deterministic ratio."},{"text":"80% of the time they move in opposite directions","correct":false,"why":"Correlation is not a proportion of days."},{"text":"The stocks are independent","correct":false,"why":"Independence implies zero correlation, not $-0.8$."}],"solution":["A strong negative correlation means a tendency to move in opposite directions relative to their means."],"hints":["What does the sign tell you?","Is correlation a probability?","Choose the statement about tendencies."],"concept":"Interpreting correlation.","misconception":"Reading correlation as a percentage of days.","why":"It measures linear association strength.","fam":"joint:diagnose"},{"id":"JNT-24","topic":"joint","sub":"zero correlation without independence","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"$X$ takes the values $-1, 0, 1$ with probability $1/3$ each, and $Y=X^2$. Which statement is true?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$X$ and $Y$ are uncorrelated but not independent"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$X$ and $Y$ are uncorrelated but not independent","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$X$ and $Y$ are independent because they are uncorrelated","correct":false,"why":"Zero correlation does not imply independence."},{"text":"$X$ and $Y$ are positively correlated","correct":false,"why":"The covariance is $E[X^3]-E[X]E[X^2]=0$."},{"text":"$X$ and $Y$ are negatively correlated","correct":false,"why":"The covariance is zero, not negative."}],"solution":["$E[X]=0$, $E[XY]=E[X^3]=0$, so $\\mathrm{Cov}=0$.","But $Y$ is determined by $X$, so they are dependent."],"hints":["Compute $E[XY]=E[X^3]$.","Find the covariance.","Does knowing $X$ determine $Y$?"],"concept":"Uncorrelated does not mean independent.","misconception":"Equating zero correlation with independence.","why":"Correlation only measures linear association.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-25","topic":"joint","sub":"conditional expectation from a table","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"With joint probabilities $(0,0)$ 0.20, $(0,1)$ 0.10, $(1,0)$ 0.15, $(1,1)$ 0.25, $(2,0)$ 0.10, $(2,1)$ 0.20, what is $E[X\\mid Y=1]$?","data":"","answer":{"value":1.1818181818181817,"display":"1.1818"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.001,"options":[],"solution":["$P(Y=1)=0.55$.","$E[X\\mid Y=1]=(0\\cdot0.10+1\\cdot0.25+2\\cdot0.20)/0.55 = 0.65/0.55\\approx1.18$."],"hints":["Restrict to the cells with $Y=1$.","Renormalise by $P(Y=1)$.","Take the weighted mean of $X$."],"concept":"Conditional expectation.","misconception":"Using the unconditional mean.","why":"Given $Y=1$, larger orders are more likely.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-26","topic":"joint","sub":"min and max of two independent uniforms","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$U$ and $V$ are independent uniform variables on $(0,1)$. What is $P(\\min(U,V)>0.3)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.48999999999999994,"display":"0.49"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["The minimum exceeds 0.3 only if both do: $0.7\\times0.7 = 0.49$."],"hints":["When is the minimum above 0.3?","Both variables must exceed 0.3.","Multiply the probabilities."],"concept":"Minimum of independent variables.","misconception":"Using $0.7$ only.","why":"Both must be large for the minimum to be large.","fam":"joint:continuous"},{"id":"JNT-27","topic":"joint","sub":"variance of a sum with dependence","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"$X$ and $Y$ each have variance 4 and covariance 1.5. What is the variance of $X-Y$?","data":"","answer":{"value":5,"display":"5"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$\\mathrm{Var}(X-Y)=\\mathrm{Var}X+\\mathrm{Var}Y-2\\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y) = 8-3 = 5$."],"hints":["Variance of a difference subtracts twice the covariance.","Add the two variances.","Subtract $2\\times1.5$."],"concept":"Variance of a difference.","misconception":"Adding the covariance.","why":"Positive covariance makes differences less variable.","fam":"joint:moments"},{"id":"JNT-28","topic":"joint","sub":"joint probability of ordered pair from draws","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A box has 3 red and 2 blue tokens. Two are drawn without replacement. Let $X$ = number of red tokens and $Y$ = 1 if the first token is red, 0 otherwise. What is $P(X=2,\\,Y=1)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.3,"display":"0.3"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["First red $3/5$, second red $2/4$.","$P = 0.3$."],"hints":["Two reds means both draws are red.","The first being red is then automatic.","Multiply $3/5$ and $2/4$."],"concept":"Joint probability with dependence.","misconception":"Using replacement probabilities.","why":"Without replacement the second probability is $2/4$.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-29","topic":"joint","sub":"portfolio expectation and risk","diff":3,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"A portfolio puts weight 0.6 on asset $A$ and 0.4 on asset $B$. Their returns have standard deviations 10% and 20% and correlation 0.25. What is the portfolio standard deviation (to 1 decimal place, in %)?","data":"","answer":{"value":11.135528725660043,"display":"11.1355"},"fmt":"d","tol":0.1,"options":[],"solution":["Variance $=0.36(100)+0.16(400)+2(0.6)(0.4)(0.25)(10)(20) = 36+64+24 = 124$.","SD $\\approx11.1\\%$."],"hints":["Write the variance of a weighted sum.","Include the covariance term using the correlation.","Take the square root."],"concept":"Portfolio variance.","misconception":"Taking a weighted average of SDs.","why":"Imperfect correlation reduces risk below the weighted average.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-30","topic":"joint","sub":"joint table with unknown cell","diff":2,"kind":"num","diag":false,"stem":"Joint probabilities for $(X,Y)$ with $X,Y\\in\\{0,1\\}$: $(0,0)$ 0.35, $(0,1)$ 0.15, $(1,0)$ 0.25 and $(1,1)$ is unknown. What is $P(X=1)$?","data":"","answer":{"value":0.5,"display":"0.5"},"fmt":"d","options":[],"solution":["$P(1,1) = 1-0.75 = 0.25$.","$P(X=1) = 0.25+0.25 = 0.50$."],"hints":["All cells sum to 1.","Find the missing cell.","Add the two probabilities in the row for X equal to 1."],"concept":"Completing a joint pmf.","misconception":"Not using the total of 1.","why":"The unknown cell is determined by normalisation.","fam":"joint:discrete"},{"id":"JNT-31","topic":"joint","sub":"conditional independence versus independence","diff":3,"kind":"mcq","diag":true,"stem":"Two analysts’ forecasts $F_1$ and $F_2$ are independent <i>given</i> the true state, but both depend on the state. Which statement is correct?","data":"","answer":{"value":null,"display":"$F_1$ and $F_2$ are generally dependent unconditionally"},"fmt":"d","options":[{"text":"$F_1$ and $F_2$ are generally dependent unconditionally","correct":true,"why":""},{"text":"$F_1$ and $F_2$ are independent unconditionally too","correct":false,"why":"Common dependence on the state links them."},{"text":"$F_1$ and $F_2$ are mutually exclusive","correct":false,"why":"Exclusivity is a different idea."},{"text":"Conditional independence implies zero covariance","correct":false,"why":"Only within each state; overall they may covary."}],"solution":["Given the state they are independent, but both reflect it.","Averaging over states typically induces positive association."],"hints":["Both forecasts are driven by the state.","What happens when you average over states?","Choose the matching statement."],"concept":"Conditional versus unconditional independence.","misconception":"Assuming conditional independence carries over.","why":"Common causes create unconditional association.","fam":"joint:discrete"}],"topics":[{"id":"space","name":"Sample spaces and events","short":"Events","pre":[],"blurb":"Outcomes, events, set operations and event algebra."},{"id":"counting","name":"Counting methods","short":"Counting","pre":[],"blurb":"Multiplication principle, permutations, combinations, partitions."},{"id":"axioms","name":"Probability rules","short":"Rules","pre":["space","counting"],"blurb":"Axioms, classical probability, complements, addition and inclusion-exclusion."},{"id":"cond","name":"Conditional probability","short":"Conditional","pre":["axioms"],"blurb":"Reduced sample spaces, the multiplication rule and sequential experiments."},{"id":"indep","name":"Independence","short":"Independence","pre":["cond"],"blurb":"Independent versus mutually exclusive events; systems and repeated trials."},{"id":"bayes","name":"Total probability and Bayes","short":"Bayes","pre":["cond","indep"],"blurb":"Partitions, the law of total probability, Bayes’ theorem and base rates."},{"id":"rv","name":"Random variables and expectation","short":"Expectation","pre":["axioms"],"blurb":"Distributions, expected value, variance and linearity."},{"id":"dist","name":"Standard distributions","short":"Distributions","pre":["rv","indep"],"blurb":"Binomial, geometric, Poisson, hypergeometric and normal models."},{"id":"joint","name":"Joint distributions","short":"Joint","pre":["rv","indep"],"blurb":"Joint and marginal distributions, covariance, sums of variables."}]};